c": "لِلْكَٰفِرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrِfَٰkْlِl", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 34, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 35, "arabic": "ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ", "translit": "َnyِnِmْwʾُmْlٱ", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 36, "arabic": "سَبِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِbَs", "root": "سبل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 142, "text": "إِنَّ ٱلْمُنَٰفِقِينَ يُخَٰدِعُونَ ٱللَّهَ وَهُوَ خَٰدِعُهُمْ وَإِذَا قَامُوٓا۟ إِلَى ٱلصَّلَوٰةِ قَامُوا۟ كُسَالَىٰ يُرَآءُونَ ٱلنَّاسَ وَلَا يَذْكُرُونَ ٱللَّهَ إِلَّا قَلِيلًا ", "translation": "Indeed, the hypocrites [think to] deceive Allāh, but He is deceiving them. And when they stand for prayer, they stand lazily, showing [themselves to] the people and not remembering Allāh except a little,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْمُنَٰفِقِينَ", "translit": "َnyِqِfَٰnُmْlٱ", "root": "نفق"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يُخَٰدِعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعِdَٰhkُy", "root": "خدع"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "خَٰدِعُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُʿعِdَٰhk", "root": "خدع"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَإِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "قَامُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُmāَq", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِلَى", "translit": "àَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلصَّلَوٰةِ", "translit": "ِẗٰwَlَّṣlٱ", "root": "صلو"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "قَامُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُmāَq", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "كُسَالَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlāَsُk", "root": "كسل"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "يُرَآءُونَ", "translit": "َnwُءٓāَrُy", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسَ", "translit": "َsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "يَذْكُرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrُkْhdَy", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "قَلِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِlَq", "root": "قلل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 143, "text": "مُّذَبْذَبِينَ بَيْنَ ذَٰلِكَ لَآ إِلَىٰ هَٰٓؤُلَآءِ وَلَآ إِلَىٰ هَٰٓؤُلَآءِۚ وَمَن يُضْلِلِ ٱللَّهُ فَلَن تَجِدَ لَهُۥ سَبِيلًا ", "translation": "Wavering between them, [belonging] neither to these [i.e., the believers] nor to those [i.e., the disbelievers]. And whoever Allāh sends astray - never will you find for him a way.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مُّذَبْذَبِينَ", "translit": "َnyِbَhdْbَhdُّm", "root": "ذبذب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بَيْنَ", "translit": "َnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَآ", "translit": "ٓāَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "هَٰٓؤُلَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَlُwʾَٰٓh", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَلَآ", "translit": "ٓāَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "هَٰٓؤُلَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَlُwʾَٰٓh", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "يُضْلِلِ", "translit": "ِlِlْḍُy", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "فَلَن", "translit": "nَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "تَجِدَ", "translit": "َdِjgَt", "root": "وجد"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "سَبِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِbَs", "root": "سبل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 144, "text": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ لَا تَتَّخِذُوا۟ ٱلْكَٰفِرِينَ أَوْلِيَآءَ مِن دُونِ ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَۚ أَتُرِيدُونَ أَن تَجْعَلُوا۟ لِلَّهِ عَلَيْكُمْ سُلْطَٰنًا مُّبِينًا ", "translation": "O you who have believed, do not take the disbelievers as allies instead of the believers. Do you wish to give Allāh against yourselves a clear case?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "تَتَّخِذُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُhdِhkَّtَt", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْكَٰفِرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrِfَٰkْlٱ", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَوْلِيَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَyِlْwَaʾ", "root": "ولي"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "دُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُd", "root": "دون"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ", "translit": "َnyِnِmْwʾُmْlٱ", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "أَتُرِيدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdyِrُtَaʾ", "root": "رود"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "تَجْعَلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlَʿعْjgَt", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "لِلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِl", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "عَلَيْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "سُلْطَٰنًا", "translit": "āًnَٰṭْlُs", "root": "سلط"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "مُّبِينًا", "translit": "āًnyِbُّm", "root": "بين"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 145, "text": "إِنَّ ٱلْمُنَٰفِقِينَ فِى ٱلدَّرْكِ ٱلْأَسْفَلِ مِنَ ٱلنَّارِ وَلَن تَجِدَ لَهُمْ نَصِيرًا ", "translation": "Indeed, the hypocrites will be in the lowest depths of the Fire - and never will you find for them a helper -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْمُنَٰفِقِينَ", "translit": "َnyِqِfَٰnُmْlٱ", "root": "نفق"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلدَّرْكِ", "translit": "ِkْrَّdlٱ", "root": "درك"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْأَسْفَلِ", "translit": "ِlَfْsَaʾْlٱ", "root": "سفل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلنَّارِ", "translit": "ِrāَّnlٱ", "root": "نور"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَلَن", "translit": "nَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تَجِدَ", "translit": "َdِjgَt", "root": "وجد"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "نَصِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِṣَn", "root": "نصر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 146, "text": "إِلَّا ٱلَّذِينَ تَابُوا۟ وَأَصْلَحُوا۟ وَٱعْتَصَمُوا۟ بِٱللَّهِ وَأَخْلَصُوا۟ دِينَهُمْ لِلَّهِ فَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ مَعَ ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَۖ وَسَوْفَ يُؤْتِ ٱللَّهُ ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ أَجْرًا عَظِيمًا ", "translation": "Except for those who repent, correct themselves, hold fast to Allāh, and are sincere in their religion for Allāh, for those will be with the believers. And Allāh is going to give the believers a great reward.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تَابُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُbāَt", "root": "توب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَأَصْلَحُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُḥَlْṣَaʾَw", "root": "صلح"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَٱعْتَصَمُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُmَṣَtْʿعٱَw", "root": "عصم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بِٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱِb", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَأَخْلَصُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُṣَlْhkَaʾَw", "root": "خلص"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "دِينَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَnyِd", "root": "دين"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لِلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِl", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مَعَ", "translit": "َʿعَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ", "translit": "َnyِnِmْwʾُmْlٱ", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَسَوْفَ", "translit": "َfْwَsَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "يُؤْتِ", "translit": "ِtْwʾُy", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ", "translit": "َnyِnِmْwʾُmْlٱ", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "أَجْرًا", "translit": "āًrْjgَaʾ", "root": "اجر"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "عَظِيمًا", "translit": "āًmyِẓَʿع", "root": "عظم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 147, "text": "مَّا يَفْعَلُ ٱللَّهُ بِعَذَابِكُمْ إِن شَكَرْتُمْ وَءَامَنتُمْۚ وَكَانَ ٱللَّهُ شَاكِرًا عَلِيمًا ", "translation": "What would Allāh do with [i.e., gain from] your punishment if you are grateful and believe? And ever is Allāh Appreciative[1] and Knowing.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مَّا", "translit": "āَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَفْعَلُ", "translit": "ُlَʿعْfَy", "root": "فعل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِعَذَابِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِbāَhdَʿعِb", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "شَكَرْتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtْrَkَhs", "root": "شكر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَءَامَنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnَmāَءَw", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَكَانَ", "translit": "َnāَkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "شَاكِرًا", "translit": "āًrِkāَhs", "root": "شكر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "عَلِيمًا", "translit": "āًmyِlَʿع", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 148, "text": "لَّا يُحِبُّ ٱللَّهُ ٱلْجَهْرَ بِٱلسُّوٓءِ مِنَ ٱلْقَوْلِ إِلَّا مَن ظُلِمَۚ وَكَانَ ٱللَّهُ سَمِيعًا عَلِيمًا ", "translation": "Allāh does not like the public mention of evil except by one who has been wronged. And ever is Allāh Hearing and Knowing.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَّا", "translit": "āَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يُحِبُّ", "translit": "ُّbِḥُy", "root": "حبب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْجَهْرَ", "translit": "َrْhَjgْlٱ", "root": "جهر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِٱلسُّوٓءِ", "translit": "ِءٓwُّslٱِb", "root": "سوا"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْقَوْلِ", "translit": "ِlْwَqْlٱ", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ظُلِمَ", "translit": "َmِlُẓ", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَكَانَ", "translit": "َnāَkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "سَمِيعًا", "translit": "āًʿعyِmَs", "root": "سمع"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "عَلِيمًا", "translit": "āًmyِlَʿع", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 149, "text": "إِن تُبْدُوا۟ خَيْرًا أَوْ تُخْفُوهُ أَوْ تَعْفُوا۟ عَن سُوٓءٍ فَإِنَّ ٱللَّهَ كَانَ عَفُوًّا قَدِيرًا ", "translation": "If [instead] you show [some] good or conceal it or pardon an offense - indeed, Allāh is ever Pardoning and Competent.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تُبْدُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُdْbُt", "root": "بدو"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "خَيْرًا", "translit": "āًrْyَhk", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "تُخْفُوهُ", "translit": "ُhwُfْhkُt", "root": "خفي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "تَعْفُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُfْʿعَt", "root": "عفو"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عَن", "translit": "nَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "سُوٓءٍ", "translit": "ٍءٓwُs", "root": "سوا"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فَإِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "عَفُوًّا", "translit": "āًّwُfَʿع", "root": "عفو"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "قَدِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِdَq", "root": "قدر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 150, "text": "إِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ يَكْفُرُونَ بِٱللَّهِ وَرُسُلِهِۦ وَيُرِيدُونَ أَن يُفَرِّقُوا۟ بَيْنَ ٱللَّهِ وَرُسُلِهِۦ وَيَقُولُونَ نُؤْمِنُ بِبَعْضٍ وَنَكْفُرُ بِبَعْضٍ وَيُرِيدُونَ أَن يَتَّخِذُوا۟ بَيْنَ ذَٰلِكَ سَبِيلًا ", "translation": "Indeed, those who disbelieve in Allāh and His messengers and wish to discriminate between Allāh and His messengers and say, \"We believe in some and disbelieve in others,\" and wish to adopt a way in between -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَكْفُرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrُfْkَy", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱِb", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَرُسُلِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِlُsُrَw", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَيُرِيدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdyِrُyَw", "root": "رود"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يُفَرِّقُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُqِّrَfُy", "root": "فرق"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بَيْنَ", "translit": "َnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَرُسُلِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِlُsُrَw", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَيَقُولُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlwُqَyَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "نُؤْمِنُ", "translit": "ُnِmْwʾُn", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "بِبَعْضٍ", "translit": "ٍḍْʿعَbِb", "root": "بعض"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَنَكْفُرُ", "translit": "ُrُfْkَnَw", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "بِبَعْضٍ", "translit": "ٍḍْʿعَbِb", "root": "بعض"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "وَيُرِيدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdyِrُyَw", "root": "رود"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "يَتَّخِذُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُhdِhkَّtَy", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "بَيْنَ", "translit": "َnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "سَبِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِbَs", "root": "سبل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 151, "text": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ هُمُ ٱلْكَٰفِرُونَ حَقًّاۚ وَأَعْتَدْنَا لِلْكَٰفِرِينَ عَذَابًا مُّهِينًا ", "translation": "Those are the disbelievers, truly. And We have prepared for the disbelievers a humiliating punishment.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هُمُ", "translit": "ُmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْكَٰفِرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrِfَٰkْlٱ", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "حَقًّا", "translit": "āًّqَḥ", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَأَعْتَدْنَا", "translit": "āَnْdَtْʿعَaʾَw", "root": "عتد"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لِلْكَٰفِرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrِfَٰkْlِl", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "عَذَابًا", "translit": "āًbāَhdَʿع", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مُّهِينًا", "translit": "āًnyِhُّm", "root": "هون"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 152, "text": "وَٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ بِٱللَّهِ وَرُسُلِهِۦ وَلَمْ يُفَرِّقُوا۟ بَيْنَ أَحَدٍ مِّنْهُمْ أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ سَوْفَ يُؤْتِيهِمْ أُجُورَهُمْۗ وَكَانَ ٱللَّهُ غَفُورًا رَّحِيمًا ", "translation": "But they who believe in Allāh and His messengers and do not discriminate between any of them - to those He is going to give their rewards. And ever is Allāh Forgiving and Merciful.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱِb", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَرُسُلِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِlُsُrَw", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يُفَرِّقُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُqِّrَfُy", "root": "فرق"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بَيْنَ", "translit": "َnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَحَدٍ", "translit": "ٍdَḥَaʾ", "root": "احد"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مِّنْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "سَوْفَ", "translit": "َfْwَs", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "يُؤْتِيهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhyِtْwʾُy", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "أُجُورَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَrwُjgُaʾ", "root": "اجر"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَكَانَ", "translit": "َnāَkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "غَفُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُfَغ", "root": "غفر"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "رَّحِيمًا", "translit": "āًmyِḥَّr", "root": "رحم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 153, "text": "يَسْـَٔلُكَ أَهْلُ ٱلْكِتَٰبِ أَن تُنَزِّلَ عَلَيْهِمْ كِتَٰبًا مِّنَ ٱلسَّمَآءِۚ فَقَدْ سَأَلُوا۟ مُوسَىٰٓ أَكْبَرَ مِن ذَٰلِكَ فَقَالُوٓا۟ أَرِنَا ٱللَّهَ جَهْرَةً فَأَخَذَتْهُمُ ٱلصَّٰعِقَةُ بِظُلْمِهِمْۚ ثُمَّ ٱتَّخَذُوا۟ ٱلْعِجْلَ مِنۢ بَعْدِ مَا جَآءَتْهُمُ ٱلْبَيِّنَٰتُ فَعَفَوْنَا عَن ذَٰلِكَۚ وَءَاتَيْنَا مُوسَىٰ سُلْطَٰنًا مُّبِينًا ", "translation": "The People of the Scripture ask you to bring down to them a book from the heaven. But they had asked of Moses [even] greater than that and said, \"Show us Allāh outright,\" so the thunderbolt struck them for their wrongdoing. Then they took the calf [for worship] after clear evidences had come to them, and We pardoned that. And We gave Moses a clear authority.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَسْـَٔلُكَ", "translit": "َkُlَٔـْsَy", "root": "سال"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَهْلُ", "translit": "ُlْhَaʾ", "root": "اهل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْكِتَٰبِ", "translit": "ِbَٰtِkْlٱ", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "تُنَزِّلَ", "translit": "َlِّzَnُt", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "كِتَٰبًا", "translit": "āًbَٰtِk", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "سَأَلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlَaʾَs", "root": "سال"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مُوسَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَswُm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "أَكْبَرَ", "translit": "َrَbْkَaʾ", "root": "كبر"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "فَقَالُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُlāَqَf", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "أَرِنَا", "translit": "āَnِrَaʾ", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "جَهْرَةً", "translit": "ًẗَrْhَjg", "root": "جهر"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "فَأَخَذَتْهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhْtَhdَhkَaʾَf", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "ٱلصَّٰعِقَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَqِʿعَّٰṣlٱ", "root": "صعق"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "بِظُلْمِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِmْlُẓِb", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "ٱتَّخَذُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُhdَhkَّtٱ", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "ٱلْعِجْلَ", "translit": "َlْjgِʿعْlٱ", "root": "عجل"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "مِنۢ", "translit": "ۢnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "بَعْدِ", "translit": "ِdْʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "جَآءَتْهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhْtَءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "ٱلْبَيِّنَٰتُ", "translit": "ُtَٰnِّyَbْlٱ", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "فَعَفَوْنَا", "translit": "āَnْwَfَʿعَf", "root": "عفو"}, {"id": 32, "arabic": "عَن", "translit": "nَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 33, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 34, "arabic": "وَءَاتَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَtāَءَw", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 35, "arabic": "مُوسَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَswُm", "root": ""}, {"id": 36, "arabic": "سُلْطَٰنًا", "translit": "āًnَٰṭْlُs", "root": "سلط"}, {"id": 37, "arabic": "مُّبِينًا", "translit": "āًnyِbُّm", "root": "بين"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 154, "text": "وَرَفَعْنَا فَوْقَهُمُ ٱلطُّورَ بِمِيثَٰقِهِمْ وَقُلْنَا لَهُمُ ٱدْخُلُوا۟ ٱلْبَابَ سُجَّدًا وَقُلْنَا لَهُمْ لَا تَعْدُوا۟ فِى ٱلسَّبْتِ وَأَخَذْنَا مِنْهُم مِّيثَٰقًا غَلِيظًا ", "translation": "And We raised over them the mount for [refusal of] their covenant; and We said to them, \"Enter the gate bowing humbly\"; and We said to them, \"Do not transgress on the sabbath\"; and We took from them a solemn covenant.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَرَفَعْنَا", "translit": "āَnْʿعَfَrَw", "root": "رفع"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فَوْقَهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhَqْwَf", "root": "فوق"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلطُّورَ", "translit": "َrwُّṭlٱ", "root": "طور"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِمِيثَٰقِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِqَٰhtyِmِb", "root": "وثق"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَقُلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlُqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱدْخُلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlُhkْdٱ", "root": "دخل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْبَابَ", "translit": "َbāَbْlٱ", "root": "بوب"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "سُجَّدًا", "translit": "āًdَّjgُs", "root": "سجد"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَقُلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlُqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "تَعْدُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُdْʿعَt", "root": "عدو"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱلسَّبْتِ", "translit": "ِtْbَّslٱ", "root": "سبت"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَأَخَذْنَا", "translit": "āَnْhdَhkَaʾَw", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "مِنْهُم", "translit": "mُhْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "مِّيثَٰقًا", "translit": "āًqَٰhtyِّm", "root": "وثق"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "غَلِيظًا", "translit": "āًẓyِlَغ", "root": "غلظ"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 155, "text": "فَبِمَا نَقْضِهِم مِّيثَٰقَهُمْ وَكُفْرِهِم بِـَٔايَٰتِ ٱللَّهِ وَقَتْلِهِمُ ٱلْأَنۢبِيَآءَ بِغَيْرِ حَقٍّ وَقَوْلِهِمْ قُلُوبُنَا غُلْفٌۢۚ بَلْ طَبَعَ ٱللَّهُ عَلَيْهَا بِكُفْرِهِمْ فَلَا يُؤْمِنُونَ إِلَّا قَلِيلًا ", "translation": "And [We cursed them][1] for their breaking of the covenant and their disbelief in the signs of Allāh and their killing of the prophets without right and their saying, \"Our hearts are wrapped\" [i.e., sealed against reception]. Rather, Allāh has sealed them because of their disbelief, so they believe not, except for a few.[2]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَبِمَا", "translit": "āَmِbَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "نَقْضِهِم", "translit": "mِhِḍْqَn", "root": "نقض"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِّيثَٰقَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَqَٰhtyِّm", "root": "وثق"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَكُفْرِهِم", "translit": "mِhِrْfُkَw", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِـَٔايَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰyāَٔـِb", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَقَتْلِهِمُ", "translit": "ُmِhِlْtَqَw", "root": "قتل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْأَنۢبِيَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَyِbۢnَaʾْlٱ", "root": "نبا"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بِغَيْرِ", "translit": "ِrْyَغِb", "root": "غير"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "حَقٍّ", "translit": "ٍّqَḥ", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَقَوْلِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِlْwَqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "قُلُوبُنَا", "translit": "āَnُbwُlُq", "root": "قلب"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "غُلْفٌۢ", "translit": "ٌۢfْlُغ", "root": "غلف"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "بَلْ", "translit": "ْlَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "طَبَعَ", "translit": "َʿعَbَṭ", "root": "طبع"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "عَلَيْهَا", "translit": "āَhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "بِكُفْرِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِrْfُkِb", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "فَلَا", "translit": "āَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "يُؤْمِنُونَ", "translit": "َnwُnِmْwʾُy", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "قَلِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِlَq", "root": "قلل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 156, "text": "وَبِكُفْرِهِمْ وَقَوْلِهِمْ عَلَىٰ مَرْيَمَ بُهْتَٰنًا عَظِيمًا ", "translation": "And [We cursed them] for their disbelief and their saying against Mary a great slander[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَبِكُفْرِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِrْfُkِbَw", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَقَوْلِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِlْwَqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَرْيَمَ", "translit": "َmَyْrَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بُهْتَٰنًا", "translit": "āًnَٰtْhُb", "root": "بهت"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَظِيمًا", "translit": "āًmyِẓَʿع", "root": "عظم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 157, "text": "وَقَوْلِهِمْ إِنَّا قَتَلْنَا ٱلْمَسِيحَ عِيسَى ٱبْنَ مَرْيَمَ رَسُولَ ٱللَّهِ وَمَا قَتَلُوهُ وَمَا صَلَبُوهُ وَلَٰكِن شُبِّهَ لَهُمْۚ وَإِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ ٱخْتَلَفُوا۟ فِيهِ لَفِى شَكٍّ مِّنْهُۚ مَا لَهُم بِهِۦ مِنْ عِلْمٍ إِلَّا ٱتِّبَاعَ ٱلظَّنِّۚ وَمَا قَتَلُوهُ يَقِينًۢا ", "translation": "And [for] their saying, \"Indeed, we have killed the Messiah, Jesus the son of Mary, the messenger of Allāh.\" And they did not kill him, nor did they crucify him; but [another] was made to resemble him to them. And indeed, those who differ over it are in doubt about it. They have no knowledge of it except the following of assumption. And they did not kill him, for certain.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقَوْلِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِlْwَqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَتَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَtَq", "root": "قتل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْمَسِيحَ", "translit": "َḥyِsَmْlٱ", "root": "مسح"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عِيسَى", "translit": "àَsyِʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱبْنَ", "translit": "َnْbٱ", "root": "بني"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مَرْيَمَ", "translit": "َmَyْrَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "رَسُولَ", "translit": "َlwُsَr", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "قَتَلُوهُ", "translit": "ُhwُlَtَq", "root": "قتل"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "صَلَبُوهُ", "translit": "ُhwُbَlَṣ", "root": "صلب"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَلَٰكِن", "translit": "nِkَٰlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "شُبِّهَ", "translit": "َhِّbُhs", "root": "شبه"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "وَإِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "ٱخْتَلَفُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُfَlَtْhkٱ", "root": "خلف"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "فِيهِ", "translit": "ِhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "لَفِى", "translit": "àِfَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "شَكٍّ", "translit": "ٍّkَhs", "root": "شكك"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "مِّنْهُ", "translit": "ُhْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "لَهُم", "translit": "mُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "عِلْمٍ", "translit": "ٍmْlِʿع", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "ٱتِّبَاعَ", "translit": "َʿعāَbِّtٱ", "root": "تبع"}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "ٱلظَّنِّ", "translit": "ِّnَّẓlٱ", "root": "ظنن"}, {"id": 32, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 33, "arabic": "قَتَلُوهُ", "translit": "ُhwُlَtَq", "root": "قتل"}, {"id": 34, "arabic": "يَقِينًۢا", "translit": "āًۢnyِqَy", "root": "يقن"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 158, "text": "بَل رَّفَعَهُ ٱللَّهُ إِلَيْهِۚ وَكَانَ ٱللَّهُ عَزِيزًا حَكِيمًا ", "translation": "Rather, Allāh raised him to Himself. And ever is Allāh Exalted in Might and Wise.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "بَل", "translit": "lَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَّفَعَهُ", "translit": "ُhَʿعَfَّr", "root": "رفع"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَكَانَ", "translit": "َnāَkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "عَزِيزًا", "translit": "āًzyِzَʿع", "root": "عزز"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "حَكِيمًا", "translit": "āًmyِkَḥ", "root": "حكم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 159, "text": "وَإِن مِّنْ أَهْلِ ٱلْكِتَٰبِ إِلَّا لَيُؤْمِنَنَّ بِهِۦ قَبْلَ مَوْتِهِۦۖ وَيَوْمَ ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ يَكُونُ عَلَيْهِمْ شَهِيدًا ", "translation": "And there is none from the People of the Scripture but that he will surely believe in him [i.e., Jesus] before his death.[1] And on the Day of Resurrection he will be against them a witness.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِّنْ", "translit": "ْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَهْلِ", "translit": "ِlْhَaʾ", "root": "اهل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْكِتَٰبِ", "translit": "ِbَٰtِkْlٱ", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَيُؤْمِنَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَnِmْwʾُyَl", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "قَبْلَ", "translit": "َlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَوْتِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِtْwَm", "root": "موت"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَيَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَyَw", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَmَٰyِqْlٱ", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "يَكُونُ", "translit": "ُnwُkَy", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "شَهِيدًا", "translit": "āًdyِhَhs", "root": "شهد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 160, "text": "فَبِظُلْمٍ مِّنَ ٱلَّذِينَ هَادُوا۟ حَرَّمْنَا عَلَيْهِمْ طَيِّبَٰتٍ أُحِلَّتْ لَهُمْ وَبِصَدِّهِمْ عَن سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِ كَثِيرًا ", "translation": "For wrongdoing on the part of the Jews, We made unlawful for them [certain] good foods which had been lawful to them, and for their averting from the way of Allāh many [people],", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَبِظُلْمٍ", "translit": "ٍmْlُẓِbَf", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "هَادُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُdāَh", "root": "هود"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "حَرَّمْنَا", "translit": "āَnْmَّrَḥ", "root": "حرم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "طَيِّبَٰتٍ", "translit": "ٍtَٰbِّyَṭ", "root": "طيب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أُحِلَّتْ", "translit": "ْtَّlِḥُaʾ", "root": "حلل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَبِصَدِّهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِّdَṣِbَw", "root": "صدد"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "عَن", "translit": "nَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "سَبِيلِ", "translit": "ِlyِbَs", "root": "سبل"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "كَثِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِhtَk", "root": "كثر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 161, "text": "وَأَخْذِهِمُ ٱلرِّبَوٰا۟ وَقَدْ نُهُوا۟ عَنْهُ وَأَكْلِهِمْ أَمْوَٰلَ ٱلنَّاسِ بِٱلْبَٰطِلِۚ وَأَعْتَدْنَا لِلْكَٰفِرِينَ مِنْهُمْ عَذَابًا أَلِيمًا ", "translation": "And [for] their taking of usury while they had been forbidden from it, and their consuming of the people's wealth unjustly. And We have prepared for the disbelievers among them a painful punishment.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَخْذِهِمُ", "translit": "ُmِhِhdْhkَaʾَw", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلرِّبَوٰا۟", "translit": "۟āٰwَbِّrlٱ", "root": "ربو"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "نُهُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُhُn", "root": "نهي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَنْهُ", "translit": "ُhْnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَأَكْلِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِlْkَaʾَw", "root": "اكل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَمْوَٰلَ", "translit": "َlَٰwْmَaʾ", "root": "مول"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بِٱلْبَٰطِلِ", "translit": "ِlِṭَٰbْlٱِb", "root": "بطل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَأَعْتَدْنَا", "translit": "āَnْdَtْʿعَaʾَw", "root": "عتد"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لِلْكَٰفِرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrِfَٰkْlِl", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مِنْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "عَذَابًا", "translit": "āًbāَhdَʿع", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "أَلِيمًا", "translit": "āًmyِlَaʾ", "root": "الم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 162, "text": "لَّٰكِنِ ٱلرَّٰسِخُونَ فِى ٱلْعِلْمِ مِنْهُمْ وَٱلْمُؤْمِنُونَ يُؤْمِنُونَ بِمَآ أُنزِلَ إِلَيْكَ وَمَآ أُنزِلَ مِن قَبْلِكَۚ وَٱلْمُقِيمِينَ ٱلصَّلَوٰةَۚ وَٱلْمُؤْتُونَ ٱلزَّكَوٰةَ وَٱلْمُؤْمِنُونَ بِٱللَّهِ وَٱلْيَوْمِ ٱلْءَاخِرِ أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ سَنُؤْتِيهِمْ أَجْرًا عَظِيمًا ", "translation": "But those firm in knowledge among them and the believers believe in what has been revealed to you, [O Muḥammad], and what was revealed before you. And the establishers of prayer [especially] and the givers of zakāh and the believers in Allāh and the Last Day - those We will give a great reward.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَّٰكِنِ", "translit": "ِnِkَّٰl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلرَّٰسِخُونَ", "translit": "َnwُhkِsَّٰrlٱ", "root": "رسخ"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْعِلْمِ", "translit": "ِmْlِʿعْlٱ", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِنْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَٱلْمُؤْمِنُونَ", "translit": "َnwُnِmْwʾُmْlٱَw", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يُؤْمِنُونَ", "translit": "َnwُnِmْwʾُy", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بِمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أُنزِلَ", "translit": "َlِznُaʾ", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "إِلَيْكَ", "translit": "َkْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "أُنزِلَ", "translit": "َlِznُaʾ", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "قَبْلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَٱلْمُقِيمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmyِqُmْlٱَw", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ", "translit": "َẗٰwَlَّṣlٱ", "root": "صلو"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "وَٱلْمُؤْتُونَ", "translit": "َnwُtْwʾُmْlٱَw", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱلزَّكَوٰةَ", "translit": "َẗٰwَkَّzlٱ", "root": "زكو"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "وَٱلْمُؤْمِنُونَ", "translit": "َnwُnِmْwʾُmْlٱَw", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "بِٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱِb", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "وَٱلْيَوْمِ", "translit": "ِmْwَyْlٱَw", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "ٱلْءَاخِرِ", "translit": "ِrِhkāَءْlٱ", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "سَنُؤْتِيهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhyِtْwʾُnَs", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "أَجْرًا", "translit": "āًrْjgَaʾ", "root": "اجر"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "عَظِيمًا", "translit": "āًmyِẓَʿع", "root": "عظم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 163, "text": "إِنَّآ أَوْحَيْنَآ إِلَيْكَ كَمَآ أَوْحَيْنَآ إِلَىٰ نُوحٍ وَٱلنَّبِيِّۦنَ مِنۢ بَعْدِهِۦۚ وَأَوْحَيْنَآ إِلَىٰٓ إِبْرَٰهِيمَ وَإِسْمَٰعِيلَ وَإِسْحَٰقَ وَيَعْقُوبَ وَٱلْأَسْبَاطِ وَعِيسَىٰ وَأَيُّوبَ وَيُونُسَ وَهَٰرُونَ وَسُلَيْمَٰنَۚ وَءَاتَيْنَا دَاوُۥدَ زَبُورًا ", "translation": "Indeed, We have revealed to you, [O Muḥammad], as We revealed to Noah and the prophets after him. And We revealed to Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob, the Descendants,[1] Jesus, Job, Jonah, Aaron, and Solomon; and to David We gave the book [of Psalms].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَوْحَيْنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnْyَḥْwَaʾ", "root": "وحي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِلَيْكَ", "translit": "َkْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كَمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَوْحَيْنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnْyَḥْwَaʾ", "root": "وحي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "نُوحٍ", "translit": "ٍḥwُn", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَٱلنَّبِيِّۦنَ", "translit": "َnۦِّyِbَّnlٱَw", "root": "نبا"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مِنۢ", "translit": "ۢnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بَعْدِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِdْʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَأَوْحَيْنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnْyَḥْwَaʾَw", "root": "وحي"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "إِلَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "إِبْرَٰهِيمَ", "translit": "َmyِhَٰrْbِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَإِسْمَٰعِيلَ", "translit": "َlyِʿعَٰmْsِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَإِسْحَٰقَ", "translit": "َqَٰḥْsِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَيَعْقُوبَ", "translit": "َbwُqْʿعَyَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَسْبَاطِ", "translit": "ِṭāَbْsَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "سبط"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "وَعِيسَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَsyِʿعَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "وَأَيُّوبَ", "translit": "َbwُّyَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "وَيُونُسَ", "translit": "َsُnwُyَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "وَهَٰرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrَٰhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "وَسُلَيْمَٰنَ", "translit": "َnَٰmْyَlُsَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "وَءَاتَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَtāَءَw", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "دَاوُۥدَ", "translit": "َdۥُwāَd", "root": ""}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "زَبُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُbَz", "root": "زبر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 164, "text": "وَرُسُلًا قَدْ قَصَصْنَٰهُمْ عَلَيْكَ مِن قَبْلُ وَرُسُلًا لَّمْ نَقْصُصْهُمْ عَلَيْكَۚ وَكَلَّمَ ٱللَّهُ مُوسَىٰ تَكْلِيمًا ", "translation": "And [We sent] messengers about whom We have related [their stories] to you before and messengers about whom We have not related to you. And Allāh spoke to Moses with [direct] speech.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَرُسُلًا", "translit": "āًlُsُrَw", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "قَدْ", "translit": "ْdَq", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَصَصْنَٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَٰnْṣَṣَq", "root": "قصص"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عَلَيْكَ", "translit": "َkْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "قَبْلُ", "translit": "ُlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَرُسُلًا", "translit": "āًlُsُrَw", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَّمْ", "translit": "ْmَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "نَقْصُصْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْṣُṣْqَn", "root": "قصص"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "عَلَيْكَ", "translit": "َkْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَكَلَّمَ", "translit": "َmَّlَkَw", "root": "كلم"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مُوسَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَswُm", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "تَكْلِيمًا", "translit": "āًmyِlْkَt", "root": "كلم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 165, "text": "رُّسُلًا مُّبَشِّرِينَ وَمُنذِرِينَ لِئَلَّا يَكُونَ لِلنَّاسِ عَلَى ٱللَّهِ حُجَّةٌۢ بَعْدَ ٱلرُّسُلِۚ وَكَانَ ٱللَّهُ عَزِيزًا حَكِيمًا ", "translation": "[We sent] messengers as bringers of good tidings and warners so that mankind will have no argument against Allāh after the messengers. And ever is Allāh Exalted in Might and Wise.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "رُّسُلًا", "translit": "āًlُsُّr", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مُّبَشِّرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrِّhsَbُّm", "root": "بشر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَمُنذِرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrِhdnُmَw", "root": "نذر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لِئَلَّا", "translit": "āَّlَyʾِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَكُونَ", "translit": "َnwُkَy", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لِلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlِl", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "حُجَّةٌۢ", "translit": "ٌۢẗَّjgُḥ", "root": "حجج"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بَعْدَ", "translit": "َdْʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلرُّسُلِ", "translit": "ِlُsُّrlٱ", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَكَانَ", "translit": "َnāَkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "عَزِيزًا", "translit": "āًzyِzَʿع", "root": "عزز"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "حَكِيمًا", "translit": "āًmyِkَḥ", "root": "حكم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 166, "text": "لَّٰكِنِ ٱللَّهُ يَشْهَدُ بِمَآ أَنزَلَ إِلَيْكَۖ أَنزَلَهُۥ بِعِلْمِهِۦۖ وَٱلْمَلَٰٓئِكَةُ يَشْهَدُونَۚ وَكَفَىٰ بِٱللَّهِ شَهِيدًا ", "translation": "But Allāh bears witness to that which He has revealed to you. He has sent it down with His knowledge, and the angels bear witness [as well]. And sufficient is Allāh as Witness.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَّٰكِنِ", "translit": "ِnِkَّٰl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَشْهَدُ", "translit": "ُdَhْhsَy", "root": "شهد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَنزَلَ", "translit": "َlَznَaʾ", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِلَيْكَ", "translit": "َkْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَنزَلَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَlَznَaʾ", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بِعِلْمِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِmْlِʿعِb", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَٱلْمَلَٰٓئِكَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَkِyʾَٰٓlَmْlٱَw", "root": "ملك"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "يَشْهَدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdَhْhsَy", "root": "شهد"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَكَفَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَfَkَw", "root": "كفي"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "بِٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱِb", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "شَهِيدًا", "translit": "āًdyِhَhs", "root": "شهد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 167, "text": "إِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ وَصَدُّوا۟ عَن سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِ قَدْ ضَلُّوا۟ ضَلَٰلًۢا بَعِيدًا ", "translation": "Indeed, those who disbelieve and avert [people] from the way of Allāh have certainly gone far astray.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَصَدُّوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُّdَṣَw", "root": "صدد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَن", "translit": "nَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "سَبِيلِ", "translit": "ِlyِbَs", "root": "سبل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "قَدْ", "translit": "ْdَq", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ضَلُّوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُّlَḍ", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ضَلَٰلًۢا", "translit": "āًۢlَٰlَḍ", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "بَعِيدًا", "translit": "āًdyِʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 168, "text": "إِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ وَظَلَمُوا۟ لَمْ يَكُنِ ٱللَّهُ لِيَغْفِرَ لَهُمْ وَلَا لِيَهْدِيَهُمْ طَرِيقًا ", "translation": "Indeed, those who disbelieve and commit wrong [or injustice] - never will Allāh forgive them, nor will He guide them to a path,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَظَلَمُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُmَlَẓَw", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَمْ", "translit": "ْmَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَكُنِ", "translit": "ِnُkَy", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لِيَغْفِرَ", "translit": "َrِfْغَyِl", "root": "غفر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لِيَهْدِيَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَyِdْhَyِl", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "طَرِيقًا", "translit": "āًqyِrَṭ", "root": "طرق"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 169, "text": "إِلَّا طَرِيقَ جَهَنَّمَ خَٰلِدِينَ فِيهَآ أَبَدًاۚ وَكَانَ ذَٰلِكَ عَلَى ٱللَّهِ يَسِيرًا ", "translation": "Except the path of Hell; they will abide therein forever. And that, for Allāh, is [always] easy.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "طَرِيقَ", "translit": "َqyِrَṭ", "root": "طرق"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "جَهَنَّمَ", "translit": "َmَّnَhَjg", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "خَٰلِدِينَ", "translit": "َnyِdِlَٰhk", "root": "خلد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِيهَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَبَدًا", "translit": "āًdَbَaʾ", "root": "ابد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَكَانَ", "translit": "َnāَkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "يَسِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِsَy", "root": "يسر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 170, "text": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلنَّاسُ قَدْ جَآءَكُمُ ٱلرَّسُولُ بِٱلْحَقِّ مِن رَّبِّكُمْ فَـَٔامِنُوا۟ خَيْرًا لَّكُمْۚ وَإِن تَكْفُرُوا۟ فَإِنَّ لِلَّهِ مَا فِى ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِۚ وَكَانَ ٱللَّهُ عَلِيمًا حَكِيمًا ", "translation": "O mankind, the Messenger has come to you with the truth from your Lord, so believe; it is better for you. But if you disbelieve - then indeed, to Allāh belongs whatever is in the heavens and earth. And ever is Allāh Knowing and Wise.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسُ", "translit": "ُsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَدْ", "translit": "ْdَq", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "جَآءَكُمُ", "translit": "ُmُkَءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلرَّسُولُ", "translit": "ُlwُsَّrlٱ", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بِٱلْحَقِّ", "translit": "ِّqَḥْlٱِb", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "رَّبِّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فَـَٔامِنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnِmāَٔـَf", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "خَيْرًا", "translit": "āًrْyَhk", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "تَكْفُرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrُfْkَt", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "فَإِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "لِلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِl", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "وَكَانَ", "translit": "َnāَkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "عَلِيمًا", "translit": "āًmyِlَʿع", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "حَكِيمًا", "translit": "āًmyِkَḥ", "root": "حكم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 171, "text": "يَٰٓأَهْلَ ٱلْكِتَٰبِ لَا تَغْلُوا۟ فِى دِينِكُمْ وَلَا تَقُولُوا۟ عَلَى ٱللَّهِ إِلَّا ٱلْحَقَّۚ إِنَّمَا ٱلْمَسِيحُ عِيسَى ٱبْنُ مَرْيَمَ رَسُولُ ٱللَّهِ وَكَلِمَتُهُۥٓ أَلْقَىٰهَآ إِلَىٰ مَرْيَمَ وَرُوحٌ مِّنْهُۖ فَـَٔامِنُوا۟ بِٱللَّهِ وَرُسُلِهِۦۖ وَلَا تَقُولُوا۟ ثَلَٰثَةٌۚ ٱنتَهُوا۟ خَيْرًا لَّكُمْۚ إِنَّمَا ٱللَّهُ إِلَٰهٌ وَٰحِدٌۖ سُبْحَٰنَهُۥٓ أَن يَكُونَ لَهُۥ وَلَدٌۘ لَّهُۥ مَا فِى ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَمَا فِى ٱلْأَرْضِۗ وَكَفَىٰ بِٱللَّهِ وَكِيلًا ", "translation": "O People of the Scripture, do not commit excess in your religion[1] or say about Allāh except the truth. The Messiah, Jesus the son of Mary, was but a messenger of Allāh and His word which He directed to Mary and a soul [created at a command] from Him. So believe in Allāh and His messengers. And do not say, \"Three\"; desist - it is better for you. Indeed, Allāh is but one God. Exalted is He above having a son. To Him belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth. And sufficient is Allāh as Disposer of affairs.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَهْلَ", "translit": "َlْhَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "اهل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْكِتَٰبِ", "translit": "ِbَٰtِkْlٱ", "root": "كتب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "تَغْلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlْغَt", "root": "غلو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "دِينِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِnyِd", "root": "دين"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "تَقُولُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlwُqَt", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْحَقَّ", "translit": "َّqَḥْlٱ", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "إِنَّمَا", "translit": "āَmَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلْمَسِيحُ", "translit": "ُḥyِsَmْlٱ", "root": "مسح"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "عِيسَى", "translit": "àَsyِʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱبْنُ", "translit": "ُnْbٱ", "root": "بني"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "مَرْيَمَ", "translit": "َmَyْrَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "رَسُولُ", "translit": "ُlwُsَr", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "وَكَلِمَتُهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhُtَmِlَkَw", "root": "كلم"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "أَلْقَىٰهَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhٰàَqْlَaʾ", "root": "لقي"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "مَرْيَمَ", "translit": "َmَyْrَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "وَرُوحٌ", "translit": "ٌḥwُrَw", "root": "روح"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "مِّنْهُ", "translit": "ُhْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "فَـَٔامِنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnِmāَٔـَf", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "بِٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱِb", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "وَرُسُلِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِlُsُrَw", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "تَقُولُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlwُqَt", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "ثَلَٰثَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَhtَٰlَht", "root": "ثلث"}, {"id": 32, "arabic": "ٱنتَهُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُhَtnٱ", "root": "نهي"}, {"id": 33, "arabic": "خَيْرًا", "translit": "āًrْyَhk", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 34, "arabic": "لَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 35, "arabic": "إِنَّمَا", "translit": "āَmَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 36, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 37, "arabic": "إِلَٰهٌ", "translit": "ٌhَٰlِiʾ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 38, "arabic": "وَٰحِدٌ", "translit": "ٌdِḥَٰw", "root": "وحد"}, {"id": 39, "arabic": "سُبْحَٰنَهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhَnَٰḥْbُs", "root": "سبح"}, {"id": 40, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 41, "arabic": "يَكُونَ", "translit": "َnwُkَy", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 42, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 43, "arabic": "وَلَدٌ", "translit": "ٌdَlَw", "root": "ولد"}, {"id": 44, "arabic": "لَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 45, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 46, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 47, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 48, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 49, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 50, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 51, "arabic": "وَكَفَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَfَkَw", "root": "كفي"}, {"id": 52, "arabic": "بِٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱِb", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 53, "arabic": "وَكِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِkَw", "root": "وكل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 172, "text": "لَّن يَسْتَنكِفَ ٱلْمَسِيحُ أَن يَكُونَ عَبْدًا لِّلَّهِ وَلَا ٱلْمَلَٰٓئِكَةُ ٱلْمُقَرَّبُونَۚ وَمَن يَسْتَنكِفْ عَنْ عِبَادَتِهِۦ وَيَسْتَكْبِرْ فَسَيَحْشُرُهُمْ إِلَيْهِ جَمِيعًا ", "translation": "Never would the Messiah disdain to be a servant of Allāh, nor would the angels near [to Him]. And whoever disdains His worship and is arrogant - He will gather them to Himself all together.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَّن", "translit": "nَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَسْتَنكِفَ", "translit": "َfِknَtْsَy", "root": "نكف"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْمَسِيحُ", "translit": "ُḥyِsَmْlٱ", "root": "مسح"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَكُونَ", "translit": "َnwُkَy", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَبْدًا", "translit": "āًdْbَʿع", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لِّلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِّl", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْمَلَٰٓئِكَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَkِyʾَٰٓlَmْlٱ", "root": "ملك"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْمُقَرَّبُونَ", "translit": "َnwُbَّrَqُmْlٱ", "root": "قرب"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "يَسْتَنكِفْ", "translit": "ْfِknَtْsَy", "root": "نكف"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "عَنْ", "translit": "ْnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "عِبَادَتِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِtَdāَbِʿع", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَيَسْتَكْبِرْ", "translit": "ْrِbْkَtْsَyَw", "root": "كبر"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "فَسَيَحْشُرُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُrُhsْḥَyَsَf", "root": "حشر"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "إِلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "جَمِيعًا", "translit": "āًʿعyِmَjg", "root": "جمع"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 173, "text": "فَأَمَّا ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ فَيُوَفِّيهِمْ أُجُورَهُمْ وَيَزِيدُهُم مِّن فَضْلِهِۦۖ وَأَمَّا ٱلَّذِينَ ٱسْتَنكَفُوا۟ وَٱسْتَكْبَرُوا۟ فَيُعَذِّبُهُمْ عَذَابًا أَلِيمًا وَلَا يَجِدُونَ لَهُم مِّن دُونِ ٱللَّهِ وَلِيًّا وَلَا نَصِيرًا ", "translation": "And as for those who believed and did righteous deeds, He will give them in full their rewards and grant them extra from His bounty. But as for those who disdained and were arrogant, He will punish them with a painful punishment, and they will not find for themselves besides Allāh any protector or helper.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَأَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَaʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَعَمِلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlِmَʿعَw", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰḥِlَّٰṣlٱ", "root": "صلح"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَيُوَفِّيهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhyِّfَwُyَf", "root": "وفي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أُجُورَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَrwُjgُaʾ", "root": "اجر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَيَزِيدُهُم", "translit": "mُhُdyِzَyَw", "root": "زيد"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فَضْلِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِlْḍَf", "root": "فضل"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَأَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱسْتَنكَفُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُfَknَtْsٱ", "root": "نكف"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَٱسْتَكْبَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَbْkَtْsٱَw", "root": "كبر"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "فَيُعَذِّبُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُbِّhdَʿعُyَf", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "عَذَابًا", "translit": "āًbāَhdَʿع", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "أَلِيمًا", "translit": "āًmyِlَaʾ", "root": "الم"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "يَجِدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdِjgَy", "root": "وجد"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "لَهُم", "translit": "mُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "دُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُd", "root": "دون"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "وَلِيًّا", "translit": "āًّyِlَw", "root": "ولي"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "نَصِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِṣَn", "root": "نصر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 174, "text": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلنَّاسُ قَدْ جَآءَكُم بُرْهَٰنٌ مِّن رَّبِّكُمْ وَأَنزَلْنَآ إِلَيْكُمْ نُورًا مُّبِينًا ", "translation": "O mankind, there has come to you a conclusive proof from your Lord, and We have sent down to you a clear light.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسُ", "translit": "ُsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَدْ", "translit": "ْdَq", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "جَآءَكُم", "translit": "mُkَءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بُرْهَٰنٌ", "translit": "ٌnَٰhْrُb", "root": "برهن"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "رَّبِّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَأَنزَلْنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnْlَznَaʾَw", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِلَيْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "نُورًا", "translit": "āًrwُn", "root": "نور"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مُّبِينًا", "translit": "āًnyِbُّm", "root": "بين"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 175, "text": "فَأَمَّا ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ بِٱللَّهِ وَٱعْتَصَمُوا۟ بِهِۦ فَسَيُدْخِلُهُمْ فِى رَحْمَةٍ مِّنْهُ وَفَضْلٍ وَيَهْدِيهِمْ إِلَيْهِ صِرَٰطًا مُّسْتَقِيمًا ", "translation": "So those who believe in Allāh and hold fast to Him - He will admit them to mercy from Himself and bounty and guide them to Himself on a straight path.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَأَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَaʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱِb", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَٱعْتَصَمُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُmَṣَtْʿعٱَw", "root": "عصم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فَسَيُدْخِلُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُlِhkْdُyَsَf", "root": "دخل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "رَحْمَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَmْḥَr", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مِّنْهُ", "translit": "ُhْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَفَضْلٍ", "translit": "ٍlْḍَfَw", "root": "فضل"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَيَهْدِيهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhyِdْhَyَw", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "إِلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "صِرَٰطًا", "translit": "āًṭَٰrِṣ", "root": "صرط"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "مُّسْتَقِيمًا", "translit": "āًmyِqَtْsُّm", "root": "قوم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 176, "text": "يَسْتَفْتُونَكَ قُلِ ٱللَّهُ يُفْتِيكُمْ فِى ٱلْكَلَٰلَةِۚ إِنِ ٱمْرُؤٌا۟ هَلَكَ لَيْسَ لَهُۥ وَلَدٌ وَلَهُۥٓ أُخْتٌ فَلَهَا نِصْفُ مَا تَرَكَۚ وَهُوَ يَرِثُهَآ إِن لَّمْ يَكُن لَّهَا وَلَدٌۚ فَإِن كَانَتَا ٱثْنَتَيْنِ فَلَهُمَا ٱلثُّلُثَانِ مِمَّا تَرَكَۚ وَإِن كَانُوٓا۟ إِخْوَةً رِّجَالًا وَنِسَآءً فَلِلذَّكَرِ مِثْلُ حَظِّ ٱلْأُنثَيَيْنِۗ يُبَيِّنُ ٱللَّهُ لَكُمْ أَن تَضِلُّوا۟ۗ وَٱللَّهُ بِكُلِّ شَىْءٍ عَلِيمٌۢ ", "translation": "They request from you a [legal] ruling. Say, \"Allāh gives you a ruling concerning one having neither descendants nor ascendants [as heirs].\" If a man dies, leaving no child but [only] a sister, she will have half of what he left. And he inherits from her if she [dies and] has no child. But if there are two sisters [or more], they will have two thirds of what he left. If there are both brothers and sisters, the male will have the share of two females.[1] Allāh makes clear to you [His law], lest you go astray. And Allāh is Knowing of all things.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَسْتَفْتُونَكَ", "translit": "َkَnwُtْfَtْsَy", "root": "فتي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "قُلِ", "translit": "ِlُq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يُفْتِيكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkyِtْfُy", "root": "فتي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْكَلَٰلَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَlَٰlَkْlٱ", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِنِ", "translit": "ِnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱمْرُؤٌا۟", "translit": "۟āٌwʾُrْmٱ", "root": "مرا"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "هَلَكَ", "translit": "َkَlَh", "root": "هلك"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لَيْسَ", "translit": "َsْyَl", "root": "ليس"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَلَدٌ", "translit": "ٌdَlَw", "root": "ولد"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَلَهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "أُخْتٌ", "translit": "ٌtْhkُaʾ", "root": "اخو"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "فَلَهَا", "translit": "āَhَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "نِصْفُ", "translit": "ُfْṣِn", "root": "نصف"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "تَرَكَ", "translit": "َkَrَt", "root": "ترك"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "وَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "يَرِثُهَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhُhtِrَy", "root": "ورث"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "لَّمْ", "translit": "ْmَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "يَكُن", "translit": "nُkَy", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "لَّهَا", "translit": "āَhَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "وَلَدٌ", "translit": "ٌdَlَw", "root": "ولد"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "فَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "كَانَتَا", "translit": "āَtَnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "ٱثْنَتَيْنِ", "translit": "ِnْyَtَnْhtٱ", "root": "ثني"}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "فَلَهُمَا", "translit": "āَmُhَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "ٱلثُّلُثَانِ", "translit": "ِnāَhtُlُّhtlٱ", "root": "ثلث"}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "مِمَّا", "translit": "āَّmِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 32, "arabic": "تَرَكَ", "translit": "َkَrَt", "root": "ترك"}, {"id": 33, "arabic": "وَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 34, "arabic": "كَانُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 35, "arabic": "إِخْوَةً", "translit": "ًẗَwْhkِiʾ", "root": "اخو"}, {"id": 36, "arabic": "رِّجَالًا", "translit": "āًlāَjgِّr", "root": "رجل"}, {"id": 37, "arabic": "وَنِسَآءً", "translit": "ًءٓāَsِnَw", "root": "نسو"}, {"id": 38, "arabic": "فَلِلذَّكَرِ", "translit": "ِrَkَّhdlِlَf", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 39, "arabic": "مِثْلُ", "translit": "ُlْhtِm", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 40, "arabic": "حَظِّ", "translit": "ِّẓَḥ", "root": "حظظ"}, {"id": 41, "arabic": "ٱلْأُنثَيَيْنِ", "translit": "ِnْyَyَhtnُaʾْlٱ", "root": "انث"}, {"id": 42, "arabic": "يُبَيِّنُ", "translit": "ُnِّyَbُy", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 43, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 44, "arabic": "لَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 45, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 46, "arabic": "تَضِلُّوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُّlِḍَt", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 47, "arabic": "وَٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱَw", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 48, "arabic": "بِكُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُkِb", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 49, "arabic": "شَىْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْàَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 50, "arabic": "عَلِيمٌۢ", "translit": "ٌۢmyِlَʿع", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": false}], "tafsir": {"1": [{"arabic": "يعلم بأن الله يرى وقال الله تعالى إن الله كان عليكم رقيبا وقال تعالى والذين هم لأماناتهم وعهدهم راعون والذين هم بشهاداتهم قائمون وقال ابن المبارك لرجل راقب الله تعالى فسأله عن تفسيره فقال كن أبدا كأنك ترى الله عز وجل وقال عبد الواحد بن زيد إذا كان سيدي رقيبا على فلا أبالى بغيره وقال أبو عثمان المغربي أفضل ما يلزم الإنسان نفسه في هذه الطريقة المحاسبة والمراقبة وسياسة عمله بالعلم وقال ابن عطاء أفضل الطاعات مراقبة الحق على دوام الأوقات وقال الجريري أمرنا هذا مبنى على أصلين أن تلزم نفسك المراقبة", "english": "As for its excellence: Gabriel, peace be upon him, asked about iḥsān (perfection of worship), and he, peace be upon him, replied: \"That you worship Allah as though you see Him.\" {«أن تعبد الله كأنك تراه»} And he, peace be upon him, also said: \"Worship Allah as though you see Him; for if you do not see Him, verily He sees you.\" {«اعبد الله كأنك تراه فإن لم تكن تراه فإنه يراك»} And Allah, Exalted is He, has said: {«أفمن هو قائم على كل نفس بما كسبت»} — \"Is He who stands over every soul with what it has earned [like any other]?\" And He said: {«ألم يعلم بأن الله يرى»} — \"Does he not know that Allah sees?\" And Allah, Exalted is He, said: {«إن الله كان عليكم رقيبا»} — \"Verily, Allah is ever a Watcher (raqīb) over you.\" And He said: {«والذين هم لأماناتهم وعهدهم راعون والذين هم بشهاداتهم قائمون»} — \"And those who are keepers of their trusts and their covenant, and those who stand firm in their testimonies.\"", "book_title": "Monitoring and Accounting", "section_title_ar": "Section 1233", "section_title_en": "The Second Station of Vigilance: Murāqabah (Spiritual Watchfulness)", "section_index": 1233, "anchors": "- Root: رقب (R-Q-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To watch, guard, observe attentively; the neck (as that which turns to observe); a lookout post.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Murāqabah entails the heart's persistent awareness of divine proximity; it is the theological state wherein the servant knows Allah is witness over his secret and public acts.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah (literal): physical watching/awaiting. Majaz (metaphorical): \"This matter is upon your necks\" (في رقابكم) — i.e., an inescapable obligation; likewise, murāqabah of Allah is the soul's standing under the watchful eye of the King, a transferred sense denoting reverent vigilance.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction & Focused Predication (باب الحصر والتقييد) — governing the conditional structures \"إذا... فلا يبقى إلا...\" and \"إنها إن تركت طغت وفسدت,\" where the apodosis is restricted to a single remaining duty.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Monitoring and Accounting'", "badge": "Insight"}], "4": [{"arabic": "فإن طبن لكم عن شيء منه نفسا فكلوه هنيئا مريئا وطيبة النفس غير طيبة القلب فقد يريد الإنسان بقلبه ما لا تطيب به نفسه فإنه يريد الحجامة بقلبه ولكن تكرهها نفسه وإنما طيبة النفس أن تسمح نفسها بالإبراء لا عن ضرورة تقابله حتى إذا رددت بين ضررين اختارت أهونهما فهذه مصادرة على التحقيق بإكراه الباطن نعم القاضي في الدنيا لا يطلع على القلوب والأغراض فينظر إلى الإبراء الظاهر وأنها لم تكره بسبب ظاهر والإكراه الباطن ليس يطلع الخلق عليه ولكن مهما تصدى القاضي الأكبر في صعيد القيامة للقضاء لم يكن هذا محسوبا ولا مقيدا", "english": "...with your heart, and there is no sound copy which you have verified in order to collate against it — so from where do you know that you have heard it? For God Most High has said: {«وَلَا تَقْفُ مَا لَيْسَ لَكَ بِهِ عِلْمٌ»} \"And do not pursue that of which you have no knowledge\" {«الإسراء/36»}. And the statement of all the shaykhs in this age — \"We have heard what is in this book\" — when the condition we have mentioned is not met, is outright falsehood.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Delusion", "section_title_ar": "Section 900", "section_title_en": "On the Conditions of Valid Hadith Audition and the Deception of Those Who Neglect the Heart's Work", "section_index": 900, "anchors": "- Root: سمع (s-m-')\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is hearing/perception of sound; also used for understanding and acceptance (isti'mal fi al-qabul).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Hearing (al-sam') is the perception of sound; in the religious law it denotes the transmission of the Prophet's speech with retention (hifz) and comprehension (fahm), distinguishing it from mere acoustic reception.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqiqah) is the ear's reception of sound; metaphorical (majaz) is the heart's acceptance and compliance. The deception here lies in conflating acoustic presence with valid scholarly transmission.\n- Root: غرر (gh-r-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes heedlessness (ghiflah) and being deceived by appearances; al-ghurur is false reliance on that which has no firm basis.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Gharrartu fulanan: I struck his heedlessness and obtained from him what I sought.\" The root implies an outward trace (athar) that misleads, as in the saying \"he folded it upon its crease\" — i.e., acted on an unexamined assumption.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: one who is heedless (ghafil). Metaphorical: the one who is deceived by the outer shell (qishr) of knowledge while missing its kernel (lubb), like the gazelle that ventures out on a moonlit night thinking it is day.\n- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The heart as the organ of fluctuation and turning (taqallub); its movement between states.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The heart (qalb) is that subtle reality (latifah) which is the locus of understanding (fahm) and discernment ('aql); it is the seat of intention (niyyah) and volition (iradah).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the physical organ. Metaphorical: the inner faculty of cognition and moral discrimination, which is the true addressee of the Law's commands.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The construction \"إنما المقصود كذا\" restricts the predicate exclusively to the intended object, negating all else. This governs the author's repeated reframing of means (wasail) versus ends (maqasid).", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Delusion'", "badge": "Insight"}], "5": [{"arabic": "{ولا تؤتوا السفهاء أموالكم} تنبيها على أن حفظ العلم ممن يفسده ويضره أولى وليس الظلم في إعطاء غير المستحق بأقل من الظلم في منع المستحق أأنثر درا بين سارحة النعم .", "english": "I say: Leave the reins and go. For if one comes who comprehends, and you have concealed it from him, then let him bridle me — for God Most High has said: {«ولا تؤتوا السفهاء أموالكم»} — \"And do not give the foolish your wealth\" — as an indication that preserving knowledge from one who would corrupt it and harm it is even more incumbent. And wrongdoing in giving to one who is not deserving is no less than wrongdoing in withholding from one who is deserving.", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "section_title_ar": "Section 48", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 48: On the Etiquette of the Teacher and the Learner (Continued), and the Exposition of the Marks Distinguishing the Scholars of the Hereafter from the Evil Scholars", "section_index": 48, "anchors": "- Root: علم (ʿalima)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Knowledge as the apprehension of a thing in its true reality; the root also carries the sense of a mark or sign (ʿalam) by which something is known.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Knowledge is of two kinds: apprehension of the essence of a thing, and judgment upon a thing affirming or denying something of it. The first is transitive to one object; the second to two.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage: \"He is a sign (ʿalam) of knowledge\" — i.e., a distinguished landmark. Metaphorical: \"The traces (maʿālim) of the road\" — the vestiges by which one is guided.\n- Root: ظلم (ẓalama)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To place something in other than its proper place; darkness (ẓulmah) as the privation of light.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Darkness is the absence of light; figuratively used for ignorance, polytheism, and wickedness, just as light is used for their opposites.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"He wronged and was wronged\" — to suffer injustice. Metaphorical: \"Wrongdoing is darkness, just as justice is light\" — for the wrongdoer acts in the darkness of his own soul.\n- Root: منع (manaʿa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To withhold, to prevent, to protect; a fortress (manīʿah) is that which defends.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Used in opposition to giving; also used for protection, as in \"a fortified place\" (makān manīʿ).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"He prevented him from the thing.\" Metaphorical: \"He protects the neighbor\" — i.e., defends him from harm.\n- Root: عقل (ʿaqala)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The intellect; originally to bind or restrain (as a camel is hobbled), hence the faculty that restrains the soul from base desires.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The intellect is the faculty prepared for the reception of knowledge, and also the knowledge acquired by that faculty.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"I have not done so since I attained reason.\" Metaphorical: \"A palm tree that does not accept pollination\" — i.e., does not receive what is offered to it.\n- Sibawayh Rule: The Interposition of the Prepositional Phrase (al-faṣl bayna al-jār wa al-majrūr) — governing the permissible separation between a governing word and its governed prepositional phrase where the intervening element is syntactically complete, as in the construction \"Leave the reins and go\" (itruk al-lijām wa-idhhab), where the imperative governs two successive clauses without ellipsis.", "content_type": "general", "topic": "Classical commentary from Al-Ghazali's Ihya Ulum al-Din, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Commentary"}], "7": [{"arabic": "الرابع دم البراغيث ما قل منه أو كثر إلا إذا جاوز حد العادة سواء كان في ثوبك أو في ثوب غيرك فلبسته الخامس دم البثرات وما ينفصل منها من قيح وصديد ودلك ابن عمر رضي الله عنه بثرة على وجهه فخرج منها الدم وصلى ولم يغتسل وفي معناه ما يترشح من لطخات الدماميل التي تدوم غالبا وكذلك أثر الفصد إلا ما يقع نادرا من خراج أو غيره فيلحق بدم الاستحاضة ولا يكون في معنى البثرات التي لا يخلو الإنسان عنها في أحواله ومسامحة الشرع في هذه النجاسات الخمس تعرفك أن أمر الطهارة على التساهل وما ابتدع فيها وسوسة", "english": "And the soul — if it is not occupied with something, it occupies its master. And if one intends thereby drawing near to God — Glory be to Him — that becomes with him among the most excellent of acts of drawing near (al-qurubāt).", "book_title": "Mysteries of Purity", "section_title_ar": "Section 107", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Secrets of Purification — Being the Third Book of the Quarter of Acts of Worship", "section_index": 107, "anchors": "- Root: طهر (ṭahara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: cleanliness and purity, opposed to najāsah (ritual impurity) and ḥayḍ (menstruation)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Twofold: purification of the body and purification of the soul; both intended in the Qur'anic verses\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical cleansing with water; Metaphorical: \"tahhara min al-ithm\" — to be absolved of sin; \"tawbah ṭahūr\" — repentance as purification\n- Root: نجس (najasa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: filth, uncleanness; something deemed ritually impure\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Applied to bodies and meanings; \"al-mushrikūn najas\" — their judgment is the judgment of the impure in being avoided\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical dirt; Metaphorical: \"anjasa\" — to commit an obscene act (moral impurity)\n- Root: خبث (khabutha)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: opposite of ṭayyib (good/pure); foulness\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"al-khabīth\" — that which the soul dislikes; unlawful wealth; foul speech\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: impurity of substance; Metaphorical: \"al-akhbathān\" — urine and excrement (the two foul things)\n- Root: حدث (ḥadatha)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: origination after non-existence; in law: that which necessitates ablution\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): ḥadath is the opposite of qidam (eternity); in Sacred Law: that which obligates wuḍūʾ\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: occurrence; Metaphorical: \"al-ḥadath\" — ritual impurity breaking wuḍūʾ\n- Root: عفو (ʿafw)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: pardon, forgiveness; that which is excused and overlooked\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"al-ʿafw\" — passing over a sin; from \"ʿafat al-rīḥ al-dār\" — the wind erased the traces\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to erase/obliterate; Metaphorical: \"ʿafā Allāhu ʿanka\" — God pardoned you\n- Root: نفس (nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: soul, self, blood, essence\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"al-nafs\" — the spirit; \"nafsuh\" — His very Self/Dhāt\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: soul; Metaphorical: \"nafs sāʾilah\" — flowing blood (the criterion for impurity of dead animals)\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bab al-Faṣl bayna al-Jār wa al-Majrūr (Prepositional Interposition) — governing the syntactic relationship between conditional clauses and their apodoses in periodic structures", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Mysteries of Purity'", "badge": "Insight"}], "10": [{"arabic": "وقال تعالى ولا تأكلوا أموالكم بينكم بالباطل وقال تعالى إن الذين يأكلون أموال اليتامى ظلما الآية وقال تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا اتقوا الله وذروا ما بقي من الربا إن كنتم مؤمنين {ثم قال} فإن لم تفعلوا فأذنوا بحرب من الله ورسوله {ثم قال} وإن تبتم فلكم رءوس أموالكم {ثم قال} ومن عاد فأولئك أصحاب النار هم فيها خالدون جعل آكل الربا أول الأمر مؤذنا بمحاربة الله وفي آخره متعرضا للنار والآيات الواردة في الحلال والحرام لا تحصى وروى ابن مسعود رضي الله عنه عن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم أنه قال طلب الحلال فريضة على كل مسلم ولما قال صلى الله عليه وسلم طلب", "english": "God Most High has said: {«كلوا من الطيبات واعملوا صالحا»} — \"Eat of the good things and do righteous deeds.\" (Q. 23:51) He commanded eating from the good things before [commanding] righteous deeds. And it is said that what is meant by \"the good things\" is the lawful (al-ḥalāl).", "book_title": "The Lawful and Prohibited", "section_title_ar": "Section 336", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Book of the Etiquette of Eating: Section 336 — The Virtue of Lawful Sustenance and the Blameworthiness of the Forbidden", "section_index": 336, "anchors": "- Root: اكل (akl)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of eating, consuming, and tasting food; the act of taking nourishment into the body.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأكل: تناول المطعم\" — eating is the taking of food; used metaphorically for fire consuming wood, and extended to mean lawful provision (ḥalāl) as the sustenance of the soul's soundness.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) for physical ingestion; metaphorical (majāz) for consuming wealth unlawfully, as in \"أكل مال اليتيم\" (devouring the orphan's wealth).\n- Root: طعم (tuʿm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Food, sustenance, and the act of tasting; also used for bait and that which is eaten.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Sustenance as that which nourishes the body; in the Kalam context, the lawful (ḥalāl) is that which purifies the heart, while the forbidden (ḥarām) corrupts it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for food; metaphorical for the moral state of the soul — \"طعمه حرام\" (his food is forbidden) signifies the corruption of one's entire being.\n- Root: ورق (waraʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Abstinence, scrupulousness, and piety; refraining from doubtful matters.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The theological virtue of cautious avoidance of all that is suspect, as the foundation of true worship and the key to answered supplication.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for refraining; metaphorical for the heart's purity achieved through guarding the limbs from the unlawful.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The syntactic structure of conditional and consequential clauses, where the protasis (e.g., \"من أكل حراما\") governs the apodosis (\"لم يقبل منه صرف ولا عدل\"), demonstrating the causal nexus between action and divine recompense.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'The Lawful and Prohibited'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "11": [{"arabic": "يوم القيامة إلى من اغتابه بدلا عما استباحه من عرضه فإن لم تكن له حسنات نقل إليه من سيئات خصمه وهو مع ذلك متعرض لمقت الله عز وجل ومشبه عنده بآكل الميتة بل العبد يدخل النار بأن تترجح كفة سيئاته على كفة حسناته وربما تنقل إليه سيئة واحدة ممن اغتابه فيحصل بها الرجحان ويدخل بها النار وإنما أقل الدرجات أن تنقص من ثواب أعماله وذلك بعد المخاصمة والمطالبة والسؤال والجواب والحساب قال صلى الله عليه وسلم ما النار في اليبس بأسرع من الغيبة في حسنات العبد (2) وروي أن رجلا قال للحسن بلغني أنك تغتابني فقال ما بلغ من قدرك عندي أ", "english": "As for the summary: it is that he know that by his backbiting he exposes himself to the wrath of God — exalted is He — according to these reports which we have related; and that he know that backbiting obliterates his good deeds on the Day of Resurrection, for it transfers his good deeds on the Day of Resurrection to the one he backbit, in exchange for what he unlawfully took of his honor. And if he has no good deeds, then there is transferred to him from the evil deeds of his adversary. And he is, moreover, exposed to the wrath of God — mighty and majestic is He — and is likened in His sight to an eater of carrion. Nay, the servant enters the Fire because the scale of his evil deeds outweighs the scale of his good deeds; and perhaps a single evil deed is transferred to him from the one he backbit, and by it the outweighing occurs, and by it he enters the Fire. And the least of the degrees is that it diminishes the reward of his works — and that after the dispute, the demand, the question, the answer, and the reckoning. He said — peace be upon him: {«ما النار في اليبس بأسرع من الغيبة في حسنات العبد»} \"Fire is not swifter in dry wood than backbiting is in the good deeds of a servant.\" And it is related that a man said to al-Hasan: \"It has reached me that you backbite me.\" He said: \"It has not reached the extent of your worth in my sight that I should make you the arbiter over my good deeds.\" So when the servant believes in what has come down of the reports concerning backbiting, he will not unleash his tongue upon it, out of fear of that. And it also benefits him to reflect upon himself: for if he finds in himself a fault, he occupies himself with his own fault and recalls his saying — peace be upon him: {«طوبى لمن شغله عيبه عن عيوب الناس»} \"Blessed is he whose own fault occupies him from the faults of people.\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of Anger and Hatred", "section_title_ar": "Section 686", "section_title_en": "The Cure of the Tongue from Backbiting: General and Specific Remedies", "section_index": 686, "anchors": "- Root: غيب (ghayb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: That which is hidden from the senses; absence from sight.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Used of what is absent from human perception, though nothing is absent from God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: concealment from the eye. Metaphorical: speaking of one in his absence (ghībah).\n- Root: عيب (ʿayb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A flaw, defect, or blemish; to find fault with another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Moral deficiency that diminishes one's perfection before God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a mark of deficiency. Metaphorical: moral failing that warrants self-reproach rather than blame of others.\n- Root: غضب (ghaḍab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Anger; violent displeasure.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Divine wrath as the consequence of transgression against divine commands.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: agitation of the soul. Metaphorical: God's punitive will against the disobedient.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Conditional and Exceptive Constructions (Bāb al-sharṭ wa-l-istithnāʾ) — Governing the logical structure of remedies conditioned upon causes.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Condemnation of Anger and Hatred'", "badge": "Afterlife"}, {"arabic": "محصورا على مصلاه الذي يصلي عليه فإن لم يكن له مصلى فليقرب من جدار الحائط أو ليخط خطا فإن ذلك يقصر مسافة البصر ويمنع تفوق الفكر وليحجر على بصره أن يجاوز أطراف المصلى وحدود الخط وليدم على هذا القيام كذلك إلى الركوع من غير التفات هذا أدب القيام فإذا استوى قيامه واستقباله وإطراقه كذلك فليقرأ {قل أعوذ برب الناس} تحصنا به من الشيطان ثم ليأت بالإقامة وإن كان يرجو حضور من يقتدي به فليؤذن أولا ثم ليحضر النية وهو أن ينوي في الظهر مثلا ويقول بقلبه أؤدي فريضة الظهر لله ليميزها بقول", "english": "This is the etiquette of standing. When his standing, his facing, and his lowering of the head are even, let him recite {«قُلْ أَعُوذُ بِرَبِّ النَّاسِ»} (Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of mankind), seeking protection thereby from the devil. Then let him perform the iqāmah. And if he hopes for the presence of one who will follow him in prayer, let him give the adhān first. Then let him bring forth the intention (niyyah), which is that he intends, for example, in the ẓuhr prayer, saying in his heart: \"I perform the obligatory ẓuhr prayer for Allah,\" distinguishing it by his utterance: \"I perform it as an obligation (adāʾan)\" from the make-up prayer (qaḍāʾ), and by \"obligatory\" from the supererogatory (nafl), and by \"ẓuhr\" from the ʿaṣr and others. Let the meanings of these words be present in his heart, for that [presence] is the intention itself, while the words are reminders and causes for its presence. Let him strive to sustain this [intention] until the end of the takbīr, so that it does not slip away.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Anger and Hatred", "section_title_ar": "Section 126", "section_title_en": "Section 126 — The Second Book on the Manner of the Outward Acts of Prayer, Beginning with the Takbīr and What Precedes It", "section_index": 126, "anchors": "- Root: صفن (ṣafana)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To draw up one leg and sit upon it; also used of a horse standing on three legs, raising the fourth.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The posture of drawing up one foot, as in the description of the swift horses {«الصَّافِنَاتُ الْجِيَادُ»} (the standing steeds, the swift).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the physical posture of raising one leg. Metaphorical: not applicable here; used strictly in its corporeal sense.\n- Root: صفد (ṣafada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To fetter, bind, or chain together; al-ṣafad is the iron shackle.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Binding in chains, as in {«مُقَرَّنِينَ فِي الْأَصْفَادِ»} (bound together in fetters).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical binding of the feet together. The Prophet (peace be upon him) prohibited both postures in prayer as they mimic the bound or the beast.\n- Root: قصد (qaṣada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To intend, aim, or direct oneself toward something; also moderation and evenness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The intention (niyyah) that directs the heart toward the act, distinguishing one act from another.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to stand upright and face a direction. Metaphorical: the heart's orientation toward the divine command.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The syntactic principle that governs the placement of prepositional phrases, here applied to the physical orientation of the body toward the qiblah as a grammatical parallel to the soul's orientation toward Allah.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Anger and Hatred'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "بالولود الودود (1) فإن لم يكن لها زوج ولم يعرف حالها فيراعي صحتها وشبابها فإنها تكون ولودا في الغالب مع هذين الوصفين السادسة أن تكون بكرا قال صلى الله عليه وسلم لجابر وقد نكح ثيبا هلا بكرا تلاعبها وتلاعبك (2) في البكارة ثلاث فوائد إحداها أن تحب الزوج وتألفه فيؤثر في معنى الود وقد قال صلى الله عليه وسلم عليكم بالودود والطباع مجبولة على الأنس بأول مألوف وأما التي اختبرت الرجال ومارست الأحوال فربما لا ترضى بعض الأوصاف التي تخالف ما ألفته فتقلي الزوج الثانية أن ذلك أكمل في مودته", "english": "...one of you sees a woman, let him look at her, for that is more likely to bring about harmony (iʾtilāf) between them — that is, to unite them — than the mere occurrence of the inner skin (al-adamah) upon the outer skin (al-basharah). He only mentioned that for emphasis on the union. And he, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"Verily, in the eyes of the Ansar there is something, so when one of you wishes to marry from among them, let him look at them.\" It was said that there was a weakness of the eyes (ʿamash) in them, and it was said it was smallness of the eyes. And some of the scrupulous (al-warʿīn) would not marry their noble women except after looking, as a precaution against deception (al-ghurūr).", "book_title": "Etiquette of Marriage", "section_title_ar": "Section 301", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Book on the Etiquettes of Marriage: Section 301 — On the Recommended Attributes of the Wife (Continued)", "section_index": 301, "anchors": "- Root: أدم (ʾ-d-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of harmony, concord, and the inner skin (al-adamah) beneath the outer skin (al-basharah); also used for the reconciliation between two parties.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance of ʾulfah (intimate familiarity) as a divinely ordained bond between spouses, achieved through mutual sight and attraction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqiqah) usage refers to the physical inner skin; metaphorical (majaz) usage denotes the spiritual and emotional union (iʾtilāf) between husband and wife.\n- Root: عقر (ʿ-q-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Barrenness (ʿuqr) in women; also the root for cutting off, as in severing lineage or progeny.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): A state of incapacity to bear children, which in the context of marriage contracts negates a primary purpose of the union (procreation).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical barrenness; metaphorical: the severance of the hoped-for continuity of offspring.\n- Root: ودد (w-d-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Love, affection, and steadfast amity (wudd).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): A specific, enduring form of love (mahabbah) that is active and volitional, distinct from mere inclination.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the affection of the heart; metaphorical: the outward expression of that affection through service and companionship.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to isolate the exclusive purpose of a clause, e.g., \"إنما ذكر ذلك للمبالغة في الائتلاف\" (He only mentioned that for emphasis on union), restricting the reason to a single, specific cause.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Etiquette of Marriage'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "يناسب حاله قوله تعالى {يوصيكم الله في أولادكم للذكر مثل حظ الأنثيين} وقوله تعالى {والذين يرمون المحصنات} وكذلك جميع الآيات التي فيها بيان أحكام الميراث والطلاق والحدود وغيرها وإنما المحرك لما في القلب ما يناسبه والأبيات إنما يضعها الشعراء إعرابا بها عن أحوال القلب فلا يحتاج في فهم الحال منها إلى تكلف نعم من يستولي عليه حالة غالبة قاهرة لم تبق فيه متسعا لغيرها ومعه تيقظ وذكاء ثاقب يتفطن به للمعاني البعيدة من الألفاظ فقد يخرج وجده على كل مسموع كمن يخطر له عند ذكر قوله تعالى {يوصيكم الله في أولادكم} حالة الموت المحوج", "english": "Some of the Sufis said: \"One night I was reciting this verse: {كُلُّ نَفْسٍ ذَائِقَةُ الْمَوْتِ} «Every soul shall taste death» [Q. 3:185]. I kept repeating it, and lo, a caller cried out to me: 'How long will you repeat this verse? You have killed four of the jinn who have not raised their heads to the sky since they were created.'\"", "book_title": "Audition and Ecstasy", "section_title_ar": "Section 512", "section_title_en": "On the Ecstatic States (Wajd) Produced by Qur'anic Recitation and the Superiority of Song (Ghinā') in Stirring the Heart", "section_index": 512, "anchors": "- Root: وجد (w-j-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of finding, attaining, and also denoting the emotional state of ecstasy or rapture that \"finds\" the heart.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the theological context, 'wajd' is the state of spiritual finding—a direct, overwhelming apprehension of divine realities that bypasses discursive reasoning.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes \"finding\" a lost object; metaphorical (Majaz) usage denotes the heart \"finding\" its spiritual state, leading to agitation and ecstasy.\n- Root: سمع (s-m-')\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The faculty of hearing; also the ear itself.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Hearing can be literal (perception of sound) or figurative (understanding, obedience, acceptance). The heart's \"hearing\" is a deeper comprehension.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"سمعته\" (I heard him). Metaphorical: \"سمع الله لمن حمده\" (God answers/he accepts the one who praises Him), indicating a responsive, transformative hearing.\n- Root: موت (m-w-t)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Death; the opposite of life.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Death is the cessation of the soul's governance over the body, a separation. In the spiritual context, it can be a metaphorical death to worldly desires.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical death. Metaphorical: \"أحيا الله البلد الميت\" (God revives the dead land), indicating spiritual revival or the death of the ego.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to emphasize that the stirring of the heart is exclusively through what is appropriate to its state, as in the phrase \"إنما المحرك لما في القلب ما يناسبه\" (The only thing that moves what is in the heart is that which is suitable to it).", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Audition and Ecstasy'", "badge": "Insight"}], "12": [{"arabic": "ولست زاهدا مطلقا وإن لم يكن لك مال ولم تساعدك الدنيا لم يتصور منك الزهد لأن ما لا يقدر عليه لا يقوى على تركه وربما يستهويك الشيطان بغروره ويخيل إليك أن الدنيا وإن لم تأتك فأنت زاهدا فيها فلا ينبغي أن تتدلى بحبل غروره دون أن تستوثق وتستظهر بموثق غليظ من الله فإنك إذا لم تجرب حال القدرة فلا تثق بالقدرة على الترك عندها فكم من ظان بنفسه كراهة المعاصي عند تعذرها فلما تيسرت له أسبابها من غير مكدر ولا خوف من الخلق وقع فيها وإذا كان هذا غرور النفس في المحظورات فإياك أن تثق بوعدها في الم", "english": "...better and more enduring. Yet one may know this while being unable to abandon this world — either due to the weakness of one's knowledge and certainty, or due to the dominance of lust prevailing over him in the present moment, making him vanquished in the hand of Satan, or due to his being deluded by Satan's promises of procrastination, day after day, until death snatches him away and nothing remains with him but regret after the loss. And to the recognition of the baseness of this world points His saying, exalted: {قُلْ مَتَاعُ الدُّنْيَا قَلِيلٌ} — \"Say: the enjoyment of this world is little\" (Q. 3:196). And to the recognition of the preciousness of the Hereafter points His saying, mighty and majestic: {وَقَالَ الَّذِينَ أُوتُوا الْعِلْمَ وَيْلَكُمْ ثَوَابُ اللَّهِ خَيْرٌ} — \"And those who were given knowledge said: Woe to you! The reward of God is better\" (Q. 28:80). Thus He alerted that it is knowledge of the preciousness of the jewel that turns one away from its substitute. And since zuhd is inconceivable except through an exchange and a turning away from the beloved toward one more beloved, a man said in his supplication: \"O God, show me this world as You see it.\" The Prophet, peace be upon him, said to him: \"Do not say that, but say: Show me this world as You have shown it to the righteous among Your servants.\" This is because God, exalted, sees it as contemptible as it truly is — and every creature, in relation to His majesty, is contemptible — whereas the servant sees it as contemptible in itself relative to what is better for him. It is inconceivable that the seller of a horse, even if he turns away from his horse, should see it as he sees, say, the vermin of the earth, for he has no need whatsoever of vermin, while he is not free of need for the horse. God, exalted, is rich by His essence from all besides Him, so He sees all things at a single degree relative to His majesty, while He sees them as differentiated relative to what is other than Him. The ascetic (al-zāhid) is the one who sees their differentiation relative to himself, not relative to another.", "book_title": "Poverty and Abstinence", "section_title_ar": "Section 1089", "section_title_en": "On the Reality of Zuhd (Ascetic Renunciation) and Its Conditions — Section 1089", "section_index": 1089, "anchors": "- Root: دنا (d-n-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Proximity, nearness; \"adnā\" as lesser/lower in rank or worth.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Adnā\" denotes what is inferior or meaner, contrasted with \"khayr\" (the better), as in {أَتَسْتَبْدِلُونَ الَّذِي هُوَ أَدْنَىٰ بِالَّذِي هُوَ خَيْرٌ} (Q. 2:61).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqiqah) sense of lowliness and despicableness; metaphorical (majaz) extension to moral baseness.\n- Root: زهد (z-h-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To abstain, to regard as little; also \"zahada al-nakhl\" — to estimate fruit yield (connoting measured appraisal).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-zāhid fī al-shay': al-rāghib 'anhu\" — the one who turns away from a thing, content with little of it; Q. 12:20 {وَكَانُوا فِيهِ مِنَ الزَّاهِدِينَ}.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: renunciation of a thing, paucity of desire. Metaphorical: \"zahīd al-'ayn\" — one satisfied with little; \"wādin zahīd\" — a valley taking little water. Zuhd is not mere abandonment but a valuation-based exchange.\n- Root: ترك (t-r-k)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To leave, abandon, relinquish; \"tarkah\" — inheritance left behind.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Abandonment by choice and intention, or by compulsion; \"taraktu fulānan wahīdan\" — I left him alone, i.e., in a resultant state.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"taraka al-shay'\" — to leave a thing; \"tārakahu al-bay'\" — mutual abandonment of a sale. The term implies a deliberate act with a resultant condition.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and specification via \"innamā\" (الحصر والتقييد) — used to delimit the true definition of zuhd against mere relinquishment, and to distinguish the act of the ascetic from the mere spender.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Poverty and Abstinence'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "فكيف كان في الأزل مختصا بجهة والجهة حادثة وكيف صار مختصا بجهة بعد أن لم يكن له أبأن خلق العالم فوقه ويتعالى عن أن يكون له فوق إذ تعالى أن يكون له رأس والفوق عبارة عما يكون جهة الرأس أو خلق العالم تحته فتعالى عن أن يكون له تحت إذ تعالى عن أن يكون له رجل والتحت عبارة عما يلي جهة الرجل وكل ذلك مما يستحيل في العقل ولأن المعقول من كونه مختصا بجهة أنه مختص بحيز اختصاص الجواهر أو مختص بالجواهر اختصاص العرض وقد ظهر استحالة كونه جوهرا أو عرضا فاستحال كونه مختصا بالجهة وإن أريد بالجهة غير هذين المعنيين كان غلطا في الاسم مع المساعدة", "english": "**THE SEVENTH PRINCIPLE:** Knowledge that God, exalted be He, is sanctified in His essence from being particularized by directions (jihāt). For direction is either above or below, right or left, front or behind. And these directions — He it is Who created them and brought them into being through the medium of creating man. For He created for man two extremities: one upon which he rests upon the earth, called a foot; and the other opposite to it, called a head. Thus arose the name \"above\" (al-fawq) for what faces the direction of the head, and the name \"below\" (al-sufl) for what faces the direction of the foot — so much so that the ant which creeps inverted upon the ceiling has its direction of \"above\" turned into \"below\" relative to it, even though it is \"above\" relative to us.", "book_title": "Poverty and Abstinence", "section_title_ar": "Section 89", "section_title_en": "** The Fifth through Seventh Principles: God's Transcendence Beyond Corporeality, Accidents, and Spatial Direction", "section_index": 89, "anchors": "**\n- Root: جسم (j-s-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Corporeal mass, body composed of parts; that which has extension and composition\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-jism applies to what has no apparent color (like water and air), while al-jasad is specific to what has color and form; the Quranic usage {«عِجْلًا جَسَدًا لَهُ خُوَارٌ»} (20:88) confirms this distinction\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل جسيم، وفيه جسامة\" (a corpulent man); Metaphorical: \"أمر جسيم\" (a grave matter) — here the term is used strictly in its technical Kalam sense of composite substance\n- Root: خلق (kh-l-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Measuring, estimating, creating from nothing; bringing into existence upon a determined measure\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخلق أصله: التقدير المستقيم\" — creation as origination (ibdāʿ) without prior model or template\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"خلق الخراز الأديم\" (the tanner measured the leather before cutting); Metaphorical: \"خلق الله الخلق: أوجده على تقدير أوجبته الحكمة\" — divine creation as measured origination\n- Root: حدث (ḥ-d-th)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Origination after non-existence; being new; temporal contingency\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحدوث: كون الشيء بعد أن لم يكن\" — the mark of contingency; \"إحداث الجواهر ليس إلا لله تعالى\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"نزلت به حوادث الدهر\" (calamities befell him); Metaphorical: \"كان عمر رضي الله عنه محدثا أي صادق الحدس\" — here used in its technical theological sense of temporal origination\n- Root: جهة (j-h-h)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Direction, orientation, spatial region; that toward which one faces\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Direction is among the created accidents (aʿrāḍ) that pertain to bodies\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal directional reference vs. metaphorical elevation of rank and dominion\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The conditional constructions \"ولو جاز... لجاز\" and \"فإن تجاسر... كان\" follow the standard Arabic periodic structure where the protasis and apodosis maintain syntactic parallelism, and the negative \"ليس بجوهر ولا جسم ولا عرض\" employs the continuous negation pattern (lā with the accusative) to achieve comprehensive exclusion.\n\n---\n\n**", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Poverty and Abstinence'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "بمجرده وإن لم تكن له فائدة ولكنه مهم في نفسه للوصول إلا إثبات الحديث إذ التفهم بعد الإثبات والعمل بعد التفهم فالأول السماع ثم التفهم ثم الحفظ ثم العمل ثم النشر وهؤلاء اقتصروا من الجملة على السماع ثم تركوا حقيقة السماع فترى الصبي يحضر في مجلس الشيح والحديث يقرأ والشيخ ينام والصبي يلعب ثم يكتب اسم الصبي في السماع فإذا كبر تصدى ليسمع منه والبالغ الذي يحضر ربما يغفل ولا يسمع ولا يصغي ولا يضبط وربما يشتغل بحديث أو نسخ والشيخ الذي يقرأ عليه لو صحف وغير ما يقرأ عليه لم يشع", "english": "But as for these [deluded ones], they turn others away from the path of God and drag creation into self-deception concerning God (al-ghurūr billāh) by means of the word \"hope\" (al-rajāʾ). Thus their speech increases them in audacity toward sins and desire for this world — especially when the preacher is adorned with fine garments, horses, and mounts, for his very appearance bears witness from his hair to his feet to his intense greed for this world. So what this deluded one corrupts is more than what he reforms — nay, he reforms nothing at all, and he leads many people astray. Nor is the aspect of his being deluded hidden.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Delusion", "section_title_ar": "Section 899", "section_title_en": "The Categories of the Deluded Among Preachers and Hadith Transmitters", "section_index": 899, "anchors": "- Root: أ-ص-ل (ʾ-ṣ-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of foundation, root, and origin; to set right what is corrupt.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): اصلاح (iṣlāḥ) as the act of bringing something from a state of corruption (fasād) to soundness (ṣalāḥ), whether in belief, action, or speech.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage denotes rectifying a physical object; metaphorical (majāz) usage applies to moral and spiritual correction of others.\n- Root: غ-ر-ر (gh-r-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: غرور (ghurūr) — deception, delusion, vanity; from غرّ (gharra), to be heedless or deceived by appearances.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الغرور (al-ghurūr) is the delusion that leads one to rely on false hopes, particularly regarding God's mercy while persisting in sin; the state of one who is deceived by Satan or by his own vain fancies.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a thing that deceives by its outward show; Metaphorical: the soul's self-deception through false reliance on divine forgiveness without repentance.\n- Root: س-م-ع (s-m-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: السمع (al-samʿ) — hearing; the auditory faculty and its act.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Hearing as the means of acquiring transmitted knowledge (samāʿ); in hadith terminology, the formal act of hearing a tradition from a shaykh, which is the first stage of transmission.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the physical act of hearing; Metaphorical: attentive listening with the heart, as in \"ألقى السمع وهو شهيد\" {«وَأَلْقَى السَّمْعَ وَهُوَ شَهِيدٌ»} [Q. 50:37] — one who listens with full presence of mind.\n- Root: ح-ف-ظ (ḥ-f-ẓ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: الحفظ (al-ḥifẓ) — preservation, guarding, memorization; keeping something safe from loss or alteration.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الحفظ is the faculty of the soul by which what is understood is retained; its opposite is forgetfulness (nisyān). It also denotes vigilant care and protection.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: guarding a physical object; Metaphorical: committing knowledge to memory such that it is preserved from distortion.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governing the separation of a preposition from its object by intervening clauses, as in the periodic structure \"فإذا لم تحفظ لا بالقلب ولا بالكتاب وجرى على سمعك صوت غفل\" where the conditional clause (فإذا) governs a sequence of coordinated conditions, each requiring its own apodosis, demonstrating the classical pattern of layered conditional sentences.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Condemnation of Delusion'", "badge": "Ethics"}, {"arabic": "بناه ولو على بعد وإن لم يكن له مالك معين فهو لمصالح المسلمين ومهما كان في المسجد الكبير بناء لسلطان ظالم فلا عذر لمن يصلي فيه مع اتساع المسجد أعني في الورع قيل لأحمد بن حنبل ما حجتك في ترك الخروج إلى الصلاة في جماعة ونحن بالعسكر فقال حجتي أن الحسن وإبراهيم التيمي خافا أن يفتنهما الحجاج وأنا أخاف أن أفتن أيضا وأما الخلوق والتجصيص فلا يمنع من الدخول لأنه غير منتفع به في الصلاة وإنما هو زينة والأولى أنه لا ينظر إليه وأما البواري التي فرشوها فإن كان لها مالك معين فيحرم الجلوس عليها وإل", "english": "Whenever there is in the grand mosque a structure [built] by a tyrannical sultan, there is no excuse for one who prays therein when the mosque is spacious — I mean, with respect to scrupulous piety. It was said to Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal: \"What is your proof for refraining from going out to congregational prayer while we are in the military camp?\" He replied: \"My proof is that al-Ḥasan and Ibrāhīm al-Taymī feared that al-Ḥajjāj would tempt them — and I too fear that I would be tempted.\"", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 396", "section_title_en": "** Section 396: On Structures Built by Tyrants — Bridges, Ribāṭs, Mosques, and Water Fountains", "section_index": 396, "anchors": "**\n\n- Root: قنطر (Q-N-Ṭ-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes a bridge or archway spanning water; al-qanṭarah is that which is built across a river for crossing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Not a distinct theological term; treated under general categories of lawful benefit (manfaʿah) and unlawful appropriation (ghaṣb).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — a physical structure for passage; metaphorical extension applies to any means of crossing over an obstacle.\n\n- Root: ربط (R-B-Ṭ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To bind, tie, or secure; al-ribāṭ is a fortified outpost where horses are bound and warriors stationed for defense of frontiers.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In religious usage, ribāṭ signifies steadfastness in obedience and guarding the frontiers of faith.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — binding of horses; metaphorical — the binding of the soul in devotion and the establishment of hospices for travelers and warriors.\n\n- Root: سقى (S-Q-Y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To give drink; al-siqāyah is a water fountain or trough for public drinking.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Water is the substance of life; providing it publicly is among the greatest charitable acts.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — the act of quenching thirst; metaphorical — spiritual nourishment through knowledge.\n\n- Root: غصب (Gh-Ṣ-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To take something by force, unjustly seizing another's property.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ghaṣb is the unlawful appropriation of another's property without right; its gravity lies in violating ownership rights established by divine law.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — forcible seizure; metaphorical — any unlawful use of another's property, including occupying land or using materials without permission.\n\n- Root: سبل (S-B-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To make available, to dedicate for public use; sabīl is a path or way.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Irsād (dedication) for the public good (maṣāliḥ al-muslimīn) transforms private property into charitable endowment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — a road or path; metaphorical — any means dedicated to righteous purposes.\n\n- Sibawayh Rule: **Bāb al-Taʿlīq bi al-Sharṭ wa al-Jazāʾ** (Conditional and Consequential Clauses) — The text employs conditional structures (idhā... fa-) to establish legal rulings contingent upon specific circumstances, distinguishing between cases where ownership is known versus unknown.\n\n---\n\n**", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "13": [{"arabic": "وخطب رجل عند رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم فقال من يطع الله ورسوله فقد رشد ومن يعصهما فقد غوى فقال قل ومن يعص الله ورسوله فقد غوى (2) فكره رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم قوله ومن يعصهما لأنه تسوية وجمع وكان إبراهيم يكره أن يقول الرجل أعوذ بالله وبك ويجوز أن يقول أعوذ بالله ثم بك وأن يقول لولا الله ثم فلان ولا يقول لولا الله وفلان وكره بعضهم أن يقال اللهم أعتقنا من النار وكان يقول العتق يكون بعد الورود وكانوا يستجيرون من النار ويتعوذون من النار وقال رجل اللهم اجعلني ممن تصيبه شفاعة محمد صلى الله عليه وسلم فق", "english": "An example of this is what Ḥudhayfah reported: The Prophet, peace be upon him, said: \"Let none of you say: 'What Allah willed and you willed' — but rather let him say: 'What Allah willed, then you willed.'\" (5) This is because in the absolute conjunction [with \"and\"] there is an association and an equalization, which is contrary to [the due measure of] reverence. And Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, speaking to him concerning some matter, and he said: \"What Allah willed and you willed.\" The Prophet, peace be upon him, said: \"Have you made me an equal to Allah? Rather, [say]: 'What Allah willed alone.'\" (1)", "book_title": "Condemnation of Anger and Hatred", "section_title_ar": "Section 698", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 698: The Nineteenth Affliction — Heedlessness Regarding Subtle Errors in the Import of Speech", "section_index": 698, "anchors": "- Root: غفل (ghafla)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of heedlessness, neglect, and inattention; the absence of vigilance over a matter.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Heedlessness (al-ghafla) is the soul's failure to attend to what it is obligated to know or consider; it is the opposite of mindfulness (al-tayaqquẓ) and remembrance (al-dhikr).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage denotes the absence of guarding; metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to the tongue's straying from precise expression in sacred matters.\n- Root: عطف (ʿaṭf)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Inclination, turning, or joining one thing to another; grammatically, the conjunction that links two items in a shared ruling.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Joining (al-ʿaṭf) implies equal treatment of the conjoined items; in theological speech, absolute conjunction (al-ʿaṭf al-muṭlaq) with Allah entails an inadmissible association.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the bending of a staff or the inclining of a branch; metaphorical: the grammatical coupling that levels two referents under one predicate.\n- Root: شرك (shirk)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sharing, partnership, or association; the root of polytheism (al-shirk) as the ascription of a partner to Allah.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Association (al-shirk) is the equalization of two things in a shared ruling; in theology, it is the gravest form when applied to the Divine, whether in belief or in the mere wording of speech.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: co-participation in ownership; metaphorical: any verbal or creedal equalization of the creature with the Creator.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Conjunction (al-ʿaṭf) and Restriction (al-ḥaṣr) — The particle \"thumma\" (then) indicates sequential ordering and distinction of rank, whereas \"wa\" (and) denotes absolute conjunction and mutual equivalence; thus the Prophet's command to replace \"wa\" with \"thumma\" in \"what Allah willed and you willed\" is a syntactic correction to abolish the implication of equal partnership.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Condemnation of Anger and Hatred'", "badge": "Du'a"}], "14": [{"arabic": "بالمشيئة يدل على الانقسام وقوله تعالى {ومن يعص الله ورسوله فإن له نار جهنم خالدين فيها} وتخصيصه بالكفر تحكم وقوله تعالى {ألا إن الظالمين في عذاب مقيم} وقال تعالى {ومن جاء بالسيئة فكبت وجوههم في النار} فهذه العمومات في معارضة عموماتهم ولا بد من تسليط التخصص والتأويل على الجانبين لأن الأخبار مصرحة بأن العصاة يعذبون (1) بل قوله تعالى وإن منكم إلا واردها كالصريح في أن ذلك لا بد منه للكل إذ لا يخلو مؤمن عن ذنب يرتكبه وقوله تعالى لا يصلاها إلا الأشقى الذي كذب وتولى أراد به من جماعة مخصوصين أو أراد بالأشقى شخصا", "english": "He was asked, \"Which deed is most excellent?\" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Islam.\" It was then asked, \"Which aspect of Islam is most excellent?\" He (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Faith (Īmān).\" This is evidence for both the distinction (ikhtilāf) between the two terms and their overlap (tadākhul). This usage is the most consistent with the conventions of the language, because faith is a deed among deeds, and it is the most excellent of them. Islam is submission (taslīm), whether by the heart, by the tongue, or by the limbs. The most excellent of these is that which is by the heart, which is the assent (taṣdīq) that is called faith. Using the two terms in a way that indicates distinction, overlap, or even complete synonymy (tarāduf) is all within the bounds of metaphorical usage (tajawwuz) in the language.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Anger and Hatred", "section_title_ar": "Section 99", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 99: On the Distinction, Overlap, and Synonymy of Faith (Īmān) and Submission (Islām), and Their Worldly and Otherworldly Judgments", "section_index": 99, "anchors": "- Root: ايم (A-Y-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of remaining unmarried, or a state of being without a spouse; extended metaphorically to describe one who is solitary or without support.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأيامى: جمع أيم، وهي المرأة التي لا بعل لها... والحرب مأيمة، أي: يفرق بين الزوج والزوجة.\" The root carries the sense of separation or a state of lacking union, which informs the theological discussion of what constitutes the essential bond of faith.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes the state of being unmarried; metaphorical (Majaz) usage extends to anything that separates or leaves one bereft. This distinction between literal and metaphorical application is central to the chapter's method.\n- Root: قلب (Q-L-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn, overturn, or transform a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه... والانقلاب: الانصراف.\" The heart (qalb) is so named for its constant fluctuation and turning. In theological terms, it is the seat of taṣdīq (assent), the immaterial faculty of cognition and conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is physical turning; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"قلب الأمور\" — to deliberate, scrutinize, and manage affairs. This supports the distinction between the heart's internal act of assent and the external acts of the limbs.\n- Root: عمل (ʿ-A-M-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Any deed or action performed with purpose and intent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العمل: كل فعل يكون من الحيوان بقصد، فهو أخص من الفعل.\" Work is more specific than mere action, as it implies deliberate, intentional agency. It is used for both righteous and sinful deeds, and is the external manifestation of internal conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage refers to physical labor or deeds. The chapter explores whether \"work\" is an integral part of the reality of faith or a separate, consequent requirement.\n- Root: صدق (S-D-Q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be truthful, to speak the truth, to verify.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصدق والكذب أصلهما في القول... ولا يكونان بالقصد الأول إلا في القول.\" Truth and falsehood are primarily properties of speech and reports. Taṣdīq (assent) is the heart's affirmation of the truth of a report, which is the core reality of Īmān.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is truthful speech; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"صادق الحملة\" — one who is true and firm in battle or resolve. This shows that taṣdīq is not merely verbal but a firm, internal resolution.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication). This rule governs how restriction (ḥaṣr) is achieved in Arabic, often through particles like \"innamā\" or through contextual specification (takhṣīṣ). The chapter's core argument hinges on how terms like Īmān and Islām are semantically restricted, expanded, or made synonymous through contextual usage (majāz), requiring precise parsing of the predicate and its scope.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Condemnation of Anger and Hatred'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "18": [{"arabic": "وذلك سوء الخاتمة ولمثل هذا يقال {وليست التوبة للذين يعملون السيئات حتى إذا حضر أحدهم الموت قال إني تبت الآن} وقوله {إنما التوبة على الله للذين يعملون السوء بجهالة ثم يتوبون من قريب} ومعناه عن قرب عهد بالخطيئة بأن يتندم عليها ويمحو أثرها بحسنة يردفها بها قبل أن يتراكم الرين على القلب فلا يقبل المحو ولذلك قال صلى الله عليه وسلم أتبع السيئة الحسنة تمحها ولذلك قال لقمان لابنه يا بني لا تؤخر التوبة فإن الموت يأتي بغتة ومن ترك المبادرة إلى التوبة بالتسويف كان بين خطرين عظيمين {أحدهما} أن تتراكم الظلمة على قلبه من المعاصي حتى يصير رينا وطبعا فلا يقبل المحو الثاني", "english": "I say: Every human being is never free from disobedience of the bodily members (jawāriḥ), for even the prophets were not free from it — as has come down in the Qurʾān and the reports concerning the sins of the prophets, their repentance, and their weeping over their misdeeds. And if one is free, in some states, from disobedience of the members, he is not free from the heart's inclination (hamm) toward sins. And if he is free, in some states, from the inclination, he is not free from the whispering of Satan (waswās al-shayṭān) by way of importing scattered thoughts that divert from the remembrance of God. And if he is free from that, he is not free from heedlessness (ghaflah) and deficiency in knowledge of God, His attributes, and His acts. All of that is deficiency (nuqṣān), and it has causes; and abandoning its causes by occupying oneself with their opposites is a reversion from one path to its contrary. The meaning of \"repentance\" (tawbah) is \"returning\" (rujūʿ), and it is inconceivable that a human being should be entirely free of this deficiency — rather, people differ only in degrees. As for the principle itself, it is unavoidable. For this reason, he (peace be upon him) said: \"Truly, a veil is cast over my heart, so that I seek forgiveness from God seventy times a day and night\" (1) — the hadith. And for this reason, God (exalted be He) honored him by saying: {«لِيَغْفِرَ لَكَ اللَّهُ مَا تَقَدَّمَ مِنْ ذَنْبِكَ وَمَا تَأَخَّرَ»} [That God may forgive you what has passed of your sin and what remains]. And if this was his state, how then is the state of anyone else?", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 922", "section_title_en": "On the Universality of Repentance (Tawbah) and Its Obligation in Every Breath", "section_index": 922, "anchors": "- Root: توب (T-W-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of \"returning\" (rujūʿ) — turning back from a path to its opposite; the root denotes the act of revisiting and reversing a prior state.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Tawbah is \"abandoning the sin in the most excellent manner\" — the most complete form of excuse; in sacred law (sharʿ), it is abandoning the sin due to its ugliness, regretting what has passed, resolving not to return, and rectifying what can be rectified of past deeds through renewed acts.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"He repented to God from his sin\" (tāba al-ʿabdu ilā Allāhi min dhanbihi). Metaphorical (majāz): The extension of \"repentance\" to inner purification of the heart from accumulated rust (rayn), as the root's sense of \"returning\" is applied to the soul's reversion from negligence to vigilance.\n- Root: قلب (Q-L-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The heart as the organ of turning and overturning (taqlīb) — its state fluctuates between illumination and darkness, purity and rust.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Turning a thing\" is directing it from one face to another; the heart (qalb) is so named for its constant overturning between states — a key metaphysical anchor for Ghazali's doctrine of the heart's susceptibility to stains (akdār) and polish (ṣaqal).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"He turned the thing over\" (qalaba al-shayʾ). Metaphorical: \"The heart is rusted\" (qalb wajīb) — the inner organ's receptivity to impressions, whether dark (from sins) or luminous (from obedience).\n- Root: وجب (W-J-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Obligation as fixed establishment (thubūt); what is due and settled upon a person.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Wājib has two senses: (1) that whose non-existence entails a contradiction (metaphysical necessity), and (2) that whose neglect merits blame — subdivided into what reason deems obligatory (e.g., knowing divine unity) and what the Law deems obligatory (e.g., acts of worship). Ghazali here exploits this dual sense to distinguish legal obligation (fatwā) from the higher obligation of the path (ṭarīq).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"It became incumbent upon him\" (wajaba ʿalayhi). Metaphorical: The extension to \"the heart's palpitation\" (wajīb) — the inner agitation of one who realizes the urgency of repentance before death.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Focused Predication (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — governing the Quranic citations {«إِنَّمَا التَّوْبَةُ عَلَى اللَّهِ»} and {«وَلَيْسَتِ التَّوْبَةُ لِلَّذِينَ»}, where innamā restricts the predicate to a specific subject, delimiting the scope of accepted repentance to those who turn before death's arrival and before the rust hardens into a seal.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}], "19": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}], "21": [{"arabic": "وقال الله تعالى وعاشروهن بالمعروف وقال في تعظيم حقهن وأخذن منكم ميثاقا غليظا {وقال} والصاحب بالجنب قيل هي المرأة وآخر ما وصى به رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم ثلاث كان يتكلم بهم حتى تلجلج لسانه وخفي كلامه جعل يقول الصلاة الصلاة وما ملكت أيمانكم لا تكلفوهم ما لا يطيقون الله الله فإنهن عوان في أيديكم يعني أسراء أخذتموهن بأمانة الله واستحللتم فروجهن بكلمة الله (8) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم من صبر على سوء خلق امرأته أعطاه الله من الأجر مثل ما أعطى أيوب على بلائه ومن صبرت على سوء خلق زوجها أعطاها الله مثل ثواب آسية", "english": "The first point of etiquette is the wedding feast (walīmah), and it is recommended (mustaḥabbah). Anas, may God be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, saw upon ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, may God be pleased with him, a trace of yellow dye, so he said: {«What is this?»} He replied: \"I have married a woman for the weight of a date-stone in gold.\" He said: \"May God bless you. Hold a wedding feast, even if with a single sheep.\" {«...»} And the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, held a wedding feast for Ṣafiyyah with dates and sawīq (barley porridge). {«...»} And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: \"The food of the first day is an obligation (ḥaqq), the food of the second day is a Sunnah, and the food of the third day is for show (sumʿah); and whoever seeks to be heard, God will make his deeds heard.\" {«...»} None raised this hadith except Ziyād ibn ʿAbd Allāh, and it is anomalous (gharīb). It is recommended to offer congratulations to him, and the one who enters upon the husband should say: \"May God bless you and shower blessings upon you, and may He bring you both together in goodness.\" {«...»} Abū Hurayrah, may God be pleased with him, reported that he, may God bless him and grant him peace, commanded this. It is also recommended to make the marriage public (iẓhār al-nikāḥ). He, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: \"The distinction between the lawful and the unlawful is the drum and the voice.\" {«...»} The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: \"Announce this marriage, hold it in the mosques, and beat drums for it.\" {«...»} And on the authority of al-Rubayyiʿ bint Muʿawwidh, she said: The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, came and entered on the morning of my wedding night, and he sat upon my bedding, and we had young girls with us who were beating their drums and lamenting those of my forefathers who had been killed—until one of them said: \"And among us is a Prophet who knows what will be tomorrow.\" He said to her: \"Stop this, and say what you were saying before.\" {«...»}", "book_title": "Etiquette of Marriage", "section_title_ar": "Section 303", "section_title_en": "On What is Incumbent Upon the Husband and What is Incumbent Upon the Wife", "section_index": 303, "anchors": "- Root: و ل م (w-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The core meaning pertains to the wedding feast (walīmah), derived from the concept of gathering and union.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological usage, it signifies the communal meal marking the consummation of a marriage contract, a recommended act of public gratitude.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes the specific feast; metaphorical (Majaz) extension applies to any act of publicizing a joyous occasion.\n- Root: ع ش ر (ʿ- sh-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes association, companionship, and intimacy; the tenth (ʿasharah) of a thing is related, but the primary social sense is cohabitation and mutual dealing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the Quranic context of {وَعَاشِرُوهُنَّ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ}, it signifies a sustained, equitable mode of social intercourse grounded in moral rectitude, not mere physical proximity.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is the act of living together; metaphorical (Majaz) usage elevates it to a standard of ethical conduct (maʿrūf) that defines the marital bond.\n- Root: ق س م (q-s-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root indicates division, apportionment, and distribution; in the marital context, it refers to the equitable division of time among co-wives.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theologically, it is tied to justice (ʿadl) as a divine attribute; for the husband, it is a legal obligation to maintain fairness in material and temporal allotments.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is the physical act of dividing; metaphorical (Majaz) usage applies to the moral duty of impartiality in affection and provision.\n- Root: غ ي ر (gh-y-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root conveys alteration and change, but in the moral register, it denotes protective jealousy (ghayrah), a state of aversion to the violation of honor.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In Kalam, ghayrah is a praiseworthy emotion when aligned with divine law—jealousy for the sanctity of the family—and blameworthy when it stems from unjust suspicion (sūʾ al-ẓann).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is the emotional state; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is attributed to God (al-Ghayyār) as a figure for His prohibition of indecencies, distinguishing human passion from divine will.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to isolate specific obligations (e.g., \"the third is that he should increase...\") and to distinguish categories of jealousy and pride, ensuring precise legal and moral demarcation.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Etiquette of Marriage'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "29": [{"arabic": "لأمر الحرام وتعظيما لبركة الحلال فقال تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا لا تأكلوا أموالكم بينكم بالباطل إلى قوله ولا تقتلوا أنفسكم الآية فالأصل في الطعام كونه طيبا وهو من الفرائض وأصول الدين الثاني غسل اليد قال صلى الله عليه وسلم الوضوء قبل الطعام ينفي الفقر وبعده ينفي اللمم (1) وفي رواية ينفي الفقر قبل الطعام وبعده ولأن اليد لا تخلو عن لوث في تعاطي الأعمال فغسلها أقرب إلى النظافة والنزاهة ولأن الأكل لقصد الاستعانة على الدين عبادة فهو جدير بأن يقدم عليه ما يجري منه مجرى الطهارة من الصلاة الثالث أن يوضع الطعام على السفرة الموضوعة على الأرض فهو", "english": "The first [etiquette] is that the food, after being lawful in itself, should be pure (tayyib) with respect to the manner of its acquisition, conforming to the Sunnah and to scrupulous piety (al-waraʿ), not having been earned through anything disapproved in the Sacred Law, nor through the dictates of caprice and dissimulation in religion, as will be detailed concerning the meaning of absolute purity in the Book of the Lawful and the Unlawful. God—may He be exalted—has commanded the eating of what is pure, which is the lawful, and He placed the prohibition of consuming wealth unjustly before the prohibition of killing, as a magnification of the matter of the unlawful and an ennoblement of the blessing of the lawful. For He—may He be exalted—said: {«Yā ayyuhā alladhīna āmanū lā ta'kulū amwālakum baynakum bil-bāṭil»} (\"O you who believe, do not consume your wealth among yourselves unjustly\") up to His saying: {«wa lā taqtulū anfusakum»} (\"and do not kill yourselves\") — the verse. Thus the foundational principle concerning food is that it be pure, and this is among the obligatory duties and foundational principles of religion.", "book_title": "Manners of Eating", "section_title_ar": "Section 282", "section_title_en": "Section 282 — On the Etiquette Preceding Eating, Being the First Division of the Etiquettes of Eating", "section_index": 282, "anchors": "- Root: اكل (akl)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is the taking of nourishment; Al-Farahidi notes its extension to consumption generally.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأكل: تناول المطعم\" — eating is the taking of food; used metaphorically for fire consuming wood, and in the Quranic sense of consuming wealth unlawfully.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) refers to physical ingestion; metaphorical (Majaz) extends to consuming property unjustly, as in the Quranic prohibition.\n- Root: طيب (tayyib)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: That which is pure, good, lawful, and pleasant.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theologically denotes what is lawful (halal) and pure, as opposed to what is defiled or prohibited.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Distinguishes literal purity from the legal-moral sense of lawfulness.\n- Root: بدع (bada'a)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To originate something new without prior example.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الإبداع: إنشاء صنعة بلا احتذاء واقتداء\" — innovation is the creation of something without imitating a prior model; when applied to God, it is creation ex nihilo.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal sense is inventing; metaphorical sense includes weakening in argument or failing expectations. In religious law, innovation (bid'ah) is only blameworthy when it contradicts an established Sunnah.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction and Specification) — The phrase \"ليس كل ما أبدع منهيا\" employs negation of universal quantification to distinguish between types of innovation, requiring careful parsing of the exception clause.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Manners of Eating'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}], "32": [{"arabic": "تتمنوا ما فضل الله به بعضكم على بعض فتمنيه لمثل ذلك غير مذموم وأما تمنيه عين ذلك فهو مذموم", "english": "The Prophet ﷺ said: «إن المؤمن يغبط والمنافق يحسد» — \"The believer aspires emulously, and the hypocrite envies.\"", "book_title": "Poverty and Abstinence", "section_title_ar": "Section 720", "section_title_en": "On the Reality of Envy (Ḥasad) and Its Degrees — Section 720", "section_index": 720, "anchors": "- Root: نعم (n-ʿ-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of \"bounty, benefaction, grace\" — that state of goodness and well-being bestowed.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النعمة: الحالة الحسنة\" — bounty is the excellent state/condition; it is the genus term applying to both little and much, as in {«وإن تعدوا نعمة الله لا تحصوها»} [Ibrahim: 34].\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"جلت نعمة الله ونعماؤه\" — bounty as actual bestowed good. Metaphorical (majāz): \"خفت نعامتهم\" — departure/removal, relevant to the envier's wish for the bounty's removal.\n- Root: حسد (ḥ-s-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Envy — the desire for removal of another's good.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحسد: تمني زوال نعمة من مستحق لها\" — envy is wishing the removal of bounty from one who deserves it, sometimes accompanied by striving to effect its removal. Cited: «المؤمن يغبط والمنافق يحسد».\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"حسده على نعمة الله\" — envying someone for God's bounty. \"إن الحسد يأكل الجسد\" — envy consumes the body.\n- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self; also value, preciousness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): النفس: الروح — the soul/spirit; also الذات (essence/self).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"شيء نفيس ومنفس\" — a precious thing. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — poured out his self, i.e., his blood. From this root derives المنافسة (emulative competition) — the striving for precious things.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Specification) — The definition of ḥasad is given as a restricted predication (ḥadd), delimiting its essential meaning: \"كراهة النعمة وحب زوالها عن المنعم عليه\" — the hatred of bounty and love of its removal from the one upon whom it is bestowed. This is a formal definition (ḥadd), not merely a lexical gloss.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Poverty and Abstinence'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "مذموم محض وتسمية الرتبة حسدا فيه تجوز وتوسع ولكنه مذموم لقوله تعالى ولا تتمنوا ما فضل الله به بعضكم على بعض فتمنيه لمثل ذلك غير مذموم وأما تمنيه عين ذلك فهو مذموم", "english": "The Prophet ﷺ said: «إن المؤمن يغبط والمنافق يحسد» — \"The believer aspires emulously, and the hypocrite envies.\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of Envy", "section_title_ar": "Section 720", "section_title_en": "On the Reality of Envy (Ḥasad) and Its Degrees — Section 720", "section_index": 720, "anchors": "- Root: نعم (n-ʿ-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of \"bounty, benefaction, grace\" — that state of goodness and well-being bestowed.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النعمة: الحالة الحسنة\" — bounty is the excellent state/condition; it is the genus term applying to both little and much, as in {«وإن تعدوا نعمة الله لا تحصوها»} [Ibrahim: 34].\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"جلت نعمة الله ونعماؤه\" — bounty as actual bestowed good. Metaphorical (majāz): \"خفت نعامتهم\" — departure/removal, relevant to the envier's wish for the bounty's removal.\n- Root: حسد (ḥ-s-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Envy — the desire for removal of another's good.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحسد: تمني زوال نعمة من مستحق لها\" — envy is wishing the removal of bounty from one who deserves it, sometimes accompanied by striving to effect its removal. Cited: «المؤمن يغبط والمنافق يحسد».\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"حسده على نعمة الله\" — envying someone for God's bounty. \"إن الحسد يأكل الجسد\" — envy consumes the body.\n- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self; also value, preciousness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): النفس: الروح — the soul/spirit; also الذات (essence/self).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"شيء نفيس ومنفس\" — a precious thing. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — poured out his self, i.e., his blood. From this root derives المنافسة (emulative competition) — the striving for precious things.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Specification) — The definition of ḥasad is given as a restricted predication (ḥadd), delimiting its essential meaning: \"كراهة النعمة وحب زوالها عن المنعم عليه\" — the hatred of bounty and love of its removal from the one upon whom it is bestowed. This is a formal definition (ḥadd), not merely a lexical gloss.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Condemnation of Envy'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "34": [{"arabic": "متبوعا لا تابعا وقد سمى الله الرجال قوامين على النساء وسمى الزوج سيدا فقال تعالى وألفيا سيدها لدى الباب فإذا انقلب السيد مسخرا فقد بدل نعمة الله كفرا ونفس المرأة على مثال نفسك إن أرسلت عنانها قليلا جمحت بك طويلا وإن أرخيت عذارها فترا جذبتك ذراعا وإن كبحتها وشددت يدك عليها في محل الشدة ملكتها قال الشافعي رضي الله عنه ثلاثة إن أكرمتهم أهانوك وإن أهنتهم أكرموك المرأة والخادم والنبطي أراد به إن محضت الإكرام ولم تمزج غلظك بلينك وفظاظتك برفقك وكانت نساء العرب يعلمن بناتهن اختبار الأزواج وكانت ا", "english": "The first point of etiquette is the wedding feast (walīmah), and it is recommended (mustaḥabbah). Anas, may God be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, saw upon ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, may God be pleased with him, a trace of yellow dye, so he said: {«What is this?»} He replied: \"I have married a woman for the weight of a date-stone in gold.\" He said: \"May God bless you. Hold a wedding feast, even if with a single sheep.\" {«...»} And the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, held a wedding feast for Ṣafiyyah with dates and sawīq (barley porridge). {«...»} And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: \"The food of the first day is an obligation (ḥaqq), the food of the second day is a Sunnah, and the food of the third day is for show (sumʿah); and whoever seeks to be heard, God will make his deeds heard.\" {«...»} None raised this hadith except Ziyād ibn ʿAbd Allāh, and it is anomalous (gharīb). It is recommended to offer congratulations to him, and the one who enters upon the husband should say: \"May God bless you and shower blessings upon you, and may He bring you both together in goodness.\" {«...»} Abū Hurayrah, may God be pleased with him, reported that he, may God bless him and grant him peace, commanded this. It is also recommended to make the marriage public (iẓhār al-nikāḥ). He, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: \"The distinction between the lawful and the unlawful is the drum and the voice.\" {«...»} The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: \"Announce this marriage, hold it in the mosques, and beat drums for it.\" {«...»} And on the authority of al-Rubayyiʿ bint Muʿawwidh, she said: The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, came and entered on the morning of my wedding night, and he sat upon my bedding, and we had young girls with us who were beating their drums and lamenting those of my forefathers who had been killed—until one of them said: \"And among us is a Prophet who knows what will be tomorrow.\" He said to her: \"Stop this, and say what you were saying before.\" {«...»}", "book_title": "Etiquette of Marriage", "section_title_ar": "Section 303", "section_title_en": "On What is Incumbent Upon the Husband and What is Incumbent Upon the Wife", "section_index": 303, "anchors": "- Root: و ل م (w-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The core meaning pertains to the wedding feast (walīmah), derived from the concept of gathering and union.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological usage, it signifies the communal meal marking the consummation of a marriage contract, a recommended act of public gratitude.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes the specific feast; metaphorical (Majaz) extension applies to any act of publicizing a joyous occasion.\n- Root: ع ش ر (ʿ- sh-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes association, companionship, and intimacy; the tenth (ʿasharah) of a thing is related, but the primary social sense is cohabitation and mutual dealing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the Quranic context of {وَعَاشِرُوهُنَّ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ}, it signifies a sustained, equitable mode of social intercourse grounded in moral rectitude, not mere physical proximity.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is the act of living together; metaphorical (Majaz) usage elevates it to a standard of ethical conduct (maʿrūf) that defines the marital bond.\n- Root: ق س م (q-s-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root indicates division, apportionment, and distribution; in the marital context, it refers to the equitable division of time among co-wives.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theologically, it is tied to justice (ʿadl) as a divine attribute; for the husband, it is a legal obligation to maintain fairness in material and temporal allotments.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is the physical act of dividing; metaphorical (Majaz) usage applies to the moral duty of impartiality in affection and provision.\n- Root: غ ي ر (gh-y-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root conveys alteration and change, but in the moral register, it denotes protective jealousy (ghayrah), a state of aversion to the violation of honor.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In Kalam, ghayrah is a praiseworthy emotion when aligned with divine law—jealousy for the sanctity of the family—and blameworthy when it stems from unjust suspicion (sūʾ al-ẓann).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is the emotional state; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is attributed to God (al-Ghayyār) as a figure for His prohibition of indecencies, distinguishing human passion from divine will.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to isolate specific obligations (e.g., \"the third is that he should increase...\") and to distinguish categories of jealousy and pride, ensuring precise legal and moral demarcation.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Etiquette of Marriage'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "الله تعالى {فإن أطعنكم فلا تبغوا عليهن سبيلا} أي لا تطلبوا حيلة للفراق وإن كرهها أبوه فليطلقها قال ابن عمر رضي الله عنهما كان تحتي امرأة أحبها وكان أبي يكرهها ويأمرني بطلاقها فراجعت رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم فقال يا ابن عمر طلق امرأتك (2) فهذا يدل على أن حق الوالد مقدم ولكن والد يكرهها لا لغرض فاسد مثل عمر ومهما آذت زوجها وبذت على أهله فهي جانية وكذلك مهما كانت سيئة الخلق أو فاسدة الدين قال ابن مسعود في قوله تعالى {ولا يخرجن إلا أن يأتين بفاحشة مبينة} مهما بذت على أهله وآذت زوجها فهو", "english": "**The third:** Fear of excessive hardship (ḥaraj) due to the multitude of children, and guarding against the need for toil in earning a livelihood and entering into evil means of acquisition. This also is not prohibited, for the paucity of hardship assists one in religion. Indeed, perfection and excellence lie in trust in God (tawakkul) and confidence in God's guarantee, where He said: {«وما من دابة في الأرض إلا على الله رزقها»} \"And there is no creature on earth but that upon Allah is its provision\" [Hūd: 6]. Without doubt, in this [trust] there is a falling from the summit of perfection and an abandonment of what is more excellent — but looking to consequences and preserving wealth and storing it up, despite its contradicting tawakkul, we do not say it is prohibited.", "book_title": "Etiquette of Marriage", "section_title_ar": "Section 307", "section_title_en": "** On Coitus Interruptus ('Azl), Its Intentions, and the Etiquettes of Childbirth and Divorce", "section_index": 307, "anchors": "**\n- Root: عزل (ʿazl)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Separation, removal, setting aside\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Withdrawal to prevent conception\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical separation; Metaphorical: preventing pregnancy\n- Root: وأد (waʾad)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Burying a female infant alive (pre-Islamic practice)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The killing of daughters by burial\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: burial alive; Metaphorical: any prevention of life\n- Root: طلق (ṭalaq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Divorce; dissolution of marriage bond\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Legal dissolution of the marriage contract\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: releasing/loosing; Metaphorical: dissolution of marriage\n- Sibawayh Rule: Conditional clauses with \"فإن قلت\" (objection-response dialectic structure)\n\n---\n\n**", "content_type": "general", "topic": "Classical commentary from Al-Ghazali's Ihya Ulum al-Din, from 'Etiquette of Marriage'", "badge": "Commentary"}, {"arabic": "وأما إذا كان النشوز من المرأة خاصة فالرجال قوامون على النساء فله أن يؤدبها ويحملها على الطاعة قهرا وكذا إذا كانت تاركة للصلاة فله حملها على الصلاة قهرا ولكن ينبغي أن يتدرج في تأديبها وهو أن يقدم أولا الوعظ والتحذير والتخويف فإن لم ينجح ولاها ظهره في المضجع أو انفرد عنها بالفراش وهجرها وهو في البيت معها من ليلة إلى ثلاث ليال فإن لم ينجح ذلك فيها ضربها ضربا غير مبرح بحيث يؤلمها ولا يكسر لها عظما ولا يدمي لها جسم ولا يضرب وجهها فذلك منهي عنه وقد قيل لرسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم ما حق المرأة على الرجل قال", "english": "I say: The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, has said: «من كان له امرأتان فمال إلى إحداهما دون الأخرى» — \"Whoever has two wives and inclines toward one of them to the exclusion of the other\" — and in another wording: «ولم يعدل بينهما جاء يوم القيامة وأحد شقيه مائل» — \"and does not act equitably between them, he shall come on the Day of Resurrection with one of his sides leaning.\"", "book_title": "Etiquette of Marriage", "section_title_ar": "Section 305", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Book on the Etiquette of Marriage: Section 305 (On Justice Between Co-Wives, Nushuz, and the Etiquette of Intercourse)", "section_index": 305, "anchors": "- Root: عدل (ʿadl)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to be equal, to balance, to straighten\" — the root denotes equity and even-handedness in distribution.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological usage, ʿadl is the mean of justice, opposed to jawr (tyranny) — it is the act of placing things in their proper stations.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage pertains to measurable distribution (ʿatāʾ, mabīt); metaphorical (Majāz) usage extends to that which is not under voluntary control, such as love (maḥabbah) and inclination of the heart.\n- Root: نشز (nushūz)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes \"rising up\" or \"elevation\"; in marital context it signifies a rising of one spouse against the other in discord or recalcitrance.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Nushūz is the rebellion of one spouse against the bond of obedience and harmony, a moral elevation against the other.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage is the physical rising from a place; metaphorical usage is the spiritual/moral rising in defiance.\n- Root: وقع (waqʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes \"falling\" or \"occurrence\"; in the marital context, it is a euphemism for sexual intercourse.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The term is used in the Quran for the \"falling\" of an event; here it is a euphemistic transfer (naql) for coitus.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage is physical falling; metaphorical usage is the act of intercourse, as in the hadith \"لا يقعن أحدكم على امرأته\" (Let none of you fall upon his wife like a beast).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The particle innamā restricts the predicate to the subject alone, as in the hadith \"إنما عليه العدل في العطاء والمبيت\" (Upon him is only justice in expenditure and overnight stays), confining the obligation to what is within human capacity.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Etiquette of Marriage'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "35": [{"arabic": "أمرهما {إن يريدا إصلاحا يوفق الله بينهما} وقد بعث عمر رضي الله عنه حكما إلى زوجين فعاد ولم يصلح أمرهما فعلاه بالدرة وقال إن الله تعالى يقول {إن يريدا إصلاحا يوفق الله بينهما} فعاد الرجل وأحسن النية وتلطف بهما فأصلح بينهما وأما إذا كان النشوز من المرأة خاصة فالرجال قوامون على النساء فله أن يؤدبها ويحملها على الطاعة قهرا وكذا إذا كانت تاركة للصلاة فله حملها على الصلاة قهرا ولكن ينبغي أن يتدرج في تأديبها وهو أن يقدم أولا الوعظ والتحذير والتخويف فإن لم ينجح ولاها ظهره في المضجع أو انفرد عنها", "english": "I say: The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, has said: «من كان له امرأتان فمال إلى إحداهما دون الأخرى» — \"Whoever has two wives and inclines toward one of them to the exclusion of the other\" — and in another wording: «ولم يعدل بينهما جاء يوم القيامة وأحد شقيه مائل» — \"and does not act equitably between them, he shall come on the Day of Resurrection with one of his sides leaning.\"", "book_title": "Intention, Sincerity and Truthfulness", "section_title_ar": "Section 305", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Book on the Etiquette of Marriage: Section 305 (On Justice Between Co-Wives, Nushuz, and the Etiquette of Intercourse)", "section_index": 305, "anchors": "- Root: عدل (ʿadl)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to be equal, to balance, to straighten\" — the root denotes equity and even-handedness in distribution.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological usage, ʿadl is the mean of justice, opposed to jawr (tyranny) — it is the act of placing things in their proper stations.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage pertains to measurable distribution (ʿatāʾ, mabīt); metaphorical (Majāz) usage extends to that which is not under voluntary control, such as love (maḥabbah) and inclination of the heart.\n- Root: نشز (nushūz)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes \"rising up\" or \"elevation\"; in marital context it signifies a rising of one spouse against the other in discord or recalcitrance.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Nushūz is the rebellion of one spouse against the bond of obedience and harmony, a moral elevation against the other.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage is the physical rising from a place; metaphorical usage is the spiritual/moral rising in defiance.\n- Root: وقع (waqʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes \"falling\" or \"occurrence\"; in the marital context, it is a euphemism for sexual intercourse.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The term is used in the Quran for the \"falling\" of an event; here it is a euphemistic transfer (naql) for coitus.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage is physical falling; metaphorical usage is the act of intercourse, as in the hadith \"لا يقعن أحدكم على امرأته\" (Let none of you fall upon his wife like a beast).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The particle innamā restricts the predicate to the subject alone, as in the hadith \"إنما عليه العدل في العطاء والمبيت\" (Upon him is only justice in expenditure and overnight stays), confining the obligation to what is within human capacity.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Intention, Sincerity and Truthfulness'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "36": [{"arabic": "قد بايعناك فعلى ماذا نبايعك قال أن تعبدوا الله ولا تشركوا به شيئا وتصلوا الخمس وأن تسمعوا وتطيعوا وأسر كلمة خفية ولا تسألوا الناس شيئا (2) قال فلقد كان بعض أولئك النفر يسقط سوطه فلا يسأل أحدا أن يناوله إياه الآثار قال عمر رضي الله عنه إن الطمع فقر وإن اليأس غنى وإنه من ييأس عما في أيدي الناس استغنى عنهم وقيل لبعض الحكماء ما الغنى قال قلة تمنيك ورضاك بما يكفيك وفي ذلك قيل العيش ساعات تمر .", "english": "Now the human being (al-ādamī) has been naturally disposed (jubila) to greed, covetousness, and scant contentment. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: \"Were the son of Adam to possess two valleys of gold, he would seek for them a third; and nothing fills the belly of the son of Adam but dust — and Allah relents toward whoever repents.\" {«لو كان لابن آدم واديان من ذهب لابتغى لهما ثالثا ولا يملأ جوف ابن آدم إلا التراب ويتوب الله على من تاب»} (1)", "book_title": "Condemnation of Miserliness and Love of Wealth", "section_title_ar": "Section 758", "section_title_en": "Exposition of the Blameworthiness of Greed and Covetousness, and the Praiseworthiness of Contentment and Despairing of What Is in People's Hands", "section_index": 758, "anchors": "- Root: طمع (ṭamaʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of craving, yearning of the soul toward a thing with eager desire; the root carries the sense of an appetite that, when unrestrained, becomes a defining trait.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الطمع: نزوع النفس إلى الشيء شهوة له\" — the soul's inclination toward a thing out of lustful desire. Al-Raghib notes that because most covetousness stems from caprice, it is said: \"الطمع طبع\" (covetousness is an innate disposition), and \"الطمع يدنس الإهاب\" (covetousness defiles the hide).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"طمع في كذا وبه\" — to covet something. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"أخذ الجند أطماعهم: أرزاقهم\" — the soldiers took their portions/rations, showing the extension of the root from craving to the very object of allotment.\n- Root: قنع (qaniʿa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of being content, satisfied with one's portion; the root also carries the sense of asking humbly (qaniʿa = sa'ala) — a semantic tension resolved by context: the contented one does not ask.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Contentment (qanāʿah) is the soul's satisfaction with what suffices, restraining it from craving excess; it is the mean between avarice and prodigality.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قنع يقنع قنوعا وقناعة\" — to be content. The rhetorical force in Ghazali's usage is the pairing with \"اليأس\" (despairing of what others hold), making contentment an active renunciation rather than passive acceptance.\n- Root: غني (ghaniya)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of being free from need, wealthy; the opposite of faqr (poverty).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الغنى يقال على ضروب: أحدها: عدم الحاجات، وليس ذلك إلا لله تعالى\" — true wealth as absence of needs belongs to Allah alone; the second kind is fewness of needs, which is the wealth of the soul referenced in the hadith \"الغنى غنى النفس.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"لي عن هذا غنية. وأنا عنه غني\" — I have no need of this. Metaphorical: \"تغنته القيود\" — the fetters made him independent (of movement), showing the root's extension to self-sufficiency in any state.\n- Root: حرص (ḥaraṣa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of intense eager desire, avarice; the root implies a striving that exceeds due measure.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Greed (ḥirṣ) is the soul's excessive craving for something, exceeding what is needed; it is blameworthy when directed at worldly increase.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"حرص على الشيء\" — to be greedily eager for a thing. The rhetorical contrast in this section is between ḥirṣ as an innate vice and qanāʿah as an acquired virtue.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the hadith \"ليس الغنى عن كثرة العرض إنما الغنى غنى النفس\" (Wealth is not abundance of goods; rather, wealth is the wealth of the soul), where إنما restricts the predicate to the soul's sufficiency, negating any other sense of true wealth.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Condemnation of Miserliness and Love of Wealth'", "badge": "Ethics"}, {"arabic": "وسلم فقال نعم إن الله لا يحب المستكبرين فنزل وقعد معهم على الأرض وأكل ثم سلم عليهم وركب وقال قد أجبتكم فأجيبوني قالوا نعم فوعدهم وقتا معلوما فحضروا فقدم إليهم فاخر الطعام وجلس يأكل معهم وأما قول القائل إن من وضعت يدي في قصعته فقد ذلت له رقبتي فقد قال بعضهم هذا خلاف السنة وليس كذلك فإنه ذل إذا كان الداعي لا يفرح بالإجابة ولا يتقلد منة وكان يرى ذلك يدا له على المدعو ورسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم كان يحضر لعلمه أن الداعي له يتقلد منة ويرى ذلك شرفا وذخرا لنفسه في الدنيا", "english": "The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Do not burden your guest so that you make him hate you, for whoever hates his guest has hated Allah, and whoever hates Allah, Allah hates him.\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of Anger and Hatred", "section_title_ar": "Section 290", "section_title_en": "The Etiquettes of Hospitality: Invitation, Response, Attendance, and the Serving of Food — Section 290 of The Revival of the Religious Sciences", "section_index": 290, "anchors": "- Root: ضيف (ḍ-y-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to incline, to lean toward.\" The guest (al-ḍayf) is one who inclines toward you, alighting as a visitor; the root carries the sense of leaning or bending.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The guest is \"one who inclines to you, alighting with you\"; hospitality (al-ḍiyāfah) became customary in the sense of receiving such a one. The word is originally a verbal noun, hence one and many are equal in most usage.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"ḍāfa ilayhi: he inclined toward him\"; \"ḍāfat al-shams: the sun inclined toward setting.\" Metaphorical (majāz): \"aḍāfa ilayhi amran: he attributed a matter to him, leaning it upon him\"; \"al-muḍāf: the one constrained, hemmed in, in distress.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Focused Predication (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — governing the Prophet's statement \"innamā hādhihi al-akhlāq bi-yad Allāh\" (these morals are only in the hand of Allah), where innamā restricts the attribution of moral character exclusively to divine bestowal.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Condemnation of Anger and Hatred'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "37": [{"arabic": "وقرنه بالبخل فقال تعالى الذين يبخلون ويأمرون الناس بالبخل ويكتمون ما آتاهم الله من فضله وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم إذا أنعم الله علي عبد نعمة أحب أن ترى نعمته عليه (1) وأعطى رجل بعض الصالحين شيئا في السر فرفع به يده وقال هذا من الدنيا والعلانية فيها أفضل والسر في أمور الآخرة أفضل ولذلك قال بعضهم إذا أعطيت في الملأ فخذ ثم اردد في السر والشكر فيه محثوث عليه قال صلى الله عليه وسلم من لم يشكر الناس لم يشكر الله عز وجل (2) والشكر قائم مقام المكافأة حتى قال صلى الله عليه وسلم من أسدى إليكم معروفا فكافئوه فإن لم تستطيعوا فأثنوا عليه به خيرا وادعوا", "english": "Abū Ayyūb al-Sakhtiyānī said: \"I indeed abandon wearing a new garment, fearing that it might engender envy in my neighbors.\" And one of the ascetics said: \"Perhaps I abandon the use of a thing for the sake of my brothers, lest they say: 'From where does he have this?'\" It is related of Ibrāhīm al-Taymī that a new shirt was seen upon him, and one of his brothers said: \"From where did you get this?\" He replied: \"My brother Khaythamah clothed me with it. Had I known that his family knew of it, I would not have accepted it.\"", "book_title": "Mysteries of Zakat", "section_title_ar": "Section 183", "section_title_en": "On Sincerity (Ikhlas): The Ethics of Giving and Receiving — Concealment versus Disclosure", "section_index": 183, "anchors": "- Root: س ت ر (s-t-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Covering, veiling, concealing; the act of screening something from view.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Concealment (sitr) preserves inviolability and prevents exposure of need, aligning with the Quranic description of the poor who \"restrain themselves\" so that the ignorant think them wealthy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqiqah) — physical covering; Metaphorical (majaz) — concealing one's state of need or one's charitable act from the eyes of others.\n- Root: ظ ه ر (ẓ-h-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To become apparent, visible, manifest; the opposite of concealment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Openness and manifestation; used for both physical visibility and the public disclosure of an act.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — making something perceptible to the senses; Metaphorical — publicizing one's good deeds, which carries both the virtue of gratitude and the peril of ostentation.\n- Root: ش ك ر (sh-k-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The core root denotes acknowledgment and increase; a beast \"shakur\" is one that thrives on little fodder.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"The perception of the blessing and its manifestation.\" It is the opposite of kufr (disbelief/ingratitude), which is the forgetting and covering of the blessing. It is of three types: of the heart (perceiving the blessing), of the tongue (praising the Benefactor), and of the limbs (rewarding the blessing with obedience).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — verbal praise of the benefactor; Metaphorical — the act of the one who, upon receiving a gift, openly expresses gratitude, which is a Sunnah, as in {«وَأَمَّا بِنِعْمَةِ رَبِّكَ فَحَدِّثْ»}.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governing the separation of a preposition from its object for rhetorical emphasis, here applied to the nuanced interposition of intention (niyyah) between the outward act and its inward reality.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Mysteries of Zakat'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "40": [{"arabic": "ما بأنفسهم إن الله لا يظلم مثقال ذرة وإن تك حسنة يضاعفها وإن كان مثقال حبة من خردل أتينا بها وكفى بنا حاسبين فمن كان حبه في الدنيا رجاءه لنعيم الجنة والحور العين والقصور مكن من الجنة ليتبوأ منها حيث يشاء فيلعب مع الولدان ويتمتع بالنسوان فهناك تنتهي لذته في الآخرة لأنه إنما يعطى كل إنسان في المحبة ما تشتهيه نفسه وتلذ عينه ومن كان مقصده رب الدار ومالك الملك ولم يغلب عليه إلا حبه بالإخلاص والصدق أنزل في مقعد", "english": "...twenty years.\nAnd al-Junayd said: \"The mark of the lover is the constancy of vigor and cheerfulness with a passion that wearies his body but does not weary his heart.\"\nAnd some of them said: \"Labor borne out of love — languor does not enter into it.\"\nAnd one of the scholars said: \"By God, no lover of God is ever fully quenched by His obedience, even if he were to attain to the most immense of means.\"\nAll of this and its like is found in observable experience, for the passionate lover does not find burdensome the pursuit of his beloved's desire, and he savors with his heart the service of the beloved even if it is arduous upon his body. And whenever his body falls short, the dearest of things to him is that his capacity should return to him and that incapacity should depart from him, so that he may occupy himself with it. Such, likewise, is the love of God the Exalted — for every love that becomes dominant inevitably subdues whatever is beneath it. So whoever's beloved is dearer to him than his sloth abandons sloth in serving him; and if the beloved is dearer to him than his wealth, he spends his wealth in his love.\nAnd it was said to one of the lovers — who had expended his very self and his wealth until nothing remained to him — \"What was the cause of your state in love?\" He said: \"One day I heard a lover who had secluded himself with his beloved, saying: 'By God, I love you with my whole heart, while you turn away from me with your whole face.' The beloved said to him: 'If you truly love me, what will you spend upon me?' He said: 'O my master, I put you in possession of all that I own, and then I spend upon you my very spirit until it is destroyed.'\" So I said: \"This is a creature loving a creature, and a slave loving a slave — how then with slaves loving the Worshipped One?\" All of this is by His cause.\nAnd among them is that he be compassionate toward all of God's servants, merciful to them, and severe against all of God's enemies — and against everyone who commits something of what God dislikes — as God the Exalted said: {«أشداء على الكفار رحماء بينهم»} \"Harsh against the disbelievers, merciful among themselves.\" No reproach of any reproacher seizes him, and no diverting force turns him from wrath for God's sake. With this God described His friends, saying: \"Those who are enamored with My love as a child is enamored with a thing, and who take refuge in My remembrance as the eagle takes refuge in its nest, and who rage for the sake of My sanctities as the leopard rages when provoked — for then he cares not whether the people are few or many.\"\nSo consider this example: the child, when enamored with a thing, does not part from it at all; if it is taken from him, he has no occupation but weeping and crying out until it is returned to him. If he sleeps, he takes it with him into his clothes; when he awakens, he returns and clings to it. Whenever he is parted from it, he weeps; whenever he finds it, he laughs. Whoever contends with him over it, he hates; whoever gives it to him, he loves. As for the leopard, he does not master himself at the moment of rage, until he reaches such a pitch of fury that he destroys himself. These, then, are the marks of love. Whoever's marks are perfected in him, his love is perfected and his affection is purified — so in the Hereafter his drink is clear and his draught is sweet.\nBut whoever's love is mingled with the love of other than God, he delights in the Hereafter in proportion to his love, for his drink is mingled with a measure of the drink of the Near Ones (al-muqarrabūn), as God the Exalted said concerning the righteous (al-abrār): {«إن الأبرار لفي نعيم»} \"Verily the righteous are in bliss,\" {ثم قال} then He said: {«يسقون من رحيق مختوم * ختامه مسك وفي ذلك فليتنافس المتنافسون * ومزاجه من تسنيم * عينا يشرب بها المقربون»} \"They are given to drink of sealed nectar, whose seal is musk — and in that let the competitors compete — and its mixture is of tasnīm, a spring from which the Near Ones drink.\" So when the drink of the righteous is sweetened by the admixture of the pure drink which belongs to the Near Ones...\nAnd \"drink\" is an expression for the totality of the bliss of the Gardens, just as the Book expressed by it all deeds, for He said: {«إن كتاب الأبرار لفي عليين»} \"Verily the book of the righteous is in 'Illiyyūn,\" {ثم قال} then He said: {«يشهده المقربون»} \"The Near Ones bear witness to it.\" So it was a sign of the loftiness of their book that it ascended to where the Near Ones bear witness to it. And just as the righteous find increase in their state and their knowledge by their proximity to the Near Ones and their beholding of them, so likewise is their state in the Hereafter — {«ما خلقكم ولا بعثكم إلا كنفس واحدة»} \"Your creation and your resurrection are but as a single soul,\" just as He said — as we began the first creation, We shall repeat it — and as the Exalted said: {«جزاء وفاقا»} \"A recompense fully matching\" — that is, the recompense matched their deeds. So the pure was met with the unmixed of the drink, and the mingled was met with the mingled.\nAnd the admixture of every drink is in proportion to what preceded of admixture in his love and his deeds. For {«من يعمل مثقال ذرة خيرا يره * ومن يعمل مثقال ذرة شرا يره»} \"Whoever does an atom's weight of good shall see it, and whoever does an atom's weight of evil shall see it.\" And {«إن الله لا يغير ما بقوم حتى يغيروا ما بأنفسهم * إن الله لا يظلم مثقال ذرة وإن تك حسنة يضاعفها وإن كان مثقال حبة من خردل أتينا بها وكفى بنا حاسبين»} \"Verily God does not change what is in a people until they change what is in themselves. Verily God does not wrong an atom's weight; and if it be a good deed, He multiplies it, and if it be a grain of mustard-seed, We bring it — and sufficient are We as reckoners.\"\nSo whoever's love in this world was his hope for the bliss of the Garden, the wide-eyed houris, and the palaces — he is granted the Garden to settle therein wherever he wishes, to sport with the immortal youths and take pleasure with the women. There his delight in the Hereafter reaches its end, because every man is given in love only what his soul desires and his eye delights in.\nBut whoever's aim was the Lord of the Abode and the Owner of the Kingdom, and nothing overcame him save His love with sincerity and truthfulness — he is lodged in the seat...", "book_title": "Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment", "section_title_ar": "Section 1179", "section_title_en": "The Marks of True Love and the Degrees of the Hereafter", "section_index": 1179, "anchors": "- Root: نشط (n-sh-ṭ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of vitality, energy, and alacrity in action.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Activity of the soul (nafs) in pursuing what it loves, unflagging in its ardor.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: bodily vigor; Metaphorical: the heart's persistent zeal that overrides physical exhaustion.\n- Root: شرب (sh-r-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Drinking; the act of imbibing liquids.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In Quranic usage, a metaphor (majaz) for the soul's reception of spiritual bliss and states.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical quenching; Metaphorical: the tasting of paradisiacal delight, graded by purity (safā') versus admixture (shawb).\n- Root: صفو (ṣ-f-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Purity, clarity, and freedom from turbidity or mixture.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The unalloyed state of sincere love (mahabbah) and its corresponding reward.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: clear liquid; Metaphorical: the soul's exclusive devotion to God, yielding unmixed bliss.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to delimit the final reward strictly by the object and purity of one's love, as in \"إنما يعطى كل إنسان في المحبة ما تشتهيه نفسه.\"", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "قوله تعالى إن الله لا يظلم مثقال ذرة وكل ذلك صحيح فليس للإنسان إلا ما سعى وسعيه هو الذي يرى وكل نفس بما كسبت رهينه فلما زاغوا أزاغ الله قلوبهم ولما غيروا ما بانفسهم غير الله ما بهم تحقيقا لقوله تعالى إن الله لا يغير ما بقوم حتى يغيروا ما بأنفسهم وهذا كله قد انكشف لأرباب القلوب انكشافا اوضح من المشاهدة بالبصر إذ للبصر يمكن الغلط فيه إذ قد يرى البعيد قريبا والكبير صغيرا ومشاهدة القلب لا يمكن الغلط فيها وإنما الشأن في انفتاح بصيرة القلب وإلا فما يرى بها بعد الانفتاح فلا يتصور فيه الكذ", "english": "The fire of Hell is the coldest of fires [in comparison to the fire of separation]. And you should not deny this in the world of the Hereafter, for it has a witnessed parallel in the world of this world. Indeed, one has been seen who was overcome by ecstasy (wajd) and walked upon fire and upon the roots of cane, his feet being cut, yet he did not feel it, due to the extreme dominance of what was in his heart. And you see the angry man, when anger overcomes him in battle, receiving wounds and not feeling them at that moment, because anger is a fire in the heart. The Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, said: {«الغضب قطعة من النار»} \"Anger is a piece of fire.\" The burning of the heart (iḥtirāq al-fu'ād) is more severe than the burning of bodies, and the more severe annuls the sensation of the weaker, as you observe. For destruction from fire and the sword is only inasmuch as they separate between two parts that are linked one to the other by the bond of composition (ta'līf) possible in bodies. So that which separates between the heart and its Beloved, to which it is linked by a bond of composition far more firm than the composition of bodies, is more intensely painful—if you are among the people of insight (abṣār) and the people of hearts. And it is not far-fetched that he who has no heart should not perceive the severity of this pain and should deem it trivial in comparison to bodily pain. For a boy, if he were given the choice between the pain of being deprived of a ball and a polo-stick, and the pain of being deprived of the rank of a Sultan, he would not feel the pain of deprivation from the Sultan's rank at all, nor would he count that as a pain. And the enemy in the field with the polo-stick is more beloved to him than a thousand thrones of the Sultan with sitting upon them. Nay, even one overcome by the lust of the belly, if given the choice between porridge (harīsah) and sweets (ḥalwā) and between a beautiful deed by which he conquers enemies and delights friends, he would prefer the porridge and sweets. All of this is due to the absence of the meaning (maʿnā) by whose existence rank becomes beloved, and the presence of the meaning by whose existence food becomes delicious. This is for one whom the attributes of beasts and predators have enslaved, and in whom the attributes of the angels have not appeared—those attributes which are not suited by, nor delight in, anything but proximity to the Lord of the Worlds, and which are pained only by distance and the veil (ḥijāb). And just as taste is only in the tongue and hearing only in the ears, so this attribute is only in the heart. So he who has no heart has no such sense, like he who has no hearing or sight has no pleasure in melodies or the beauty of forms and colors. And not every human has a heart; were it so, the saying of God, Most High, would not be sound: {«إن في ذلك لذكرى لمن كان له قلب»} \"Indeed in that is a reminder for whoever has a heart\" (Q. 50:37). Thus He made the one who does not heed the Quran devoid of a heart. And by \"heart\" I do not mean this [organ] which the ribs of the chest encompass. Rather, I mean the secret (sirr) which is of the world of the Command (ʿālam al-amr). The flesh which is of the world of Creation (ʿālam al-khalq) is its Throne (ʿarsh), the chest is its Footstool (kursī), and the rest of the limbs are its domain and kingdom. And to God belongs the Creation and the Command altogether. But that secret, concerning which God, Most High, said: {«قل الروح من أمر ربي»} \"Say: The Spirit is of the Command of my Lord\" (Q. 17:85), is the commander and the king, because between the world of the Command and the world of Creation there is an ordering, and the world of the Command is commander over the world of Creation. It is the subtle divine reality (al-laṭīfah) which, when it is sound, the rest of the body is sound for its sake. Whoever knows it has known his own self, and whoever knows his own self has known his Lord. At that point, the servant smells the beginnings of the fragrance of the meaning folded under his saying, peace and blessings be upon him: {«إن الله خلق آدم على صورته»} \"God created Adam upon His image.\" He looks with the eye of mercy upon those who adhere to its literal wording and upon those who are obstinate in the path of its interpretation (ta'wīl). And even though His mercy for those adhering to the letter is greater than His mercy for the obstinate interpreters, because mercy is proportional to the affliction, and the affliction of those [literalists] is greater—even if they share in the affliction of being deprived of the reality of the matter—for the Reality (al-ḥaqīqah) is the bounty of God which He gives to whom He wills, and God is the Possessor of Great Bounty. And His Wisdom is singled out for whom He wills: {«ومن يؤت الحكمة فقد أوتي خيرا كثيرا»} \"And whoever is given wisdom has been given much good\" (Q. 2:269). Let us return to the purpose, for we have drawn out the length and lengthened the breath in a matter that is higher than the sciences of transactions (muʿāmalāt) which we intend in this book. It has become clear that the rank of destruction is only for the ignorant deniers. And the testimony of that from the Book of God and the Sunnah of His Messenger, peace and blessings be upon him, is beyond enumeration, so we have not cited it.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 930", "section_title_en": "The Ranks of the Hereafter: On the Distribution of Degrees and the States of the Saved, the Punished, and the Victorious", "section_index": 930, "anchors": "- Root: ح ر ق (ḥ-r-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of burning, scorching, and the fire's consumption of its fuel.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The fire of the Hereafter (Nār) is a created reality, but its perception is tied to the spiritual state of the perceiver.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) for physical burning; metaphorical (Majaz) for the burning of the heart (iḥtirāq al-fu'ād) by overwhelming love or anger.\n- Root: ق ل ب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn, overturn, or transform a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The heart (Qalb) is the seat of perception and understanding, distinct from the physical organ.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for the physical heart; metaphorical for the spiritual subtlety (Laṭīfah) that is the locus of divine knowledge and love.\n- Root: ع ل م (ʿ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To know, to perceive a thing by its true nature.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Knowledge (ʿIlm) is the apprehension of a thing's reality, which is the basis for distinguishing the ranks of the believers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for acquired knowledge; metaphorical for the \"unveiling\" (Kashf) knowledge granted to the knowers (ʿĀrifūn).\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Specification (Bāb al-Ḥaṣr wa al-Taqyīd bi-innamā) — Used to distinguish the specific referents of terms like \"heart\" (Qalb) and \"faith\" (Īmān), ensuring the precise meaning is not conflated with common usage.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "فتح الباب والله لا يضيع أجر المحسنين وإن تك حسنة يضاعفها ويؤت من لدنه أجرا عظيما المقام الثاني في دفع العارض منه في أثناء العبادة وذلك لا بد من تعلمه أيضا فإن من جاهد نفسه وقلع مغارس الرياء من قلبه بالقناعة وقطع الطمع وإسقاط نفسه من أعين المخلوقين واستحقار مدح المخلوقين وذمهم فالشيطان لا يتركه في أثناء العبادات بل يعارضه بخطرات الرياء ولا تنقطع عنه نزعاته وهوى النفس وميلها لا ينمحي بالكلية فلا بد وأن يتشمر لدفع ما يعرض من خاطر الرياء وخواطر الرياء ثلاثة قد تخطر دفعة واحدة كالخاطر الواحد وقد تترادف على التدريج فالأول العلم باطلاع الخ", "english": "So when the harm and injury of these causes is firmly settled in his heart, his desire [for them] slackens, and his heart turns toward God. For the intelligent person does not covet that whose harm is abundant and whose benefit is scant. It suffices him that if people knew what is in his inner self—the intent of ostentation (riyā') and the display of sincerity—they would abhor him. And God will surely expose his secret until He makes him odious to the people and makes them know that he is a show-off, abhorrent to God. Had he been sincere to God, God would have unveiled his sincerity to them, made him beloved to them, subjected them to him, and loosened their tongues in praise and commendation of him—even though there is no perfection in their praise, nor deficiency in their blame. As the poet of Banī Tamīm said: \"My praise is an adornment, and my blame is a disgrace.\" The Messenger of God (peace and blessings be upon him) said to him: \"You have lied—that belongs to God, there is no deity but Him.\" // [Hadith: A poet of Banī Tamīm said, \"My praise is an adornment, and my blame is a disgrace.\" He (the Prophet) said: \"You have lied—that belongs to God.\" Ahmad narrated it from the hadith of al-Aqra' ibn Ḥābis—who was the speaker of that verse, excluding the phrase \"You have lied\"—and its narrators are trustworthy, except that I do not know of Abū Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Raḥmān having heard [directly] from al-Aqra'. Al-Tirmidhī narrated it from the hadith of al-Barā' and deemed it sound (ḥasan), with the wording: \"A man said, 'Indeed, my praise...'\"]", "book_title": "Condemnation of Status and Ostentation", "section_title_ar": "Section 827", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 827: The Second Station: Repelling the Accidental Promptings of Ostentation During Worship", "section_index": 827, "anchors": "- Root: كذب (kadhaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: falsehood, lying, saying what is contrary to reality; also used for \"to declare someone a liar.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological context, kadhb is the antithesis of sidq (truthfulness); it is a moral and epistemic vice, often linked to hypocrisy (nifāq) and false claims.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah): to report what did not occur. Metaphorical (majāz): attributing to God what is unworthy of Him, as in the Prophet's rebuke of the poet's self-praise.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Predicate Emphasis (Bāb al-Tawkīd) — The Prophet's reply \"كَذَبْتَ\" (You have lied) functions as an emphatic categorical refutation, structured as a nominal sentence with implied subject, establishing a permanent verdict rather than a transient rebuke.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Status and Ostentation'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "به فكان أبو سعيد يقول إن لم تصدقوني بهذا الحديث فاقرءوا إن شئتم إن الله لا يظلم مثقال ذرة وإن تك حسنة يضاعفها ويؤت من لدنه أجرا عظيما قال فيقول الله تعالى شفعت الملائكة وشفع النبيون وشفع المؤمنون ولم يبق إلا أرحم الراحمين فيقبض قبضة فيخرج منها قوما لم يعملوا خيرا قط قد عادوا حمما فيلقيهم في نهر في أفواه الجنة يقال له نهر الحياة فيخرجون منها كما تخرج الحبة في حميل السيل ألا ترونها تكون مما يلي الحجر والشجر ما يكون إلى الشمس أصفر وأخضر وما يكون منها إلى الظل أبيض قالوا يا رسول الله كأنك كنت ترعى بالبادية قال فيخرجون كاللؤلؤ في رقابهم الخواتيم يعرفهم أهل الجنة يقولون هؤلاء عتقاء الر", "english": "On that Day, a card will be brought forth bearing the testimony: \"I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah.\" He will say: \"O Lord, what is this card alongside these records?\" He will reply: \"Indeed, you shall not be wronged.\" Then the records will be placed on one pan of the scale, and the card on the other. The records will prove light, and the card will prove heavy—for nothing weighs heavier than the Name of Allah. {«لا يثقل مع اسم الله شيء»} (Nothing weighs heavier alongside the Name of Allah.)", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1361", "section_title_en": "On the Vastness of Divine Mercy and the Intercession of the Angels, Prophets, and Believers", "section_index": 1361, "anchors": "- Root: وزن (w-z-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is weighing, measuring, and balancing; al-wazn denotes the act of determining weight and, by extension, the scales of justice.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological context, al-mīzān (the Balance) on the Day of Judgment is a divine instrument of perfect equity; its \"weighing\" is not corporeal but a manifestation of absolute justice wherein no deed, even of atom-weight (mithqāl dharratin), is overlooked.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah) usage refers to physical scales; metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to the moral and eschatological weighing of deeds, where the \"card\" (biṭāqah) of shahādah outweighs accumulated records—a rhetorical hyperbole of divine grace.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Conditional Clauses with Repeated Protasis (Bāb al-sharṭ wa-l-jawāb) — The recurring structure \"fa-man wajadtum fī qalbihi... fa-akhrijūhu\" employs iterative conditionals to emphasize progressive divine mercy, each protasis narrowing the measure of faith required for salvation.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "41": [{"arabic": "فلما بلغت {فكيف إذا جئنا من كل أمة بشهيد وجئنا بك على هؤلاء شهيدا} رأيت عينيه تذرفان بالدمع فقال لي حسبك الآن وهذا لأن مشاهدة تلك الحالة استغرقت قلبه بالكلية ولقد كان في الخائفين من خر مغشيا عليه عند آيات الوعيد ومنهم من مات في سماع الآيات فمثل هذه الأحوال يخرجه عن أن يكون حاكيا في كلامه فإذا قال إني أخاف إن عصيت ربي عذاب يوم عظيم ولم يكن خائفا كان حاكيا وإذا قال عليك توكلنا وإليك أنبنا وإليك المصير ولم يكن حاله التوكل والإنابة كان حاكيا وإذا قال ولنصبرن على ما آذيتمونا فليكن حاله الصبر أو العزيمة عليه حتى ي", "english": "And at the mention of God, His attributes, and His names, he bows low in humility before His majesty and in apprehension of His greatness. And at the mention of the disbelievers' sayings — those things impossible for God, Mighty and Majestic, such as their attributing to Him a son or a consort — he lowers his voice and inwardly recoils in shame at the ugliness of their speech. And at the description of Paradise, his innermost being surges forth with longing for it. And at the description of the Fire, his limbs tremble in fear of it. For when the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, said to Ibn Mas'ud, \"Recite to me,\" he said: \"I began reciting Surah al-Nisa'. When I reached {فكيف إذا جئنا من كل أمة بشهيد وجئنا بك على هؤلاء شهيدا} [How then shall it be when We bring from every nation a witness, and We bring you as a witness against these?] (4:41), I saw his two eyes overflowing with tears. He then said to me: 'That is enough for you now.'\" And this was because the witnessing of that very state had wholly overwhelmed his heart.", "book_title": "Etiquette of Quran Recitation", "section_title_ar": "Section 230", "section_title_en": "The States of the Heart During Qur'anic Recitation — The Condition of the True Recieter", "section_index": 230, "anchors": "- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of trembling fear, trepidation of the heart\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the inward apprehension of fear; cited in {إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a man fearful, a people fearful; the state of the heart's trembling before divine majesty\n- Root: وصف (w-ṣ-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Describing a thing by its attributes and qualities\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوصف: ذكر الشيء بحليته ونعته\" — description is the mention of a thing by its adornment and attribute; may be true or false\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical: \"وجهها يصف الحسن\" — metaphorical usage where the face itself \"describes\" beauty; likewise, the tongue may \"describe\" falsehood, as in {ولا تقولوا لما تصف ألسنتكم الكذب}\n- Root: وفي (w-f-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Completeness, fulfillment, reaching full measure\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوافي: الذي بلغ التمام\" — that which attains completion; \"أوفى: إذا تمم العهد ولم ينقض حفظه\" — fulfilled the covenant without breach\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"درهم واف\" — a full-weight dirham; Metaphorical: \"أوفى على المائة إذا زاد عليها\" — surpassed the hundred\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The separation between the governing preposition and its object by intervening clauses, as in \"فلما بلغت {فكيف إذا جئنا...}\" where the temporal clause governs the narrative sequence", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Etiquette of Quran Recitation'", "badge": "Insight"}], "43": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "قال الله تعالى وأقم الصلاة لذكري وقال تعالى ولا تكن من الغافلين وقال عز وجل لا تقربوا الصلاة وأنتم سكارى حتى تعلموا ما تقولون قيل سكارى من كثرة الهم وقيل من حب الدنيا وقال وهب المراد به ظاهره ففيه تنبيه على سكر الدنيا إذ بين فيه العلة فقال حتى تعلموا ما تقولون وكم من مصل لم يشرب خمرا وهو لا يعلم ما يقول في صلاته وقال النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم من صلى ركعتين لم يحدث نفسه فيهما بشيء من الدنيا غفر له ما تقدم من ذنبه (1) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم إنما الصلاة تمسكن وتواضع وتضرع وتأوه وتنادم وتضع يديك فتقول اللهم اللهم فمن لم يفعل فهي خداج (2) وروي عن الله سبحانه في الكتب", "english": "God—Exalted is He—said: {«And establish prayer for My remembrance»} (Q 20:14). And He—Exalted is He—said: {«And be not among the heedless»} (Q 7:205). And He—Mighty and Majestic—said: {«Do not approach prayer while you are intoxicated until you know what you are saying»} (Q 4:43). It was said: \"intoxicated\" from the excess of worldly concern (al-hamm), and it was said: from love of this world.", "book_title": "Mysteries of Prayer", "section_title_ar": "Section 124", "section_title_en": "The Virtue of Humble Devotion (Khusḥūʿ) in Prayer", "section_index": 124, "anchors": "- Root: خشع (Khashaʿa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الخشوع: التواضع والسكون، وخشع بصره: غضه وأطرقه، وخشعت الأصوات: سكنت وخفضت\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخشوع: الضراعة والاستكانة، وأصله الانخفاض والتطامن، ويقال للصوت الخفيض والطرف المنكسر\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"Literal: خشع الرجل: خفض رأسه وتواضع. Metaphorical: خشع القلب: خضع وذل وسكن لله\"\n- Root: سكر (Sukr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"السكر: نقيض الصحو، والسكران: الذي غطى عقله، وكل شيء غطى فقد سكر\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"السكر: حالة تعرض بين العقل وبين إدراكه، ويستعار لما يغطي العقل من الهم وحب الدنيا\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"Literal: سكر من الشراب. Metaphorical: سكر الهم وحب الدنيا — ما يغطي العقل عن إدراك المقام\"\n- Root: ندم (Nadama)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الندم: التحسر على ما فات، والندامة: التوبة، والنادم: المتحسر\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الندم: انكسار النفس لما فرط، وهو من لوازم التوبة\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"Literal: ندم على الشيء: تحسر. Metaphorical: تنادم في الصلاة: استحضار التقصير والانكسار بين يدي الله\"\n- Root: وجب (Wajaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"وجب القلب: اضطرب وخفق، ووجب الشيء: ثبت ولزم، والوجيب: صوت خفقان القلب\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجيب: اضطراب القلب من هيبة الأمر وعظمته\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"Literal: وجب الباب: أغلقه. Metaphorical: وجيب القلب: خفقانه من الخوف والهيبة\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — \"إنما الصلاة تمسكن\" restricts prayer's essence to humility, excluding distraction and heedlessness.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Mysteries of Prayer'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "عز وجل لا تقربوا الصلاة وأنتم سكارى حتى تعلموا ما تقولون قيل سكارى من كثرة الهم وقيل من حب الدنيا وقال وهب المراد به ظاهره ففيه تنبيه على سكر الدنيا إذ بين فيه العلة فقال حتى تعلموا ما تقولون وكم من مصل لم يشرب خمرا وهو لا يعلم ما يقول في صلاته وقال النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم من صلى ركعتين لم يحدث نفسه فيهما بشيء من الدنيا غفر له ما تقدم من ذنبه (1) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم إنما الصلاة تمسكن وتواضع وتضرع وتأوه وتنادم وتضع يديك فتقول اللهم اللهم فمن لم يفعل فهي خداج (2) وروي عن الله سبحانه في الك", "english": "God—Exalted is He—said: {«And establish prayer for My remembrance»} (Q 20:14). And He—Exalted is He—said: {«And be not among the heedless»} (Q 7:205). And He—Mighty and Majestic—said: {«Do not approach prayer while you are intoxicated until you know what you are saying»} (Q 4:43). It was said: \"intoxicated\" from the excess of worldly concern (al-hamm), and it was said: from love of this world.", "book_title": "Mysteries of Prayer", "section_title_ar": "Section 124", "section_title_en": "The Virtue of Humble Devotion (Khusḥūʿ) in Prayer", "section_index": 124, "anchors": "- Root: خشع (Khashaʿa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الخشوع: التواضع والسكون، وخشع بصره: غضه وأطرقه، وخشعت الأصوات: سكنت وخفضت\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخشوع: الضراعة والاستكانة، وأصله الانخفاض والتطامن، ويقال للصوت الخفيض والطرف المنكسر\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"Literal: خشع الرجل: خفض رأسه وتواضع. Metaphorical: خشع القلب: خضع وذل وسكن لله\"\n- Root: سكر (Sukr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"السكر: نقيض الصحو، والسكران: الذي غطى عقله، وكل شيء غطى فقد سكر\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"السكر: حالة تعرض بين العقل وبين إدراكه، ويستعار لما يغطي العقل من الهم وحب الدنيا\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"Literal: سكر من الشراب. Metaphorical: سكر الهم وحب الدنيا — ما يغطي العقل عن إدراك المقام\"\n- Root: ندم (Nadama)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الندم: التحسر على ما فات، والندامة: التوبة، والنادم: المتحسر\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الندم: انكسار النفس لما فرط، وهو من لوازم التوبة\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"Literal: ندم على الشيء: تحسر. Metaphorical: تنادم في الصلاة: استحضار التقصير والانكسار بين يدي الله\"\n- Root: وجب (Wajaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"وجب القلب: اضطرب وخفق، ووجب الشيء: ثبت ولزم، والوجيب: صوت خفقان القلب\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجيب: اضطراب القلب من هيبة الأمر وعظمته\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"Literal: وجب الباب: أغلقه. Metaphorical: وجيب القلب: خفقانه من الخوف والهيبة\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — \"إنما الصلاة تمسكن\" restricts prayer's essence to humility, excluding distraction and heedlessness.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Mysteries of Prayer'", "badge": "Du'a"}], "45": [{"arabic": "ولذلك قال تعالى والله أعلم بأعدائكم وكفى بالله وليا وكفى بالله نصيرا فإن قلت: فالعصيان هل يضاد أصل المحبة فأقول: إنه يضاد كمالها ولا يضاد أصلها فكم من إنسان يحب نفسه وهو مريض ويحب الصحة ويأكل ما يضره مع العلم بأنه يضره وذلك لا يدل على عدم حبه لنفسه ولكن المعرفة قد تضعف والشهوة قد تغلب فيعجز عن القيام بحق المحبة ويدل عليه ما روي أن نعيمان كان يؤتى به رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم في كل قليل فيحده في معصية يرتكبها إلى أن أتي به يوما فحده فلعنه رجل وقال: ما أكثر ما يؤتى به رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم فقال صل", "english": "Rather, when love conquers, it suppresses caprice (al-hawa), and no delight remains for the lover save in the Beloved. This is as it was narrated that Zulaykha, when she believed and Yusuf (peace be upon him) married her, withdrew apart from him and devoted herself exclusively to worship, cutting herself off entirely for God Almighty. He would summon her to his bed during the day, and she would put him off until night; and when he summoned her at night, she would defer him until the day, saying: \"O Yusuf, I loved you only before I knew Him. But now that I have known Him, His love has left no room in me for love of any other, and I desire no substitute for Him.\" This continued until he said to her: \"God, glorified be His mention, has commanded me to do this and has informed me that He will bring forth from you two sons and make them both prophets.\" She replied: \"Since God Almighty has commanded you to this and has made me a path to Him, then it is obedience to the command of God Almighty.\" At that point she inclined to him. So whoever loves God does not disobey Him. For this reason Ibn al-Mubarak said concerning this matter:", "book_title": "Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment", "section_title_ar": "Section 1176", "section_title_en": "On the Signs of the Servant's Love for God Almighty", "section_index": 1176, "anchors": "- Root: و د د (W-D-D)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes pure, unalloyed love (al-wudd) — the inclination of the heart toward that which pleases it, distinct from passionate desire (hawa).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological usage, mahabbah (love) is the will's inclination toward the Beloved for His own sake, not for benefit or pleasure; it is the soul's movement toward what it deems good (khayr).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqiqah) sense is the heart's attachment; metaphorical (majaz) extension applies to obedience and following as proof of love, since love is invisible and known only by its traces.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction & Focused Predication (باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما) — \"إنما\" confines the predicate to the subject alone, negating all else; used here to sever any rival love once true love of God is established.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'", "badge": "Insight"}], "48": [{"arabic": "تعالى {إن الله لا يغفر أن يشرك به ويغفر ما دون ذلك لمن يشاء} والاستثناء بالمشيئة يدل على الانقسام وقوله تعالى {ومن يعص الله ورسوله فإن له نار جهنم خالدين فيها} وتخصيصه بالكفر تحكم وقوله تعالى {ألا إن الظالمين في عذاب مقيم} وقال تعالى {ومن جاء بالسيئة فكبت وجوههم في النار} فهذه العمومات في معارضة عموماتهم ولا بد من تسليط التخصص والتأويل على الجانبين لأن الأخبار مصرحة بأن العصاة يعذبون (1) بل قوله تعالى وإن منكم إلا واردها كالصريح في أن ذلك لا بد منه للكل إذ لا يخلو مؤمن عن ذنب يرتكبه وقوله تعالى لا يصلاها إلا ا", "english": "He was asked, \"Which deed is most excellent?\" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Islam.\" It was then asked, \"Which aspect of Islam is most excellent?\" He (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Faith (Īmān).\" This is evidence for both the distinction (ikhtilāf) between the two terms and their overlap (tadākhul). This usage is the most consistent with the conventions of the language, because faith is a deed among deeds, and it is the most excellent of them. Islam is submission (taslīm), whether by the heart, by the tongue, or by the limbs. The most excellent of these is that which is by the heart, which is the assent (taṣdīq) that is called faith. Using the two terms in a way that indicates distinction, overlap, or even complete synonymy (tarāduf) is all within the bounds of metaphorical usage (tajawwuz) in the language.", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 99", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 99: On the Distinction, Overlap, and Synonymy of Faith (Īmān) and Submission (Islām), and Their Worldly and Otherworldly Judgments", "section_index": 99, "anchors": "- Root: ايم (A-Y-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of remaining unmarried, or a state of being without a spouse; extended metaphorically to describe one who is solitary or without support.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأيامى: جمع أيم، وهي المرأة التي لا بعل لها... والحرب مأيمة، أي: يفرق بين الزوج والزوجة.\" The root carries the sense of separation or a state of lacking union, which informs the theological discussion of what constitutes the essential bond of faith.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes the state of being unmarried; metaphorical (Majaz) usage extends to anything that separates or leaves one bereft. This distinction between literal and metaphorical application is central to the chapter's method.\n- Root: قلب (Q-L-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn, overturn, or transform a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه... والانقلاب: الانصراف.\" The heart (qalb) is so named for its constant fluctuation and turning. In theological terms, it is the seat of taṣdīq (assent), the immaterial faculty of cognition and conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is physical turning; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"قلب الأمور\" — to deliberate, scrutinize, and manage affairs. This supports the distinction between the heart's internal act of assent and the external acts of the limbs.\n- Root: عمل (ʿ-A-M-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Any deed or action performed with purpose and intent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العمل: كل فعل يكون من الحيوان بقصد، فهو أخص من الفعل.\" Work is more specific than mere action, as it implies deliberate, intentional agency. It is used for both righteous and sinful deeds, and is the external manifestation of internal conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage refers to physical labor or deeds. The chapter explores whether \"work\" is an integral part of the reality of faith or a separate, consequent requirement.\n- Root: صدق (S-D-Q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be truthful, to speak the truth, to verify.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصدق والكذب أصلهما في القول... ولا يكونان بالقصد الأول إلا في القول.\" Truth and falsehood are primarily properties of speech and reports. Taṣdīq (assent) is the heart's affirmation of the truth of a report, which is the core reality of Īmān.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is truthful speech; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"صادق الحملة\" — one who is true and firm in battle or resolve. This shows that taṣdīq is not merely verbal but a firm, internal resolution.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication). This rule governs how restriction (ḥaṣr) is achieved in Arabic, often through particles like \"innamā\" or through contextual specification (takhṣīṣ). The chapter's core argument hinges on how terms like Īmān and Islām are semantically restricted, expanded, or made synonymous through contextual usage (majāz), requiring precise parsing of the predicate and its scope.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "49": [{"arabic": "فيه من تزكية النفس قال الله تعالى فلا تزكوا أنفسكم {وقال} ألم تر إلى الذين يزكون أنفسهم وقال تعالى انظر كيف يفترون على الله الكذب وقيل لحكيم ما الصدق القبيح فقال ثناء المرء على نفسه والإيمان من أعلى صفات المجد والجزم تزكية مطلقة وصيغة الاستثناء كأنها ثقل من عرف التزكية كما يقال للإنسان أنت طبيب أو فقيه أو مفسر فيقول نعم إن شاء الله لا في معرض التشكيك ولكن لإخراج نفسه عن تزكية نفسه فالصيغة صيغة الترديد والتضعيف لنفس الخبر ومعناه التضعيف اللازم من لوازم الخبر وهو التزكية وبهذا التأويل لو سئل عن وصف ذم لم يحسن الاستثناء الوجه الثاني ا", "english": "If you say: \"What is the meaning of the Salaf's statement, 'I am a believer, if God wills' — given that the exception (istithnā') implies doubt, and doubt in faith is unbelief? Yet they all refrained from giving an unqualified answer affirming faith and were cautious about it.\"", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 102", "section_title_en": "On the Meaning of the Salaf's Statement \"I am a Believer, If God Wills\" — Section 102", "section_index": 102, "anchors": "- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, the soul; that which is breathed; the innermost reality of a person.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): النفس is used for the rūḥ (spirit), for the dhāt (essence/self), and for the seat of intention and knowledge. When one says \"he knows in himself (nafsihi) that he is a believer,\" it denotes certitude residing in the very core of one's being, not a corporeal faculty.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage refers to the soul's reflexive self-awareness; metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to \"blood\" (dam) or \"the eye\" (ʿayn) of envy. Here, \"in himself\" (fī nafsihi) is literal self-knowledge, not mere outward profession.\n- Root: وجه (w-j-h)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Face, countenance; the foremost part; a mode, aspect, or direction.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The face is the most noble apparent part of a thing; hence it is used for the \"aspect\" or \"manner\" (wajh) of an affair. \"Four aspects\" (wujūh) means four distinct modes of interpretation or rational grounds.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the face of a person or object. Metaphorical: \"the face of the matter\" (wajh al-amr) — its most salient and honorable dimension. Here, the \"aspects\" are hermeneutical angles, not physical parts.\n- Root: ايم (a-y-m)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The unmarried state (aym); one who has no spouse. Not applicable here directly, but the root's semantic field of \"being alone/without attachment\" contrasts with īmān's root (a-m-n) — security and trust — which is the operative term in this text.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Not directly relevant; the operative root is أمن (a-m-n), meaning security from fear, and by extension, faith (īmān) as the state of being secure in God's truth.\n- Root: كان (k-w-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be, to exist; the copula of predication.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Kāna denotes existence and becoming; in theological usage, it grounds the distinction between what is (mawjūd) and what is merely asserted. The question \"Are you a believer?\" (a-mu'min anta?) uses kāna implicitly to ask about actual, present state — not future contingency.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: existence. Metaphorical: the \"being-so\" of a predicate. The istithnā' (exception) with \"in shā' Allāh\" suspends the copula's certainty, shifting from assertive existence to contingent hope.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الاستثناء والتعليق بالمشيئة (The Chapter of Exception and Suspension by the Divine Will) — When an exception clause (istithnā') follows a positive assertion, it grammatically suspends the universality or certainty of the predication. In theological discourse, this suspension may be semantic (doubt) or pragmatic (deference, etiquette, or self-deprecation), and Sibawayh's parsing of conditional suspension (taʿlīq) shows that the apodosis need not be negated — only its absolute, unqualified force is tempered.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "50": [{"arabic": "الذين يزكون أنفسهم وقال تعالى انظر كيف يفترون على الله الكذب وقيل لحكيم ما الصدق القبيح فقال ثناء المرء على نفسه والإيمان من أعلى صفات المجد والجزم تزكية مطلقة وصيغة الاستثناء كأنها ثقل من عرف التزكية كما يقال للإنسان أنت طبيب أو فقيه أو مفسر فيقول نعم إن شاء الله لا في معرض التشكيك ولكن لإخراج نفسه عن تزكية نفسه فالصيغة صيغة الترديد والتضعيف لنفس الخبر ومعناه التضعيف اللازم من لوازم الخبر وهو التزكية وبهذا التأويل لو سئل عن وصف ذم لم يحسن الاستثناء الوجه الثاني التأدب بذكر الله تعالى في كل حال وإحالة الأ", "english": "If you say: \"What is the meaning of the Salaf's statement, 'I am a believer, if God wills' — given that the exception (istithnā') implies doubt, and doubt in faith is unbelief? Yet they all refrained from giving an unqualified answer affirming faith and were cautious about it.\"", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 102", "section_title_en": "On the Meaning of the Salaf's Statement \"I am a Believer, If God Wills\" — Section 102", "section_index": 102, "anchors": "- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, the soul; that which is breathed; the innermost reality of a person.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): النفس is used for the rūḥ (spirit), for the dhāt (essence/self), and for the seat of intention and knowledge. When one says \"he knows in himself (nafsihi) that he is a believer,\" it denotes certitude residing in the very core of one's being, not a corporeal faculty.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage refers to the soul's reflexive self-awareness; metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to \"blood\" (dam) or \"the eye\" (ʿayn) of envy. Here, \"in himself\" (fī nafsihi) is literal self-knowledge, not mere outward profession.\n- Root: وجه (w-j-h)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Face, countenance; the foremost part; a mode, aspect, or direction.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The face is the most noble apparent part of a thing; hence it is used for the \"aspect\" or \"manner\" (wajh) of an affair. \"Four aspects\" (wujūh) means four distinct modes of interpretation or rational grounds.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the face of a person or object. Metaphorical: \"the face of the matter\" (wajh al-amr) — its most salient and honorable dimension. Here, the \"aspects\" are hermeneutical angles, not physical parts.\n- Root: ايم (a-y-m)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The unmarried state (aym); one who has no spouse. Not applicable here directly, but the root's semantic field of \"being alone/without attachment\" contrasts with īmān's root (a-m-n) — security and trust — which is the operative term in this text.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Not directly relevant; the operative root is أمن (a-m-n), meaning security from fear, and by extension, faith (īmān) as the state of being secure in God's truth.\n- Root: كان (k-w-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be, to exist; the copula of predication.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Kāna denotes existence and becoming; in theological usage, it grounds the distinction between what is (mawjūd) and what is merely asserted. The question \"Are you a believer?\" (a-mu'min anta?) uses kāna implicitly to ask about actual, present state — not future contingency.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: existence. Metaphorical: the \"being-so\" of a predicate. The istithnā' (exception) with \"in shā' Allāh\" suspends the copula's certainty, shifting from assertive existence to contingent hope.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الاستثناء والتعليق بالمشيئة (The Chapter of Exception and Suspension by the Divine Will) — When an exception clause (istithnā') follows a positive assertion, it grammatically suspends the universality or certainty of the predication. In theological discourse, this suspension may be semantic (doubt) or pragmatic (deference, etiquette, or self-deprecation), and Sibawayh's parsing of conditional suspension (taʿlīq) shows that the apodosis need not be negated — only its absolute, unqualified force is tempered.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Insight"}], "54": [{"arabic": "تعالى أن يخبره باسمه فلم يخبره وقال أحدثك من عمله بثلاث كان لا يحسد الناس على ما آتاهم الله من فضله وكان لا يعق والديه ولا يمشي بالنميمة وقال زكريا عليه السلام قال الله تعالى الحاسد عدو لنعمتي متسخط لقضائي غير راض بقسمتي التي قسمت بين عبادي وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم أخوف ما أخاف على أمتي أن يكثر فيهم المال فيتحاسدون ويقتتلون (1) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم استعينوا على قضاء الحوائج بالكتمان فإن كل ذي نعمة محسود (2) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم إن لنعم الله أعداء فقيل ومن هم فقال الذين يحسدون الناس على ما آتاهم الله من فضله (3) حديث ستة يدخلون النار قبل", "english": "Know that envy (ḥasad) is likewise among the fruits of rancor (ḥiqd), and rancor is among the fruits of anger (ghaḍab) — so envy is a branch of a branch, while anger is the root of the root. Then envy has, among its blameworthy branches, that which can scarcely be enumerated. And there have come down, specifically in condemnation of envy, many reports (akhbār). The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«Envy consumes good deeds just as fire consumes wood»} (1). The ḥadīth: {«Do not cut one another off, nor turn your backs on one another, nor hate one another»} — agreed upon (muttafaq ʿalayh), and it has already preceded. (2) The ḥadīth of Anas: \"We were sitting one day with the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, when he said: 'There will now come upon you from this mountain pass a man from among the people of Paradise'\" — the ḥadīth in its entirety, and in it: that man said, \"I do not find in myself any rancor toward any of the Muslims, nor envy of anyone for the good that God has given him.\" Narrated by Aḥmad with a sound chain (isnād) upon the condition of the two Shaykhs (al-Bukhārī and Muslim), and narrated by al-Bazzār, and the man was named in one of its narrations as Saʿd — though in it there is Ibn Lahīʿah. (3) The ḥadīth: {«Three things from which no one escapes: suspicion, slander, and envy»} — and in another narration: \"And few are those who escape them.\" It was related by Ibn Abī al-Dunyā in his book \"Dhamm al-Ḥasad\" (The Condemnation of Envy) from the ḥadīth of Abū Hurayrah — though in its chain there are Yaʿqūb ibn Muḥammad al-Zuhrī and Mūsā ibn Yaʿqūb al-Zamaʿī, both of whom the majority of scholars have declared weak. The second narration was also related by Ibn Abī al-Dunyā from the narration of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muʿāwiyah, and it is mursal (broken chain) and weak. Al-Ṭabarānī has something similar from the ḥadīth of Ḥārithah ibn al-Nuʿmān, and it has already preceded in the section on the afflictions of the tongue. And in one narration: {«Three things from which no one escapes — and few are those who escape them»} — so this narration affirms the possibility of deliverance. And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«There has crept upon you the disease of the nations before you: envy and hatred. And hatred is the clipper — I do not say it clips the hair, but rather it clips the religion. By Him in whose hand is the soul of Muḥammad, you shall not enter Paradise until you believe, and you shall not believe until you love one another. Shall I not inform you of that which will establish this for you? Spread peace (salām) among you»} (4). And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«Poverty almost becomes unbelief, and envy almost overcomes destiny (qadar)»} (5). And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«Indeed, my community will be afflicted with the disease of the nations.» They said: \"And what is the disease of the nations?\" He said: {«Extravagance, arrogance, accumulation, rivalry in worldly things, mutual distance, and mutual envy — until transgression (baghy) comes, and then destruction (harj)»} (6). And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«Do not show gloating (shamātah) over your brother, lest God heal him and afflict you»} (7). And it is narrated that Moses, peace be upon him, when he hastened to his Lord, Exalted is He, saw a man in the shade of the Throne (ʿArsh) and envied him his station (ghabaṭahu), saying: \"This one is honored before his Lord.\" So he asked his Lord, Exalted is He, to inform him of his name, but He did not inform him, and said: \"I shall tell you of three of his deeds: he did not envy people for what God had given them of His bounty, he was not undutiful to his parents, and he did not go about with slander (namīmah).\" And Zechariah, peace be upon him, said: God, Exalted is He, said: {«The envier is an enemy of My blessing, resentful of My decree, dissatisfied with My apportioning which I have apportioned among My servants»}. And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«The thing I most fear for my community is that wealth will increase among them, and they will envy one another and fight one another»} (1). And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«Seek help in fulfilling your needs by keeping them secret, for everyone who possesses a blessing is envied»} (2). And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«Indeed, the blessings of God have enemies.» It was said: \"And who are they?\" He said: {«Those who envy people for what God has given them of His bounty»} (3). The ḥadīth: {«Six will enter the Fire before the Reckoning by a year.» It was said: \"O Messenger of God, who are they?\" He said: {«The unjust rulers»} — the ḥadīth — and in it: {«And the scholars who envy»}. Narrated by Abū Manṣūr al-Daylamī from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUmar and Anas with two weak chains of transmission.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Envy", "section_title_ar": "Section 718", "section_title_en": "Section 718 — On Envy (Ḥasad): Its Roots, Branches, and Blameworthy Fruits", "section_index": 718, "anchors": "- Root: حسد (Ḥ-S-D)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of wishing for removal of a blessing from its possessor, rooted in spite and resentment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحسد: تمني زوال نعمة من مستحق لها\" — Envy is the desire for the removal of a blessing from one who rightly deserves it, sometimes accompanied by active striving to effect its removal. The believer envies not, but the hypocrite does.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"حسده على نعمة الله\" — to envy him for God's blessing. Metaphorical (majāz): \"إن الحسد يأكل الجسد\" — envy consumes the body, as fire consumes wood, indicating its destructive spiritual effect.\n- Root: حدث (Ḥ-D-TH)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Origination after non-existence; bringing into being after nothingness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحدوث: كون الشيء بعد أن لم يكن\" — origination is a thing's coming to be after it was not. The ḥadīth (report) is so-called for its temporal proximity to the event it narrates.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"هو حدث من الأحداث\" — he is a youth among youths. Metaphorical: \"صاروا أحاديث\" — they became subjects of talk, indicating fame or infamy spread by tongues.\n- Root: ملك (M-L-K)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Possession, dominion, sovereignty; the one who possesses and disposes.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الملك: هو المتصرف بالأمر والنهي\" — dominion is disposal by command and prohibition. Two kinds: actual possession/sovereignty, and the power/capacity to exercise it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"ملكت فلانة\" — he married her (possessed her in wedlock). Metaphorical: \"ملك نفسه عند الغضب\" — he mastered himself at the moment of anger, i.e., self-restraint.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — When the Prophet (ﷺ) says \"إنما قتل ابن آدم أخاه حين حسده\" (It was only envy that led the son of Adam to kill his brother), the restrictive particle إنما confines the cause of the first murder to envy alone, isolating it as the sole efficient cause of that primordial transgression.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Condemnation of Envy'", "badge": "Ethics"}, {"arabic": "الله عليه وسلم إن لنعم الله أعداء فقيل ومن هم فقال الذين يحسدون الناس على ما آتاهم الله من فضله (3) حديث ستة يدخلون النار قبل الحساب بسنة قيل يا رسول الله ومن هم قال الأمراء بالجور الحديث وفيه والعلماء بالحسد أخرجه أبو منصور الديلمي من حديث ابن عمر وأنس بسندين ضعيفين // الآثار قال بعض السلف أول خطيئة هي الحسد حسد إبليس آدم عليه السلام على رتبته فأبى أن يسجد له فحمله على الحسد والمعصية وحكي أن عون بن عبد الله دخل على الفضل بن المهلب وكان يومئذ على واسط فقال إني أريد أن أعظك بشيء فقال وما هو قال إياك والكبر فإنه أول ذنب عصي الله", "english": "Know that envy (ḥasad) is likewise among the fruits of rancor (ḥiqd), and rancor is among the fruits of anger (ghaḍab) — so envy is a branch of a branch, while anger is the root of the root. Then envy has, among its blameworthy branches, that which can scarcely be enumerated. And there have come down, specifically in condemnation of envy, many reports (akhbār). The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«Envy consumes good deeds just as fire consumes wood»} (1). The ḥadīth: {«Do not cut one another off, nor turn your backs on one another, nor hate one another»} — agreed upon (muttafaq ʿalayh), and it has already preceded. (2) The ḥadīth of Anas: \"We were sitting one day with the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, when he said: 'There will now come upon you from this mountain pass a man from among the people of Paradise'\" — the ḥadīth in its entirety, and in it: that man said, \"I do not find in myself any rancor toward any of the Muslims, nor envy of anyone for the good that God has given him.\" Narrated by Aḥmad with a sound chain (isnād) upon the condition of the two Shaykhs (al-Bukhārī and Muslim), and narrated by al-Bazzār, and the man was named in one of its narrations as Saʿd — though in it there is Ibn Lahīʿah. (3) The ḥadīth: {«Three things from which no one escapes: suspicion, slander, and envy»} — and in another narration: \"And few are those who escape them.\" It was related by Ibn Abī al-Dunyā in his book \"Dhamm al-Ḥasad\" (The Condemnation of Envy) from the ḥadīth of Abū Hurayrah — though in its chain there are Yaʿqūb ibn Muḥammad al-Zuhrī and Mūsā ibn Yaʿqūb al-Zamaʿī, both of whom the majority of scholars have declared weak. The second narration was also related by Ibn Abī al-Dunyā from the narration of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muʿāwiyah, and it is mursal (broken chain) and weak. Al-Ṭabarānī has something similar from the ḥadīth of Ḥārithah ibn al-Nuʿmān, and it has already preceded in the section on the afflictions of the tongue. And in one narration: {«Three things from which no one escapes — and few are those who escape them»} — so this narration affirms the possibility of deliverance. And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«There has crept upon you the disease of the nations before you: envy and hatred. And hatred is the clipper — I do not say it clips the hair, but rather it clips the religion. By Him in whose hand is the soul of Muḥammad, you shall not enter Paradise until you believe, and you shall not believe until you love one another. Shall I not inform you of that which will establish this for you? Spread peace (salām) among you»} (4). And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«Poverty almost becomes unbelief, and envy almost overcomes destiny (qadar)»} (5). And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«Indeed, my community will be afflicted with the disease of the nations.» They said: \"And what is the disease of the nations?\" He said: {«Extravagance, arrogance, accumulation, rivalry in worldly things, mutual distance, and mutual envy — until transgression (baghy) comes, and then destruction (harj)»} (6). And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«Do not show gloating (shamātah) over your brother, lest God heal him and afflict you»} (7). And it is narrated that Moses, peace be upon him, when he hastened to his Lord, Exalted is He, saw a man in the shade of the Throne (ʿArsh) and envied him his station (ghabaṭahu), saying: \"This one is honored before his Lord.\" So he asked his Lord, Exalted is He, to inform him of his name, but He did not inform him, and said: \"I shall tell you of three of his deeds: he did not envy people for what God had given them of His bounty, he was not undutiful to his parents, and he did not go about with slander (namīmah).\" And Zechariah, peace be upon him, said: God, Exalted is He, said: {«The envier is an enemy of My blessing, resentful of My decree, dissatisfied with My apportioning which I have apportioned among My servants»}. And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«The thing I most fear for my community is that wealth will increase among them, and they will envy one another and fight one another»} (1). And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«Seek help in fulfilling your needs by keeping them secret, for everyone who possesses a blessing is envied»} (2). And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: {«Indeed, the blessings of God have enemies.» It was said: \"And who are they?\" He said: {«Those who envy people for what God has given them of His bounty»} (3). The ḥadīth: {«Six will enter the Fire before the Reckoning by a year.» It was said: \"O Messenger of God, who are they?\" He said: {«The unjust rulers»} — the ḥadīth — and in it: {«And the scholars who envy»}. Narrated by Abū Manṣūr al-Daylamī from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUmar and Anas with two weak chains of transmission.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Envy", "section_title_ar": "Section 718", "section_title_en": "Section 718 — On Envy (Ḥasad): Its Roots, Branches, and Blameworthy Fruits", "section_index": 718, "anchors": "- Root: حسد (Ḥ-S-D)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of wishing for removal of a blessing from its possessor, rooted in spite and resentment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحسد: تمني زوال نعمة من مستحق لها\" — Envy is the desire for the removal of a blessing from one who rightly deserves it, sometimes accompanied by active striving to effect its removal. The believer envies not, but the hypocrite does.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"حسده على نعمة الله\" — to envy him for God's blessing. Metaphorical (majāz): \"إن الحسد يأكل الجسد\" — envy consumes the body, as fire consumes wood, indicating its destructive spiritual effect.\n- Root: حدث (Ḥ-D-TH)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Origination after non-existence; bringing into being after nothingness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحدوث: كون الشيء بعد أن لم يكن\" — origination is a thing's coming to be after it was not. The ḥadīth (report) is so-called for its temporal proximity to the event it narrates.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"هو حدث من الأحداث\" — he is a youth among youths. Metaphorical: \"صاروا أحاديث\" — they became subjects of talk, indicating fame or infamy spread by tongues.\n- Root: ملك (M-L-K)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Possession, dominion, sovereignty; the one who possesses and disposes.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الملك: هو المتصرف بالأمر والنهي\" — dominion is disposal by command and prohibition. Two kinds: actual possession/sovereignty, and the power/capacity to exercise it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"ملكت فلانة\" — he married her (possessed her in wedlock). Metaphorical: \"ملك نفسه عند الغضب\" — he mastered himself at the moment of anger, i.e., self-restraint.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — When the Prophet (ﷺ) says \"إنما قتل ابن آدم أخاه حين حسده\" (It was only envy that led the son of Adam to kill his brother), the restrictive particle إنما confines the cause of the first murder to envy alone, isolating it as the sole efficient cause of that primordial transgression.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Condemnation of Envy'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "الإنكار {أم يحسدون الناس على ما آتاهم الله من فضله} وقال تعالى {كان الناس أمة واحدة} إلى قوله {إلا الذين أوتوه من بعد ما جاءتهم البينات بغيا بينهم} قيل في التفسير حسدا وقال تعالى {وما تفرقوا إلا من بعد ما جاءهم العلم بغيا بينهم} فأنزل الله العلم ليجمعهم ويؤلف بينهم على طاعته وأمرهم أن يتألفوا بالعلم فتحاسدوا واختلفوا إذ أراد كل واحد منهم أن ينفرد بالرياسة وقبول القول فرد بعضهم على بعض قال ابن عباس كانت اليهود قبل أن يبعث النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم إذا قاتلوا قوما قالوا نسألك بالنبي الذي وعدتنا أن ت", "english": "The Prophet ﷺ said: «إن المؤمن يغبط والمنافق يحسد» — \"The believer aspires emulously, and the hypocrite envies.\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of Envy", "section_title_ar": "Section 720", "section_title_en": "On the Reality of Envy (Ḥasad) and Its Degrees — Section 720", "section_index": 720, "anchors": "- Root: نعم (n-ʿ-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of \"bounty, benefaction, grace\" — that state of goodness and well-being bestowed.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النعمة: الحالة الحسنة\" — bounty is the excellent state/condition; it is the genus term applying to both little and much, as in {«وإن تعدوا نعمة الله لا تحصوها»} [Ibrahim: 34].\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"جلت نعمة الله ونعماؤه\" — bounty as actual bestowed good. Metaphorical (majāz): \"خفت نعامتهم\" — departure/removal, relevant to the envier's wish for the bounty's removal.\n- Root: حسد (ḥ-s-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Envy — the desire for removal of another's good.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحسد: تمني زوال نعمة من مستحق لها\" — envy is wishing the removal of bounty from one who deserves it, sometimes accompanied by striving to effect its removal. Cited: «المؤمن يغبط والمنافق يحسد».\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"حسده على نعمة الله\" — envying someone for God's bounty. \"إن الحسد يأكل الجسد\" — envy consumes the body.\n- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self; also value, preciousness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): النفس: الروح — the soul/spirit; also الذات (essence/self).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"شيء نفيس ومنفس\" — a precious thing. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — poured out his self, i.e., his blood. From this root derives المنافسة (emulative competition) — the striving for precious things.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Specification) — The definition of ḥasad is given as a restricted predication (ḥadd), delimiting its essential meaning: \"كراهة النعمة وحب زوالها عن المنعم عليه\" — the hatred of bounty and love of its removal from the one upon whom it is bestowed. This is a formal definition (ḥadd), not merely a lexical gloss.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Condemnation of Envy'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "56": [{"arabic": "مرات فتجدد جلودههم ولحومهم قال الحسن في قوله تعالى كلما نضجت جلودهم بدلناهم جلودا غيرها قال تأكلهم النار كل يوم سبعين ألف مرة كلما أكلتهم قيل لهم عودوا فيعودون كما كانوا ثم تفكر الآن في بكاء أهل النار وشهيقهم ودعاتهم بالويل والثبور فإن ذلك يسلط عليهم في أول إلقائهم في النار قال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم يؤتى بجهنم يومئذ لها سبعون ألف زمام مع كل زمام سبعون ألف ملك (7) وقال أنس قال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم يرسل على أهل النار البكاء فيبكون حتى تنقطع الدموع ثم يبكون الدم حتى يرى في وجوههم كهيئة الأخدود لو أرسلت فيها السفن ل", "english": "At that point they despair of every good, and at that point they take to sighing, regret, and wailing. Abu Umamah said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said concerning His words, \"And he is given to drink of festering water, which he sips but can hardly swallow\" {«وَيُسْقَىٰ مِن مَّاءٍ صَدِيدٍ يَتَجَرَّعُهُ وَلَا يَكَادُ يُسِيغُهُ»}: \"It is brought near to him, and he loathes it. When it is drawn close to him, it scorches his face, and the skin of his scalp falls off. When he drinks it, it severs his intestines until it exits from his anus.\" God Almighty says: {«وَسُقُوا مَاءً حَمِيمًا فَقَطَّعَ أَمْعَاءَهُمْ»} \"And they are given to drink boiling water, and it severs their intestines.\" And He says: {«وَإِن يَسْتَغِيثُوا يُغَاثُوا بِمَاءٍ كَالْمُهْلِ يَشْوِي الْوُجُوهَ»} \"And if they cry for relief, they are relieved with water like molten brass, which scalds the faces.\" So this is their food and drink in their hunger and thirst.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1350", "section_title_en": "The Torments of the Fire: Serpents, Scorpions, and the Anguish of the Damned", "section_index": 1350, "anchors": "- Root: حس (ḥ-s-s) — [AVOIDED per instruction]\n- Root: نار (n-r) — Fire\n  * Lisan al-Arab: \"النار: معروفة، وهي نار جهنم، ذات لفح وحرّ.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النار: قد تطلق على نار الدنيا ونار الآخرة، وهي التي أعدها الله للكافرين، وفيها من أصناف العذاب ما لا يعلمه إلا الله.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"نار: حقيقة في النار المحرقة، ومجاز في نار الحرب والحرب.\"\n- Root: حي (ḥ-y) — Serpent\n  * Lisan al-Arab: \"الحية: من الهوام، وتجمع على حيات، وأعظمها ما كان كأعناق البخت.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحية: دابة مؤذية، وفي الآخرة تكون أعظم وأشد إيلامًا.\"\n- Root: عقر (ʿ-q-r) — Scorpion\n  * Lisan al-Arab: \"العقرب: دويبة مؤذية تلدغ، وفي النار تكون كالبغال.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to specify that serpents and scorpions are only empowered over those who were dominated by miserliness and evil character in the world.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}], "57": [{"arabic": "دونه وادعى الإجماع فيه واستدل بأدلة تشعر بنقيض غرضه كقوله تعالى {الذين آمنوا وعملوا الصالحات} إذ هذا يدل على أن العمل وراء الإيمان لا من نفس الإيمان وإلا فيكون العمل في حكم المعاد والعجب أنه ادعى الإجماع في هذا وهو مع ذلك ينقل قوله صلى الله عليه وسلم لا يكفر أحد إلا بعد جحوده لما أقر به (1) وينكر على المعتزلة قولهم بالتخليد في النار بسبب الكبائر والقائل بهذا قائل بنفس مذهب المعتزلة إذ يقال له من صدق بقلبه وشهد بلسانه ومات في الحال فهل هو في الجنة فلا بد أن يقول نعم وفيه حكم بوجود الإيمان دون العمل فنزيد ونقول لو بقي حيا حتى دخل عليه وقت", "english": "He was asked, \"Which deed is most excellent?\" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Islam.\" It was then asked, \"Which aspect of Islam is most excellent?\" He (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Faith (Īmān).\" This is evidence for both the distinction (ikhtilāf) between the two terms and their overlap (tadākhul). This usage is the most consistent with the conventions of the language, because faith is a deed among deeds, and it is the most excellent of them. Islam is submission (taslīm), whether by the heart, by the tongue, or by the limbs. The most excellent of these is that which is by the heart, which is the assent (taṣdīq) that is called faith. Using the two terms in a way that indicates distinction, overlap, or even complete synonymy (tarāduf) is all within the bounds of metaphorical usage (tajawwuz) in the language.", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 99", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 99: On the Distinction, Overlap, and Synonymy of Faith (Īmān) and Submission (Islām), and Their Worldly and Otherworldly Judgments", "section_index": 99, "anchors": "- Root: ايم (A-Y-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of remaining unmarried, or a state of being without a spouse; extended metaphorically to describe one who is solitary or without support.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأيامى: جمع أيم، وهي المرأة التي لا بعل لها... والحرب مأيمة، أي: يفرق بين الزوج والزوجة.\" The root carries the sense of separation or a state of lacking union, which informs the theological discussion of what constitutes the essential bond of faith.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes the state of being unmarried; metaphorical (Majaz) usage extends to anything that separates or leaves one bereft. This distinction between literal and metaphorical application is central to the chapter's method.\n- Root: قلب (Q-L-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn, overturn, or transform a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه... والانقلاب: الانصراف.\" The heart (qalb) is so named for its constant fluctuation and turning. In theological terms, it is the seat of taṣdīq (assent), the immaterial faculty of cognition and conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is physical turning; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"قلب الأمور\" — to deliberate, scrutinize, and manage affairs. This supports the distinction between the heart's internal act of assent and the external acts of the limbs.\n- Root: عمل (ʿ-A-M-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Any deed or action performed with purpose and intent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العمل: كل فعل يكون من الحيوان بقصد، فهو أخص من الفعل.\" Work is more specific than mere action, as it implies deliberate, intentional agency. It is used for both righteous and sinful deeds, and is the external manifestation of internal conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage refers to physical labor or deeds. The chapter explores whether \"work\" is an integral part of the reality of faith or a separate, consequent requirement.\n- Root: صدق (S-D-Q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be truthful, to speak the truth, to verify.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصدق والكذب أصلهما في القول... ولا يكونان بالقصد الأول إلا في القول.\" Truth and falsehood are primarily properties of speech and reports. Taṣdīq (assent) is the heart's affirmation of the truth of a report, which is the core reality of Īmān.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is truthful speech; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"صادق الحملة\" — one who is true and firm in battle or resolve. This shows that taṣdīq is not merely verbal but a firm, internal resolution.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication). This rule governs how restriction (ḥaṣr) is achieved in Arabic, often through particles like \"innamā\" or through contextual specification (takhṣīṣ). The chapter's core argument hinges on how terms like Īmān and Islām are semantically restricted, expanded, or made synonymous through contextual usage (majāz), requiring precise parsing of the predicate and its scope.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Insight"}], "58": [{"arabic": "ولما تلا قوله عز وجل إن الله يأمركم أن تؤدوا الأمانات إلى أهلها وضع يده على سمعه وبصره فقال السمع أمانة والبصر أمانة (2) ولولا أنه من أمانات الصوم لما قال صلى الله عليه وسلم فليقل إني صائم أي إني أودعت لساني لأحفظه فكيف أطلقه بجوابك فإذن قد ظهر أن لكل عبادة ظاهرا وباطنا", "english": "As for the fast of the elite of the elite, it is the fasting of the heart from base concerns (al-himam al-daniyyah) and worldly thoughts (al-afkār al-dunyawiyyah), and its complete restraint from everything other than God, Mighty and Majestic. The breaking of the fast in this degree occurs through thinking upon other than God, Mighty and Majestic, and the Last Day, and through thinking upon this world — except a worldly matter intended for the sake of religion, for that is of the provisions of the Hereafter and is not of this world. Indeed, the masters of hearts have said: \"Whosoever's aspiration stirs him to act during his day to arrange what he will break his fast upon, a sin is recorded against him\" — for that stems from lack of trust in the bounty of God, Mighty and Majestic, and lack of certainty in His promised provision. This is the station of the prophets, the veracious (al-ṣiddīqīn), and those brought near (al-muqarrabīn). There is no need to prolong the discussion of its details in speech, but rather in actualizing it in deed — for it is the turning of one's whole aspiration toward God, Mighty and Majestic, and the turning away from other than God, Glorified be He, and the clothing of oneself with the meaning of His saying, Mighty and Majestic: {«Say: God — then leave them in their vain play»} (Q. 6:91).", "book_title": "Mysteries of Fasting", "section_title_ar": "Section 188", "section_title_en": "The Three Degrees of Fasting: The Fast of the Common People, the Fast of the Elite, and the Fast of the Elite of the Elite", "section_index": 188, "anchors": "- Root: صوم (ṣ-w-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core linguistic root meaning — \"abstention from action, whether from food, speech, or walking; the horse that refrains from moving or feeding is called ṣā'im\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصوم في الأصل: الإمساك عن الفعل مطعما كان، أو كلاما، أو مشيا\" — fasting in origin is abstention from any act, whether food, speech, or movement; theological nuance extends to abstention of the heart from all save God\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) — \"شهر الصوم والصيام\" the month of fasting; Metaphorical (Majaz) — \"صام الفرس على آريه إذا لم يعتلف\" the horse fasting upon its stable when it refrains from fodder; hence the heart's fasting from worldly concerns is a metaphorical extension of the same root abstention\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core linguistic root meaning — \"fear, trepidation, the stirring of dread within the breast\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — wajal is the inward sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»} (Q. 8:2) — the believers are those whose hearts tremble when God is mentioned\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" a fearful man, a fearful people; the heart suspended between fear and hope after the fast partakes of this root's semantic field\n- Root: عله (ʿ-l-h)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core linguistic root meaning — \"to occupy, to divert, to preoccupy; the one preoccupied is mashghūl\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The heart's occupation with other than God is the very breaking of the fast of the elite of the elite\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical — the heart's being \"occupied\" (mushtaghil) with worldly concerns is the metaphorical breaking of the spiritual fast, just as the stomach's occupation with food breaks the literal fast\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — the tripartite division of fasting (صوم العموم، صوم الخصوص، صوم خصوص الخصوص) employs graded specification, each level restricting and elevating the prior, moving from the corporeal (al-jawāhir) to the immaterial spiritual realities (al-laṭā'if)", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Mysteries of Fasting'", "badge": "Insight"}], "59": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}], "64": [{"arabic": "الله عليه وسلم ثم يقول اللهم إنك قد قلت وقولك الحق ولو أنهم إذ ظلموا أنفسهم جاءوك فاستغفروا الله واستغفر لهم الرسول لوجدوا الله توابا رحيما اللهم إنا قد سمعنا قولك وأطعنا أمرك وقصدنا نبيك متشفعين به إليك في ذنوبنا وما أثقل ظهورنا من أوزارنا تائبين من زللنا معترفين بخطايانا وتقصيرنا فتب اللهم علينا وشفع نبيك هذا فينا وارفعنا بمنزلته عندك وحقه عليك اللهم اغفر للمهاجرين والأنصار واغفر لنا ولإخواننا الذين سبقونا بالإيمان اللهم لا تجعله آخر العهد من قبر نبيك ومن حرمك يا أرحم الراحمين ثم يأتي الروضة فيصلي فيها ركعتين ويكثر من الدعاء ما استطاع لقوله صلى الله عليه وسلم", "english": "I say: The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Whoever visits me after my death, it is as though he visited me during my life.\" {من زارني بعد وفاتي فكأنما زارني في حياتي}", "book_title": "Mysteries of Hajj", "section_title_ar": "Section 210", "section_title_en": "Section 210 — The Etiquette of Visiting the Prophet's Mosque and Grave in Medina", "section_index": 210, "anchors": "- Root: زور (z-w-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of inclining toward, visiting, or intending a place or person; the visitor (zā'ir) is one who turns away from his own affairs toward another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological context, ziyārah (visitation) of the Prophet ﷺ after death is an act of spiritual devotion (taqarrub), not a mere physical journey; its reward is tied to the sincerity of intention (niyyah).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah) usage denotes physical approach; metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to seeking intercession (shafā'ah) and spiritual connection with the visited.\n- Root: سلم (s-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Root denotes safety, peace, and deliverance from harm; the greeting \"al-salāmu ʿalaykum\" is a prayer for the recipient's immunity from all deficiency.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The greeting upon the Prophet ﷺ is a form of supplication (duʿā') and acknowledgment of his rank; it is also a renewal of the covenant (mīthāq) of obedience.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage is the exchange of safety; metaphorical usage in the visitation context signifies the visitor's submission and the Prophet's reciprocal blessing.\n- Root: شفع (sh-f-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Root denotes pairing or joining; shafā'ah (intercession) is the act of the intercessor joining his own plea to that of the petitioner.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In Kalam, shafā'ah is a divinely granted honor (karāmah) to the Prophet ﷺ, conditional upon divine will; it is not an independent power but a mediated mercy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: making a number even by adding one; metaphorical: the intercessor's elevation of the petitioner's rank before the king.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Prepositional Interposition (al-faṣl bayna al-jār wa al-majrūr) — The text employs extended vocative and prepositional structures (e.g., \"al-salāmu ʿalayka yā Rasūl Allāh\") where the syntactic pause after each invocation is permissible and rhetorically deliberate, mirroring the classical allowance for separation between the governing particle and its object for emphasis.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Mysteries of Hajj'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "66": [{"arabic": "إنا نحب ربنا ولو علمنا في أي شيء محبته لفعلناه حتى نزل قوله تعالى ولو أنا كتبنا عليهم أن اقتلوا أنفسكم أو اخرجوا من دياركم ما فعلوه إلا قليل منهم (1) قال ابن مسعود رحمه الله قال لي رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم أنت منهم يعني من القليل قال وما عرفت أن فينا من يحب الدنيا حتى نزل قوله تعالى منكم من يريد الدنيا ومنكم من يريد الآخرة واعلم أنه ليس من الزهد ترك المال وبذله على سبيل السخاء والفتوة وعلى سبيل استمالة القلوب وعلى سبيل الطمع فذلك كله من محاسن العادات ولكن لا مدخل لشيء منه في العبادات وإنما الزهد أن تترك الدنيا لعلمك بحقارتها بالإضافة إلى نفاسة الآخرة فأما كل نوع من الترك فإنه يتصو", "english": "...better and more enduring. Yet one may know this while being unable to abandon this world — either due to the weakness of one's knowledge and certainty, or due to the dominance of lust prevailing over him in the present moment, making him vanquished in the hand of Satan, or due to his being deluded by Satan's promises of procrastination, day after day, until death snatches him away and nothing remains with him but regret after the loss. And to the recognition of the baseness of this world points His saying, exalted: {قُلْ مَتَاعُ الدُّنْيَا قَلِيلٌ} — \"Say: the enjoyment of this world is little\" (Q. 3:196). And to the recognition of the preciousness of the Hereafter points His saying, mighty and majestic: {وَقَالَ الَّذِينَ أُوتُوا الْعِلْمَ وَيْلَكُمْ ثَوَابُ اللَّهِ خَيْرٌ} — \"And those who were given knowledge said: Woe to you! The reward of God is better\" (Q. 28:80). Thus He alerted that it is knowledge of the preciousness of the jewel that turns one away from its substitute. And since zuhd is inconceivable except through an exchange and a turning away from the beloved toward one more beloved, a man said in his supplication: \"O God, show me this world as You see it.\" The Prophet, peace be upon him, said to him: \"Do not say that, but say: Show me this world as You have shown it to the righteous among Your servants.\" This is because God, exalted, sees it as contemptible as it truly is — and every creature, in relation to His majesty, is contemptible — whereas the servant sees it as contemptible in itself relative to what is better for him. It is inconceivable that the seller of a horse, even if he turns away from his horse, should see it as he sees, say, the vermin of the earth, for he has no need whatsoever of vermin, while he is not free of need for the horse. God, exalted, is rich by His essence from all besides Him, so He sees all things at a single degree relative to His majesty, while He sees them as differentiated relative to what is other than Him. The ascetic (al-zāhid) is the one who sees their differentiation relative to himself, not relative to another.", "book_title": "Poverty and Abstinence", "section_title_ar": "Section 1089", "section_title_en": "On the Reality of Zuhd (Ascetic Renunciation) and Its Conditions — Section 1089", "section_index": 1089, "anchors": "- Root: دنا (d-n-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Proximity, nearness; \"adnā\" as lesser/lower in rank or worth.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Adnā\" denotes what is inferior or meaner, contrasted with \"khayr\" (the better), as in {أَتَسْتَبْدِلُونَ الَّذِي هُوَ أَدْنَىٰ بِالَّذِي هُوَ خَيْرٌ} (Q. 2:61).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqiqah) sense of lowliness and despicableness; metaphorical (majaz) extension to moral baseness.\n- Root: زهد (z-h-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To abstain, to regard as little; also \"zahada al-nakhl\" — to estimate fruit yield (connoting measured appraisal).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-zāhid fī al-shay': al-rāghib 'anhu\" — the one who turns away from a thing, content with little of it; Q. 12:20 {وَكَانُوا فِيهِ مِنَ الزَّاهِدِينَ}.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: renunciation of a thing, paucity of desire. Metaphorical: \"zahīd al-'ayn\" — one satisfied with little; \"wādin zahīd\" — a valley taking little water. Zuhd is not mere abandonment but a valuation-based exchange.\n- Root: ترك (t-r-k)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To leave, abandon, relinquish; \"tarkah\" — inheritance left behind.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Abandonment by choice and intention, or by compulsion; \"taraktu fulānan wahīdan\" — I left him alone, i.e., in a resultant state.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"taraka al-shay'\" — to leave a thing; \"tārakahu al-bay'\" — mutual abandonment of a sale. The term implies a deliberate act with a resultant condition.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and specification via \"innamā\" (الحصر والتقييد) — used to delimit the true definition of zuhd against mere relinquishment, and to distinguish the act of the ascetic from the mere spender.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Poverty and Abstinence'", "badge": "Du'a"}], "69": [{"arabic": "المقربين من النبيين والصديقين والشهداء والصالحين وحسن أولئك رفيقا ولما كان الوسط الحقيقي بين الطرفين في غاية الغموض بل هو أدق من الشعر وأحد من السيف فلا جرم أن من استوى على هذا الصراط المستقيم في الدنيا جاز على مثل هذا الصراط في الآخرة وقلما ينفك العبد عن ميل عن الصراط المستقيم أعني الوسط حتى لا يميل إلى أحد الجانبين فيكون قلبه معلقا بالجانب الذي مال إليه", "english": "I say: The soul (nafs) that belongs to the human being — that by which he is distinguished from the beasts — for he is not distinguished from them by the power of eating, copulation, sight, or any other such thing. Rather, he is distinguished by the knowledge of things as they truly are. And the Origin of all things, their Existentiator and their Inventor, is God, Mighty and Majestic — He who made them things. So if one knew everything but did not know God, Mighty and Majestic, it would be as though he knew nothing at all. And the sign of knowledge is love: whoever knows God Most High loves Him. And the sign of love is that one does not prefer over Him this world nor any other beloved thing. As God Most High said: {«Say: If your fathers, your sons, your brothers, your spouses, your clans — the wealth you have acquired, commerce whose decline you fear, and dwellings you find pleasing — are more beloved to you than God and His Messenger and striving in His cause, then wait until God brings about His command»} [Q 9:24]. So whoever possesses something more beloved to him than God — his heart is sick. Just as any stomach to which clay has become more beloved than bread and water, or whose appetite for bread and water has fallen away — it is sick. These are the signs of sickness. And by this it is known that all hearts are sick — except what God wills. Yet among sicknesses there are those the sufferer does not recognize; and the sickness of the heart is among those the sufferer does not recognize. For this reason he is heedless of it. And even if he recognized it, patience upon the bitterness of its medicine would be difficult for him — for its medicine is opposing the appetites, and that is the tearing out of the spirit (nafs). And if he found in himself the strength of patience upon it, he would not find a skillful physician to treat him — for the physicians are the scholars, and sickness has overcome them. So the sick physician rarely attends to his own cure. For this reason the malady has become chronic and the sickness chronic. This science has become effaced, and the medicine of hearts is denied entirely, and the sickness of the heart is denied. And creation has turned to the love of this world and to deeds whose outward form is acts of worship but whose inward reality is habit and ostentation. These are the signs of the roots of sicknesses.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 624", "section_title_en": "The Soul's Distinction, the Sickness of the Heart, and the Path of Moderation", "section_index": 624, "anchors": "- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, or spirit; that by which a thing is distinguished in its essential reality\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The soul (nafs) as the principle of rational discrimination and knowledge, distinguishing humans from beasts\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the self or person; Metaphorical: the rational soul as the seat of knowledge and moral responsibility\n- Root: مرض (m-r-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Departure from the state of health and equilibrium\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Sickness of the heart as a metaphor for moral and spiritual vices — ignorance, cowardice, avarice, hypocrisy\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: bodily illness; Metaphorical: spiritual malady whereby the heart inclines toward what is harmful and away from what is beneficial\n- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The heart; that which is ever-turning and fluctuating between states\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The heart as the seat of understanding and moral perception; its \"turning\" (taqlīb) signifies its instability between soundness and sickness\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the physical organ; Metaphorical: the spiritual faculty of cognition and moral discernment\n- Root: خلق (kh-l-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Measured, proportioned disposition; character and temperament\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Moral character as the inward form (ṣūrah bāṭinah) corresponding to the outward physical form\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: measuring and proportioning; Metaphorical: the ingrained disposition that governs action\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The use of restrictive constructions to isolate the defining characteristic of the human soul as knowledge of God, not animal appetites", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "{فأولئك مع الذين أنعم الله عليهم من النبيين والصديقين والشهداء والصالحين وحسن أولئك رفيقا} // حديث سادات المؤمنين في الجنة من إذا تغدى لم يجد عشاء الحديث عزاه صاحب مسند الفردوس للطبراني من رواية أبي حازم عن أبي هريرة مختصرا بلفظ سادة الفقراء في الجنة الحديث ولم أره في معاجم الطبراني ألا يا أخي متى جمعت هذا المال بعد هذا البيان فإنك مبطل فيما ادعيت أنك للبر والفضل تجمعه لا ولكنك خوفا من الفقر تجمعه وللنعيم والزينة والتكاثر والفخر والعلو والرياء والسمعة والتعظيم والتكرمة تجمعه ثم تزعم إنك لأعمال البر تجم", "english": "O you who are deluded! Have you not known that your fear of plunging into ambiguous matters (shubuhāt) is higher, more virtuous, and greater for your rank with God than acquiring ambiguous wealth and expending it in the path of God and the path of righteousness? This has been conveyed to us from some of the people of knowledge, who said: \"That you abandon a single dirham out of fear that it might not be lawful is better for you than that you give in charity a thousand dīnārs from ambiguous wealth, not knowing whether it is lawful for you or not.\"", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 787", "section_title_en": "The Book of Lawful and Unlawful — Beginning of the Discourse", "section_index": 787, "anchors": "- Root: شبه (Sh-B-H)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of resemblance and ambiguity; what is not clearly distinguished from its opposite.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-Shubhah denotes uncertainty in discernment between the lawful and unlawful — a state where the heart cannot attain decisive knowledge (yaqīn).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage pertains to visual resemblance; metaphorical (majāz) usage applies to moral and legal ambiguity that clouds the conscience.\n- Root: ورق (W-R-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Abstinence and scrupulous avoidance; the root conveys turning away from what is doubtful.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-Waraʿ is the highest station of the God-fearing — abstaining not only from the unlawful but from the doubtful to safeguard one's religion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical avoidance; metaphorical: the soul's restraint from contamination by ambiguous gains.\n- Root: حسب (H-S-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Reckoning, enumeration, and accounting.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-Ḥisāb on the Day of Resurrection is the divine scrutiny of one's deeds — its severity is proportional to one's wealth and worldly engagements.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: arithmetic computation; metaphorical: the moral audit of one's life before God.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب النداء والتحذير (The Chapter of Vocative Address and Warning) — The repeated vocative \"ويحك\" (Woe to you) functions as a rhetorical warning (taḥdhīr) and lamentation, structuring the author's direct admonitory address to the deluded soul.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "النبيين والصديقين والشهداء والصالحين دون الذين غضب عليهم من الكفار والزائغين من اليهود والنصارى والصابئين ثم التمس الإجابة وقل آمين فإذا تلوت الفاتحة كذلك فيشبه أن تكون من الذين قال الله تعالى فيهم فيما أخبر عنه النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم قسمت الصلاة بيني وبين عبدي نصفين نصفها لي ونصفها لعبدي ولعبدي ما سأل (1) يقول العبد الحمد لله رب العالمين فيقول الله عز وجل حمدني عبدي وأثنى علي وهو معنى قوله سمع الله لمن حمده الحديث الخ فلو لم يكن لك من صلاتك حظ سوى ذكر الله لك في جلاله وعظمته فناهيك", "english": "For this reason, he, peace be upon him, said: \"Give us rest, O Bilāl\" (1) — meaning, give us rest through it (the prayer) and through the call to it, for his delight, peace be upon him, was in it.", "book_title": "On Invocations and Supplications", "section_title_ar": "Section 138", "section_title_en": "The Inner Works of Prayer: Unveiling the Heart's Secrets in the Ritual Prayer (Asrār al-Ṣalāh)", "section_index": 138, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (Q-L-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn a thing over, to revolve it, to divert it from one state to another; the heart as that which is constantly turning and fluctuating.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — The heart is that which is turned and diverted; its rectitude lies in its orientation towards God, its ruin in its turning towards other than Him.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical (Majaz): \"قلب المعلم الصبيان: صرفهم إلى بيوتهم\" (the teacher turned the boys towards their homes) — thus the heart's turning is its direction and intent.\n- Root: وجه (W-J-H)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The face; the foremost, noblest, and most apparent part of a thing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"ولما كان الوجه أول ما يستقبلك، وأشرف ما في ظاهر البدن استعمل في مستقبل كل شيء، وفي أشرفه ومبدئه\" — The face is used metaphorically for the essence and the direction of a thing; the \"face of the heart\" is its intention and aspiration.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"واجهته مواجهة ووجاها\" (to face one another). Metaphorical: \"هذا وجه الثوب. ووجه القوم، وهؤلاء وجوه البلد\" (the face of a garment, the chiefs of a people) — thus the \"face\" of the heart is its foremost orientation.\n- Root: وجل (W-J-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A trembling of the heart from fear or awe.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — The sensing of fear; the heart's tremor before the majesty of the Divine.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال، وقد وجل وجلا\" — A man trembling with fear.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The particle \"innamā\" restricts and confines the predicate to the subject alone, negating it from all else. This is crucial for understanding \"إنما هذه الظواهر تحريكات للبواطن\" — the outward acts are *only* movements to stir the inward realities, nothing more.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'", "badge": "Du'a"}], "71": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}], "74": [{"arabic": "مائة فارس كلهم يقاتل في سبيل الله (1) وإنما ذكر السقط تنبيها بالأدنى على الأعلى وإلا فالثواب على قدر محل الولد من القلب وقال زيد بن أسلم توفي ابن لداود عليك السلام فحزن عليه حزنا شديدا فقيل له ما كان عدله عندك قال ملء الأرض ذهبا قيل له فإن لك من الأجر في الآخرة مثل ذلك وقال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم لا يموت لأحد من المسلمين ثلاثة من الولد فيحتسبهم إلا كانوا له جنة من النار فقالت امرأة عند رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم أو اثنان قال أو اثنان (2) وليخلص الوالد الدعاء لولد", "english": "And thus is death: its meaning is precedence to the homeland, until the one delayed catches up. And when one holds this conviction, his agitation and sorrow diminish—especially since there has come down regarding the death of a child such reward as consoles every afflicted one. The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: \"That I should be presented with a stillborn child is dearer to me than that I should leave behind a hundred horsemen, all of them fighting in the path of God\" {«لأن أقدم سقطا أحب إلي من أن أخلف مائة فارس كلهم يقاتل في سبيل الله»}. He only mentioned the stillborn as an intimation from the lesser to the greater—for otherwise, the reward is according to the station of the child in the heart.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1315", "section_title_en": "Exposition of Their Sayings at the Death of a Child", "section_index": 1315, "anchors": "- Root: ولد (w-l-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Offspring, child—applies to singular, plural, young, and old alike; also used for an adopted child.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Walad denotes the born one; in theological usage, it carries the sense of progeny as a trust and trial from God, with reward proportioned to the child's station in the parent's heart.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqiqah) for biological birth and kinship; metaphorical (majaz) for \"newly generated\" speech or emergent states—here, the literal sense governs the parent-child relation.\n- Root: موت (m-w-t)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Death as the cessation of life; mawt is the common term for dying, whether of humans or beasts.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Death is departure from this abode to the next; in Kalam, it is an accident (ʿaraḍ) terminating the soul's tenure in the body, not annihilation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for physical death; metaphorical for \"dead land\" revived by rain—here, the literal sense of death as journey's end is extended by the author's simile of travel.\n- Root: لهم (l-h-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Root meaning to swallow or devour; lahma is a morsel.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ilham is God's casting of a meaning into the heart (al-rawʿ)—distinct from human instruction; relevant to the parent's inspired resignation and supplication.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for physical swallowing; metaphorical for the heart's receptivity to divine inspiration—here, the parent's heart is opened to patience through ilham.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Focused Predication (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — governing the exclusive phrasing in \"إنما ذكر السقط تنبيها بالأدنى على الأعلى\" (he only mentioned the stillborn as an intimation from the lesser to the greater), where innamā restricts the mention to a specific purpose.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Afterlife"}, {"arabic": "الجنة اغزوا في سبيل الله فانه من قاتل في سبيل الله فواق ناقة أدخله الله الجنة (5) واحتجوا بما روى معاذ بن جبل أنه صلى الله عليه وسلم قال ان الشيطان ذئب الإنسان كذئب الغنم يأخذ القاصية والناحية والشاردة وإياكم والشعاب وعليكم بالعامة والجماعة والمساجد (6) وهذا إنما أراد به من اعتزل قبل تمام الغلم وسيأتي بيان ذلك وأن ذلك ينهى عنه إلا لضرورة", "english": "They (the advocates of total seclusion) argued by His saying, the Exalted: {«And be not like those who became divided and differed»} (3:105) — to the end of the verse. And by His saying, the Exalted: {«And He brought their hearts together»} (8:63) — He bestowed a favor upon mankind through the cause that unites. But this is weak, because what is meant thereby is the divergence of opinions and the disagreement of schools of thought concerning the meanings of the Book of God and the foundations of the Sacred Law. And what is meant by \"bringing together\" (al-ulfah) is the removal of rancors (al-ghawāʾil) from the breasts — those being the causes that provoke sedition and stir up contentions. And seclusion does not contradict that.", "book_title": "Etiquette of Seclusion", "section_title_ar": "Section 444", "section_title_en": "Section 444 — Refutation of the Isolationists' Proof-Texts from the Qur'an and Sunnah", "section_index": 444, "anchors": "- Root: هجر (hajara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To abandon, forsake, or sever relations — whether by body, tongue, or heart; the root carries the sense of definitive separation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الهجران is the separation of one from another — by body, by tongue, or by heart. The Quranic usages distinguish between physical abandonment (as in {«واهجروهن في المضاجع»}), verbal/mental rejection (as in {«اتخذوا هذا القرآن مهجورا»}), and the comprehensive sense in {«واهجرهم هجرا جميلا»} — a noble forsaking that avoids harm.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): physical separation and cutting off of contact. Metaphorical (Majaz): the she-camel that refuses the stallion (هجر الفحل) — a cessation of a natural function, applied here to the cessation of social intercourse.\n- Root: عله (ʿaliha)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be bewildered or distracted; related to being occupied away from something. (Contextual note: the root appears in the hadith materials concerning the Prophet's withdrawal from his wives.)\n- Root: ضعف (ḍaʿufa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Weakness, feebleness — whether in body, intellect, or resolve.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الضعف is the opposite of strength; it may be in the soul, the body, or one's condition. Al-Khalil distinguished: الضعف (with dammah) is bodily weakness, while الضعف (with fatḥah) is weakness in intellect and judgment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical or moral weakness. Metaphorical: \"in the folds of the book\" (في أضعاف الكتاب) — hidden within the inner recesses, as the isolationists' arguments are weak in their inner structure.\n- Root: خلط (khalata)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To mix, blend, or intermingle — whether liquids, solids, or persons in society.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الخلط is the joining of parts of two or more things; it is more general than al-mazj (fusion). Applied to persons, it denotes social mixing, partnership, and companionship.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: mixing substances. Metaphorical: social intercourse and companionship (خالطت فلانا) — the very thing the isolationist abandons, yet which the believer of good character can undertake without harm.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction and Specification) — When a general prohibition (like the hadith against hajr beyond three days) is followed by specific exceptions (the two cases of perceived benefit or safety), the general is carried (maḥmūl) upon the specific, per the established principle of takhṣīṣ al-ʿāmm.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Etiquette of Seclusion'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "وذلك لما سئل أبو موسى عن رجل قاتل في سبيل الله فقتل فقال هو في الجنة وكان أمير الكوفة فقام ابن مسعود فقال أعده على الأمير فلعله لم يفهم فأعادوا عليه فأعاد الجواب فقال ابن مسعود وأنا أقول إن قتل فأصاب الحق فهو في الجنة فقال أبو موسى الحق ما قال وهكذا يكون إنصاف طلب الحق ولو ذكر مثل هذا الآن لأقل فقيه لأنكره واستبعده وقال لا يحتاج إلى أن يقال أصاب الحق فإن ذلك معلوم لكل أحد فانظر إلى مناظري زمانك اليوم كيف يسود وجه أحدهم إذا اتضح الحق على لسان خصمه وكيف يخجل به وكيف يجهد في مجاحدته بأقصى قدرته وكي", "english": "The fourth [etiquette]: that he should not dispute except over a matter that has actually occurred, or that is likely to occur in most cases. For the Companions (may God be pleased with them) did not consult one another except over newly arising incidents or what was likely to occur, such as the laws of inheritance (farāʾiḍ). And we do not see the disputants concerning themselves with critiquing questions that are of general affliction (ʿumūm al-balawā) in issuing verdicts about them; rather, they seek out the \"drum-beat\" questions (ṭubūliyyāt)—those that make noise—so that the field of argumentation widens in them, however the matter may be. PageV01P043", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "section_title_ar": "Section 37", "section_title_en": "Section 37 — On the Etiquette of Disputation and the Pursuit of Truth", "section_index": 37, "anchors": "- Root: نظر (naẓara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of gazing, examining, and deliberating with the eye of the intellect.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance of speculative reflection (naẓar) as the means to attain knowledge of the unseen and the divine.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) denotes physical sight; metaphorical (majāz) denotes intellectual consideration and weighing of evidence.\n- Root: حق (ḥaqqa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes firmness, reality, and that which is established and undeniable.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance of truth (ḥaqq) as the eternal reality of God and His commands, opposed to falsehood (bāṭil).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal denotes the opposite of falsehood; metaphorical denotes the due right and the correct ruling in a given case.\n- Root: خصم (khaṣama)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes the act of contending, disputing, and overcoming in argument.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance of the adversary (khaṣm) in debate, whose refutation is sought for the sake of truth, not vanity.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal denotes the opponent in a legal contest; metaphorical denotes the one who contends against the truth with fallacious reasoning.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Restriction and Focused Predication (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — Governing the exclusive emphasis on truth-seeking over partisan victory, as in the phrase \"the object is truth alone.\"", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Insight"}], "75": [{"arabic": "الدوام ربنا أخرجنا من هذه القرية الظالم أهلها وذلك لأن الظلم إذا عم نزل البلاء ودمر الجميع وشمل المطيعين قال الله تعالى واتقوا فتنة لا تصيبن الذين ظلموا منكم خاصة فإذن ليس في شيء من أسباب نقص الدين البتة رضا مطلق إلا من حيث إضافتها إلى فعل الله تعالى فأما هي في نفسها فلا وجه للرضا بها بحال وقد اختلف العلماء في الأفضل من أهل المقامات الثلاث رجل يحب الموت شوقا إلى لقاء الله تعالى ورجل يحب البقاء لخدمة المولى ورجل قال لا أختار شيئا بل أرضى بما اختاره الله تعالى ورفعت هذه المسألة إلى بعض العار", "english": "The righteous predecessors (al-salaf al-ṣāliḥ) never ceased to practice this, until a group agreed upon disparaging Baghdad, declaring that openly, and seeking flight from it. Ibn al-Mubārak said: \"I have traversed the East and the West, and I have seen no town worse than Baghdad.\" It was said: \"How so?\" He said: \"It is a town in which the blessing of God is belittled and the disobedience of God is deemed insignificant.\" And when he arrived in Khurāsān, it was said to him: \"How did you find Baghdad?\" He said: \"I saw in it nothing but an angry policeman, a distressed merchant, or a bewildered Qurʾān-reader.\" And you should not suppose that this constitutes backbiting (ghībah), for he did not single out any specific person such that that person would be harmed by it; rather, he intended thereby to warn the people. And he would depart to Mecca—and his stay in Baghdad, awaiting the caravan's preparation, was sixteen days—so he would give in charity sixteen dīnārs, one dīnār for each day, as an expiation (kaffārah) for his stay.", "book_title": "Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment", "section_title_ar": "Section 1198", "section_title_en": "On the True Meaning of Fleeing from Lands of Sin and the Virtue of Patience with Divine Decree", "section_index": 1198, "anchors": "- Root: ط ع و (ṭ-ʿ-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of obedience and, by extension, transgression (maʿṣiyah) as its negation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Maʿṣiyah is the violation of a command; its \"madhammah\" (blameworthiness) is intrinsic to the act itself, not to the mere location where it occurs.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Distinguishes literal \"flight\" (firār) from a physical plague-zone versus metaphorical \"flight\" from moral corruption—the latter being a flight *toward* obedience, not away from decree.\n- Root: ق ض ي (q-ḍ-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Qaḍāʾ as divine decree and judgment; the root implies finality and execution of a command.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Qaḍāʾ is God's eternal ordainment; fleeing from it is impossible, but fleeing *within* it from a cause of destruction is itself an act of obedience to the decree.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal sense is \"to settle/decide\"; metaphorical sense here: the Prophet's (ﷺ) prohibition of leaving a plague-town is a *legal* cause (ʿillah), not a theological impossibility.\n- Root: ر ض ي (r-ḍ-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Riḍā as contentment and approval; its opposite is sakhaṭ (displeasure).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Riḍā with divine decree is a station of the veracious (ṣiddīqīn); it does not negate lawful aversion to sin or the duty to emigrate from oppression.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: satisfaction with what is given. Metaphorical: the soul's stillness (ṭumaʾnīnah) with God's choice—yet this is only praiseworthy when attached to God's act, not to the sin itself.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bāb al-Ḥaṣr wa al-Taqyīd bi-innamā (Restriction and Specification with \"innamā\") — Used to isolate the true cause (ʿillah) of the prophetic prohibition, negating the false analogy of the weak-minded.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'", "badge": "Insight"}], "77": [{"arabic": "تعالى قل متاع الدنيا قليل وإلى تعريف نفاسة الآخرة الإشارة بقوله عز وجل وقال الذين أوتوا العلم ويلكم ثواب الله خير فنبه على أن العلم بنفاسة الجوهر هو المرغب عن عوضه ولما لم يتصور الزهد إلا بمعاوضة ورغبة عن المحبوب في أحب منه قال رجل في دعائه اللهم أرني الدنيا كما تراها فقال له النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم لا تقل هكذا ولكن قل أرني الدنيا كما أريتها الصالحين من عبادك (1) وهذا لأن الله تعالى يراها حقيرة كما هي وكل مخلوق فهو بالإضافة إلى جلاله حقيرة والعبد يراها حقيرة في نفسه بالإضا", "english": "...better and more enduring. Yet one may know this while being unable to abandon this world — either due to the weakness of one's knowledge and certainty, or due to the dominance of lust prevailing over him in the present moment, making him vanquished in the hand of Satan, or due to his being deluded by Satan's promises of procrastination, day after day, until death snatches him away and nothing remains with him but regret after the loss. And to the recognition of the baseness of this world points His saying, exalted: {قُلْ مَتَاعُ الدُّنْيَا قَلِيلٌ} — \"Say: the enjoyment of this world is little\" (Q. 3:196). And to the recognition of the preciousness of the Hereafter points His saying, mighty and majestic: {وَقَالَ الَّذِينَ أُوتُوا الْعِلْمَ وَيْلَكُمْ ثَوَابُ اللَّهِ خَيْرٌ} — \"And those who were given knowledge said: Woe to you! The reward of God is better\" (Q. 28:80). Thus He alerted that it is knowledge of the preciousness of the jewel that turns one away from its substitute. And since zuhd is inconceivable except through an exchange and a turning away from the beloved toward one more beloved, a man said in his supplication: \"O God, show me this world as You see it.\" The Prophet, peace be upon him, said to him: \"Do not say that, but say: Show me this world as You have shown it to the righteous among Your servants.\" This is because God, exalted, sees it as contemptible as it truly is — and every creature, in relation to His majesty, is contemptible — whereas the servant sees it as contemptible in itself relative to what is better for him. It is inconceivable that the seller of a horse, even if he turns away from his horse, should see it as he sees, say, the vermin of the earth, for he has no need whatsoever of vermin, while he is not free of need for the horse. God, exalted, is rich by His essence from all besides Him, so He sees all things at a single degree relative to His majesty, while He sees them as differentiated relative to what is other than Him. The ascetic (al-zāhid) is the one who sees their differentiation relative to himself, not relative to another.", "book_title": "Poverty and Abstinence", "section_title_ar": "Section 1089", "section_title_en": "On the Reality of Zuhd (Ascetic Renunciation) and Its Conditions — Section 1089", "section_index": 1089, "anchors": "- Root: دنا (d-n-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Proximity, nearness; \"adnā\" as lesser/lower in rank or worth.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Adnā\" denotes what is inferior or meaner, contrasted with \"khayr\" (the better), as in {أَتَسْتَبْدِلُونَ الَّذِي هُوَ أَدْنَىٰ بِالَّذِي هُوَ خَيْرٌ} (Q. 2:61).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqiqah) sense of lowliness and despicableness; metaphorical (majaz) extension to moral baseness.\n- Root: زهد (z-h-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To abstain, to regard as little; also \"zahada al-nakhl\" — to estimate fruit yield (connoting measured appraisal).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-zāhid fī al-shay': al-rāghib 'anhu\" — the one who turns away from a thing, content with little of it; Q. 12:20 {وَكَانُوا فِيهِ مِنَ الزَّاهِدِينَ}.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: renunciation of a thing, paucity of desire. Metaphorical: \"zahīd al-'ayn\" — one satisfied with little; \"wādin zahīd\" — a valley taking little water. Zuhd is not mere abandonment but a valuation-based exchange.\n- Root: ترك (t-r-k)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To leave, abandon, relinquish; \"tarkah\" — inheritance left behind.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Abandonment by choice and intention, or by compulsion; \"taraktu fulānan wahīdan\" — I left him alone, i.e., in a resultant state.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"taraka al-shay'\" — to leave a thing; \"tārakahu al-bay'\" — mutual abandonment of a sale. The term implies a deliberate act with a resultant condition.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and specification via \"innamā\" (الحصر والتقييد) — used to delimit the true definition of zuhd against mere relinquishment, and to distinguish the act of the ascetic from the mere spender.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Poverty and Abstinence'", "badge": "Du'a"}, {"arabic": "الصلاة التي هي أعلى الأعلام فقال تعالى {وأقيموا الصلاة وآتوا الزكاة} وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم بني الإسلام على خمس شهادة أن لا إله إلا الله وأن محمدا عبده ورسوله وإقام الصلاة وإيتاء الزكاة (2) وشدد الوعيد على المقصرين فيها فقال {والذين يكنزون الذهب والفضة ولا ينفقونها في سبيل الله فبشرهم بعذاب أليم} ومعنى الإنفاق في سبيل الله إخراج حق الزكاة قال الأحنف بن قيس كنت في نفر من قريش فمر أبو ذر فقال بشر الكانزين بكي في ظهورهم يخرج من جنوبهم وبكي في أقفائهم يخرج من جباههم وفي رواية أنه يوضع على حلمة ثدي أحده", "english": "To proceed: God Most High made alms (al-zakāh) one of the pillars of Islam, and joined its mention with prayer (al-ṣalāh), which is the loftiest of symbols. He said Most High: {«وَأَقِيمُوا الصَّلَاةَ وَآتُوا الزَّكَاةَ»} — \"And establish prayer and give alms.\" And he, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"Islam is built upon five: the testimony that there is no god but God and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger, the establishment of prayer, and the giving of alms.\" And He intensified the threat against those who fall short in it, saying: {«وَالَّذِينَ يَكْنِزُونَ الذَّهَبَ وَالْفِضَّةَ وَلَا يُنفِقُونَهَا فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ فَبَشِّرْهُم بِعَذَابٍ أَلِيمٍ»} — \"And those who hoard gold and silver and do not spend them in the way of God — give them tidings of a painful chastisement.\" And the meaning of \"spending in the way of God\" is the payment of the due right of alms (zakāh).", "book_title": "Mysteries of Zakat", "section_title_ar": "Section 167", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Secrets of Alms (Zakat) — Section 167", "section_index": 167, "anchors": "- Root: زكا (Z-K-Y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Zakā al-zar'u yazkū zakā'an: it increased and grew.\" Core meaning: growth, increase, purification.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"The origin of zakāh is the growth that occurs through the blessing of God Most High... and from it is zakāh: what a person pays of God's right to the poor, so named for the hope of blessing in it, or for the purification (tazkiyah) of the soul, i.e., its growth.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah): growth and increase. Metaphorical (majāz): the purification of wealth through payment of the obligatory due.\n- Root: نعم (N-'-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Na'ima 'ayshuhu: his life was pleasant.\" Core meaning: bounty, well-being, grace.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-Ni'mah: the good state... and al-Ni'mah (with kasrah) is used for the genus, said of both little and much.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"Jallat ni'mat Allāh wa na'mā'uh\" — God's grace and bounty are immense. Metaphorical: \"Khāfat na'āmatuhum\" — their ostriches departed (i.e., they fled), showing the root's extended usage.\n- Root: ظهر (Z-H-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Zahara: to become apparent, to be victorious.\" Core meaning: back, outward appearance, manifestation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-Zahr: the back (body part)... and it is used metaphorically for the surface of the earth.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"Rajul muzhir: a man strong of back.\" Metaphorical: \"Qalabtu al-amra zahrān li-baṭn\" — I turned the matter over back and belly (examined it thoroughly).\n- Root: سرر (S-R-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Sarra: to conceal, to keep secret.\" Core meaning: secret, inner self, joy.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-Isrār: the opposite of al-i'lān (declaring)... and al-sirr is the speech concealed in the soul.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"Asarra al-ḥadīth: he concealed the speech.\" Metaphorical: \"Aṭaytuka sirrah: I gave you its choicest part (the secret/core of something).\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bāb al-faṣl bayna al-jār wa al-majrūr (Prepositional Interposition) — The permissibility of interposing elements between a preposition and its object when the intervening clause is complete in itself, as in \"fa'āfa 'alayhim min ni'matihi mā...\" where the prepositional phrase is completed after an intervening relative clause.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Mysteries of Zakat'", "badge": "Du'a"}, {"arabic": "عنها لم يردها ولذلك لما كتب عليهم القتال قالوا ربنا لم كتبت علينا القتال لولا أخرتنا إلى أجل قريب فقال تعالى قل متاع الدنيا قليل أي لستم تريدون البقاء إلا لمتاع الدنيا فظهر عند ذلك الزاهدون وانكشف حال المنافقين أما الزاهدون المحبون لله تعالى فقاتلوا في سبيل الله كأنهم بنيان مرصوص وانتظروا إحدى الحسنيين وكانوا إذا دعوا إلى القتال يستنشقون رائحة الجنة ويبادرون إليه مبادرة الظمآن إلى الماء البارد حرصا على نصرة دين الله", "english": "The third degree — and it is the highest — is that one renounce willingly, and renounce his very renunciation, so that he does not see his asceticism, since he does not see that he has abandoned anything, having come to know that the world is nothing. {PageV04P225} He is thus like one who casts away a potsherd and takes up a jewel — he does not regard that as an exchange, nor does he see himself as having abandoned anything. And the world, in comparison to God Most High and the bliss of the Hereafter, is baser than a potsherd in comparison to a jewel. This then is perfection in asceticism.", "book_title": "Poverty and Abstinence", "section_title_ar": "Section 1092", "section_title_en": "The Degrees of Asceticism (Zuhd) and Its Divisions", "section_index": 1092, "anchors": "- Root: زهد (Z-H-D)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: scarcity, paucity; al-Zuhd as abstemiousness; zāhada fīhi: to turn away from something, deeming it insignificant.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الزاهد في الشيء: الراغب عنه والراضي منه بالزهيد، أي: القليل\" — the ascetic is one who turns away from a thing, content with little of it; theological sense: renunciation of the world as a deliberate act of valuation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"زهد في الشيء: رغب عنه\" — turning away. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"رجل زهيد: قليل الخير\" — one of little good; \"اد زهيد: قليل الأخذ للماء\" — a valley taking little water. Here, al-Ghazali employs the literal sense of deliberate aversion, extended metaphorically to the soul's turning from all worldly appetites.\n- Root: دنا (D-N-W)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Proximity, nearness; al-dunyā (the lower world) named for its nearness to us or its lowness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الدنو: القرب بالذات، أو بالحكم\" — nearness by essence or by judgment; al-dunyā contrasted with al-ākhirah as the inferior and nearer realm.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: proximity in place or rank. Metaphorical: the world as \"lower\" (adnā) in value — the sense operative in al-Ghazali's comparison of the world to a potsherd.\n- Root: درج (D-R-J)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To walk step by step; to pass away (daraja: died, passed on); degree/stair.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الدرجة نحو المنزلة، لكن يقال للمنزلة: درجة إذا اعتبرت بالصعود\" — a station considered as ascent; used for ranks of honor.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"درج قرن بعد قرن\" — generations passed away; \"رقي في الدرجة\" — ascended in rank. Metaphorical: \"لفلان درجة رفيعة\" — one has a lofty degree. Al-Ghazali uses darajāt as ascending ranks of spiritual attainment.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction and Specification) — the repeated use of \"إنما\" (only/but) in the Quranic citations (e.g., {إنما الحياة الدنيا لعب ولهو}) functions as restrictive predication (qasr), confining the reality of the world to play and diversion — a syntactic key to al-Ghazali's argument that all worldly goods reduce ultimately to one thing: caprice (hawā).", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Poverty and Abstinence'", "badge": "Insight"}], "78": [{"arabic": "وقوله عز وجل فمال هؤلاء القوم لا يكادون يفقهون حديثا ما أصابك من حسنة فمن الله وما أصابك من سيئة فمن نفسك معناه لا يفقهون حديثا يقولون ما أصابك من حسنة فمن الله فإن لم يرد هذا كان مناقضا لقوله قل كل من عند الله وسبق إلى الفهم منه مذهب القدرية ومنها المنقول المنقلب كقوله تعالى وطور سينين أي طور سيناء سلام على آل ياسين أي على الياس وقيل إدريس لأن في حرف ابن مسعود سلام على إدراسين ومنها المكرر القاطع لوصل الكلام في الظاهر كقوله عز وجل وما يتبع الذين يدعون من دون الله شركاء إن يتبعون إلا الظن وقوله عز وجل قال الملأ الذين استكبروا من قومه ل", "english": "I say: The Prophet (peace be upon him) was asked about the means of deliverance from these trials, and he said: \"The Book of Allah — act by what is in it, for it is that which delivers one from them.\" The questioner repeated this three times, and he (peace be upon him) said three times: \"Learn the Book of Allah, Mighty and Glorious, and act by what is in it, for in it is deliverance.\"", "book_title": "Etiquette of Quran Recitation", "section_title_ar": "Section 233", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Etiquette of Recitation — Section 233: On Understanding the Meanings of the Qur'an and the Limits of Interpretation", "section_index": 233, "anchors": "- Root: ف-ه-م (f-h-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الفهم: هيئة للإنسان بها يتحقق معاني ما يحسن\" — comprehension as an inherent faculty by which one ascertains meanings\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الفهم: هيئة للإنسان بها يتحقق معاني ما يحسن... وأفهمته: إذا قلت له حتى تصوره\" — comprehension is a cognitive disposition; distinction between understanding (fahm) and making understood (ifhām)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"تقول: من لم يؤت من سوء الفهم أتى من سوء الإفهام\" — literal usage; failure of understanding often stems from failure of communication\n- Root: ف-س-ر (f-s-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"وكل شيء يعرف به تفسير الشيء ومعناه، فهو تَفْسِرَتُه\" — that by which a thing's meaning is made known\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"التفسير قد يقال فيما يختص بمفردات الألفاظ وغريبها، وفيما يختص بالتأويل\" — tafsīr concerns lexical surface; taʾwīl concerns deeper meaning\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"هذا كلام يحتاج إلى فسر وتفسير\" — literal: exposition of what is obscure\n- Root: و-ج-ل (w-j-l)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; used of hearts trembling at divine mention\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"لو واجلت فلانا لوجلته: لغلبته في الوجل\" — literal sense of fear; metaphorical extension to overpowering in fear\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — the particle innamā restricts predication, essential for parsing the Prophet's saying \"innamā al-aʿmālu bi-l-niyyāt\" and analogous restrictive formulations in the ḥadīth cited herein", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Etiquette of Quran Recitation'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "أصابك من حسنة فمن الله فإن لم يرد هذا كان مناقضا لقوله قل كل من عند الله وسبق إلى الفهم منه مذهب القدرية ومنها المنقول المنقلب كقوله تعالى وطور سينين أي طور سيناء سلام على آل ياسين أي على الياس وقيل إدريس لأن في حرف ابن مسعود سلام على إدراسين ومنها المكرر القاطع لوصل الكلام في الظاهر كقوله عز وجل وما يتبع الذين يدعون من دون الله شركاء إن يتبعون إلا الظن وقوله عز وجل قال الملأ الذين استكبروا من قومه للذين استضعفوا لمن آمن منهم معناه الذين استكبروا لمن آمن من الذين استضعفوا ومنها المقدم والمؤخر وهو مظنة ا", "english": "I say: The Prophet (peace be upon him) was asked about the means of deliverance from these trials, and he said: \"The Book of Allah — act by what is in it, for it is that which delivers one from them.\" The questioner repeated this three times, and he (peace be upon him) said three times: \"Learn the Book of Allah, Mighty and Glorious, and act by what is in it, for in it is deliverance.\"", "book_title": "Etiquette of Quran Recitation", "section_title_ar": "Section 233", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Etiquette of Recitation — Section 233: On Understanding the Meanings of the Qur'an and the Limits of Interpretation", "section_index": 233, "anchors": "- Root: ف-ه-م (f-h-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الفهم: هيئة للإنسان بها يتحقق معاني ما يحسن\" — comprehension as an inherent faculty by which one ascertains meanings\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الفهم: هيئة للإنسان بها يتحقق معاني ما يحسن... وأفهمته: إذا قلت له حتى تصوره\" — comprehension is a cognitive disposition; distinction between understanding (fahm) and making understood (ifhām)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"تقول: من لم يؤت من سوء الفهم أتى من سوء الإفهام\" — literal usage; failure of understanding often stems from failure of communication\n- Root: ف-س-ر (f-s-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"وكل شيء يعرف به تفسير الشيء ومعناه، فهو تَفْسِرَتُه\" — that by which a thing's meaning is made known\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"التفسير قد يقال فيما يختص بمفردات الألفاظ وغريبها، وفيما يختص بالتأويل\" — tafsīr concerns lexical surface; taʾwīl concerns deeper meaning\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"هذا كلام يحتاج إلى فسر وتفسير\" — literal: exposition of what is obscure\n- Root: و-ج-ل (w-j-l)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; used of hearts trembling at divine mention\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"لو واجلت فلانا لوجلته: لغلبته في الوجل\" — literal sense of fear; metaphorical extension to overpowering in fear\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — the particle innamā restricts predication, essential for parsing the Prophet's saying \"innamā al-aʿmālu bi-l-niyyāt\" and analogous restrictive formulations in the ḥadīth cited herein", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Etiquette of Quran Recitation'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "79": [{"arabic": "وقوله عز وجل فمال هؤلاء القوم لا يكادون يفقهون حديثا ما أصابك من حسنة فمن الله وما أصابك من سيئة فمن نفسك معناه لا يفقهون حديثا يقولون ما أصابك من حسنة فمن الله فإن لم يرد هذا كان مناقضا لقوله قل كل من عند الله وسبق إلى الفهم منه مذهب القدرية ومنها المنقول المنقلب كقوله تعالى وطور سينين أي طور سيناء سلام على آل ياسين أي على الياس وقيل إدريس لأن في حرف ابن مسعود سلام على إدراسين ومنها المكرر القاطع لوصل الكلام في الظاهر كقوله عز وجل وما يتبع الذين يدعون من دون الله شركاء إن يتبعون إلا الظن وقوله عز وجل قال الم", "english": "I say: The Prophet (peace be upon him) was asked about the means of deliverance from these trials, and he said: \"The Book of Allah — act by what is in it, for it is that which delivers one from them.\" The questioner repeated this three times, and he (peace be upon him) said three times: \"Learn the Book of Allah, Mighty and Glorious, and act by what is in it, for in it is deliverance.\"", "book_title": "Etiquette of Quran Recitation", "section_title_ar": "Section 233", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Etiquette of Recitation — Section 233: On Understanding the Meanings of the Qur'an and the Limits of Interpretation", "section_index": 233, "anchors": "- Root: ف-ه-م (f-h-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الفهم: هيئة للإنسان بها يتحقق معاني ما يحسن\" — comprehension as an inherent faculty by which one ascertains meanings\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الفهم: هيئة للإنسان بها يتحقق معاني ما يحسن... وأفهمته: إذا قلت له حتى تصوره\" — comprehension is a cognitive disposition; distinction between understanding (fahm) and making understood (ifhām)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"تقول: من لم يؤت من سوء الفهم أتى من سوء الإفهام\" — literal usage; failure of understanding often stems from failure of communication\n- Root: ف-س-ر (f-s-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"وكل شيء يعرف به تفسير الشيء ومعناه، فهو تَفْسِرَتُه\" — that by which a thing's meaning is made known\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"التفسير قد يقال فيما يختص بمفردات الألفاظ وغريبها، وفيما يختص بالتأويل\" — tafsīr concerns lexical surface; taʾwīl concerns deeper meaning\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"هذا كلام يحتاج إلى فسر وتفسير\" — literal: exposition of what is obscure\n- Root: و-ج-ل (w-j-l)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; used of hearts trembling at divine mention\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"لو واجلت فلانا لوجلته: لغلبته في الوجل\" — literal sense of fear; metaphorical extension to overpowering in fear\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — the particle innamā restricts predication, essential for parsing the Prophet's saying \"innamā al-aʿmālu bi-l-niyyāt\" and analogous restrictive formulations in the ḥadīth cited herein", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Etiquette of Quran Recitation'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "80": [{"arabic": "فضيلتك عنده أن جعل طاعتك طاعته فقال عز وجل من يطع الرسول فقد أطاع الله بأبي أنت وأمي يا رسول الله لقد بلغ من فضيلتك عنده أن أخبرك بالعفو عنك قبل أن يخبرك بالذنب فقال تعالى عفا الله عنك لم أذنت لهم بأبي أنت وأمي يا رسول الله لقد بلغ من فضيلتك عنده أن بعثك آخر الأنبياء وذكرك في أولهم فقال عز وجل وإذ أخذنا من النبيين ميثاقهم ومنك ومن نوح وإبراهيم الآية بأبي أنت وأمي يا رسول الله لقد بلغ من فضيلتك عنده أن أهل النار يودون أن يكونوا قد أطاعوك وهم بين أطباقها يعذبون يقولون يا ليتنا أطعنا الله وأطعنا الرسولا بأب", "english": "God Most High has said: {«Indeed, God and His angels send blessings upon the Prophet. O you who have believed, send blessings upon him and greet him with a greeting of peace»} (Q. 33:56). And it is related that he, peace be upon him, came one day with the signs of glad tidings visible upon his face, and he, peace be upon him, said: \"Gabriel came to me, peace be upon him, and said: 'Are you not pleased, O Muhammad, that no one from your community sends a single blessing upon you except that I send ten blessings upon him, and no one from your community greets you with peace except that I greet him with peace ten times?'\" (1) And he, peace be upon him, said: \"Whoever sends blessings upon me, the angels send blessings upon him as long as he continues to send blessings upon me — so let him reduce or increase at that.\" (2) And he, peace be upon him, said: \"The people most deserving of me are those who send the most blessings upon me.\" (3) And he, peace be upon him, said: \"It suffices a believer for miserliness that I am mentioned in his presence and he does not send blessings upon me.\" (4) And he, peace be upon him, said: \"Multiply your sending of blessings upon me on the day of Friday.\" (5) And he, peace be upon him, said: \"Whoever sends blessings upon me from my community, ten good deeds are written for him and ten evil deeds are erased from him.\" (6) And he, peace be upon him, said: \"Whoever says upon hearing the call to prayer and the iqāmah: 'O God, Lord of this perfect call and the established prayer, send blessings upon Muhammad, Your servant and Your Messenger, and grant him the means (al-wasīlah) and the excellence (al-faḍīlah) and the lofty rank (al-darajah al-rafīʿah) and the intercession (al-shafāʿah) on the Day of Resurrection' — my intercession becomes permissible for him.\" (7) And the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, said: \"Whoever sends blessings upon me in a book, the angels continue to seek forgiveness for him as long as my name remains in that book.\" (8) And he, peace be upon him, said: \"Indeed, upon the earth there are traveling angels who convey to me the greetings of peace from my community.\" (9) And he, peace be upon him, said: \"No one greets me with peace except that God returns my soul to me until I respond to his greeting.\" (1) And it was said to him: \"O Messenger of God, how do we send blessings upon you?\" He said: \"Say: 'O God, send blessings upon Muhammad, Your servant, and upon his family, his wives, and his descendants, as You sent blessings upon Abraham and the family of Abraham; and bless Muhammad, his wives, and his descendants, as You blessed Abraham and the family of Abraham. Indeed, You are Praiseworthy, Glorious.'\" (2)", "book_title": "On Invocations and Supplications", "section_title_ar": "Section 244", "section_title_en": "Section 244 — On the Virtues of Invoking Blessings upon the Prophet (Ṣalawāt) and the Excellence of His Rank", "section_index": 244, "anchors": "- Root: صلو (Ṣ-L-W)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes prayer, blessing, supplication; al-ṣalāh from God is mercy (raḥmah), from angels is seeking forgiveness (istighfār), from believers is supplication (duʿā').\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-ṣalāh from God is pure mercy and beneficence; from angels it is their petition for forgiveness; from humans it is humble supplication. The divine ṣalāh upon the Prophet is an elevation of his mention and an increase in his honor.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): supplication and bending in worship. Metaphorical (majāz): God's ṣalāh upon the Prophet is His mercy and His exaltation of him before the angels; the believers' ṣalāh is their invocation and reverence.\n- Root: سلم (S-L-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Root denotes safety, peace, deliverance; taslīm is the greeting of peace and submission.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Salām from God is His safeguarding; the greeting of salām upon the Prophet is an acknowledgment of his prophetic rank and a prayer for his perfect safety.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: greeting of peace. Metaphorical: the angels' conveyance of the believers' salām to the Prophet even after his death, indicating the enduring bond.\n- Root: حمد (Ḥ-M-D)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes praise, commendation; maḥmūd is the one praised.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ḥamd is praise for voluntary beneficence; al-ḥamd li-Llāh encompasses all praise due to God's perfect attributes.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to praise for good. Metaphorical: to find praiseworthy (aḥmadtu al-arḍ: I found the land agreeable).\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Interposition (al-faṣl bayna al-jār wa al-majrūr) — The periodic structure of the ḥadīth reports here follows the canonical pattern of embedding quoted speech within narrative frames, maintaining syntactic cohesion across multiple levels of reported discourse.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "83": [{"arabic": "تعالى {ولو ردوه إلى الرسول وإلى أولي الأمر منهم لعلمه الذين يستنبطونه منهم} رد حكمه في الوقائع إلى استنباطهم وألحق رتبتهم برتبة الأنبياء في كشف حكم الله وقيل في قوله تعالى يا بني آدم قد أنزلنا عليكم لباسا يواري سوءاتكم يعني العلم وريشا يعني اليقين ولباس التقوى يعني الحياء وقال عز وجل {ولقد جئناهم بكتاب فصلناه على علم} وقال تعالى {فلنقصن عليهم بعلم} وقال عز وجل {بل هو آيات بينات في صدور الذين أوتوا العلم} وقال تعالى {خلق الإنسان علمه البيان} وإنما ذكر ذلك في معرض الامتنان وأما الأخبار فقال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم", "english": "How He, Glorified and Exalted, began with Himself, then seconded with the angels, and thirded with the people of knowledge — and sufficient for you is this as honor, excellence, splendor, and nobility. And God, Exalted, said: {«يرفع الله الذين آمنوا منكم والذين أوتوا العلم درجات»} — \"God will raise those who have believed among you and those who have been given knowledge, in degrees.\" Ibn ʿAbbās, may God be pleased with them both, said: For the scholars there are degrees above the believers, seven hundred degrees, and between each two degrees is a distance of five hundred years' journey.", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "section_title_ar": "Section 4", "section_title_en": "The Book of Knowledge — Section 4: The Excellence of Knowledge and the Scholars", "section_index": 4, "anchors": "- Root: علم (ʿ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of knowing, perceiving a thing in its true reality; the act of apprehension and judgment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality, divided into apprehension of the essence and judgment of existence or non-existence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage denotes genuine knowing; metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to signs, landmarks, and traces that guide to knowledge.\n- Root: درج (d-r-j)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Degrees, steps, ascending stations; the root denotes gradation and progressive elevation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الدرجة نحو المنزلة، لكن يقال للمنزلة: درجة إذا اعتبرت بالصعود\" — a degree is a station considered by ascent, not horizontal extension.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical steps of ascent. Metaphorical: \"لفلان درجة رفيعة\" — lofty rank; \"امش في مدارج الحق\" — walk in the paths of truth.\n- Root: يوم (y-w-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A day; a period of time from sunrise to sunset, or any duration whatsoever.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اليوم يعبر به عن وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها. وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — the day denotes the time of the sun's rising to its setting, and may denote any period of time whatsoever.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the solar day. Metaphorical: \"ذكر في أيام العرب كذا\" — historical battles and events; \"وذكرهم بأيام الله\" — His mighty deeds against the disbelievers.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction and Focused Predication) — The particle \"innamā\" restricts and confines the predicate to the subject alone, as in {«إنما يخشى الله من عباده العلماء»}, where fear of God is restricted exclusively to the scholars.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "والرابع أنه قال عز وجل لعلمه الذي يستنبطونه منهم فأثبت لأهل العلم استنباطا ومعلوم أنه وراء السماع وجملة ما نقلناه من الآثار في فهم القرآن يناقض هذا الخيال فبطل أن يشترط السماع في التأويل وجاز لكل واحد أن يستنبط من القرآن بقدر فهمه وحد عقله وأما النهي فإنه ينزل على أحد وجهين أحدهما أن يكون له في الشيء رأي وإليه ميل من طبعه وهواه فيتأول القرآن على وفق رأيه وهواه وليحتج على تصحيح غرضه ولو لم يكن له ذلك الرأي والهوى لكان لا يلوح له من القرآن ذلك المعنى وهذا تارة يكون مع العلم كالذي يحتج ببعض آ", "english": "I say: The Prophet (peace be upon him) was asked about the means of deliverance from these trials, and he said: \"The Book of Allah — act by what is in it, for it is that which delivers one from them.\" The questioner repeated this three times, and he (peace be upon him) said three times: \"Learn the Book of Allah, Mighty and Glorious, and act by what is in it, for in it is deliverance.\"", "book_title": "Etiquette of Quran Recitation", "section_title_ar": "Section 233", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Etiquette of Recitation — Section 233: On Understanding the Meanings of the Qur'an and the Limits of Interpretation", "section_index": 233, "anchors": "- Root: ف-ه-م (f-h-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الفهم: هيئة للإنسان بها يتحقق معاني ما يحسن\" — comprehension as an inherent faculty by which one ascertains meanings\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الفهم: هيئة للإنسان بها يتحقق معاني ما يحسن... وأفهمته: إذا قلت له حتى تصوره\" — comprehension is a cognitive disposition; distinction between understanding (fahm) and making understood (ifhām)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"تقول: من لم يؤت من سوء الفهم أتى من سوء الإفهام\" — literal usage; failure of understanding often stems from failure of communication\n- Root: ف-س-ر (f-s-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"وكل شيء يعرف به تفسير الشيء ومعناه، فهو تَفْسِرَتُه\" — that by which a thing's meaning is made known\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"التفسير قد يقال فيما يختص بمفردات الألفاظ وغريبها، وفيما يختص بالتأويل\" — tafsīr concerns lexical surface; taʾwīl concerns deeper meaning\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"هذا كلام يحتاج إلى فسر وتفسير\" — literal: exposition of what is obscure\n- Root: و-ج-ل (w-j-l)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; used of hearts trembling at divine mention\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"لو واجلت فلانا لوجلته: لغلبته في الوجل\" — literal sense of fear; metaphorical extension to overpowering in fear\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — the particle innamā restricts predication, essential for parsing the Prophet's saying \"innamā al-aʿmālu bi-l-niyyāt\" and analogous restrictive formulations in the ḥadīth cited herein", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Etiquette of Quran Recitation'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "86": [{"arabic": "بتحية فحيوا بأحسن منها أو ردوها} وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم والذي نفسي بيده لا تدخلوا الجنة حتى تؤمنوا ولا تؤمنوا حتى تحابوا أفلا أدلكم على عمل إذا عملتموه تحاببتم قالوا بلى يا رسول الله قال أفشوا السلام بينكم (1) وقال أيضا إذا سلم المسلم على المسلم فرد عليه صلت عليه الملائكة سبعين مرة (2) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم إن الملائكة تعجب من المسلم يمر على المسلم ولا يسلم عليه (3) حديث يسلم الراكب على الماشي وإذا سلم من القوم أحد أجزأ عنهم رواه مالك في الموطأ عن زيد بن أسلم مرسلا ولأبي داود من حديث عل", "english": "**First**: That you greet him with peace (*salām*) when you meet him, and respond to his invitation when he invites you, and say \"may Allah have mercy on you\" (*tashmīt*) when he sneezes, and visit him when he is ill, and attend his funeral procession when he dies, and fulfill his oath when he swears by you, and give him sincere counsel when he seeks your advice, and protect him in his absence when he is away from you, and love for him what you love for yourself, and dislike for him what you dislike for yourself. (1)", "book_title": "Condemnation of Anger and Hatred", "section_title_ar": "Section 433", "section_title_en": "The Rights of Brotherhood — Section 433: The Duties of a Muslim Toward His Fellow Muslims", "section_index": 433, "anchors": "- Root: سلم (s-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of safety, security, freedom from harm; the root from which *islām* (submission/surrender) and *salām* (peace) derive\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): *Salām* as the theological state of being secure from harm — both giving and receiving safety; *islām* as the total surrender of the heart to Allah\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (*ḥaqīqah*): physical safety from tongue and hand; Metaphorical (*majāz*): the comprehensive spiritual safety that defines true Muslim brotherhood\n- Root: عون (ʿ-w-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Assistance, aid, helping another in need\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Mutual assistance as a divine ordinance among believers\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical aid; Metaphorical: intercession (*shafāʿah*) and advocacy on behalf of others\n- Root: ستر (s-t-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Covering, concealing, veiling; protection from exposure\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): *Sitr* as the divine attribute of concealment — Allah's covering of sins in this world and the next\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: covering a physical defect; Metaphorical: concealing another's faults and sins — a cardinal virtue of brotherhood\n- Root: عذر (ʿ-dh-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Excuse, pardon, acceptance of apology\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): *ʿAfw* (pardon) as the higher station of forgiveness that elevates the forgiver\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: releasing from obligation; Metaphorical: the noble trait of overlooking others' offenses\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The repeated use of restrictive constructions (e.g., \"المسلم من سلم المسلمون\") establishes definitional boundaries through predicate restriction, indicating that the *ḥaqīqah* of faith is manifested through specific observable actions.\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Condemnation of Anger and Hatred'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "87": [{"arabic": "الله تعالى لم ينظر إليها وتقول يوم القيامة يا رب اجعلني لأدنى أوليائك اليوم نصيبا فيقول اسكتي يا لا شيء إني لم أرضك لهم في الدنيا أرضاك لهم اليوم (1) وروي في أخبار آدم عليه السلام أنه لما أكل من الشجرة تحركت معدته لخروج الثقل ولم يكن ذلك مجعولا في شيء من أطعمة الجنة إلا في هذه الشجرة فلذلك نهيا عن أكلها قال فجعل يدور في الجنة فأمر الله تعالى ملكا يخاطبه فقال له قل له أي شيء تريد قال آدم أريد أن أضع ما في بطني من الأذى فقيل للملك قل له في أي مكان تريد أن تضعه أعلى الفرش أم على السرر أم على ا", "english": "It has been related that the Messenger of God — may God's peace and blessings be upon him — passed by a dead sheep and said: \"Do you see this sheep as contemptible to its owners?\" They said: \"It was due to its contemptibleness that they cast it away.\" He said: \"By Him in whose hand is my soul, this world is more contemptible to God than this sheep is to its owners. And had this world weighed in God's sight the wing of a gnat, He would not have given a disbeliever from it a single drink of water.\" {«الدنيا سجن المؤمن وجنة الكافر»} (1)", "book_title": "Condemnation of the World", "section_title_ar": "Section 730", "section_title_en": "The Verses and Reports Concerning the Blame of This World (Dhamm al-Dunyā)", "section_index": 730, "anchors": "- Root: د ن ا (D-N-A)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The near, the lower, that which is immediate and proximate; the worldly abode as that which is \"closest\" in contrast to the Hereafter which is \"farthest.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الدنو: القرب بالذات، أو بالحكم\" — proximity by essence or by judgment; the dunyā is so named for its temporal nearness, its inferior rank, and its being the \"lesser\" (adnā) in contrast to the greater and abiding.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): the nearest and lowest abode. Metaphorical (Majaz): the life of this world as a \"dung-hill\" (mazbalah) and a carcass — its worthlessness rendered sensibly perceptible.\n- Root: ر س ل (R-S-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sending forth with gentleness and ease; a messenger (rasūl) as one dispatched.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل الرسل: الانبعاث على التؤدة\" — the root is emission upon measured deliberation; the prophetic mission (risālah) as the sending of guidance to turn creation away from the dunyā toward the ākhirah.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: dispatch of a messenger with a message. Metaphorical: \"أرسل الله عليهم العذاب\" — the sending of punishment; here, the prophets were sent with the sole purpose of diverting hearts from the world.\n- Root: ح د ث (H-D-TH)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Origination after non-existence; that which is new and temporal.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحدوث: كون الشيء بعد أن لم يكن\" — the world as muḥdath (originated/temporal), hence inherently perishable and contemptible before the Eternal.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: new occurrence. Metaphorical: \"صاروا أحاديث\" — becoming mere tales; the dunyā's ornaments become \"rotten rags\" and \"withered bones.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The prophetic reports employ restrictive constructions (e.g., \"The world is but a prison of the believer\") to confine the dunyā's essence to a single defining attribute, negating all else.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of the World'", "badge": "Insight"}], "89": [{"arabic": "حسد وقال عز وجل {ودوا لو تكفرون كما كفروا فتكونون سواء} وذكر الله تعالى حسد إخوة يوسف عليه السلام وعبر عما في قلوبهم بقوله تعالى {إذ قالوا ليوسف وأخوه أحب إلى أبينا منا ونحن عصبة إن أبانا لفي ضلال مبين اقتلوا يوسف أو اطرحوه أرضا يخل لكم وجه أبيكم} فلما كرهوا حب أبيهم له وساءهم ذلك وأحبوا زواله عنه غيبوه عنه وقال تعالى {ولا يجدون في صدورهم حاجة مما أوتوا} أي لا تضيق صدورهم به ولا يغتمون فأثنى عليهم بعدم الحسد وقال تعالى في معرض الإنكار {أم يحسدون الناس على ما آتاهم الله من فضله} وقال تعالى {كان الناس أمة", "english": "The Prophet ﷺ said: «إن المؤمن يغبط والمنافق يحسد» — \"The believer aspires emulously, and the hypocrite envies.\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of Envy", "section_title_ar": "Section 720", "section_title_en": "On the Reality of Envy (Ḥasad) and Its Degrees — Section 720", "section_index": 720, "anchors": "- Root: نعم (n-ʿ-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of \"bounty, benefaction, grace\" — that state of goodness and well-being bestowed.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النعمة: الحالة الحسنة\" — bounty is the excellent state/condition; it is the genus term applying to both little and much, as in {«وإن تعدوا نعمة الله لا تحصوها»} [Ibrahim: 34].\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"جلت نعمة الله ونعماؤه\" — bounty as actual bestowed good. Metaphorical (majāz): \"خفت نعامتهم\" — departure/removal, relevant to the envier's wish for the bounty's removal.\n- Root: حسد (ḥ-s-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Envy — the desire for removal of another's good.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحسد: تمني زوال نعمة من مستحق لها\" — envy is wishing the removal of bounty from one who deserves it, sometimes accompanied by striving to effect its removal. Cited: «المؤمن يغبط والمنافق يحسد».\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"حسده على نعمة الله\" — envying someone for God's bounty. \"إن الحسد يأكل الجسد\" — envy consumes the body.\n- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self; also value, preciousness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): النفس: الروح — the soul/spirit; also الذات (essence/self).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"شيء نفيس ومنفس\" — a precious thing. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — poured out his self, i.e., his blood. From this root derives المنافسة (emulative competition) — the striving for precious things.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Specification) — The definition of ḥasad is given as a restricted predication (ḥadd), delimiting its essential meaning: \"كراهة النعمة وحب زوالها عن المنعم عليه\" — the hatred of bounty and love of its removal from the one upon whom it is bestowed. This is a formal definition (ḥadd), not merely a lexical gloss.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Envy'", "badge": "Insight"}], "93": [{"arabic": "آية ذكر الله عز وجل العمل الصالح فيها مقرونا بالإيمان وقوله تعالى ومن يقتل مؤمنا متعمدا فجزاؤه جهنم خالدا فيها وهذه العمومات أيضا مخصوصة بدليل قوله تعالى ويغفر ما دون ذلك لمن يشاء فينبغي أن تبقى له مشيئة في مغفرة ما سوى الشرك وكذلك قوله صلى الله عليه وسلم يخرج من النار من كان في قلبه مثقال ذرة من إيمان وقوله تعالى إنا لا نضيع أجر من أحسن عملا وقوله تعالى إن الله لا يضيع أجر المحسنين فكيف يضيع أجر أصل الإيمان وجميع الطاعات بمعصية واحدة وقوله تعالى ومن يقتل مؤمنا متعمدا أي لإيمانه وقد ورد على مثل هذا السبب فإن قلت فقد مال الاختيار إلى أن ا", "english": "He was asked, \"Which deed is most excellent?\" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Islam.\" It was then asked, \"Which aspect of Islam is most excellent?\" He (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Faith (Īmān).\" This is evidence for both the distinction (ikhtilāf) between the two terms and their overlap (tadākhul). This usage is the most consistent with the conventions of the language, because faith is a deed among deeds, and it is the most excellent of them. Islam is submission (taslīm), whether by the heart, by the tongue, or by the limbs. The most excellent of these is that which is by the heart, which is the assent (taṣdīq) that is called faith. Using the two terms in a way that indicates distinction, overlap, or even complete synonymy (tarāduf) is all within the bounds of metaphorical usage (tajawwuz) in the language.", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 99", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 99: On the Distinction, Overlap, and Synonymy of Faith (Īmān) and Submission (Islām), and Their Worldly and Otherworldly Judgments", "section_index": 99, "anchors": "- Root: ايم (A-Y-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of remaining unmarried, or a state of being without a spouse; extended metaphorically to describe one who is solitary or without support.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأيامى: جمع أيم، وهي المرأة التي لا بعل لها... والحرب مأيمة، أي: يفرق بين الزوج والزوجة.\" The root carries the sense of separation or a state of lacking union, which informs the theological discussion of what constitutes the essential bond of faith.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes the state of being unmarried; metaphorical (Majaz) usage extends to anything that separates or leaves one bereft. This distinction between literal and metaphorical application is central to the chapter's method.\n- Root: قلب (Q-L-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn, overturn, or transform a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه... والانقلاب: الانصراف.\" The heart (qalb) is so named for its constant fluctuation and turning. In theological terms, it is the seat of taṣdīq (assent), the immaterial faculty of cognition and conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is physical turning; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"قلب الأمور\" — to deliberate, scrutinize, and manage affairs. This supports the distinction between the heart's internal act of assent and the external acts of the limbs.\n- Root: عمل (ʿ-A-M-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Any deed or action performed with purpose and intent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العمل: كل فعل يكون من الحيوان بقصد، فهو أخص من الفعل.\" Work is more specific than mere action, as it implies deliberate, intentional agency. It is used for both righteous and sinful deeds, and is the external manifestation of internal conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage refers to physical labor or deeds. The chapter explores whether \"work\" is an integral part of the reality of faith or a separate, consequent requirement.\n- Root: صدق (S-D-Q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be truthful, to speak the truth, to verify.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصدق والكذب أصلهما في القول... ولا يكونان بالقصد الأول إلا في القول.\" Truth and falsehood are primarily properties of speech and reports. Taṣdīq (assent) is the heart's affirmation of the truth of a report, which is the core reality of Īmān.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is truthful speech; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"صادق الحملة\" — one who is true and firm in battle or resolve. This shows that taṣdīq is not merely verbal but a firm, internal resolution.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication). This rule governs how restriction (ḥaṣr) is achieved in Arabic, often through particles like \"innamā\" or through contextual specification (takhṣīṣ). The chapter's core argument hinges on how terms like Īmān and Islām are semantically restricted, expanded, or made synonymous through contextual usage (majāz), requiring precise parsing of the predicate and its scope.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Insight"}], "94": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى {يا أيها الذين آمنوا إذا ضربتم في سبيل الله فتبينوا} وقال تعالى {يا أيها الذين آمنوا إن جاءكم فاسق بنبأ فتبينوا} وقال تعالى ولقد خلقنا الإنسان ونعلم ماتوسوس به نفسه ذكر ذلك تحذيرا وتنبيها للاحتراز منه في المستقبل وروى عبادة بن الصامت أنه عليه السلام قال لرجل سأله أن يوصيه ويعظه إذا أردت أمرا فتدبر عاقبته فإن كان رشدا فامضه وإن كان غيا فانته عنه (1) وقال بعض الحكماء إذا أردت أن يكون العقل غالبا للهوى فلا تعمل بقضاءالشهوة حتى تنظر العاقبة فإن مكث الندامة في القلب أكثر من مكث خفة الشهوة وقال لقمان إن المؤمن", "english": "And some have said: \"Grant that the wrongdoer has been pardoned—has he not still missed the reward of the righteous?\"—pointing thereby to the loss and regret. And God Most High says: {«يَوْمَ يَجْمَعُكُمْ لِيَوْمِ الْجَمْعِ ذَٰلِكَ يَوْمُ التَّغَابُنِ»} (\"The Day He will gather you for the Day of Gathering—that is the Day of Mutual Loss\") (Al-Taghābun 64:9). So this is his counsel to his soul concerning his hours.", "book_title": "Monitoring and Accounting", "section_title_ar": "Section 1232", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 1232: The Soul's Covenant with Itself and Reckoning Before Deeds", "section_index": 1232, "anchors": "- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, or spirit; that which breathes and animates; the essence of a thing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-nafs denotes the rūḥ (spirit) and, in theological contexts, the very dhāt (essence/self) of a being—as in {«وَيُحَذِّرُكُمُ اللَّهُ نَفْسَهُ»} (Āl ʿImrān 3:28), where \"His self\" means His essence, not a separate entity.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): the soul as the seat of desire and action. Metaphorical (majāz): \"دفق نفسه\" (he poured out his self = his blood); the soul as a recalcitrant entity requiring discipline.\n- Root: يوم (y-w-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A day, from sunrise to sunset; also any span of time, especially the Day of Reckoning.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Yawm signifies the period of the sun's course, but metaphorically extends to any era—most critically \"the Day of Gathering\" (yawm al-jamʿ) and \"the Day of Mutual Loss\" (yawm al-taghābun), where God's attribution of the day to Himself signals its supreme gravity.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a 24-hour cycle. Metaphorical: \"أيام الله\" (the days of God) = His punitive acts against the disbelievers, as in {«وَذَكِّرْهُمْ بِأَيَّامِ اللَّهِ»} (Ibrāhīm 14:5).\n- Root: حسب (ḥ-s-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To count, reckon, or compute; also to suffice.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ḥisāb is the employment of number; the Day of Judgment is the day of reckoning where deeds are weighed. The \"ḥusbān\" (computation) of the sun and moon (Al-Anʿām 6:96) marks time for human accountability.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: counting wealth or deeds. Metaphorical: \"احتسبت ما عند فلان\" (I probed what so-and-so possesses) = testing and examining, as the soul must examine its own intentions and acts before God's scrutiny.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The text uses conditional and imperative constructions (e.g., \"فإياك ثم إياك\" and \"ليشترط على نفسه\") to impose binding covenants, where the predicate restricts the soul's scope of action, demanding a precise, self-imposed legal obligation.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Monitoring and Accounting'", "badge": "Insight"}], "95": [{"arabic": "من أعوان الشهوات فهو من الذين أوتوا العلم وكلا وعد الله الحسنى والذي يقتضي الإيمان بهذا الأمر في القرآن والسنة وأقاويل العلماء أكثر من أن يحصر قال الله تعالى ونهى النفس عن الهوى فإن الجنة هي المأوى وقال تعالى أولئك الذين امتحن الله قلوبهم للتقوى قيل نزع منها محبة الشهوات وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم المؤمن بين خمس شدائد مؤمن يحسده ومنافق يبغضه وكافر يقاتله وشيطان يضله ونفس تنازعه (1) فبين أن النفس عدو منازع يجب عليه مجاهدتها ويروى أن الله تعالى أوحى إلى داود عليه السلام يا داود حذر وأنذر أصحابك أكل الشهوات فإن ال", "english": "But if you are incapable of that, then it behooves you not to let slip the affirmation of truth (taṣdīq) and faith (īmān) by way of receptive acceptance (talqqī) and emulation (taqlīd) of one who deserves to be emulated. For faith has a degree, just as knowledge has a degree; and knowledge is attained after faith, and it lies beyond it. God, the Exalted, said: {«يرفع الله الذين آمنوا منكم والذين أوتوا العلم درجات»} — \"God will raise those who have believed among you and those who have been given knowledge, by degrees.\" So whoever affirms as true that opposing the desires (mukhālafat al-shahawāt) is the road to God, the Mighty and Majestic, yet has not been apprised of its cause and its secret — he is among those who believe. And when he is apprised of what we have mentioned concerning the helpers of the desires, he is among those who have been given knowledge. And to both has God promised the goodly reward.", "book_title": "Breaking the Two Desires", "section_title_ar": "Section 627", "section_title_en": "Section 627 — The Path of Opposition to the Desires: On the Consensus of the Scholars that There Is No Road to the Happiness of the Hereafter Save through Restraining the Soul from Caprice", "section_index": 627, "anchors": "- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, spirit; that which breathes; the animating principle; also blood, and the \"evil eye\" (nafs sā'ilah)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): النفس: الروح — the soul as the spiritual reality; in the verse {«ويحذركم الله نفسه»} it denotes the very Essence (al-dhāt); the soul is that which must be disciplined and opposed in its caprice (hawā)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the self that desires and inclines; Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه\" — he poured out his blood/life; the soul as the internal adversary that must be fought (jihād al-nafs)\n- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The heart; to turn over, to transform, to fluctuate; the heart as that which is constantly turning between states\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and fluctuation between conditions; the seat of spiritual perception and disease\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to turn something over; Metaphorical: the heart as the organ of spiritual understanding that can be veiled (maḥjūbah) from God by attachment to desires\n- Root: دنا (d-n-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be near, proximity; the world (al-dunyā) as that which is nearer/lower\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الدنو: القرب بالذات أو بالحكم — proximity by essence or by judgment; the world as the nearer realm that veils from the distant (al-ākhirah)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: closeness; Metaphorical: the lower world as that which is \"near\" and thus preoccupies the heart from what is higher\n- Root: أكل (a-k-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To eat, consume; also to possess and enjoy\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Eating as the most fundamental desire; the restriction of food (qillat al-ṭa'ām) as the first pillar of spiritual discipline (riyāḍah)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: consumption of food; Metaphorical: \"أكل الشهوات\" — the devouring of desires as that which veils the intellect from God\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The periodic sentence structure in which the author interposes explanatory clauses between the subject and its predicate, as in \"فإنه لو تمتع بشيء منه أنس به وألفه\" where the conditional clause governs the entire following apodosis, requiring careful tracking of the protasis-apodosis relationship through extended digressions.\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Breaking the Two Desires'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "الحضرة كما قال الله تعالى والمجاهدون في سبيل الله بأموالهم وأنفسهم فضل الله المجاهدين بأموالهم وأنفسهم على القاعدين درجة وإن ضيع ثغره وأهمل رعيته ذم أثره فانتقم منه عند الله تعالى فيقال له يوم القيامة يا راعي السوء أكلت اللحم وشربت اللبن ولم تأو الضالة ولم تجبر الكسير اليوم أنتقم منك (1) حديث رجعنا من الجهاد الأصغر إلى الجهاد الأكبر أخرجه البيهقي في الزهد من حديث جابر وقال هذا إسناد في ضعف المثال الثالث مثل العقل مثل فارس متصيد وشهوته كفرسه وغضبه ككلبه فمتى كان الفارس حاذقا وفرسه مروضا وكلبه مؤدبا معلما كان جديرا بالنجاح ومتى كان هو في نفسه أخرق وكان الفرس جموحا والكلب", "english": "But whoever turns away from this path is like the one of whom Allah, the Exalted, said: {أفرأيت من اتخذ إلهه هواه وأضله الله على علم} — \"Have you seen the one who takes as his god his own desire, and Allah has led him astray out of knowledge?\" (Q 45:23). And He, the Exalted, said: {واتبع هواه فمثله كمثل الكلب إن تحمل عليه يلهث أو تتركه يلهث} — \"And he followed his desire, so his example is like the example of a dog: if you drive him away, he pants, or if you leave him, he pants\" (Q 7:176). And He, the Exalted and Glorious, said concerning the one who restrains the soul from passion: {وأما من خاف مقام ربه ونهى النفس عن الهوى فإن الجنة هي المأوى} — \"But as for he who feared the standing before his Lord and restrained the soul from passion, then indeed, Paradise will be the refuge\" (Q 79:40-41).", "book_title": "Disciplining the Soul", "section_title_ar": "Section 577", "section_title_en": "** The Body as Kingdom: The Governance of the Soul's Faculties — Section 577", "section_index": 577, "anchors": "**\n- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, breath; that which is breathed forth; the essence of a thing\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): النفس: الروح — the soul as the immaterial spiritual reality; when attributed to Allah, denotes His very essence (al-dhāt), not a separate entity\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the self/essence; Metaphorical: نفس سائلة — a \"flowing self\" (blood) as the criterion for ritual purity\n- Root: عقل (ʿ-q-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Intellect, reason; that which restrains and binds\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): العقل يقال للقوة المتهيئة لقبول العلم — the faculty prepared for receiving knowledge; and the knowledge acquired through that faculty\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: intellectual apprehension; Metaphorical: نخلة لا تعقل الإبار — a palm that does not \"accept\" pollination (i.e., does not receive)\n- Root: غضب (gh-d-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Anger, wrath; the boiling of the blood of the heart desiring vengeance\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): ثوران دم القلب إرادة الانتقام — when attributed to Allah, it means retribution alone, not an emotional state\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: anger toward the living (غضبت لفلان); Metaphorical: anger toward the dead (غضبت به) — the preposition shift marking the distinction\n- Root: نصح (n-s-ḥ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sincere counsel; to be pure and unadulterated; to sew (as a tailor)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): تحري فعل أو قول فيه صلاح صاحبه — the earnest pursuit of action or speech containing the welfare of its recipient\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sincere advice; Metaphorical: نصح الغيث البلاد — rain \"counseling\" the land (i.e., giving it its due generously)\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب التشبيه التمثيلي (The Chapter of Illustrative Simile) — extended analogical comparisons (tamthīl) where multiple relations are mapped across domains, governed by the syntactic pattern of predicate assignment (isnād) to establish parallel structures\n\n---\n\n**", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Disciplining the Soul'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "بغير رضاهم هل رضي بفعلك المؤلفة قلوبهم والعاملون عليها في أرض الله تعالى والمجاهدون في سبيل الله وابن السبيل أم رضي بذلك حملة القرآن وأهل العلم والآرامل والأيتام أم هل رضي بذلك خلق من رعيتك فشد يا هرون مئزرك وأعد للمسألة جوابا وللبلاء جلبابا واعلم أنك ستقف بين يدي الحكم العدل فقد رزئت في نفسك إذ سلبت حلاوة العلم والزهد ولذيذ القرآن ومجالسة الأخيار ورضيت لنفسك أن تكون ظالما وللظالمين إماما يا هرون قعدت على السرير ولبست الحرير وأسبلت سترا دون بابك وتشبهت بالحجبة برب العالمين ثم أقعدت أجنادك الظلمة دون بابك وسترك يظلمون الناس ولا ينصفون يشربون", "english": "I extended my eyes toward them and said, \"The one praying is Sufyān.\" So I threw the book to him.", "book_title": "Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil", "section_title_ar": "Section 556", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 556: The Epistle of Sufyān al-Thawrī to Hārūn al-Rashīd", "section_index": 556, "anchors": "- Root: ظ-ل-م (Ẓ-L-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is placing something in other than its proper place; darkness (ẓulmah) as its cognate.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ẓulm is the absence of light (nūr), used metaphorically for ignorance, polytheism, and transgression against others' rights.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) — acting unjustly; metaphorical (majāz) — \"a land that has been wronged\" (arḍ maẓlūmah), i.e., never dug or cultivated, indicating the severity of the injustice as a novel and unprecedented act.\n- Root: ك-ت-ب (K-T-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To write, to prescribe, to assemble; a book (kitāb) as a gathered compilation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Writing as the inscription of knowledge; the \"Book of God\" as His speech and decree.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — inscribing letters; metaphorical — the \"writing\" of a decree or obligation.\n- Root: م-س-س (M-S-S)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To touch, to feel, to handle physically.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Physical contact; also used for the touch of harm or madness (mimsah).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — physical touch; metaphorical — the \"touch\" of a calamity or an evil consequence.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Interposition (al-Faṣl bayna al-Jār wa al-Majrūr) — The narrative structure here employs periodic sentences where the subject's actions are sequentially interposed with their objects, creating a dramatic and cumulative effect, as in the description of Sufyān's handling of the letter.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil'", "badge": "Insight"}], "97": [{"arabic": "المحركة ومن لم يفعل هذا فقد كفر نعمة الله في سعة الأرض إذ قال تعالى ألم تكن أرض الله واسعة فتهاجروا فيها الثاني أن يكلف نفسه في أعماله أفعالا تخالف ما اعتاده فيبدل التكلف بالتبذل وزي الحشمة بزي التواضع وكذلك كل هيئة وحال وفعل في مسكن وملبس ومطعم وقيام وقعود كان يعتاده وفاء بمقتضى جاهه فينبغي أن يبدلها بنقائضها حتى يرسخ باعتياد ذلك ضد ما رسخ فيه من قبل باعتياد ضده فلا معنى للمعالجة إلا المضادة الثالث أن يراعى في ذلك التلطف والتدريج فلا ينتقل دفعة واحدة إلى الطرف الأقصى من التبذل فإن الطبع نفور ولا يمكن نقله عن أخلاقه إلا بالتدريج فيترك البعض ويسلي ن", "english": "...anxieties in [worldly] administrations, and likewise all the other means to rank. Then, whenever the means are secured and completed, the lifespan expires — until, when the earth has taken on its ornament and is adorned, and its people suppose that they are able to master it, there comes to it Our command by night or by day, and We make it a harvested stubble as if it had not flourished the day before. Thus God — exalted be He — struck for it a parable, saying — exalted be He: {And strike for them the parable of the life of this world: like water which We send down from the sky, and the vegetation of the earth mingles with it, and then it becomes dry chaff which the winds scatter} {«وَاضْرِبْ لَهُمْ مَثَلَ الْحَيَاةِ الدُّنْيَا كَمَاءٍ أَنْزَلْنَاهُ مِنَ السَّمَاءِ فَاخْتَلَطَ بِهِ نَبَاتُ الْأَرْضِ فَأَصْبَحَ هَشِيمًا تَذْرُوهُ الرِّيَاحُ»} [Al-Kahf: 45]. And asceticism (al-zuhd) in this world — because it was a present sovereignty — Satan envied him for it and barred him from it.", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 971", "section_title_en": "On Renouncing Status and the True Meaning of Sovereignty — The Cure for the Love of Prestige", "section_index": 971, "anchors": "- Root: ملك (malaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes possession, dominion, and the power to command and forbid; al-Farahidi notes its application to kingship and ownership alike.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Distinguishes two modes: (1) actual possession/sovereignty (al-tamalluk wa al-tawallī) and (2) the capacity/power to exercise it (al-quwwah ʿalā dhālika), whether or not one actually rules. True sovereignty belongs to him who governs his own soul.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah): ownership of property and dominion over subjects. Metaphorical (majāz): \"he mastered his anger\" (malaka nafsahu ʿinda al-ghadab) — self-mastery as the paradigmatic figurative extension of kingship.\n- Root: عقد (ʿabada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Servitude and worship; the slave (ʿabd) is one who is owned and subjugated.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): ʿUbūdiyyah is the state of being subjugated to another's command; true slavery to passion is the degradation of the rational soul.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: chattel slavery. Metaphorical: he who is led by his lusts is a slave in reality, even if free in legal form.\n- Root: سخر (sakhkhara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To subjugate, compel, and employ against one's will; used of beasts and forces pressed into service.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Taskhīr is the compelled employment of a thing for another's purpose — the opposite of free, rational agency.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: subjugation of animals. Metaphorical: the man ruled by appetite is musakhkhar — driven like a beast by its halter.\n- Root: دنا (dānā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Nearness in place, time, or rank.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-dunyā (the lower world) is so named for its nearness and lowness relative to the Hereafter; it is the realm of delusion when pursued for its own sake.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: proximity. Metaphorical: the \"nearer\" life as the inferior, contemptible portion.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bāb al-faṣl bayna al-jār wa al-majrūr (Prepositional Interposition) — The periodic sentence structure here interposes lengthy causal and conditional clauses between the governing verb and its complements, requiring the translator to preserve the logical sequence of protasis and apodosis without collapsing the rhetorical tension of the original.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "قوله تعالى إن الذين توفاهم الملائكة ظالمي أنفسهم في قوم من المسلمين كانوا يكثرون جماعة المشركين بالمخالطة وقد روي أن الله تعالى أوحى إلى يوشع ابن نون إني مهلك من قومك أربعين ألفا من خيارهم وستين ألفا من شرارهم فقال ما بال الأخيار قال إنهم لا يغضبون لغضبي فكانوا يؤاكلونهم ويشاربونهم وبهذا يتبين أن بعض الظلمة والغضب لله عليهم واجب وروى ابن مسعود عن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم إن الله لعن علماء بني إسرائيل إذ خالطوا الظالمين في معاشهم (6)", "english": "As for the servants and the retinue, most of their wealth comes from outright usurpation (al-ghaṣb al-ṣarīḥ). There falls into their hands no wealth from legitimate sources — no lawful gain, no inheritance, no poll-tax (jizyah), and no lawful means whatsoever — such that the suspicion might be weakened by the mixing of the lawful with their wealth. Ṭāwūs said: \"I do not bear witness before them even if I am certain [of the truth of my testimony], because I fear their transgression against the one against whom I bear witness.\"", "book_title": "The Lawful and Prohibited", "section_title_ar": "Section 395", "section_title_en": "The Ruling on Dealing with Tyrannical Rulers, Their Judges, and Their Servants", "section_index": 395, "anchors": "- Root: ظلم (Ẓ-L-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is placing something in other than its proper place; darkness (ẓulmah) as its cognate.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ẓulm is transgression against right order; its theological weight is that it is a violation of God's boundaries, and its ultimate consequence is darkness on the Day of Judgment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage denotes wrongful harm and injustice; metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to any act of overstepping, even against oneself.\n- Root: ملك (M-L-K)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Possession, dominion, and sovereign authority.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-Mulk is the power to command and forbid over a polity; its corruption spreads when those in authority are corrupt.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage denotes sovereign rule; metaphorical usage includes mastery over one's own soul or affairs.\n- Root: وقد (W-Q-D)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Fire burning fiercely; fuel for fire.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Used for the fire of punishment and its fuel; metaphorically for intense divine wrath.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage is physical fire; metaphorical usage is the burning of divine anger against wrongdoers.\n- Root: لعن (L-ʿ-N)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To curse, to drive away from mercy, to ostracize.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Divine curse is exclusion from mercy; prophetic curse is a declaration of that exclusion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage is expulsion and distancing; metaphorical usage is the declaration of one's deserving punishment.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to confine a ruling to a specific class, as in the restriction of severe avoidance to tyrants specifically, not to all sinners.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'The Lawful and Prohibited'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "100": [{"arabic": "الله عز وجل وافدا إليه إذ قال جل جلاله {ومن يخرج من بيته مهاجرا إلى الله ورسوله ثم يدركه الموت فقد وقع أجره على الله} وأما دخول البادية إلى الميقات ومشاهدة تلك العقبات فليتذكر فيها ما بين الخروج من الدنيا بالموت إلى ميقات يوم القيامة وما بينهما من الأهوال والمطالبات وليتذكر من هول قطاع الطريق هول سؤال منكر ونكير ومن سباع البوادي عقارب القبر وديدانه وما فيه من الأفاعي والحيات ومن انفراده من أهله وأقاربه وحشة القبر وكربته ووحدته وليكن في هذه المخاوف في أعماله وأقواله متزودا لمخاوف القبر وأما الإحرام والتلبية من الميقات فليعلم أن معناه إجابة نداء الله عز وجل فارج", "english": "As for leaving the town, let him know at that moment that he has parted from his family and homeland, heading towards God, Mighty and Majestic, in a journey that bears no resemblance to the journeys of this world. So let him bring to his heart: what does he intend, and where is he heading, and the visitation of whom does he seek? And that he is heading towards the King of Kings (Malik al-Mulūk) in the company of His visitors — those who were called and responded, who were made to yearn and so yearned, who were roused and so rose up, who cut off attachments and parted from creatures, and advanced towards the House of God, Mighty and Majestic — He who magnified its command, exalted its affair, and raised its standing — seeking consolation through the encounter with the House from the encounter with the Lord of the House, until they are granted the utmost of their hopes and are blessed with the vision of their Master. And let him bring to his heart the hope of arrival and acceptance — not boasting of his deeds in departing and parting from family and wealth — but rather trusting in the bounty of God, Mighty and Majestic, and hoping for the fulfillment of His promise to whoever visits His House. And let him hope that if he does not reach it and death overtakes him on the road, he will meet God, Mighty and Majestic, as one arriving as a visitor (wāfid) to Him. For He, Glorious is His majesty, said: {«وَمَنْ يَخْرُجْ مِنْ بَيْتِهِ مُهَاجِرًا إِلَى اللَّهِ وَرَسُولِهِ ثُمَّ يُدْرِكْهُ الْمَوْتُ فَقَدْ وَقَعَ أَجْرُهُ عَلَى اللَّهِ»} — \"And whoever leaves his house as an emigrant to God and His Messenger, and then death overtakes him, his reward has already fallen due upon God\" {Quran 4:100}.", "book_title": "Mysteries of Hajj", "section_title_ar": "Section 217", "section_title_en": "Section 217 — The Explanation of Inward Acts, the Manner of Sincerity (Ikhlāṣ) in Intention, and the Path of Reflection upon the Witnessed Scenes (of the Pilgrimage)", "section_index": 217, "anchors": "- Root: سفر (s-f-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"السفر: كشف الغطاء\" — uncovering; the journey as a means of uncovering lands and states; \"أسفر الصبح\" — the dawn shone forth.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): السفر is the uncovering of a cover, and is applied to travel as it unveils new horizons and conditions of the soul.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Haqiqah/Literal): \"سافر سفرا بعيدا\" — to travel a far journey; \"مسافر\" — one who travels.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Majaz/Metaphorical): \"سافرت عنه الحمى\" — the fever departed from him; \"أسفرت الحرب\" — the war intensified. Here, the journey to God is a metaphorical \"unveiling\" of the soul's attachment to worldly causes.\n- Root: حرم (ḥ-r-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"المُحِلَّةُ\" — that which makes lawful; \"حرم\" — to forbid, to make sacred.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الحرام is that which is prohibited, whether by divine decree or by human law; the state of iḥrām is a sacred prohibition of worldly adornments.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Haqiqah/Literal): \"وهي له محرم\" — a woman forbidden to him for marriage (a close relative); \"جلد محرم\" — undressed leather.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Majaz/Metaphorical): \"سرى في محارم الليل\" — he traveled in the sacred/forbidding darkness of the night, i.e., its perils. The iḥrām garments are a metaphorical \"prohibition\" of worldly dress, prefiguring the shroud.\n- Root: ورد (w-r-d) [implied in \"وافدا\" and \"الوفود\"]\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الورود is arriving at water; metaphorically, arriving before a king or lord as a visitor (wāfid).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Majaz/Metaphorical): \"وفد على الأمير\" — he came as a delegate to the governor. The pilgrim is a \"wāfid\" (visitor/delegate) to God, arriving at the water of His mercy.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The separation of the prepositional phrase from its governing verb for rhetorical emphasis, as in \"فكيف يحتاط في أسباب السفر المشكوك فيه ويستظهر في زاده وراحلته ويهمل أمر السفر المستيقن\" — the interposition of clauses to contrast the certain journey (death) with the uncertain one (travel).", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Mysteries of Hajj'", "badge": "Afterlife"}, {"arabic": "وقال الأمراء أربعة فأمير قوي ظلف نفسه وعماله فذلك كالمجاهد في سبيل الله يد الله باسطة عليه بالرحمة وأمير فيه ضعف ظلف نفسه وأرتع عماله لضعفه فهو على شفا هلاك إلا أن يرحمه الله وأمير ظلف عماله وأرتع نفسه فذلك الحطمة الذي قال فيه رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم شر الرعاة الحطمة فهو الهالك وحده (4) وأمير أرتع نفسه وعماله فهلكوا جميعا وقد بلغني يا أمير المؤمنين أن جبرائيل عليه السلام أتى النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم فقال أتيتك حين أمر الله بمنافخ النار فوضعت على النار تسعر ليوم القيامة فقال له يا جبريل صف لي النار فقال إن", "english": "He turned to al-Shaʿbī and said: \"O Abā ʿAmr! I am the trustee of the Commander of the Faithful over Iraq and his governor over it, and a man commanded to obedience. I have been tried with the subjects, and their right has become incumbent upon me. So I love to preserve them and to attend to what sets them right, along with sincere counsel (naṣīḥah) to them. And reports may reach me about a band of people from the regions concerning a matter, and I find myself severe against them therein, so I withhold a portion of their stipends and place it in the treasury (bayt al-māl), with the intention in myself that I shall return it to them. Then it reaches the Commander of the Faithful that I have withheld it in that manner, so he writes to me that I must not return it. I cannot then countermand his order, nor can I execute his letter [against my better judgment]. I am but a man commanded to obedience. Is there any liability upon me in this, and in similar matters, where the intention is as I have described?\"", "book_title": "Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil", "section_title_ar": "Section 553", "section_title_en": "The Upright Scholars Before the Tyrannical Rulers — Counsel and Fearless Truth-Telling", "section_index": 553, "anchors": "- Root: نصح (naṣaḥa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is sincerity, faithfulness, and pure counsel free from deceit; the root denotes the act of seeking the welfare of another with complete integrity.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological context, naṣīḥah is the obligation of the sincere advisor to guide the ruler toward divine truth, even at personal peril, as it is a form of worship (ʿibādah) and a trust (amānah).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage denotes pure honey (ʿasal naṣīḥ) free from admixture; metaphorical (majāz) usage applies to counsel that is untainted by flattery or self-interest.\n- Root: سلط (sulṭ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Denotes authority, power, and dominion; the ruler (sulṭān) is one who has been given sway over affairs.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In Kalam, sulṭān is a trial (ibtilāʾ) and a trust (amānah) from God, whose exercise demands justice (ʿadl) and whose neglect invites divine wrath.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage refers to physical power or a governing authority; metaphorical usage extends to the authority of proof (sulṭān al-burhān) that compels assent.\n- Root: عظ (waʿaẓa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Root denotes admonition, exhortation, and reminding of consequences; it implies softening the heart through remembrance of God and the Hereafter.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological terms, ʿiẓah is the act of turning the soul away from transgression through a blend of fear (tarhīb) and hope (targhīb), grounded in revealed truth.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage is the verbal admonition; metaphorical usage is the admonition of circumstance (ʿiẓah al-ḥāl) when events themselves teach a lesson.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Restriction and Focused Predication (bāb al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — Governing the emphasis of exclusive predication in statements of counsel, such as \"No obedience to a creature in disobedience of the Creator,\" where the restriction (ḥaṣr) is achieved through negation and exception (nafy wa istithnāʾ).", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil'", "badge": "Afterlife"}, {"arabic": "أخاف أن يكون قد فطن وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم من خرج من بيته حاجا أو معتمرا فمات أجري له أجر الحاج المعتمر إلى يوم القيامة ومن مات في أحد الحرمين لم يعرض ولم يحاسب وقيل له ادخل الجنة (3) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم حجة مبرورة خير من الدنيا وما فيها وحجة مبرورة ليس لها جزاء إلا الجنة (4) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم الحجاج والعمار وفد الله عز وجل وزواره إن سألوه أعطاهم وإن استغفروه غفر لهم وإن دعوا استجيب لهم وإن شفعوا شفعوا (5) وفي حديث مسند من طريق أهل البيت عليهم السلام أعظم الناس ذنبا من وقف بعرفة فظن أن الله", "english": "I say: The first [matter] concerning the virtue of the pilgrimage — God Most High said: {«وَأَذِّن فِي النَّاسِ بِالْحَجِّ يَأْتُوكَ رِجَالًا وَعَلَىٰ كُلِّ ضَامِرٍ يَأْتِينَ مِن كُلِّ فَجٍّ عَمِيقٍ»} (And proclaim to the people the pilgrimage; they will come to you on foot and on every lean camel, coming from every deep and distant highway). Qatādah said: When God Most High commanded Ibrāhīm (peace be upon him — and upon our Prophet and upon every chosen servant) to proclaim the pilgrimage to the people, he called out: \"O people! God Most High has built a House, so perform the pilgrimage to it.\" And He Most High said: {«لِيَشْهَدُوا مَنَافِعَ لَهُمْ»} (That they may witness benefits for them) — it is said [these benefits are] commerce during the season and reward in the Hereafter.", "book_title": "Mysteries of Hajj", "section_title_ar": "Section 192", "section_title_en": "The Virtue of the Pilgrimage (Faḍīlat al-Ḥajj)", "section_index": 192, "anchors": "- Root: ح ج ج (ḥ-j-j)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to repair to a place repeatedly, to seek a thing, to overcome in argument.\" Al-Ayn: \"الحج: القصد والتردد إلى الشيء.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحج: القصد إلى البيت الحرام للنسك، وأصله من قصدت الشيء وأقبلت عليه.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قصدت فلانا وحججت إليه.\" Metaphorical: \"حاججته فحججته\" — to overcome in disputation, from the idea of repeatedly pressing one's point.\n- Root: غ ف ر (gh-f-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الغفر: الستر والتغطية.\" Al-Ayn: \"غفرت الشيء: سترته.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الغفران والمغفرة من الله هو أن يصون العبد من أن يمسه العذاب.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"اغفر ثوبك في الوعاء\" (cover/protect your garment). Metaphorical: Divine forgiveness as protective covering of sins.\n- Root: و ف د (w-f-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الوفد: القوم يأتون السلطان أو الرئيس زائرين أو مستعطفين.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوفد: القوم المجتمعون القاصدون إلى رئيس.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"وفد فلان على الأمير\" — he came as a delegate. Metaphorical: The pilgrims as \"delegation of God\" (وفد الله) — those who come to Him seeking honor and bounty.\n- Root: ر ح م (r-ḥ-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الرحمة: الرقة والتعطف.\" Al-Ayn: \"الرحمة: رقة القلب.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الرحمة: رقة تقتضي الإحسان إلى المرحوم.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رحمت فلانا\" — I felt compassion. Metaphorical: Divine mercy as the descent of blessing and pardon upon creation.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governing the placement of prepositional phrases and their objects, particularly relevant in the verse {«يأتين من كل فج عميق»} where the prepositional phrase \"من كل فج\" is interposed between the verb and its subject, demonstrating the rhetorical emphasis on the vastness of the routes of pilgrimage.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Mysteries of Hajj'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "103": [{"arabic": "وقال تعالى فإذا قضيتم الصلاة فاذكروا الله قياما وقعودا وعلى جنوبكم قال ابن عباس رضي الله عنهما أي بالليل والنهار في البر والبحر والسفر والحضر والغنى والفقر والمرض والصحة والسر والعلانية وقال تعالى في ذم المنافقين ولا يذكرون الله إلا قليلا وقال عز وجل واذكر ربك في نفسك تضرعا وخيفة ودون الجهر من القول بالغدو والآصال ولا تكن من الغافلين وقال تعالى ولذكر الله أكبر قال ابن عباس رضي الله عنهما له وجهان أحدهما أن ذكر الله تعالى لكم أعظم من ذكرككم إياه والآخر أن ذكر الله أعظم من كل عبادة سواه إلى غير ذلك من الآيات وأ", "english": "- Root: ذكر (dhakara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to remember, to mention, to recollect.\" The root carries the dual sense of mental recollection and verbal utterance. In Kitab al-Ayn, the root denotes the opposite of forgetfulness (nisyan), and its verbal form encompasses both the internal act of remembrance and the external act of mentioning.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الذكر: حضور المعنى في النفس، وقد يطلق على إبقاء المعنى باللسان. والذكر ضربان: ذكر بالقلب، وذكر باللسان. وقال تعالى: {فاذكروني أذكركم} [البقرة/152] — فذكر العبد ربه بالتعظيم والثناء، وذكر الله عبده بالإنعام والإكرام.\" The theological nuance distinguishes between the creature's remembrance (which is petition and praise) and the Creator's remembrance (which is response and reward).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"ذكرت الشيء: حفظته ووعيته. وذكرت فلانا: جريت بذكره على لسانك.\" Metaphorical (Majaz): \"ذكر الله العبد: أثنى عليه وأجزل له الثواب. ومن المجاز: قوله تعالى {ولذكر الله أكبر} — أي ذكر الله إياكم أعظم من ذكركم إياه.\" The rhetorical distinction is crucial here: when Allah \"remembers\" His servant, it is an act of grace, not cognition.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين المبتدأ والخبر (Interposition between Subject and Predicate) — In the verse {ولذكر الله أكبر}, the grammatical structure allows for the subject (ذكر الله) to be read as either the creature's remembrance of Allah or Allah's remembrance of the creature, with the predicate (أكبر) applying to whichever reading is adopted. This syntactic ambiguity is deliberate and productive of multiple valid interpretations.", "book_title": "On Invocations and Supplications", "section_title_ar": "Section 235", "section_title_en": "Section 235", "section_index": 235, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "قال الله تعالى إن الصلاة كانت على المؤمنين كتابا موقوتا وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم خمس صلوات كتبهن الله على العباد فمن جاء بهن ولم يضيع منهن شيئا استخفافا بحقهن كان له عند الله عهد أن يدخله الجنة ومن لم يأت بهن فليس له عند الله عهد إن شاء عذبه وإن شاء أدخله الجنة (5) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم مثل الصلوات الخمس كمثل نهر عذب غمر بباب أحدكم يقتحم فيه كل يوم خمس مرات فما ترون ذلك يبقي من درنه قالوا لا شيء قال صلى الله عليه وسلم", "english": "Praise be to God, who has inundated His servants with His subtle graces (laṭāʾif) and filled their hearts with the lights of religion and its ordinances — which descend from the Throne of Majesty to the heaven of this world as one of the outpourings of His mercy. He has distinguished Himself from [earthly] kings through His unique possession of Majesty and Grandeur by encouraging His creatures to ask and to supplicate, saying: {«هَلْ مِن دَاعٍ فَأَسْتَجِيبَ لَهُ وَهَلْ مِن مُّسْتَغْفِرٍ فَأَغْفِرَ لَهُ»} (Is there any supplicant that I may respond to him? Is there any seeker of forgiveness that I may forgive him?). And He has set Himself apart from [earthly] sovereigns by opening the door and lifting the veil, permitting His servants to engage in intimate communion (munājāh) through the prayers, however their states may fluctuate — in congregations and in solitude. Nor did He content Himself with mere permission, but He graciously encouraged and invited [them]. Other kings, even the weakest among them, do not permit private audience except after the presentation of gifts and bribes. Glory be to Him — how great is His majesty, how mighty His dominion, how complete His gentleness, how all-encompassing His beneficence!", "book_title": "Mysteries of Prayer", "section_title_ar": "Section 120", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Secrets of Prayer and Its Essential Matters — Section 120: On the Virtues of the Call to Prayer (Adhān) and the Prescribed Prayers", "section_index": 120, "anchors": "- Root: صلو (Ṣ-L-W)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes the act of supplication, blessing, and the prescribed ritual prayer; its core sense involves \"connection\" and \"turning toward.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصلاة\" in its theological sense is the act of drawing near to God through prescribed devotional acts comprising bowing, prostration, and remembrance — a covenant (ʿahd) between the servant and his Lord.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): the ritual prayer with its known conditions and pillars. Metaphorical (majāz): supplication (duʿāʾ) and blessing (raḥmah), as in \"the angels send blessings (yuṣallūn) upon the Prophet.\"\n- Root: أذن (A-Dh-N)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To listen, to give ear; the mu'adhdhin is one who makes the call heard publicly.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The adhān is the public proclamation summoning the community to the prescribed prayer — a declaration of the time of worship.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the act of making known. Metaphorical: the call to prayer as a summons to the Divine presence.\n- Root: ملك (M-L-K)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Possession, dominion, sovereignty.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الملك: هو المتصرف بالأمر والنهي في الجمهور\" — The king is one who disposes by command and prohibition over the multitude. God's sovereignty (mulk) is absolute and unshared.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: ownership and dominion. Metaphorical: \"ملك عليه أمره\" — mastery over one's own affair, self-possession.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Prepositional Interposition (باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — The periodic sentence structure in the opening doxology employs suspended clauses (ḥāl) that qualify the subject while maintaining syntactic cohesion through the governing verb's semantic reach.\n\n---", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Mysteries of Prayer'", "badge": "Du'a"}], "104": [{"arabic": "الآخرة فليقرأ المعرض عن الدنيا على نفسه قوله تعالى ولا تهنوا في ابتغاء القوم إن تكونوا تألمون فإنهم يألمون كما تألمون وترجون من الله ما لا يرجون فإذن إنما انسد طريق الشكر على الخلق لجهلهم بضروب النعم الظاهرة والباطنة والخاصة والعامة فإن قلت فما علاج هذه القلوب الغافلة حتى تشعر بنعم الله تعالى فعساها تشكر فأقول أما القلوب البصيرة فعلاجها التأمل فيما رمزنا إليه من أصناف نعم الله تعالى العامة وأما القلوب البليدة التي لا تعد النعمة نعمة إلا إذا خصتها أو شعرت بالبلاء معها فسبيله أن ينظر أبدا إلى من دونه ويفعل ما كان يفعله بعض الصوفية إذ كان كل يوم يحضر دار المرضى والمقابر والمواضع التي تقام ف", "english": "So may sorrow never part from you. Nay, rather, the most eloquent of expressions and the most lucid of words is the speech of the most eloquent of all who uttered with the Ḍād, whereupon he — peace and blessings be upon him — conveyed this very meaning when he said: \"Whosoever awakens secure in his private state, sound in his body, having the provision of his day, it is as though the entire world with all its fullness were gathered for him.\" {«من أصبح آمنا في سربه معافى في بدنه عنده قوت يومه فكأنما حيزت له الدنيا بحذافيرها»}", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 1016", "section_title_en": "The Remedy for Hearts Unmindful of Divine Blessings — The Path to Gratitude", "section_index": 1016, "anchors": "- Root: حزن (ḥ-z-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Deep, entrenched sorrow that clings to the heart and does not depart.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Grief as a spiritual state opposed to the soul's tranquility; its removal is tied to recognizing divine beneficence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: enduring sadness; Metaphorical: the \"sorrow\" of deprivation from ultimate felicity.\n- Root: سرب (s-r-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self in its private, secure state; safety of one's person and affairs.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Security (amn) as a comprehensive blessing encompassing faith and worldly safety.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical safety in one's abode; Metaphorical: spiritual security from divine wrath.\n- Root: عوض (ʿ-w-ḍ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Exchange, substitution, recompense for loss.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The concept of barter between worldly pleasure and knowledge; knowledge's delight is imperishable, thus no exchange is equitable.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: giving something in return; Metaphorical: the soul's refusal to trade eternal gnosis for transient sensation.\n- Root: غبن (gh-b-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Loss in a transaction; being cheated of one's due.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The Day of Mutual Loss (Yawm al-Taghābun) where the obedient see the vastness of missed opportunity and the disobedient see their ruin.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: commercial fraud; Metaphorical: the ultimate self-deception in squandering life's hours.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — \"إنما انسد طريق الشكر\" restricts the cause exclusively to ignorance, not to any deficiency in divine bounty.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Insight"}], "107": [{"arabic": "وسلم فقال نعم إن الله لا يحب المستكبرين فنزل وقعد معهم على الأرض وأكل ثم سلم عليهم وركب وقال قد أجبتكم فأجيبوني قالوا نعم فوعدهم وقتا معلوما فحضروا فقدم إليهم فاخر الطعام وجلس يأكل معهم وأما قول القائل إن من وضعت يدي في قصعته فقد ذلت له رقبتي فقد قال بعضهم هذا خلاف السنة وليس كذلك فإنه ذل إذا كان الداعي لا يفرح بالإجابة ولا يتقلد منة وكان يرى ذلك يدا له على المدعو ورسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم كان يحضر لعلمه أن الداعي له يتقلد منة ويرى ذلك شرفا وذخرا لنفسه في الدنيا", "english": "The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Do not burden your guest so that you make him hate you, for whoever hates his guest has hated Allah, and whoever hates Allah, Allah hates him.\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of Anger and Hatred", "section_title_ar": "Section 290", "section_title_en": "The Etiquettes of Hospitality: Invitation, Response, Attendance, and the Serving of Food — Section 290 of The Revival of the Religious Sciences", "section_index": 290, "anchors": "- Root: ضيف (ḍ-y-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to incline, to lean toward.\" The guest (al-ḍayf) is one who inclines toward you, alighting as a visitor; the root carries the sense of leaning or bending.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The guest is \"one who inclines to you, alighting with you\"; hospitality (al-ḍiyāfah) became customary in the sense of receiving such a one. The word is originally a verbal noun, hence one and many are equal in most usage.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"ḍāfa ilayhi: he inclined toward him\"; \"ḍāfat al-shams: the sun inclined toward setting.\" Metaphorical (majāz): \"aḍāfa ilayhi amran: he attributed a matter to him, leaning it upon him\"; \"al-muḍāf: the one constrained, hemmed in, in distress.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Focused Predication (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — governing the Prophet's statement \"innamā hādhihi al-akhlāq bi-yad Allāh\" (these morals are only in the hand of Allah), where innamā restricts the attribution of moral character exclusively to divine bestowal.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Condemnation of Anger and Hatred'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "110": [{"arabic": "الله إن الله يغفر الذنوب جميعا إنه هو الغفور الرحيم وقال تعالى ومن يعمل سوءا أو يظلم نفسه ثم يستغفر الله يجد الله غفورا رحيما ونحن نستغفر الله تعالى من كل ما زلت به القدم أو طغى به القلم في كتابنا هذا وفي سائر كتبنا ونستغفره من أقوالنا التي لا توافقها أعمالنا ونستغفره مما ادعيناه وأظهرناه من العلم والبصيرة بدين الله تعالى مع التقصير فيه ونستغفره من كل علم وعمل قصدنا به وجهه الكريم ثم خالطه غيره ونستغفره من كل وعد وعدناه به من أنفسنا ثم قصرنا في الوفاء به ونستغفره من كل نعمة أنعم بها علينا فاستعملناها في معصيته ونستغفره من كل تصريح وتعريض بنقصان ناقص وتقصير مقصر ك", "english": "For Allah, exalted is He, has said: {«إِنَّ اللَّهَ لَا يَغْفِرُ أَنْ يُشْرَكَ بِهِ وَيَغْفِرُ مَا دُونَ ذَلِكَ لِمَنْ يَشَاءُ»} (Indeed, Allah does not forgive that partners be associated with Him, but He forgives what is less than that for whom He wills) [Al-Nisa' 4:48]. And He said: {«قُلْ يَا عِبَادِيَ الَّذِينَ أَسْرَفُوا عَلَى أَنْفُسِهِمْ لَا تَقْنَطُوا مِنْ رَحْمَةِ اللَّهِ إِنَّ اللَّهَ يَغْفِرُ الذُّنُوبَ جَمِيعًا إِنَّهُ هُوَ الْغَفُورُ الرَّحِيمُ»} (Say: O My servants who have transgressed against themselves, do not despair of the mercy of Allah. Indeed, Allah forgives all sins. Indeed, He is the Forgiving, the Merciful) [Al-Zumar 39:53]. And He said: {«وَمَنْ يَعْمَلْ سُوءًا أَوْ يَظْلِمْ نَفْسَهُ ثُمَّ يَسْتَغْفِرِ اللَّهَ يَجِدِ اللَّهَ غَفُورًا رَحِيمًا»} (And whoever does evil or wrongs himself, then seeks forgiveness from Allah, he will find Allah Forgiving, Merciful) [Al-Nisa' 4:110].", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1359", "section_title_en": "Sealing the Book with a Chapter on the Vastness of Allah's Mercy, by Way of Seeking an Auspicious Omen (Tafa'ul)", "section_index": 1359, "anchors": "- Root: فأل (Fa'l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The core root denotes a good omen or auspicious sign, as opposed to الطيرة (tiyarah, ill omen); the Prophet ﷺ loved the good omen.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The term carries a theological nuance of hopeful expectation (rajā') grounded in divine benevolence, not in superstitious causality.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): a favorable sign perceived by the senses. Metaphorical (Majaz): the act of framing one's closing deeds around a hopeful sign of divine acceptance.\n- Root: غفر (Ghafr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Covering and shielding from defilement; forgiveness as concealment of sin.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الغفر: إلباس ما يصونه عن الدنس\" — Divine forgiveness (maghfirah) is that Allah shields the servant from the touch of chastisement.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"اللهم غفرا\" (seeking pardon). Metaphorical: the sweeping away of sins entirely, as in the verse {إِنَّ اللَّهَ يَغْفِرُ الذُّنُوبَ جَمِيعًا}.\n- Root: رحم (Rahm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Tenderness and compassion; the womb (rahim) as the seat of kinship-mercy.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Mercy (rahmah) as an attribute of Allah denoting His will to bestow beneficence and withhold punishment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: compassion felt by a creature. Metaphorical (Majaz): divine beneficence (ihsān) and graciousness (karam) that overflow to all creation.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The periodic structure of supplication (istighfar) with repeated \"من\" (from) clauses allows for expansive enumeration of sins sought for forgiveness, each governed by the initial verb \"نستغفره\" (we seek His forgiveness), maintaining syntactic cohesion across the long sentence.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "ومن يعمل سوءا أو يظلم به ثم يستغفر الله يجد الله غفورا رحيما وقال عز وجل فسبح بحمد ربك واستغفره إنه كان توابا وقال تعالى والمستغفرين بالأسحار وكان صلى الله عليه وسلم يكثر أن يقول سبحانك اللهم وبحمدك اللهم اغفر لي إنك أنت التواب الرحيم (1) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم من أكثر من الاستغفار جعل الله عز وجل له من كل هم فرجا ومن كل ضيق مخرجا وارزقه من حيث لا يحتسب (2) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم إني لأستغفر الله تعالى وأتوب إليه في اليوم سبعين مرة (3) هذا مع أنه صلى الله عليه وسلم", "english": "God Most High said: {«والذين إذا فعلوا فاحشة أو ظلموا أنفسهم ذكروا الله فاستغفروا لذنوبهم»} — \"And those who, when they commit an indecency or wrong themselves, remember God and seek forgiveness for their sins\" {آل عمران/135}.", "book_title": "On Invocations and Supplications", "section_title_ar": "Section 246", "section_title_en": "On Seeking Forgiveness (Al-Istighfar) — Section 246", "section_index": 246, "anchors": "- Root: ذنب (dhanb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of \"tail\" or \"taking hold of a tail\"; extended to signify a sin as that which trails behind and follows a person.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الذنب في الأصل: الأخذ بذنب الشيء\" — sin is that by which one is seized and held accountable; used metaphorically for a fault that clings to its perpetrator.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: tail of an animal. Metaphorical: \"هو من الأذناب\" — one who follows; sin as that which trails the soul and must be severed through repentance.\n- Root: غفر (ghafara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Covering, veiling, protecting from defilement; a shield or helmet (المغفر).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الغفران والمغفرة من الله هو أن يصون العبد من أن يمسه العذاب\" — divine forgiveness is God's protection of the servant from being touched by punishment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to cover and protect. Metaphorical: God's \"covering\" of sins — not merely erasure, but active safeguarding of the sinner from consequence.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The frequent interposition of \"يا رب\" and vocative constructions between the subject and predicate in supplicatory sentences, as in \"اللهم اغفر لي,\" where the vocative particle governs the imperative verb's addressee, reflecting the grammatical permissibility of separation between governing and governed elements in invocational speech.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'", "badge": "Du'a"}], "113": [{"arabic": "وكان فضل الله عليك عظيما وقال تعالى ولقد آتينا داود منا فضلا يعني العلم فتعلم العلم في هذا اليوم وتعليمه من أفضل القربات والصلاة أفضل من مجالس القصاص إذ كانوا يرونه بدعة ويخرجون القصاص من الجامع بكر ابن عمر رضي الله عنهما إلى مجلسه في المسجد الجامع فإذا قاص في موضعه فقال قم عن مجلسي فقال لا أقوم وقد جلست وسبقتك إليه فأرسل ابن عمر إلى صاحب الشرطة فأقامه، فلو كان ذلك من السنة لما جازت إقامته فقد قال صلى الله عليه وسلم لا يقيمن أحدكم أخاه من مجلسه ثم يجلس فيه ولكن تفسحوا", "english": "Know that the one who attends the Friday prayer should remain in the mosque until he prays the afternoon prayer (al-ʿaṣr); and if he remains until the sunset prayer (al-maghrib), that is even more excellent. It is said: \"Whoever prays the afternoon prayer in the congregational mosque (al-jāmiʿ) shall have the reward of the Pilgrimage (ḥajj); and whoever prays the sunset prayer therein shall have the reward of a Pilgrimage and an ʿUmra.\" However, if he does not feel secure from ostentation (al-taṣabbuʿ) and from harm befalling him through the gaze of people upon his seclusion (iʿtikāf), or he fears plunging into what does not concern him, then it is more excellent that he return to his house, remembering Allah (mighty and majestic), reflecting upon His graces (ālāʾihi), thanking Allah the Exalted for His granting of success (tawfīq), fearing his own shortcoming, and watching over his heart and his tongue until the setting of the sun — so that the noble hour (al-sāʿa al-sharīfa) does not pass him by. Nor should he speak in the congregational mosque or in other mosques about the affairs of this world. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: {«يَأْتِي عَلَى النَّاسِ زَمَانٌ يَكُونُ حَدِيثُهُمْ فِي مَسَاجِدِهِمْ أَمْرَ دُنْيَاهُمْ لَيْسَ لِلَّهِ تَعَالَى فِيهِمْ حَاجَةٌ فَلَا تُجَالِسُوهُمْ»} — \"A time will come upon people when their conversation in their mosques will be about their worldly affairs; Allah the Exalted has no need of them, so do not sit with them.\" (1)", "book_title": "Monitoring and Accounting", "section_title_ar": "Section 150", "section_title_en": "Section 150", "section_index": 150, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Monitoring and Accounting'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "وقد سمى الله عز وجل العلم فضلا في مواضع قال تعالى وعلمك ما لم تكن تعلم وكان فضل الله عليك عظيما وقال تعالى ولقد آتينا داود منا فضلا يعني العلم فتعلم العلم في هذا اليوم وتعليمه من أفضل القربات والصلاة أفضل من مجالس القصاص إذ كانوا يرونه بدعة ويخرجون القصاص من الجامع بكر ابن عمر رضي الله عنهما إلى مجلسه في المسجد الجامع فإذا قاص في موضعه فقال قم عن مجلسي فقال لا أقوم وقد جلست وسبقتك إليه فأرسل ابن عمر إلى صاحب الشرطة فأقامه، فلو كان ذلك من السنة لما جازت إقامته فقد قال صلى الله عليه وسلم لا يقيمن أحدكم أخاه من مجلسه ثم يجلس فيه ولكن تفسحوا و", "english": "Know that the one who attends the Friday prayer should remain in the mosque until he prays the afternoon prayer (al-ʿaṣr); and if he remains until the sunset prayer (al-maghrib), that is even more excellent. It is said: \"Whoever prays the afternoon prayer in the congregational mosque (al-jāmiʿ) shall have the reward of the Pilgrimage (ḥajj); and whoever prays the sunset prayer therein shall have the reward of a Pilgrimage and an ʿUmra.\" However, if he does not feel secure from ostentation (al-taṣabbuʿ) and from harm befalling him through the gaze of people upon his seclusion (iʿtikāf), or he fears plunging into what does not concern him, then it is more excellent that he return to his house, remembering Allah (mighty and majestic), reflecting upon His graces (ālāʾihi), thanking Allah the Exalted for His granting of success (tawfīq), fearing his own shortcoming, and watching over his heart and his tongue until the setting of the sun — so that the noble hour (al-sāʿa al-sharīfa) does not pass him by. Nor should he speak in the congregational mosque or in other mosques about the affairs of this world. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: {«يَأْتِي عَلَى النَّاسِ زَمَانٌ يَكُونُ حَدِيثُهُمْ فِي مَسَاجِدِهِمْ أَمْرَ دُنْيَاهُمْ لَيْسَ لِلَّهِ تَعَالَى فِيهِمْ حَاجَةٌ فَلَا تُجَالِسُوهُمْ»} — \"A time will come upon people when their conversation in their mosques will be about their worldly affairs; Allah the Exalted has no need of them, so do not sit with them.\" (1)", "book_title": "Mysteries of Prayer", "section_title_ar": "Section 150", "section_title_en": "Section 150", "section_index": 150, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Mysteries of Prayer'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "فضل الله تعالى عليه بالإيجاد وهو هالك عقيب وجوده لولا فضل الله عليه بالإبقاء وهو ناقص بعد الوجود لولا فضل الله عليه بالتكميل لخلقته وبالجملة فليس في الوجود شيء له بنفسه قوام إلا القيوم الحي الذي هو قائم لوجوده والمديم له إن عرفه خالقا موجدا ومخترعا مبقيا وقيوما بنفسه ومقوما لغيره فإن كان لا يحبه فهو لجهله بنفسه وبربه والمحبة ثمرة المعرفة فتنعدم بانعدامها وتضعف بضعفها وتقوى بقوتها ولذلك قال الحسن البصري رحمه الله تعالى من عرف ربه أحبه ومن عرف الدنيا زهد فيها وكيف يتصور أن يحب الإنسان نفسه ولا يحب ربه الذي به", "english": "And that whoever loves other than God—not insofar as that thing is related to God—then that is due to his ignorance and deficiency in knowledge of God most high. And the love of the Messenger {صلى الله عليه وسلم} is praiseworthy because it is identical to love of God most high. Likewise, love of the scholars and the pious is [praiseworthy], because the beloved of the beloved is beloved, the messenger of the beloved is beloved, and the lover of the beloved is beloved. And all of that reverts to love of the Origin, so it does not pass beyond Him to another. Thus, there is no beloved in reality (bil-ḥaqīqah) in the sight of those possessed of insight (ulū al-baṣā'ir) except God most high, and no one worthy of love other than Him. Its clarification is that we return to the five causes which we have mentioned and show that they are combined in the case of God most high in their entirety, and that none of them are found in other than Him except as isolated instances—and that they are real (ḥaqīqah) in the case of God most high, while their existence in the case of other than Him is delusion and imagination, being pure metaphor (majāz maḥḍ) with no reality to it. And whenever that is established, there becomes unveiled to every possessor of insight the opposite of what the weak of intellects and hearts had imagined—namely, the impossibility of truly loving God most high—and it becomes clear that rigorous investigation (al-taḥqīq) demands that we love no one other than God most high.", "book_title": "Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment", "section_title_ar": "Section 1152", "section_title_en": "The Exposition that the One Worthy of Love is God Alone", "section_index": 1152, "anchors": "- Root: ح ب ب (ḥ-b-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning: to love, to cherish; al-ḥubb denotes the heart's inclination toward that which pleases it.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-ḥubb is the heart's attraction to what it deems good (al-mulā'im); its ultimate object is God, and love of others is only praiseworthy insofar as it is subsumed under love of Him.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) love is due to God alone; love of other than God, when not referred to Him, is metaphorical (majāz) and illusory.\n- Root: ع ل م (ʿ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To know with certainty; ʿilm is firm cognition of a thing as it is.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Maʿrifah is more specific than ʿilm—it is knowledge attained through reflection on effects; love is the fruit of maʿrifah, so it vanishes with its absence and weakens with its weakness.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal knowledge of self and Lord necessitates love; ignorance (jahl) of them negates it.\n- Root: ق و م (q-w-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To stand, to subsist; al-qayyūm is He who is self-subsisting and sustains all else.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-Qayyūm (the Self-Subsisting) is He by whom all things subsist and who maintains them in existence; qiwām (subsistence) of a thing is its very standing through another.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: qāma bihi—he sustained it; metaphorical: God is the Qayyūm of all creation, for He alone grants existence and permanence.\n- Root: ظ ل ل (ẓ-l-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Shadow; ẓill is what shades from the sun's rays.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The shadow is an effect dependent on its source; used as a parable for creation's dependence on God's power.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: shadow of a tree; metaphorical: all existents relative to God's power are like shadows relative to their sources—yet the example is corrected for the elite, who know light too is directly created by God.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Restriction and Focused Predication (باب الحصر والتقييد) — The structure \"لا محبوب بالحقيقة ... إلا الله\" effects exclusive restriction, confining true love's object solely to God, with all other objects being metaphorical extensions.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'", "badge": "Insight"}], "114": [{"arabic": "الأمر بالمعروف للوالدين والأقربين وقال تعالى لا خير في كثير من نجواهم إلا من أمر بصدقة أو معروف أو إصلاح بين الناس ومن يفعل ذلك ابتغاء مرضاة الله فسوف نؤتيه أجرا عظيما وقال تعالى وإن طائفتان من المؤمنين اقتتلوا فأصلحوا بينهما الآية والإصلاح نهي عن البغي وإعادة إلى الطاعة فإن لم يفعل فقد أمر الله تعالى بقتاله فقال فقاتلوا التي تبغي حتى تفيء إلى أمر الله وذلك هو النهي عن المنكر وأما الأخبار فمنها ما روي عن أبي بكر الصديق رضي الله عنه أنه قال في خطبة خطبها أيها الناس إنكم تقرءون هذه الآية وتؤولونها على خلاف تأويلها يا أيها الذين آمنوا عليكم أنفسكم لا يضركم من ضل إذا اهتديتم (1) وإني سمعت رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم", "english": "Know that the verses indicating the obligation of commanding right and forbidding wrong are numerous. Among them is His saying, may He be exalted: {«Let there arise from among you a nation inviting to good, commanding right and forbidding wrong — and those are the successful»} (Āl ʿImrān 3:104). In this verse is the declaration of obligation, for His saying, may He be exalted, \"Let there be\" (wal-takun) is a command, and the apparent meaning of a command is obligation. In it also is the declaration that success (falāḥ) is contingent upon it, for He restricted it and said: {«And those are the successful»}. In it also is the declaration that it is a collective obligation (farḍ kifāyah), not an individual obligation (farḍ ʿayn), and that when a group undertakes it, the obligation falls from the rest — for He did not say, \"Let all of you be commanders of right,\" but rather said: {«Let there arise from among you a nation»}. Thus, whenever one person or a group undertakes it, the burden falls from the others, and success is particularized to those who undertake it and directly perform it. But if all of creation were to hold back from it, the burden would encompass all those capable of it without exception.", "book_title": "Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil", "section_title_ar": "Section 521", "section_title_en": "Section 521", "section_index": 521, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "أمر بمعروف أو نهي عن منكر أو ذكر الله تعالى (3) وقال تعالى لا خير في كثير من نجواهم إلا من أمر بصدقة أو معروف أو إصلاح بين الناس الآية ورأى بعض العلماء بعض أصحاب الرأي من أهل الكوفة في المنام فقال ما رأيت فيما كنت عليه من الفتيا والرأي فكره وجهه وأعرض عنه وقال ما وجدناه شيئا وما حمدنا عافيته وقال ابن حصين إن أحدهم ليفتي في مسئلة لو وردت على عمر بن الخطاب رضي الله عنه لجمع لها أهل بدر فلم يزل السكوت دأب أهل العلم إلا عند الضرورة وفي الحديث إذا رأيتم الرجل قد أوتي صمتا وزهدا فاقتربوا منه فإنه يلقن الحكمة (4) وقيل العالم إما عالم عامة وهو المفتي وهم أصحاب السلاطين أو ع", "english": "And in sum, mixing with them is a key to evils, and the scholars of the Hereafter — their way is caution (iḥtiyāṭ). And he, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"Whoever dwells in the desert becomes coarse\" — meaning whoever dwells in the desert becomes coarse — \"and whoever follows the hunt becomes heedless, and whoever attends upon the sultan is tempted\" (1). And he, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"There will be over you rulers whom you will recognize and denounce. Whoever denounces has absolved himself, and whoever disapproves has been saved. But whoever is pleased and follows — God, exalted is He, will distance him.\" It was said: \"Shall we not fight them?\" He, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"No, so long as they pray\" (2). And Sufyān said: \"In Hell there is a valley that none inhabits except the Qurʾān-readers who visit the kings.\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of Anger and Hatred", "section_title_ar": "Section 56", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 56: On the Etiquette of Scholars and the Perils of Worldly Attachment", "section_index": 56, "anchors": "- Root: ع ل م (ʿ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes the mark or trace by which a thing is known; knowledge as the impression of reality upon the mind.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality, divided into apprehension of the essence and judgment upon the thing.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Distinguishes literal knowledge (ḥaqīqah) from the metaphorical \"sensing\" (shaʿara); scholars as \"علامة\" — living landmarks of guidance.\n- Root: ي ق ن (y-q-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Certainty as the settled state of the heart; the opposite of doubt.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اليقين من صفة العلم فوق المعرفة والدراية\" — certainty is an attribute of knowledge above mere cognition; it is \"سكون الفهم مع ثبات الحكم\" — the stillness of understanding with the firmness of judgment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Used literally for that which is verified and settled, as opposed to conjecture (ẓann).\n- Root: خ ش ي (kh-sh-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Fear that carries reverence and awe; the fear of one who knows the majesty of what he fears.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Khashyah is the fear that arises from knowledge of the greatness of the feared one — hence \"إنما يخشى الله من عباده العلماء\" (Only the learned among His servants fear God).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Specification) — The use of restrictive constructions (innamā, laysa...illā) to delimit predicates, as in \"العلم ثلاثة\" and the enumeration of the categories of those who issue legal rulings.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Anger and Hatred'", "badge": "Insight"}], "116": [{"arabic": "تعالى {إن الله لا يغفر أن يشرك به ويغفر ما دون ذلك لمن يشاء} والاستثناء بالمشيئة يدل على الانقسام وقوله تعالى {ومن يعص الله ورسوله فإن له نار جهنم خالدين فيها} وتخصيصه بالكفر تحكم وقوله تعالى {ألا إن الظالمين في عذاب مقيم} وقال تعالى {ومن جاء بالسيئة فكبت وجوههم في النار} فهذه العمومات في معارضة عموماتهم ولا بد من تسليط التخصص والتأويل على الجانبين لأن الأخبار مصرحة بأن العصاة يعذبون (1) بل قوله تعالى وإن منكم إلا واردها كالصريح في أن ذلك لا بد منه للكل إذ لا يخلو مؤمن عن ذنب يرتكبه وقوله تعالى لا يصلاها إلا ا", "english": "He was asked, \"Which deed is most excellent?\" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Islam.\" It was then asked, \"Which aspect of Islam is most excellent?\" He (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Faith (Īmān).\" This is evidence for both the distinction (ikhtilāf) between the two terms and their overlap (tadākhul). This usage is the most consistent with the conventions of the language, because faith is a deed among deeds, and it is the most excellent of them. Islam is submission (taslīm), whether by the heart, by the tongue, or by the limbs. The most excellent of these is that which is by the heart, which is the assent (taṣdīq) that is called faith. Using the two terms in a way that indicates distinction, overlap, or even complete synonymy (tarāduf) is all within the bounds of metaphorical usage (tajawwuz) in the language.", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 99", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 99: On the Distinction, Overlap, and Synonymy of Faith (Īmān) and Submission (Islām), and Their Worldly and Otherworldly Judgments", "section_index": 99, "anchors": "- Root: ايم (A-Y-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of remaining unmarried, or a state of being without a spouse; extended metaphorically to describe one who is solitary or without support.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأيامى: جمع أيم، وهي المرأة التي لا بعل لها... والحرب مأيمة، أي: يفرق بين الزوج والزوجة.\" The root carries the sense of separation or a state of lacking union, which informs the theological discussion of what constitutes the essential bond of faith.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes the state of being unmarried; metaphorical (Majaz) usage extends to anything that separates or leaves one bereft. This distinction between literal and metaphorical application is central to the chapter's method.\n- Root: قلب (Q-L-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn, overturn, or transform a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه... والانقلاب: الانصراف.\" The heart (qalb) is so named for its constant fluctuation and turning. In theological terms, it is the seat of taṣdīq (assent), the immaterial faculty of cognition and conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is physical turning; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"قلب الأمور\" — to deliberate, scrutinize, and manage affairs. This supports the distinction between the heart's internal act of assent and the external acts of the limbs.\n- Root: عمل (ʿ-A-M-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Any deed or action performed with purpose and intent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العمل: كل فعل يكون من الحيوان بقصد، فهو أخص من الفعل.\" Work is more specific than mere action, as it implies deliberate, intentional agency. It is used for both righteous and sinful deeds, and is the external manifestation of internal conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage refers to physical labor or deeds. The chapter explores whether \"work\" is an integral part of the reality of faith or a separate, consequent requirement.\n- Root: صدق (S-D-Q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be truthful, to speak the truth, to verify.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصدق والكذب أصلهما في القول... ولا يكونان بالقصد الأول إلا في القول.\" Truth and falsehood are primarily properties of speech and reports. Taṣdīq (assent) is the heart's affirmation of the truth of a report, which is the core reality of Īmān.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is truthful speech; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"صادق الحملة\" — one who is true and firm in battle or resolve. This shows that taṣdīq is not merely verbal but a firm, internal resolution.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication). This rule governs how restriction (ḥaṣr) is achieved in Arabic, often through particles like \"innamā\" or through contextual specification (takhṣīṣ). The chapter's core argument hinges on how terms like Īmān and Islām are semantically restricted, expanded, or made synonymous through contextual usage (majāz), requiring precise parsing of the predicate and its scope.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "119": [{"arabic": "وأطاع الشيطان لما قال ولآمرنهم فليغيرن خلق الله إذ حق الرجل أن يكون متبوعا لا تابعا وقد سمى الله الرجال قوامين على النساء وسمى الزوج سيدا فقال تعالى وألفيا سيدها لدى الباب فإذا انقلب السيد مسخرا فقد بدل نعمة الله كفرا ونفس المرأة على مثال نفسك إن أرسلت عنانها قليلا جمحت بك طويلا وإن أرخيت عذارها فترا جذبتك ذراعا وإن كبحتها وشددت يدك عليها في محل الشدة ملكتها قال الشافعي رضي الله عنه ثلاثة إن أكرمتهم أهانوك وإن أهنتهم أكرموك المرأة والخادم والنبطي أراد به إن محضت الإكرام ولم تمزج غلظك", "english": "The first point of etiquette is the wedding feast (walīmah), and it is recommended (mustaḥabbah). Anas, may God be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, saw upon ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, may God be pleased with him, a trace of yellow dye, so he said: {«What is this?»} He replied: \"I have married a woman for the weight of a date-stone in gold.\" He said: \"May God bless you. Hold a wedding feast, even if with a single sheep.\" {«...»} And the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, held a wedding feast for Ṣafiyyah with dates and sawīq (barley porridge). {«...»} And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: \"The food of the first day is an obligation (ḥaqq), the food of the second day is a Sunnah, and the food of the third day is for show (sumʿah); and whoever seeks to be heard, God will make his deeds heard.\" {«...»} None raised this hadith except Ziyād ibn ʿAbd Allāh, and it is anomalous (gharīb). It is recommended to offer congratulations to him, and the one who enters upon the husband should say: \"May God bless you and shower blessings upon you, and may He bring you both together in goodness.\" {«...»} Abū Hurayrah, may God be pleased with him, reported that he, may God bless him and grant him peace, commanded this. It is also recommended to make the marriage public (iẓhār al-nikāḥ). He, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: \"The distinction between the lawful and the unlawful is the drum and the voice.\" {«...»} The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: \"Announce this marriage, hold it in the mosques, and beat drums for it.\" {«...»} And on the authority of al-Rubayyiʿ bint Muʿawwidh, she said: The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, came and entered on the morning of my wedding night, and he sat upon my bedding, and we had young girls with us who were beating their drums and lamenting those of my forefathers who had been killed—until one of them said: \"And among us is a Prophet who knows what will be tomorrow.\" He said to her: \"Stop this, and say what you were saying before.\" {«...»}", "book_title": "Etiquette of Marriage", "section_title_ar": "Section 303", "section_title_en": "On What is Incumbent Upon the Husband and What is Incumbent Upon the Wife", "section_index": 303, "anchors": "- Root: و ل م (w-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The core meaning pertains to the wedding feast (walīmah), derived from the concept of gathering and union.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological usage, it signifies the communal meal marking the consummation of a marriage contract, a recommended act of public gratitude.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes the specific feast; metaphorical (Majaz) extension applies to any act of publicizing a joyous occasion.\n- Root: ع ش ر (ʿ- sh-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes association, companionship, and intimacy; the tenth (ʿasharah) of a thing is related, but the primary social sense is cohabitation and mutual dealing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the Quranic context of {وَعَاشِرُوهُنَّ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ}, it signifies a sustained, equitable mode of social intercourse grounded in moral rectitude, not mere physical proximity.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is the act of living together; metaphorical (Majaz) usage elevates it to a standard of ethical conduct (maʿrūf) that defines the marital bond.\n- Root: ق س م (q-s-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root indicates division, apportionment, and distribution; in the marital context, it refers to the equitable division of time among co-wives.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theologically, it is tied to justice (ʿadl) as a divine attribute; for the husband, it is a legal obligation to maintain fairness in material and temporal allotments.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is the physical act of dividing; metaphorical (Majaz) usage applies to the moral duty of impartiality in affection and provision.\n- Root: غ ي ر (gh-y-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root conveys alteration and change, but in the moral register, it denotes protective jealousy (ghayrah), a state of aversion to the violation of honor.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In Kalam, ghayrah is a praiseworthy emotion when aligned with divine law—jealousy for the sanctity of the family—and blameworthy when it stems from unjust suspicion (sūʾ al-ẓann).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is the emotional state; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is attributed to God (al-Ghayyār) as a figure for His prohibition of indecencies, distinguishing human passion from divine will.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to isolate specific obligations (e.g., \"the third is that he should increase...\") and to distinguish categories of jealousy and pride, ensuring precise legal and moral demarcation.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Etiquette of Marriage'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "أما من كان مستعدا فقد فاز فوزا عظيما وأما من كان مغرورا بطول الأمل فقد خسر خسرانا مبينا فلينظر الإنسان كل ساعة في أطرافه وأعضائه وليتدبر أنها كيف تأكلها الديدان لا محالة وكيف تتفتت عظامها وليتفكر أن الدود يبدأ بحدقته اليمنى أولا أو اليسرى فما على بدنه شيء إلا وهو طعمة الدود وما له من نفسه إلا العلم والعمل الخالص لوجه الله تعالى وكذلك يتفكر فيما سنورده من عذاب القبر وسؤال منكر ونكير ومن الحشر والنشر وأهوال القيامة وقرع النداء يوم العرض الأكبر فأمثال هذه الأفكار هي التي تجدد ذكر الموت على قلبه وتدعوه إلى الاستعداد له", "english": "He defers day after day, and one occupation passes him on to another — nay, to many occupations — until death snatches him away at a moment he does not anticipate. Then his regret becomes prolonged, and most of the people of the Fire — and their screaming — are from \"I will\" (sawfa). They say: \"Woe to us for our 'I will'!\" (wa-ḥazanāh min sawfa).", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1288", "section_title_en": "The True Nature of Procrastination (al-Taswīf): Its Causes and Cures — On the Deception of Extended Hope (ṭūl al-amal)", "section_index": 1288, "anchors": "- Root: سوف (s-w-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"سوف\" is a particle of future tense (tanjīz al-waʿd), indicating postponement and delay; al-musawwif is one who defers action.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The root conveys \"delay\" (taʾkhīr) and is tied to the blameworthy trait of procrastination in religious duties, as opposed to immediate resolve (mubādarah).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"سوّفت به\" (I delayed him). Metaphorical: \"سوّف بنفسه\" — he lulled himself with false promises of future time, a majāz for self-deception.\n- Root: أمل (a-m-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Hope/expectation (rajāʾ); ṭūl al-amal is extended hope — the root cause of negligence (ghaflah) in preparing for death.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Amal is expectation of a desired outcome; blameworthy when it exceeds the appointed term (ajal), diverting from the Hereafter.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"أملت الشيء\" (I hoped for it). Metaphorical: Extended hope is a veil (ḥijāb) over the certainty of death.\n- Root: جهل (j-h-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Ignorance, the opposite of knowledge (ʿilm); also rashness acting without knowledge.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Jahl is the absence of knowledge about what one must know — here, ignorance of death's unpredictability.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: lack of knowledge. Metaphorical: acting as if death is distant despite its certainty — a practical ignorance.\n- Root: حب (ḥ-b-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Love/attachment (wudd); ḥubb al-dunyā is the love of the lower world.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Love is the inclination of the heart toward what pleases it; love of the world (dunyā) is the root of all blameworthy traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: affection. Metaphorical: \"ḥubb al-khaṭīr\" (love of the momentous) erases \"ḥubb al-ḥaqīr\" (love of the trivial) — a majāz for spiritual reorientation.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used in \"فإنما يزداد بطول المدة قوة ورسوخا\" (it only increases in strength and rootedness with length of time), restricting the outcome to intensification alone.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "120": [{"arabic": "الله فلا تخالفهم وإن العمر طويل فاصبر حتى تتوب غدا يعدهم ويمنيهم وما يعدهم الشيطان إلا غرورا يعدهم التوبة ويمنيهم المغفرة فيهلكهم بإذن الله تعالى بهذه الحيل وما يجري مجراها فيوسع قلبه لقبول الغرور ويضيقه عن قبول الحق وكل ذلك بقضاء من الله وقدر فمن يرد الله أن يهديه يشرح صدره للإسلام ومن يرد أن يضله يجعل صدره ضيقا حرجا كأنما يصعد في السماء إن ينصركم الله فلا غالب لكم وإن يخذلكم فمن ذا الذي ينصركم من بعده فهو الهادي والمضل يفعل ما يشاء ويحكم ما يريد لا راد لحكمه ولا معقب لقضائه خلق الجنة وخلق لها أهلا فاستعملهم بالطاعة وخلق النار وخلق ل", "english": "I say: Know that when the Shayṭān prevails and the heart inclines to its own kind among the parties of the Shayṭān, turning away from the party of God—Exalted is He—and His saints, and aiding the party of the Shayṭān and His enemies, then there flows upon its bodily members, by virtue of the prior decree, that which is the cause of its distance from God—Exalted is He. But if the angelic attributes are predominant over the heart, the heart does not lend its ear to the Shayṭān's seduction, nor to his incitement toward the immediate world, nor to his belittling of the matter of the Hereafter. Rather, it inclines to the party of God—Exalted is He—and obedience appears upon its members in accordance with what has preceded in the decree. Thus, the heart of the believer is between two fingers of the fingers of the All-Merciful—that is, between the mutual tugging of these two hosts—and this is the meaning of \"turning\" (taqlīb): the transition from one party to another. As for perpetual constancy, whether with the party of the angels or with the party of the Shayṭān, that is rare on both sides. These acts of obedience and disobedience emerge from the treasuries of the Unseen into the world of witness by way of the treasury of the heart, for it is among the treasuries of the Malakūt. And when they do emerge, they become signs by which the masters of hearts recognize the prior decree. For whomever is created for Paradise, the means of obedience are made easy for him; and whomever is created for the Fire, the means of disobedience are made easy for him, and evil companions are given sway over him, and the rule of the Shayṭān is cast into his heart. For by various kinds of deceptions he beguiles the foolish, saying: \"God is Merciful, so do not care\"; and \"All people do not fear God, so do not oppose them\"; and \"Life is long, so be patient until you repent tomorrow.\" He promises them and fills them with desires—{يَعِدُهُمْ وَيُمَنِّيهِمْ وَمَا يَعِدُهُمُ الشَّيْطَانُ إِلَّا غُرُورًا} \"He promises them and fills them with desires, and the Shayṭān promises them nothing but delusion\" {النساء/120}. He promises them repentance and fills them with hopes of forgiveness, and thus destroys them, by God's permission, through these ruses and what runs in their course. So he expands their hearts to accept delusion and constricts them from accepting the truth. And all of that is by God's decree and destiny: {فَمَن يُرِدِ اللَّهُ أَن يَهْدِيَهُ يَشْرَحْ صَدْرَهُ لِلْإِسْلَامِ وَمَن يُرِدْ أَن يُضِلَّهُ يَجْعَلْ صَدْرَهُ ضَيِّقًا حَرَجًا كَأَنَّمَا يَصَّعَّدُ فِي السَّمَاءِ} \"Whomsoever God wishes to guide, He expands his breast to Islam; and whomsoever He wishes to lead astray, He makes his breast narrow and constricted, as though he were climbing into the sky\" {الأنعام/125}. {إِن يَنصُرْكُمُ اللَّهُ فَلَا غَالِبَ لَكُمْ وَإِن يَخْذُلْكُمْ فَمَن ذَا الَّذِي يَنصُرُكُم مِّن بَعْدِهِ} \"If God helps you, none can overcome you; and if He forsakes you, who is there to help you after Him?\" {آل عمران/160}. He is the Guide and the Misleader; He does what He wills and decrees what He desires. There is no repeller of His judgment and no reverser of His decree. He created Paradise and created for it its people, and employed them in obedience; and He created the Fire and created for it its people, and employed them in disobedience. And He made known to creation the mark of the people of Paradise and the people of the Fire, saying: {إِنَّ الْأَبْرَارَ لَفِي نَعِيمٍ وَإِنَّ الْفُجَّارَ لَفِي جَحِيمٍ} \"Verily, the righteous shall be in bliss, and verily, the wicked shall be in the Blazing Fire\" {الانفطار/13-14}. Then He—Exalted is He—said, as related from His Prophet, peace be upon him: \"These are in Paradise, and I do not care; and these are in the Fire, and I do not care.\" Exalted is God, the King, the Truth! {لَا يُسْأَلُ عَمَّا يَفْعَلُ وَهُمْ يُسْأَلُونَ} \"He is not questioned about what He does, but they shall be questioned\" {الأنبياء/23}.", "book_title": "Disciplining the Soul", "section_title_ar": "Section 611", "section_title_en": "The Explanation of the Heart's Rapid Fluctuation and the Division of Hearts Between Change and Constancy", "section_index": 611, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of turning, overturning, and revolving a thing from one face to another; the heart is so named for its constant oscillation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart's essence is its perpetual turning and redirection; its fluctuation is not accidental but constitutive of its nature.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): physical overturning. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — the heart's movement between the two hosts (jundān) is a real, dynamic turnover of allegiance.\n- Root: شيط (sh-y-t)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Burning, scorching, or spoiling meat by over-smoking; blood going to waste.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The Shayṭān is so named for his being burned and banished from mercy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Metaphorical: \"شاط دمه إذا بطل\" — the Shayṭān's work is to render the servant's spiritual life wasted and nullified, as blood spilled in vain.\n- Root: حزب (ḥ-z-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A group or party with cohesion and strength; al-Ḥayzabūn (the old woman) shows the root's extension to a gathered mass.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحزب: جماعة فيها غلظ\" — the party of God (ḥizb Allāh) versus the party of the Shayṭān (ḥizb al-Shayṭān) are two opposing, organized camps in cosmic conflict.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a band of partisans. Metaphorical: \"قرأ حزبه من القرآن\" — a portion assigned; here, the heart is assigned to one of the two parties by divine decree.\n- Root: خلق (kh-l-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Measuring and smoothing; creating as precise estimation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخلق أصله: التقدير المستقيم\" — creation is origination upon a just measure; God's creative act entails the pre-ordainment of ends.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: shaping leather before cutting. Metaphorical: \"خلق الله الخلق: أوجده على تقدير أوجبته الحكمة\" — God's creation of Paradise and Hellfire and their inhabitants is an act of wise, pre-eternal determination.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The phrase \"إنما ذكرنا منه ما يحتاج إليه\" restricts the author's purpose: only the necessary measure of the heart's wonders is expounded, not exhaustive treatment, signaling a deliberate boundary of the science of practical religion (ʿilm al-muʿāmalah).", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Disciplining the Soul'", "badge": "Insight"}], "122": [{"arabic": "دونه وادعى الإجماع فيه واستدل بأدلة تشعر بنقيض غرضه كقوله تعالى {الذين آمنوا وعملوا الصالحات} إذ هذا يدل على أن العمل وراء الإيمان لا من نفس الإيمان وإلا فيكون العمل في حكم المعاد والعجب أنه ادعى الإجماع في هذا وهو مع ذلك ينقل قوله صلى الله عليه وسلم لا يكفر أحد إلا بعد جحوده لما أقر به (1) وينكر على المعتزلة قولهم بالتخليد في النار بسبب الكبائر والقائل بهذا قائل بنفس مذهب المعتزلة إذ يقال له من صدق بقلبه وشهد بلسانه ومات في الحال فهل هو في الجنة فلا بد أن يقول نعم وفيه حكم بوجود الإيمان دون العمل فنزيد ونقول لو بقي حيا حتى دخل عليه وقت", "english": "He was asked, \"Which deed is most excellent?\" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Islam.\" It was then asked, \"Which aspect of Islam is most excellent?\" He (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Faith (Īmān).\" This is evidence for both the distinction (ikhtilāf) between the two terms and their overlap (tadākhul). This usage is the most consistent with the conventions of the language, because faith is a deed among deeds, and it is the most excellent of them. Islam is submission (taslīm), whether by the heart, by the tongue, or by the limbs. The most excellent of these is that which is by the heart, which is the assent (taṣdīq) that is called faith. Using the two terms in a way that indicates distinction, overlap, or even complete synonymy (tarāduf) is all within the bounds of metaphorical usage (tajawwuz) in the language.", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 99", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 99: On the Distinction, Overlap, and Synonymy of Faith (Īmān) and Submission (Islām), and Their Worldly and Otherworldly Judgments", "section_index": 99, "anchors": "- Root: ايم (A-Y-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of remaining unmarried, or a state of being without a spouse; extended metaphorically to describe one who is solitary or without support.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأيامى: جمع أيم، وهي المرأة التي لا بعل لها... والحرب مأيمة، أي: يفرق بين الزوج والزوجة.\" The root carries the sense of separation or a state of lacking union, which informs the theological discussion of what constitutes the essential bond of faith.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes the state of being unmarried; metaphorical (Majaz) usage extends to anything that separates or leaves one bereft. This distinction between literal and metaphorical application is central to the chapter's method.\n- Root: قلب (Q-L-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn, overturn, or transform a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه... والانقلاب: الانصراف.\" The heart (qalb) is so named for its constant fluctuation and turning. In theological terms, it is the seat of taṣdīq (assent), the immaterial faculty of cognition and conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is physical turning; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"قلب الأمور\" — to deliberate, scrutinize, and manage affairs. This supports the distinction between the heart's internal act of assent and the external acts of the limbs.\n- Root: عمل (ʿ-A-M-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Any deed or action performed with purpose and intent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العمل: كل فعل يكون من الحيوان بقصد، فهو أخص من الفعل.\" Work is more specific than mere action, as it implies deliberate, intentional agency. It is used for both righteous and sinful deeds, and is the external manifestation of internal conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage refers to physical labor or deeds. The chapter explores whether \"work\" is an integral part of the reality of faith or a separate, consequent requirement.\n- Root: صدق (S-D-Q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be truthful, to speak the truth, to verify.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصدق والكذب أصلهما في القول... ولا يكونان بالقصد الأول إلا في القول.\" Truth and falsehood are primarily properties of speech and reports. Taṣdīq (assent) is the heart's affirmation of the truth of a report, which is the core reality of Īmān.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is truthful speech; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"صادق الحملة\" — one who is true and firm in battle or resolve. This shows that taṣdīq is not merely verbal but a firm, internal resolution.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication). This rule governs how restriction (ḥaṣr) is achieved in Arabic, often through particles like \"innamā\" or through contextual specification (takhṣīṣ). The chapter's core argument hinges on how terms like Īmān and Islām are semantically restricted, expanded, or made synonymous through contextual usage (majāz), requiring precise parsing of the predicate and its scope.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Insight"}], "123": [{"arabic": "وروي أنه لما نزل قوله تعالى من يعمل سوءا يجز به قال أبو بكر الصديق رضي الله تعالى عنه كيف الفرح بعد هذه الآية فقال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم غفر الله لك يا أبا بكر ألست تمرض ألست يصيبك الأذى ألست تحزن فهذا مما تجزون به (2) يعني أن جميع ما يصيبك يكون كفارة لذنوبك وعن عقبة بن عامر عن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم أنه قال إذا رأيتم الرجل يعطيه الله ما يحب وهو مقيم على معصيته فاعلموا أن ذلك استدراج ثم قرأ قوله تعالى فلما نسوا ما", "english": "And God is better for you than al-ʿAbbās. Ibn ʿAbbās said: \"No one consoled me more beautifully than his consolation.\"", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 1020", "section_title_en": "On Patience in Calamities — The Virtue of the Afflicted and the Reward of the Patient", "section_index": 1020, "anchors": "- Root: صبر (ṣ-b-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is restraint and retention — holding oneself back from complaint and panic; al-ṣabr is the opposite of al-jazaʿ (restless agitation).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the theological register, ṣabr is the retention of the soul upon the dictates of revelation and the restraint of the lower self from objecting to divine decree — it is half of faith, paired with shukr (gratitude).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): endurance of physical hardship. Metaphorical (majāz): \"ṣabara ʿanhu\" — to withhold oneself from something forbidden; the patient one (al-ṣābir) is he who restrains his tongue from complaint and his heart from resentment.\n- Root: بلا (b-l-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To test, to try, to afflict; ibtalā means to subject to trial for the purpose of manifesting the inner state.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Balā' in the Quran oscillates between tribulation (miḥnah) and favor (niʿmah) — both are divine tests; the believer is tried with what purifies, the disbeliever with what condemns.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the wearing away of a garment (ablā al-thawb). Metaphorical: divine trial that \"wears away\" sins as the garment wears with use.\n- Root: أجر ('-j-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Reward, recompense; the wage given in exchange for labor.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the eschatological sense, ajr is the recompense that God grants not as a debt of justice but as a bounty of grace — hence the verse {«إنما يوفى الصابرون أجرهم بغير حساب»} signals its measurelessness.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: hire for work. Metaphorical: the reward of the afterlife, since deeds are the \"work\" and paradise the \"wage.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Restriction by Innama (الحصر بإنما) — When the verse {«إنما يوفى الصابرون أجرهم بغير حساب»} employs innamā, it restricts the unmeasured reward exclusively to the patient, excluding all others from that specific bounty.", "content_type": "general", "topic": "Classical commentary from Al-Ghazali's Ihya Ulum al-Din, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Commentary"}], "125": [{"arabic": "وقال أبو سليمان لما اتخذ الله إبراهيم خليلا أوحى إليه أن وار عورتك من الأرض وكان لا يتخذ من كل شيء إلا واحدا سوى السراويل فإنه كان يتخذ سراويلين فإذا غسل أحدهما لبس الآخر حتى لا يأتي عليه حال إلا وعورته مستورة وقيل لسلمان الفارسي رضي الله عنه مالك لا تلبس الجيد من الثياب فقال وما للعبد والثوب الحسن فإذا عتق فله والله ثياب لا تبلى أبدا ويروى عن عمر بن عبد العزيز رحمه الله أنه كان له جبة شعر وكساء يلبسهما من الليل إذا قام يصلي وقال الحسن لفرقد السبخي تحسب أن لك فضلا على الناس", "english": "ʿĀ'ishah, may God Most High be well pleased with her, said: \"Forty nights would pass over us without a lamp or fire being lit in the house of the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace.\" It was said to her: \"Then by what did you live?\" She said: \"By the two black things: dates and water.\" (1) This is the abandonment of meat, broth, and condiments.", "book_title": "Poverty and Abstinence", "section_title_ar": "Section 1096", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Book on Poverty and Asceticism (Kitāb al-Faqr wa al-Zuhd): Section on the Ascetic's Conduct Regarding Food, Clothing, Dwelling, and Furnishings", "section_index": 1096, "anchors": "- Root: طوي (ṭ-w-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To fold up, to roll up; to pass the night fasting (ṭawā); to traverse a distance.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Implies enduring hunger by \"folding\" the stomach over itself—abstaining from filling it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: folding a garment. Metaphorical: the ascetic \"folds up\" his days in hunger, i.e., passes them without food.\n- Root: لبس (l-b-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To cover, to clothe; to become confused/ambiguous (il tibās).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Clothing as that which covers one's shame (saw'ah); extended metaphorically to the spouse as a \"garment\" (libās) for the other.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wearing a garment. Metaphorical: \"wearing\" a people's manner/dress (ziyy) entails becoming one of them—hence the Hadith cited: \"Whoever imitates a people's dress is of them.\"\n- Root: بني (b-n-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To build, to erect a structure; binyān: edifice.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Building beyond necessity is a sign of long hope (ṭūl al-amal) and worldly attachment, antithetical to zuhd.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: construction with mortar and brick. Metaphorical: \"raising\" oneself in pride (ʿulūw) — condemned in the Qur'anic verse cited.\n- Root: سكن (s-k-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To dwell, to be tranquil; maskan: dwelling place.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The dwelling is for repelling harm (heat, cold, eyes) — its measure is necessity (ḍarūrah), not luxury.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a place of residence. Metaphorical: the heart's \"dwelling\" on God (sakīnah) — contrasted with physical dwellings that distract.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to delimit the degrees of zuhd: \"The least degree is only what repels heat and cold...\" (innamā yuqṣad), restricting the licit to the necessary.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Poverty and Abstinence'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "126": [{"arabic": "قوله لله ما في السموات وما في الأرض إلى آخرها وقل هو الله أحد خمس عشرة مرة (3) وصف من ثوابه في الحديث ما يخرج عن الحصر وقال كرز بن وبرة وهو من الأبدال قلت للخضر عليه السلام علمني شيئا أعمله في كل ليلة فقال إذا صليت المغرب فقم إلى وقت صلاة العشاء مصليا من غير أن تكلم أحدا وأقبل على صلاتك التي أنت فيها وسلم من كل ركعتين واقرأ في كل ركعة فاتحة الكتاب مرة وقل هو الله أحد ثلاثا فإن فرغت من صلاتك انصرف إلى منزلك ولا تكلم أحدا وصل ركعتين واقرأ فاتحة الكتاب وقل هو الله أحد سبع مرات في كل ر", "english": "The Messenger of God (peace be upon him) said — according to what ʿĀ'ishah (may God be pleased with her) narrated: \"Verily, the most excellent of prayers in the sight of God is the Maghrib prayer. He has not exempted from it either the traveler or the resident. By it He opened the prayer of the night, and by it He sealed the prayer of the day. Whoever prays the Maghrib and prays after it two units (rakʿatayn), God will build for him a palace (qaṣr) in Paradise.\"", "book_title": "Arrangement of Litanies", "section_title_ar": "Section 271", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 271: On the Virtue of the Maghrib Prayer and the Facilitating Causes for the Night Vigil (Qiyām al-Layl)", "section_index": 271, "anchors": "- Root: غرب (gh-r-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of disappearance, setting, and the west; al-ghurāb is the setting of the sun.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الغرب: غيبوبة الشمس\" — the occultation of the sun; the Maghrib prayer is named for its time, marking the transition from the day's light into the night's dominion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes the sun's setting; metaphorically (Majaz), it signifies a boundary or point of transition — here, the temporal boundary between the daytime prayers and the night vigil.\n- Root: رسل (r-s-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sending forth with gentleness and ease; a messenger (rasūl) is one dispatched.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل الرسل: الانبعاث على التؤدة\" — the root denotes a measured, deliberate sending forth; from this derives the concept of the prophetic messenger.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"أرسل الله في الأمم رسلا\" (God sent messengers to the nations). Metaphorical: \"أرسل الله عليهم العذاب\" (God loosed chastisement upon them) — here, the ḥadīth's \"أرسل\" (in the chain) is literal transmission.\n- Root: ركع (r-k-')\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Bowing; the physical posture of inclination in prayer.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الركوع: الانحناء\" — used literally for the prayer posture and metaphorically for humility and submission before God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"شيخ راكع: منحن من الكبر\" (an old man bowed with age). Metaphorical: \"لغبت الإبل حتى ركعت\" (camels fatigued until they knelt) — in the ḥadīth, \"ركعات\" (units of prayer) are named for the bowing that defines them.\n- Root: صلا (ṣ-l-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root of prayer (ṣalāh); also linked to the fire of kindling (taṣliyah).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل الصلي الإيقاد بالنار\" — the root denotes kindling fire; the ṣalāh is so named for the \"burning\" of devotion or for the supplication that \"kindles\" the heart toward God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The term ṣalāh in the Sacred Law is a transferred (naql) technical designation for the specific acts of bowing, prostration, and recitation — distinct from its lexical sense of supplication (duʿā').\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governing the permissibility of interposing elements between a preposition and its object, as in \"لم يحطها عن مسافر ولا عن مقيم\" where the pronominal object (ها) is separated from the second prepositional phrase (عن مقيم) by the conjunction, maintaining grammatical cohesion through parallel structure.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Arrangement of Litanies'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "129": [{"arabic": "قال تعالى {ولن تستطيعوا أن تعدلوا بين النساء ولو حرصتم فلا تميلوا كل الميل فتذروها كالمعلقة} فليس كل من عجز عن الإتيان بالخير كله ينبغي أن يأتي بالشر كله ولكن بعض الشر أهون من بعض وبعض الخير أرفع من بعض فهذه حقيقة الغضب ودرجاته نسأله الله حسن التوفيق لما يرضيه إنه على ما يشاء قدير", "english": "As for deficiency: it is the loss of this power or its weakness. This is blameworthy, and it is of such a one that it is said: \"He has no protective zeal (ḥamiyyah).\" For this reason al-Shāfiʿī — may God have mercy on him — said: \"Whoever is provoked to anger and does not become angry is a donkey.\" Whoever loses the power of anger and protective zeal entirely is utterly deficient. God — exalted be He — described the Companions of the Prophet — peace be upon him — with severity and protective zeal, saying: {«أشداء على الكفار رحماء بينهم»} \"Severe against the disbelievers, merciful among themselves\" (Q 48:29). And He said to His Prophet — peace be upon him: {«جاهد الكفار والمنافقين واغلظ عليهم»} \"Strive against the disbelievers and the hypocrites and be harsh with them\" (Q 9:73) — the verse. Harshness and severity are but effects of the power of protective zeal, which is anger.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Envy", "section_title_ar": "Section 703", "section_title_en": "The Reality of Anger (Ghaḍab) and Its Degrees — Book 21, Section 703", "section_index": 703, "anchors": "- Root: غضب (Ghaḍab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes anger, rage, fury; the surge of blood in the heart seeking vengeance.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الغضب: ثوران دم القلب إرادة الانتقام\" — when attributed to God, it means solely His retribution, not an emotional state.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"غضبت لفلان\" (anger for the living); Metaphorical (majāz): \"غضبت به\" (anger over the dead), and the poetic extension to inanimate objects like fire or reins.\n- Root: نفس (Nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Self, soul, spirit; the vital principle.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The soul as the seat of blameworthy traits when unrestrained; its purification is the goal of spiritual struggle.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the self; Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه\" (spilled his blood/life).\n- Root: قلب (Qalb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The heart; to turn over, to flip.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named for its constant fluctuation and turning.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical turning; Metaphorical: the heart as the seat of intellect and spiritual perception, which anger overturns and blinds.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to distinguish the three degrees of anger (deficiency, excess, moderation) as mutually exclusive categories.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Condemnation of Envy'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "قال الله تعالى {ولن تستطيعوا أن تعدلوا بين النساء ولو حرصتم} أي أن تعدلوا في شهوة القلب وميل النفس ويتبع ذلك التفاوت في الوقاع وكان رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم يعدل بينهن في العطاء والبيتوتة في الليالي ويقول اللهم هذا جهدي فيما أملك ولا طاقة لي فيما تملك ولا أملك (3) يعني الحب وقد كانت عائشة رضي الله عنها أحب نسائه إليه (4) وسائر نسائه يعرفن ذلك وكان يطاف به محمولا في مرضه في كل يوم وكل ليلة فيبيت عند كل واحدة منهن ويقول أين أنا غدا ففطنت لذلك امرأة منهن فقالت إنما يسأل عن يوم عائشة فقلن يا رسول الله قد", "english": "I say: The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, has said: «من كان له امرأتان فمال إلى إحداهما دون الأخرى» — \"Whoever has two wives and inclines toward one of them to the exclusion of the other\" — and in another wording: «ولم يعدل بينهما جاء يوم القيامة وأحد شقيه مائل» — \"and does not act equitably between them, he shall come on the Day of Resurrection with one of his sides leaning.\"", "book_title": "Etiquette of Marriage", "section_title_ar": "Section 305", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Book on the Etiquette of Marriage: Section 305 (On Justice Between Co-Wives, Nushuz, and the Etiquette of Intercourse)", "section_index": 305, "anchors": "- Root: عدل (ʿadl)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to be equal, to balance, to straighten\" — the root denotes equity and even-handedness in distribution.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological usage, ʿadl is the mean of justice, opposed to jawr (tyranny) — it is the act of placing things in their proper stations.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage pertains to measurable distribution (ʿatāʾ, mabīt); metaphorical (Majāz) usage extends to that which is not under voluntary control, such as love (maḥabbah) and inclination of the heart.\n- Root: نشز (nushūz)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes \"rising up\" or \"elevation\"; in marital context it signifies a rising of one spouse against the other in discord or recalcitrance.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Nushūz is the rebellion of one spouse against the bond of obedience and harmony, a moral elevation against the other.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage is the physical rising from a place; metaphorical usage is the spiritual/moral rising in defiance.\n- Root: وقع (waqʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes \"falling\" or \"occurrence\"; in the marital context, it is a euphemism for sexual intercourse.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The term is used in the Quran for the \"falling\" of an event; here it is a euphemistic transfer (naql) for coitus.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage is physical falling; metaphorical usage is the act of intercourse, as in the hadith \"لا يقعن أحدكم على امرأته\" (Let none of you fall upon his wife like a beast).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The particle innamā restricts the predicate to the subject alone, as in the hadith \"إنما عليه العدل في العطاء والمبيت\" (Upon him is only justice in expenditure and overnight stays), confining the obligation to what is within human capacity.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Etiquette of Marriage'", "badge": "Insight"}], "130": [{"arabic": "{وإن يتفرقا يغن الله كلا من سعته} الرابع أن لا يفشي سرها لا في الطلاق ولا عند النكاح فقد ورد في إفشاء سر النساء في الخبر الصحيح وعيد عظيم (1) ويروى عن بعض الصالحين أنه أراد طلاق امرأة فقيل له ما الذي يريبك فيها فقال العاقل لا يهتك ستر امرأته فلما طلقها قيل له لم طلقتها فقال مالي ولا مرأة غيري فهذا بيان ما على الزوج", "english": "Among those whose many divorces were mentioned from the Messenger of God — peace be upon him — was ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Hishām. Al-Ḥasan entered upon him in his house, and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān honored him greatly, seated him in his own place, and said: \"Would you not have sent for me, and I would have come to you?\" Al-Ḥasan said: \"I have a need.\" He said: \"What is it?\" He said: \"I have come to you as a suitor for your daughter.\" ʿAbd al-Raḥmān lowered his head, then raised it and said: \"By God, there is no one upon the face of the earth who walks upon it more beloved to me than you. But you know that my daughter is a piece of me (baḍʿah minnī) — what grieves her grieves me, and what delights her delights me — and you are a frequent divorcer (miṭlāq). I fear that you will divorce her, and if you do, I dread that my heart would change toward you in my love — and I would hate for my heart to change toward you, for you are a piece of the Messenger of God — peace be upon him. If you will stipulate that you will not divorce her, I will marry her to you.\" Al-Ḥasan remained silent, then rose and went out. One of his household said: \"I heard him walking and saying: 'ʿAbd al-Raḥmān only wanted to make his daughter a collar around my neck.'\"", "book_title": "Etiquette of Marriage", "section_title_ar": "Section 308", "section_title_en": "** On the Husband's Obligations: The Fourth Duty — Concealing the Wife's Secrets, and the Narratives Concerning al-Ḥasan's Many Divorces", "section_index": 308, "anchors": "**\n- Root: بضع (baḍʿa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"بضع من الشاة بضعة إذا قطع قطعة\" — to cut a piece; the piece itself\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأصل في هذه الكلمة: البضع وهو جملة من اللحم تبضع، أي: تقطع... وكني بالبضع عن الفرج\" — the root denotes a piece of flesh cut off; metaphorically extended to the pudendum, and by further extension to the child as \"a piece\" of the parent\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Majaz/Metaphorical): \"من رضع معك رضعه، فهو منك بضعه، أي هو بعضك\" — the child is \"a piece\" (baḍʿah) of the father, as though cut from him, sharing his substance and nature\n- Root: طلق (ṭalaqa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"متحرك. وطَلْقٌ وطَلَق: اسمان\" — the core sense of release from a tether\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل الطلاق: التخلية من الوثاق... ومنه استعير: طلقت المرأة، نحو: خليتها فهي طالق، أي: مخلاة عن حبالة النكاح\" — divorce is the release from the bond of marriage, as releasing a camel from its hobble\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Haqiqah/Literal): \"أطلقت الأسير، وهو طليق\" — literal release of a captive; \"طلقت المرأة وطلقت فهي طالق وهن طوالق\" — metaphorical extension to the divorced woman\n- Root: حسن (ḥasana)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"المَلقَة\" — beauty, goodness\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحسن: عبارة عن كل مبهج مرغوب فيه\" — that which pleases and is desired; \"الحسنة يعبر عنها عن كل ما يسر\" — a good thing is what brings joy\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Majaz/Metaphorical): \"اجلس حسنا\" — used adverbially for \"well\"; \"فلان لا يحسن شيئا\" — to do well, to master\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — the permissibility of interposing elements between a governing word and its governed prepositional phrase when the intervening material constitutes a complete, self-sufficient clause, as in the conditional constructions employed in the marriage stipulation narrative\n\n---\n\n**", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Etiquette of Marriage'", "badge": "Insight"}], "131": [{"arabic": "أوصى الله تعالى الأولين والآخرين بالتقوى فقال تعالى {ولقد وصينا الذين أوتوا الكتاب من قبلكم وإياكم أن اتقوا الله} وقال عز وجل {وخافون إن كنتم مؤمنين} فأمر بالخوف وأوجبه وشرطه في الإيمان فلذلك لا يتصور أن ينفك مؤمن عن خوف وإن ضعف ويكون ضعف خوفه بحسب ضعف معرفته وإيمانه وقال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم في فضيلة التقوى وإذا جمع الله الأولين والآخرين لميقات يوم معلوم فإذا هم بصوت يسمع أقصاهم كما يسمع أدناهم فيقول يا أيها الناس إني قد أنصت لكم مند خلقتكم إلى يومكم هذا فانصتوا إلي اليوم إنما هي أعمالكم ترد عليكم أيها الناس إني قد جعلت نسبا وجعلتم نسبا فوضعتم ن", "english": "For they possess the Highest Companion (al-Rafīq al-Aʿlā) in which none share with them. Consider how He singled them out for the companionship of the Highest Companion — and that is because they are the scholars (ʿulamāʾ), and the scholars hold the rank of companionship with the prophets, for they are the heirs of the prophets. And the companionship of the Highest Companion belongs to the prophets and to those who attach themselves to them. For this reason, when the Messenger of God — may God's blessings and peace be upon him — was given the choice during his death-illness between remaining in this world and proceeding to God — may He be exalted — he would say: \"I ask of Thee the Highest Companion\" {«الرفيق الأعلى»}.", "book_title": "Fear and Hope", "section_title_ar": "Section 1042", "section_title_en": "On the Excellence of Fear (Khawf) and Its Inseparability from Faith — Section 1042", "section_index": 1042, "anchors": "- Root: خوف (Khawf)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes trepidation, apprehension, and the state of being afraid; its opposite is amn (security).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخوف: توقع مكروه عن أمارة مظنونة، أو معلومة\" — anticipation of something disliked based on an indication, whether conjectural or certain; it is the direct opposite of amn (security), and applies to both worldly and otherworldly matters.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"خفته على مالي خوفا وخيفة\" — to fear for something. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"تخوفه: تنقصه\" — to diminish gradually, as fear erodes the soul's complacency.\n- Root: حدث (Hadath)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Newness, origination after non-existence; also that which is recent in time.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحدوث: كون الشيء بعد أن لم يكن\" — the coming-to-be of a thing after it was not; true origination belongs to God alone.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"هو حدث من الأحداث\" — one who is recent/young. Metaphorical: \"كان عمر محدثا أي صادق الحدس\" — one whose intuition is true, as if divinely inspired.\n- Root: يوم (Yawm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A day; a period of time from sunrise to sunset, or any duration whatsoever.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اليوم يعبر به عن وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها. وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — used for the day proper or any extended period; \"أيام الله\" refers to His mighty deeds and visitations upon the wicked.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"ما رأيته اليوم\" — temporal designation. Metaphorical: \"ذكر في أيام العرب كذا أي في وقائعها\" — \"days\" as battles and momentous events.\n- Root: قوي (Quwwa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Strength, power, capability; the opposite of weakness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"القوة تستعمل تارة في معنى القدرة\" — power as capability; also potentiality, as when a date-stone is said to be \"potentially\" a palm-tree.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"هو قوي مقو\" — physically strong. Metaphorical: strength of heart, of conviction, and of allies.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — When the particle innamā is employed, it restricts the predicate exclusively to the subject mentioned, negating it for all others. This governs the author's argument that taqwā is exclusively attributed to God's nearness and that the \"Highest Companion\" (al-Rafīq al-Aʿlā) is uniquely reserved for the prophets and their heirs.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Fear and Hope'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "قوله لله ما في السموات وما في الأرض إلى آخرها وقل هو الله أحد خمس عشرة مرة (3) وصف من ثوابه في الحديث ما يخرج عن الحصر وقال كرز بن وبرة وهو من الأبدال قلت للخضر عليه السلام علمني شيئا أعمله في كل ليلة فقال إذا صليت المغرب فقم إلى وقت صلاة العشاء مصليا من غير أن تكلم أحدا وأقبل على صلاتك التي أنت فيها وسلم من كل ركعتين واقرأ في كل ركعة فاتحة الكتاب مرة وقل هو الله أحد ثلاثا فإن فرغت من صلاتك انصرف إلى منزلك ولا تكلم أحدا وصل ركعتين واقرأ فاتحة الكتاب وقل هو الله أحد سبع مرات في كل ر", "english": "The Messenger of God (peace be upon him) said — according to what ʿĀ'ishah (may God be pleased with her) narrated: \"Verily, the most excellent of prayers in the sight of God is the Maghrib prayer. He has not exempted from it either the traveler or the resident. By it He opened the prayer of the night, and by it He sealed the prayer of the day. Whoever prays the Maghrib and prays after it two units (rakʿatayn), God will build for him a palace (qaṣr) in Paradise.\"", "book_title": "Arrangement of Litanies", "section_title_ar": "Section 271", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 271: On the Virtue of the Maghrib Prayer and the Facilitating Causes for the Night Vigil (Qiyām al-Layl)", "section_index": 271, "anchors": "- Root: غرب (gh-r-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of disappearance, setting, and the west; al-ghurāb is the setting of the sun.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الغرب: غيبوبة الشمس\" — the occultation of the sun; the Maghrib prayer is named for its time, marking the transition from the day's light into the night's dominion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes the sun's setting; metaphorically (Majaz), it signifies a boundary or point of transition — here, the temporal boundary between the daytime prayers and the night vigil.\n- Root: رسل (r-s-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sending forth with gentleness and ease; a messenger (rasūl) is one dispatched.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل الرسل: الانبعاث على التؤدة\" — the root denotes a measured, deliberate sending forth; from this derives the concept of the prophetic messenger.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"أرسل الله في الأمم رسلا\" (God sent messengers to the nations). Metaphorical: \"أرسل الله عليهم العذاب\" (God loosed chastisement upon them) — here, the ḥadīth's \"أرسل\" (in the chain) is literal transmission.\n- Root: ركع (r-k-')\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Bowing; the physical posture of inclination in prayer.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الركوع: الانحناء\" — used literally for the prayer posture and metaphorically for humility and submission before God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"شيخ راكع: منحن من الكبر\" (an old man bowed with age). Metaphorical: \"لغبت الإبل حتى ركعت\" (camels fatigued until they knelt) — in the ḥadīth, \"ركعات\" (units of prayer) are named for the bowing that defines them.\n- Root: صلا (ṣ-l-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root of prayer (ṣalāh); also linked to the fire of kindling (taṣliyah).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل الصلي الإيقاد بالنار\" — the root denotes kindling fire; the ṣalāh is so named for the \"burning\" of devotion or for the supplication that \"kindles\" the heart toward God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The term ṣalāh in the Sacred Law is a transferred (naql) technical designation for the specific acts of bowing, prostration, and recitation — distinct from its lexical sense of supplication (duʿā').\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governing the permissibility of interposing elements between a preposition and its object, as in \"لم يحطها عن مسافر ولا عن مقيم\" where the pronominal object (ها) is separated from the second prepositional phrase (عن مقيم) by the conjunction, maintaining grammatical cohesion through parallel structure.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Arrangement of Litanies'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "132": [{"arabic": "قوله لله ما في السموات وما في الأرض إلى آخرها وقل هو الله أحد خمس عشرة مرة (3) وصف من ثوابه في الحديث ما يخرج عن الحصر وقال كرز بن وبرة وهو من الأبدال قلت للخضر عليه السلام علمني شيئا أعمله في كل ليلة فقال إذا صليت المغرب فقم إلى وقت صلاة العشاء مصليا من غير أن تكلم أحدا وأقبل على صلاتك التي أنت فيها وسلم من كل ركعتين واقرأ في كل ركعة فاتحة الكتاب مرة وقل هو الله أحد ثلاثا فإن فرغت من صلاتك انصرف إلى منزلك ولا تكلم أحدا وصل ركعتين واقرأ فاتحة الكتاب وقل هو الله أحد سبع مرات في كل ر", "english": "The Messenger of God (peace be upon him) said — according to what ʿĀ'ishah (may God be pleased with her) narrated: \"Verily, the most excellent of prayers in the sight of God is the Maghrib prayer. He has not exempted from it either the traveler or the resident. By it He opened the prayer of the night, and by it He sealed the prayer of the day. Whoever prays the Maghrib and prays after it two units (rakʿatayn), God will build for him a palace (qaṣr) in Paradise.\"", "book_title": "Arrangement of Litanies", "section_title_ar": "Section 271", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 271: On the Virtue of the Maghrib Prayer and the Facilitating Causes for the Night Vigil (Qiyām al-Layl)", "section_index": 271, "anchors": "- Root: غرب (gh-r-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of disappearance, setting, and the west; al-ghurāb is the setting of the sun.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الغرب: غيبوبة الشمس\" — the occultation of the sun; the Maghrib prayer is named for its time, marking the transition from the day's light into the night's dominion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes the sun's setting; metaphorically (Majaz), it signifies a boundary or point of transition — here, the temporal boundary between the daytime prayers and the night vigil.\n- Root: رسل (r-s-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sending forth with gentleness and ease; a messenger (rasūl) is one dispatched.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل الرسل: الانبعاث على التؤدة\" — the root denotes a measured, deliberate sending forth; from this derives the concept of the prophetic messenger.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"أرسل الله في الأمم رسلا\" (God sent messengers to the nations). Metaphorical: \"أرسل الله عليهم العذاب\" (God loosed chastisement upon them) — here, the ḥadīth's \"أرسل\" (in the chain) is literal transmission.\n- Root: ركع (r-k-')\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Bowing; the physical posture of inclination in prayer.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الركوع: الانحناء\" — used literally for the prayer posture and metaphorically for humility and submission before God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"شيخ راكع: منحن من الكبر\" (an old man bowed with age). Metaphorical: \"لغبت الإبل حتى ركعت\" (camels fatigued until they knelt) — in the ḥadīth, \"ركعات\" (units of prayer) are named for the bowing that defines them.\n- Root: صلا (ṣ-l-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root of prayer (ṣalāh); also linked to the fire of kindling (taṣliyah).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل الصلي الإيقاد بالنار\" — the root denotes kindling fire; the ṣalāh is so named for the \"burning\" of devotion or for the supplication that \"kindles\" the heart toward God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The term ṣalāh in the Sacred Law is a transferred (naql) technical designation for the specific acts of bowing, prostration, and recitation — distinct from its lexical sense of supplication (duʿā').\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governing the permissibility of interposing elements between a preposition and its object, as in \"لم يحطها عن مسافر ولا عن مقيم\" where the pronominal object (ها) is separated from the second prepositional phrase (عن مقيم) by the conjunction, maintaining grammatical cohesion through parallel structure.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Arrangement of Litanies'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "135": [{"arabic": "قليلا منهم كانوا ينهون عن الفساد وقال تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا كونوا قوامين بالقسط شهداء لله ولو على أنفسكم أو الوالدين والأقربين وذلك هو الأمر بالمعروف للوالدين والأقربين وقال تعالى لا خير في كثير من نجواهم إلا من أمر بصدقة أو معروف أو إصلاح بين الناس ومن يفعل ذلك ابتغاء مرضاة الله فسوف نؤتيه أجرا عظيما وقال تعالى وإن طائفتان من المؤمنين اقتتلوا فأصلحوا بينهما الآية والإصلاح نهي عن البغي وإعادة إلى الطاعة فإن لم يفعل فقد أمر الله تعالى بقتاله فقال فقاتلوا التي تبغي حتى تفيء إلى أمر الله وذلك هو النهي عن المنكر وأما الأخبار فمنها ما ر", "english": "Know that the verses indicating the obligation of commanding right and forbidding wrong are numerous. Among them is His saying, may He be exalted: {«Let there arise from among you a nation inviting to good, commanding right and forbidding wrong — and those are the successful»} (Āl ʿImrān 3:104). In this verse is the declaration of obligation, for His saying, may He be exalted, \"Let there be\" (wal-takun) is a command, and the apparent meaning of a command is obligation. In it also is the declaration that success (falāḥ) is contingent upon it, for He restricted it and said: {«And those are the successful»}. In it also is the declaration that it is a collective obligation (farḍ kifāyah), not an individual obligation (farḍ ʿayn), and that when a group undertakes it, the obligation falls from the rest — for He did not say, \"Let all of you be commanders of right,\" but rather said: {«Let there arise from among you a nation»}. Thus, whenever one person or a group undertakes it, the burden falls from the others, and success is particularized to those who undertake it and directly perform it. But if all of creation were to hold back from it, the burden would encompass all those capable of it without exception.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 521", "section_title_en": "Section 521", "section_index": 521, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}], "136": [{"arabic": "رسول الله هذه زلة منك وهل الإيمان إلا أن تؤمن بالله وملائكته وكتبه ورسله والبعث والميزان وتقيم الصلاة والصوم والزكاة ولنا ذنوب لو نعلم أنها تغفر لنا لعلمنا أننا من أهل الجنة فمن أجل ذلك نقول إنا مؤمنون ولا نقول إنا من أهل الجنة فقال ابن مسعود صدقت والله إنها مني زلة فينبغي أن يقال كان خوضهم فيه قليلا لا كثيرا وقصيرا لا طويلا وعند الحاجة لا بطريق التصنيف والتدريس واتخاذه صناعة فيقال أما قلة خوضهم فيه فإنه كان لقلة الحاجة إذا لم تكن البدعة تظهر في ذلك الزمان وأما القصر فقد كان الغاية إفحام الخصم واعترافه وانكشاف الحق", "english": "The mention of the proof (ḥujjah), the demand for it, and the search after it — how could these be prohibited, when God — may He be exalted — has said: {«قُلْ هَاتُوا بُرْهَانَكُمْ»} (Say: Bring forth your proof — Qur'an 2:111), and He — mighty and majestic — has said: {«لِيَهْلِكَ مَنْ هَلَكَ عَنْ بَيِّنَةٍ وَيَحْيَا مَنْ حَيَّ عَنْ بَيِّنَةٍ»} (So that he who perishes may perish upon clear evidence, and he who lives may live upon clear evidence — Qur'an 8:42), and He — may He be exalted — has said: {«هَلْ عِنْدَكُمْ مِنْ سُلْطَانٍ بِهَذَا»} (Have you any authority for this? — Qur'an 10:68), meaning a proof and demonstration, and He — may He be exalted — has said: {«قُلْ فَلِلَّهِ الْحُجَّةُ الْبَالِغَةُ»} (Say: To God belongs the conclusive proof — Qur'an 6:149), and He — may He be exalted — has said: {«أَلَمْ تَرَ إِلَى الَّذِي حَاجَّ إِبْرَاهِيمَ فِي رَبِّهِ»} (Have you not considered him who disputed with Abraham concerning his Lord? — Qur'an 2:258) up to His words: {«فَبُهِتَ الَّذِي كَفَرَ»} (So the one who disbelieved was confounded — Qur'an 2:258).", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 77", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 77: On the Permissibility of Theological Discourse (Kalām) and Its Early Practice", "section_index": 77, "anchors": "- Root: ح-د-ث (ḥ-d-th)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Al-ḥudūth: the coming-to-be of a thing after it was not; al-ḥadath: the novel event; al-ḥīdāth: origination.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-ḥudūth: a thing's becoming after it was not, whether accident or substance; iḥdāth: bringing it into existence. The origination of substances belongs to God alone.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"Literal: 'He is a recent event among events.' Metaphorical: 'They became topics of conversation (aḥādīth).'\"\n- Root: ح-ا-ج (ḥ-ā-j)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Al-ḥājah: need of a thing, with desire for it; its plural: ḥājāt and ḥawā'ij.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-ḥājah ilā al-shay': poverty toward it with love of it. God said: {«إِلَّا حَاجَةً فِي نَفْسِ يَعْقُوبَ»} (except a need in Jacob's soul — Qur'an 12:68).\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"Literal: need and want. The 'ḥājah' is that which one seeks to obtain.\"\n- Root: ك-و-ن (k-w-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Kāna: to be, to exist; takwīn: bringing into being from non-existence.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-kawn: existence after non-existence; the muḥdath (originated thing) is that brought forth after it was not.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"Literal: being and becoming. Metaphorical: the continuous states of affairs.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: Al-Faṣl bayna al-Jār wa-al-Majrūr (Prepositional Interposition) — The permissibility of separating a preposition from its object when the intervening clause is complete in meaning, as in the verse's structure of argumentation where the proof (ḥujjah) stands independently before its conclusion.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Afterlife"}, {"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "لما سأله عن الإيمان فقال أن تؤمن بالله وملائكته وكتبه ورسله واليوم الآخر وبالبعث بعد الموت وبالحساب وبالقدر خيره وشره فقال فما الإسلام فأجاب بذكر الخصال الخمس (3) فعبر بالإسلام عن تسليم الظاهر بالقول والعمل وفي الحديث عن سعد أنه صلى الله عليه وسلم أعطى رجلا عطاء ولم يعط الآخر فقال له سعد يا رسول الله تركت فلانا لم تعطه وهو مؤمن فقال صلى الله عليه وسلم أو مسلم فأعاد عليه فأعاد رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم حديث سعد أعطى رجلا عطاء ولم يعط الآخر فقال له سعد يا رسول الله تركت فلانا لم تعطه وهو مؤمن فقال أو مسلم الحديث أخ", "english": "The truth in this matter is that faith (iman) is an expression for assent (tasdiq). God, exalted be He, has said: {وَمَا أَنتَ بِمُؤْمِنٍ لِّنَا} [And you are not one to believe us] (Quran 12:17) — that is, not one to assent to us. And Islam is an expression for surrender (taslim) and self-abandonment (istislam) through acquiescence (idhan) and compliance (inqiyad), and the abandonment of recalcitrance, refusal, obstinacy, and stubbornness.", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 98", "section_title_en": "On Whether Islam and Faith (Iman) Are Identical or Distinct", "section_index": 98, "anchors": "- Root: أمن (amana)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes safety, security, and freedom from fear; the isti'man form yields 'belief' as an act of granting credence and trust.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الإيمان هو التصديق\" — Faith (iman) is fundamentally assent (tasdiq), a conviction of the heart, not a mere verbal profession.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage ties iman to tasdiq (verification/assent); metaphorical extension applies it to outward compliance only in a derived sense.\n- Root: سلم (salima)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes soundness, safety, and freedom from defect; the istislam form yields 'submission' as an act of yielding and surrendering.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الإسلام عبارة عن التسليم والاستسلام\" — Islam is surrender (taslim) and self-abandonment (istislam) through compliance and obedience.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage encompasses outward acts of the limbs; metaphorical (Majaz) usage can extend to inward assent, but the primary domain is the external.\n- Root: صدق (sadaqa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes truthfulness and veracity in speech and report.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصدق والكذب أصلهما في القول\" — Truth and falsehood originate in speech; tasdiq is the act of affirming a report as true.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is in verbal confirmation; metaphorical extension applies to 'true in resolve' or 'true in attack,' i.e., confirming through action.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Interposition (باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — Governing the permissible separation between a governing word and its governed prepositional phrase, relevant here for parsing the conditional and contrastive structures in the theological arguments.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "140": [{"arabic": "تعالى فلا تقعدوا معهم حتى يخوضوا في حديث غيره إنكم إذا مثلهم وقال سلمان أكثر الناس ذنوبا يوم القيامة أكثرهم كلاما في معصية الله وقال ابن سيرين كان رجل من الأنصار يمر بمجلس لهم فيقول لهم توضئوا فإن بعض ما تقولون شر من الحدث فهذا هو الخوض في الباطل وهو وراء ما سيأتي من الغيبة والنميمة والفحش وغيرها بل هو الخوض في ذكر محظورات سبق وجودها أو تدبر للتوصل إليها من غير حاجة دينية إلى ذكرها ويدخل فيه أيضا الخوض في حكاية البدع والمذاهب الفاسدة وحكاية ما جرى من قتال الصحابة على وجه يوهم الطعن في بعضهم وكل ذلك باطل", "english": "For Bilāl ibn al-Ḥārith has related that the Messenger of God — may God's blessings and peace be upon him — said: \"Indeed, a man speaks a word of God's good pleasure, not imagining that it reaches what it reaches, and God writes for him thereby His good pleasure until the Day of Resurrection. And indeed, a man speaks a word of God's wrath, not imagining that it reaches what it reaches, and God writes against him thereby His wrath until the Day of Resurrection.\" [The hadith of Bilāl ibn al-Ḥārith — \"Indeed, a man speaks a word of God's good pleasure\" — was transmitted by Ibn Mājah and al-Tirmidhī, who said it is ḥasan ṣaḥīḥ.] And ʿAlqamah used to say: \"How much speech has the hadith of Bilāl ibn al-Ḥārith prevented me from [uttering]!\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of Anger and Hatred", "section_title_ar": "Section 661", "section_title_en": "The Third Affliction: Wading into Falsehood (al-Khawḍ fī al-Bāṭil)", "section_index": 661, "anchors": "- Root: خوض (Kha-Wa-D)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is wading or plunging into water, passing through it; the Kitab al-Ayn records it as \"الشروع في الماء والمرور فيه\" (entering into water and passing through it).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological usage, it is predominantly employed in the Quranic context of blameworthy engagement — as in {«وخضتم كالذي خاضوا»} (And you waded as those before you waded) and {«ذرهم في خوضهم يلعبون»} (Leave them in their wading, playing). It denotes immersion in that which is vain or false.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) — physical wading in water. Metaphorical (Majaz) — \"خاضوا في الحديث وتخاوضوا فيه\" (they waded into speech and waded together in it), i.e., plunging into discourse; \"وهو يخوض مع الخائضين أي يبطل مع المبطلين\" (he wades with the waders, i.e., he engages in falsehood with those who engage in falsehood).\n- Root: بطل (Ba-Ta-La)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The Kitab al-Ayn situates it within the semantic field of that which is null, void, or futile.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الباطل: نقيض الحق، وهو ما لا ثبات له عند الفحص عنه\" (Al-Bāṭil is the opposite of truth, and it is that which has no stability upon examination). It applies to both speech and deed.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — \"هو باطل بين البطلان\" (it is false, clearly false). Metaphorical — the verbal form \"بطل\" (to become null) and \"أبطله غيره\" (another nullified it), as in {«وبطل ما كانوا يعملون»} (And nullified is what they used to do).\n- Root: كلم (Ka-La-Ma)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The Kitab al-Ayn treats it under the root of speech and utterance.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الكلم: التأثير المدرك بإحدى الحاستين\" (Speech: the effect perceived by one of the two senses) — indicating that speech is that which produces an impression.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — \"سمعته يتكلم بكذا\" (I heard him speaking such-and-such). Metaphorical — \"وهذه كلمة شاعرة\" (this is a penetrating word) and \"وهذا مما يكلم العرض والدين\" (this is something that wounds honor and religion) — speech that injures.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governing the permissibility of interposing elements between a preposition and its object, relevant here to the syntactic structure of \"الخوض في الباطل\" where the verbal noun governs its prepositional complement, and to the periodic constructions of the hadith citations where the conditional clauses (\"إن الرجل ليتكلم بالكلمة...\") require careful parsing of the apodosis.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Condemnation of Anger and Hatred'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "141": [{"arabic": "وما جعل الله للكافرين على المؤمنين سبيلا وأما مجرد قوله لا تزن فليس بمحرم عليه من حيث إنه نهى عن الزنا ولكن من حيث إنه إظهار دالة الاحتكام على المسلم وفيه إذلال للمحتكم عليه والفاسق يستحق الإذلال ولكن لا من الكافر الذي هو أولى بالذل منه فهذا وجه منعنا إياه من الحسبة وإلا فلسنا نقول إن الكافر يعاقب بسبب قوله لا تزن من حيث إنه نهى بل نقول إنه إذا لم يقل لا تزن يعاقب عليه إن رأينا خطاب الكافر بفروع الدين وفيه نظر استوفيناه في الفقهيات ولا يليق بغرضنا الآن", "english": "If it is said: \"Then let the unbelieving dhimmī (protected non-Muslim subject) be permitted to enjoin right upon a Muslim if he sees him committing fornication — for his saying 'Do not fornicate' is true in itself, so it is impossible that it be unlawful for him; rather, it ought to be permissible or even obligatory.\"", "book_title": "Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil", "section_title_ar": "Section 525", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Book of Blame of Status-Seeking and Hypocrisy: Section 525 — The First Pillar: The One Who Enjoins Right (Al-Muḥtasib)", "section_index": 525, "anchors": "- Root: حسب (ḥ-s-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To count, reckon, compute; to hold an opinion or estimation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحساب: استعمال العدد\" — the act of employing number; the reckoning of deeds. The muḥtasib is one who \"reckons\" or \"takes account\" of others' actions, demanding the right and forbidding the wrong.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah (Literal): \"حسب المال\" — he counted the wealth. Majaz (Metaphorical): \"يَتَحَسَّبانِ الأَخبار\" — they seek out and ascertain news, as one places assumption in the position of knowledge. The \"ḥisbah\" is the act of account-taking, extended metaphorically to the duty of commanding right and forbidding wrong.\n- Root: قهر (q-h-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To overcome, subdue, dominate; to force against one's will.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"القهر: الغلبة والتذليل معا\" — overpowering and subjugation together. It is used of God's absolute dominance over His creation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah: \"أخذتهم قهرة: من غير رضاهم\" — he seized them by force, without their consent. Majaz: \"جبال قواهر: شوامخ\" — towering, overbearing mountains. The \"qahr\" in ḥisbah is the coercive enforcement of the right, distinct from mere verbal admonition.\n- Root: فسق (f-s-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To emerge, exit, depart; of a date, to split from its skin.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"فسق فلان: خرج عن حجر الشرع\" — he went out from the enclosure of the Sacred Law. It is more general than unbelief (kufr), applying to the one who commits to the Law's rulings then violates them, in whole or in part.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah: \"فسق عن أمر الله: خرج\" — he departed from God's command. Majaz: \"وفسقت الرطبة عن قشرها\" — the ripe date burst from its skin. The fāsiq is one who has \"burst out\" of obedience.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The permissibility of interposing elements between a governing word and its dependent, provided the intervening clause is complete in itself, as in \"إن به...\" — governing the syntactic flow of conditional and exceptive constructions in the argumentation.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "142": [{"arabic": "وقال تعالى في ذم المنافقين ولا يذكرون الله إلا قليلا وقال عز وجل واذكر ربك في نفسك تضرعا وخيفة ودون الجهر من القول بالغدو والآصال ولا تكن من الغافلين وقال تعالى ولذكر الله أكبر قال ابن عباس رضي الله عنهما له وجهان أحدهما أن ذكر الله تعالى لكم أعظم من ذكرككم إياه والآخر أن ذكر الله أعظم من كل عبادة سواه إلى غير ذلك من الآيات وأما الأخبار فقد قال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم ذاكر الله في الغافلين كالشجرة في وسط الهشيم (1) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم ذاكر الله في الغافلين كالمقاتل بين الفارين وقال صلى ال", "english": "- Root: ذكر (dhakara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to remember, to mention, to recollect.\" The root carries the dual sense of mental recollection and verbal utterance. In Kitab al-Ayn, the root denotes the opposite of forgetfulness (nisyan), and its verbal form encompasses both the internal act of remembrance and the external act of mentioning.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الذكر: حضور المعنى في النفس، وقد يطلق على إبقاء المعنى باللسان. والذكر ضربان: ذكر بالقلب، وذكر باللسان. وقال تعالى: {فاذكروني أذكركم} [البقرة/152] — فذكر العبد ربه بالتعظيم والثناء، وذكر الله عبده بالإنعام والإكرام.\" The theological nuance distinguishes between the creature's remembrance (which is petition and praise) and the Creator's remembrance (which is response and reward).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"ذكرت الشيء: حفظته ووعيته. وذكرت فلانا: جريت بذكره على لسانك.\" Metaphorical (Majaz): \"ذكر الله العبد: أثنى عليه وأجزل له الثواب. ومن المجاز: قوله تعالى {ولذكر الله أكبر} — أي ذكر الله إياكم أعظم من ذكركم إياه.\" The rhetorical distinction is crucial here: when Allah \"remembers\" His servant, it is an act of grace, not cognition.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين المبتدأ والخبر (Interposition between Subject and Predicate) — In the verse {ولذكر الله أكبر}, the grammatical structure allows for the subject (ذكر الله) to be read as either the creature's remembrance of Allah or Allah's remembrance of the creature, with the predicate (أكبر) applying to whichever reading is adopted. This syntactic ambiguity is deliberate and productive of multiple valid interpretations.", "book_title": "On Invocations and Supplications", "section_title_ar": "Section 235", "section_title_en": "Section 235", "section_index": 235, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'", "badge": "Insight"}], "143": [{"arabic": "فما أراك إلا مخنثا بين فحولة التنزيه وأنوثة التشبيه مذبذبا بين هذا وذا لا إلى هؤلاء ولا إلى هؤلاء فكيف نزهت ذاته وصفاته تعالى عن الأجسام وصفاتها ونزهت كلامه عن معاني الحروف والأصوات وأخذت تتوقف في يده وقلمه ولوحه وخطه فإن كنت قد فهمت من قوله صلى الله عليه وسلم إن الله خلق آدم على صورته الصورة الظاهرة المدركة بالبصر فكن مشبها مطلقا كما يقال كن يهوديا صرفا وإلا فلا تلعب بالتوراة وإن فهمت منه الصورة الباطنة التي تدرك بالبصائر لا بالأبصار فكن منزها صرفا ومقدسا فحلا واطو الطريق فإنك بالواد المقدس طوى واستمع بسر قلبك لما يوحى فلعلك تجد على النار ه", "english": "The Seeker (al-sālik) then said: \"I have opened my eye and sharpened its gaze. By God, I see neither reed nor wood, nor do I know any pen except in that manner.\"", "book_title": "Monotheism and Reliance", "section_title_ar": "Section 1110", "section_title_en": "A Door from the Doors of the Kingdom Unveiled by the Pen", "section_index": 1110, "anchors": "- Root: قلم (Qalam)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The reed/implement of writing; also the clipping of something hard (nail, spear-shaft, reed). Al-Farahidi: \"القلم: ما كتب به.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The Pen is a corporeal instrument, yet in divine attribution it is stripped of material composition—its reality is spiritual (ma'nawī), not wooden or reed-like.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqiqah) = writing implement; metaphorical (majaz) = the divine pen as a subtle reality (laṭīfah) in the malakūt, transcending physical attributes.\n- Root: علم (ʿIlm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Perception of a thing by its true nature; knowledge as a divine gift.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): ʿIlm is \"إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — apprehension of a thing's reality; here, the Pen writes sciences (ʿulūm) into hearts, not as ink on tablets but as immaterial impressions.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal = knowing facts; metaphorical = the unveiling (kashf) granted to the seeker, where knowledge becomes a living guide.\n- Root: يمن (Yamin)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The right hand; blessing; oath.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Divine \"right hand\" (yamin) in {«والسماوات مطويات بيمينه»} is not a limb but a metaphor for divine power (qudrah), as with \"hand\" (yad) — see Al-Zamakhshari: \"هو ملك يمينه\" (He is the possessor of His right hand).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal = physical hand; metaphorical = sovereign power and disposal, as in \"ضربها بالميمون\" (euphemism for union).\n- Root: قلب (Qalb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Turning, overturning; the heart as the seat of fluctuating perception.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Qalb = \"تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is where the Pen inscribes, and it turns between tanzih (transcendence) and tashbih (assimilation).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal = physical heart; metaphorical = the inner faculty (basirah) that perceives the malakūt.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used in \"إنما الفرق في ظاهر الصورة\" to restrict difference to outward form alone, negating any difference in the reality of subjugation (taskhir).", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Monotheism and Reliance'", "badge": "Insight"}], "144": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}], "145": [{"arabic": "علم ولذلك قال الله عز وجل إن المنافقين في الدرك الأسفل من النار لأنهم جحدوا بعد العلم وجعل اليهود شرا من النصارى مع أنهم ما جعلوا لله سبحانه ولدا ولا قالوا إنه ثالث ثلاثة إلا أنهم أنكروا بعد المعرفة إذ قال الله يعرفونه كما يعرفون أبناءهم وقال تعالى فلما جاءهم ما عرفوا كفروا به فلعنة الله على الكافرين وقال تعالى في قصة بلعام بن باعوراء واتل عليهم نبأ الذي آتيناه آياتنا فانسلخ منها فأتبعه الشيطان فكان من الغاوين حتى قال فمثله كمثل الكلب إن تحمل عليه يلهث أو تتركه يلهث فكذلك العالم الفاجر فإن بلعام أوتي كتاب", "english": "I say: The Prophet — peace and blessings be upon him — said: \"The scholar will indeed be tormented with a torment at which the inhabitants of the Fire will circle around, marveling at the severity of his punishment.\" By it he meant the wicked scholar.", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "section_title_ar": "Section 50", "section_title_en": "The Worldly Scholar's Punishment and the Marks of the Hereafter-Scholars", "section_index": 50, "anchors": "- Root: فجر (fajara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To split, cleave, or rend something widely; to break open\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The splitting of something with wide rupture — from which the dawn (al-fajr) is named, as it splits the night; used for both physical and moral rupture\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the cleaving of water or the breaking of dawn. Metaphorical: the eruption of punishment upon one suddenly and overwhelmingly\n- Root: علم (ʿalima)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Knowledge; the faculty of apprehending a thing as it truly is\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Twofold: (1) apprehension of a thing's essence; (2) judgment upon a thing affirming or denying something of it. The scholar (al-ʿālim) is one who possesses such apprehension\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: knowledge as certain apprehension. Metaphorical: the \"signs\" (maʿālim) of the path — that by which one is guided; the scholar is a \"sign\" pointing either to guidance or to ruin\n- Root: دنا (dana)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be near, to draw close; al-dunyā (the nearer world) as that which is immediate and low\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Proximity by essence or by judgment; used of place, time, and station. The \"nearer\" (al-adnā) is contrasted with the greater and the better\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: nearness. Metaphorical: baseness and lowness — the dunyā as the inferior, the contemptible\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The particle innamā restricts the predicate exclusively to its subject, as in \"the winners and the brought-near are only the scholars of the Hereafter,\" effecting complete exclusion of all else.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "146": [{"arabic": "لله الدين الخالص وقال تعالى إلا الذين تابوا وأصلحوا واعتصموا بالله وأخلصوا دينهم لله وقال تعالى فمن كان يرجو لقاء ربه فليعمل عملا صالحا ولا يشرك بعبادة ربه أحدا نزلت فيمن يعمل لله ويحب أن يحمد عليه وقال النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم ثلاث لا يغل عليهن قلب رجل مسلم إخلاص العمل لله (1) وعن مصعب بن سعد عن أبيه قال ظن أبي أن له فضلا على من هو دونه من أصحاب رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم فقال النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم إنما نصر الله عز وجل هذه الأمة بضعفائها ودعوتهم وإخلاصهم (2) وعن الحسن قال قال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم يقول الله تعالى الإخلا", "english": "God Most High said: {وما أمروا إلا ليعبدوا الله مخلصين له الدين} \"And they were not commanded except to worship God, devoting their religion purely to Him.\" And He said: {ألا لله الدين الخالص} \"Verily, to God belongs the pure religion.\" And He, Most High, said: {إلا الذين تابوا وأصلحوا واعتصموا بالله وأخلصوا دينهم لله} \"Except those who repent, and make amends, and hold fast to God, and make their religion pure for God.\" And He, Most High, said: {فمن كان يرجو لقاء ربه فليعمل عملا صالحا ولا يشرك بعبادة ربه أحدا} \"So whoever hopes for the meeting with his Lord, let him do righteous work, and not associate anyone in the worship of his Lord.\" This was revealed concerning the one who works for God but loves to be praised for it.", "book_title": "Intention, Sincerity and Truthfulness", "section_title_ar": "Section 1217", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section on Sincerity (Ikhlas): Its Virtue and the Peril of Ostentation (Riya')", "section_index": 1217, "anchors": "- Root: أخلَصَ (akhlaṣa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is purity and clarity; khāliṣ is that which is unmixed and free from adulteration—pure gold, pure milk, pure friendship.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological usage, ikhlāṣ is the purification of one's act and intention (niyyah) exclusively for God, stripping away every other motive or partner. It is the essential condition for the acceptance of worship.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage refers to the physical purity of a substance; metaphorical (Majaz) usage transfers this to the heart and deed, signifying the purification of intention from all that is not God.\n- Root: دين (dīn)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning includes obedience, recompense, judgment, and the established way or custom.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The \"religion\" (dīn) of God is the way of obedience and submission to Him; \"pure religion\" (al-dīn al-khāliṣ) is that which is free from shirk (associationism) and hypocrisy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the debt or obligation one owes; Metaphorical: the total submission and worship owed exclusively to the Creator.\n- Root: راءى (rā'ā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: From the root رأى (ra'ā), to see. The third form implies showing off, making one's deeds seen by others.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Riya' is the seeking of status and praise from creatures, which nullifies the sincerity of the act directed to the Creator.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to make something visible to the eye; Metaphorical: to perform an act of worship not for God, but so that people may see it and praise the doer.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Restriction and Focused Predication (باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما) — The particle \"innamā\" (إنما) restricts the predicate to the subject alone, negating all else. This is critical in the opening verse: \"وما أمروا إلا ليعبدوا الله\" — the restriction (hasr) via \"illā\" (except) confines their command solely to the worship of God with pure sincerity.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Intention, Sincerity and Truthfulness'", "badge": "Insight"}], "147": [{"arabic": "أكبر فقال تعالى فاذكروني أذكركم واشكروا لي ولا تكفرون وقال تعالى ما يفعل الله بعذابكم إن شكرتم وآمنتم وقال تعالى وسنجزي الشاكرين وقال عز وجل إخبارا عن إبليس اللعين لأقعدن لهم صراطك المستقيم قيل هو طريق الشكر ولعلو رتبة الشكر طعن اللعين في الخلق فقال ولا تجد اكثرم شاكرين وقال تعالى وقليل من عبادي الشكور وقد قطع الله تعالى بالمزيد مع الشكر ولم يستثن فقال تعالى لئن شكرتم لأزيدنكم واستثنى في خمسة أشياء في الإغناء والإجابة والرزق والمغفرة والتوبة فقال تعالى فسوف يغنيكم الله من فضله إن شاء {وقال} فيكشف ما تدعون إليه إن شاء {وقال} يرزق من يشاء بغ", "english": "So then, what we have mentioned concerning the treatment of patience (ṣabr) regarding whispered insinuation (waswās), regarding lust (shahwah), and regarding status (jāh) — annex this to what we have already set down concerning the canons of the paths of spiritual striving (mujāhadah) in the Book of the Discipline of the Soul (Kitāb Riyāḍat al-Nafs) from the Quarter of the Destroyers (rubʿ al-muhlikāt). Adopt it as your manual (dastūr) by which you may come to know the treatment of patience in all the categories which we have previously detailed; for the detailing of individual cases is prolix. Whoever observes gradual progression (tadrīj), patience will elevate him to a state wherein patience without it becomes as arduous as it once was with it — so his affairs become inverted, and what was once beloved to him becomes loathed, and what was once detested to him becomes a sweet, wholesome draught from which he cannot bear to be patient [in abstaining]. This is known only through experience (tajribah) and tasting (dhawq). It has an analogue in customary habits: for a boy is compelled to learning in the beginning by force, and so patience with play and patience with knowledge are both arduous for him — until, when his inner sight is opened and he finds intimacy with knowledge, the matter is inverted, and it becomes arduous for him to be patient in abstaining from knowledge, and to endure play. To this points what is narrated of one of the knowers (ʿārifīn) that he asked al-Shiblī: \"Patience — which of its kinds is the most severe?\" He said: \"Patience in God (fī Allāh).\" He said: \"No.\" He said: \"Patience for God (li-Allāh).\" He said: \"No.\" He said: \"Patience with God (maʿa Allāh).\" He said: \"No.\" He said: \"Then what?\" He said: \"Patience from God (ʿan Allāh).\" At that al-Shiblī cried out a cry by which his soul nearly perished. And it has been said concerning the meaning of His saying, the Exalted: {«اصبروا وصابروا ورابطوا»} \"Be patient, and vie in patience, and be steadfast\" (Q. 3:200): \"Be patient in God, vie in patience by God, and be steadfast with God.\" And it was said: \"Patience for God is sufficiency (ghināʾ); patience by God is endurance (baqāʾ); patience with God is fidelity (wafāʾ); and patience from God is abandonment (jafāʾ).\" And it was said concerning its meaning:", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 972", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 972: On Patience (al-Sabr) and the Commencement of Gratitude (al-Shukr)", "section_index": 972, "anchors": "- Root: صبر (ṣ-b-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of restraint and withholding; al-Khalil notes its application to holding back an animal from fodder, and to enduring constraint.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصبر: الإمساك في ضيق\" — the withholding of the self in constriction; it is a general term whose specific names (shajāʿah, ḥilm, etc.) vary by context, and its opposite is al-jazaʿ (panic/vexation).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"صبرت على ما أكره. وصبرت عما أحب\" — enduring what is disliked and restraining from what is loved. Metaphorical (majāz): \"صبرت يمينه\" — binding an oath strenuously; \"أصبر على الضرب من الأرض\" — more enduring than the earth.\n- Root: شكر (sh-k-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes manifestation and fullness; al-Ayn links it to an overflowing udder (shukrā).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الشكر: تصور النعمة وإظهارها\" — the perception of a blessing and its display; it is the opposite of kufr (covering/denial). It has three degrees: of the heart (perception), of the tongue (praise), and of the limbs (requital).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"شكرت لله تعالى نعمته\" — thanking God for His grace. Metaphorical: \"دابة شكور\" — a beast that thrives on little fodder, displaying its owner's care through its fatness.\n- Root: علم (ʿ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Knowledge as the apprehension of a thing's reality; al-Ayn treats it under the entry of umm (source/origin), indicating that knowledge is the root to which all else is annexed.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — apprehension of a thing by its true reality, either by grasping its essence or by affirming/denying a predicate of it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"ما علمت بخبرك: ما شعرت به\" — to be aware/perceive. It is also used for the landmarks (maʿālim) of the road, i.e., traces by which one is guided.\n- Root: وقد (w-q-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The kindling and blazing of fire; al-Ayn records it under the entry of w-q-d with the sense of fuel and flame.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"وقدت النار تقد وقودا ووقدا\" — the fire blazed; al-wuqūd is both the fuel and the resulting flame, as in {«وقودها الناس والحجارة»} (Q. 2:24).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"وقدت النار وقودا ووقدا، واتقدت وتوقدت\" — the fire blazed and blazed fiercely. Metaphorical: \"قلب وقاد\" — a blazing, keen heart; \"وقدة الصيف\" — the blazing heat of summer.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Interposition between the operative word and its object (al-faṣl bayna al-ʿāmil wa al-maʿmūl fīhi) — governing the placement of circumstantial qualifiers (ḥāl) and prepositional phrases to preserve the intended semantic restriction, as in the verse \"اصبروا وصابروا ورابطوا\" where each imperative carries a distinct operative scope.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Insight"}], "170": [{"arabic": "يفعل وهم يسئلون وامتلأت مشكاة بعضهم نورا مقتبسا من نور الله تعالى في السموات والأرض وكان زيتهم أولا صافيا يكاد يضيء ولو لم تمسسه نار فمسته نار فاشتعل نورا على نور فأشرقت أقطار الملكوت بين أيديهم بنور ربها فأدركوا الأمور كلها كما هي عليه فقيل لهم تأدبوا بآداب الله تعالى واسكنوا وإذا", "english": "...in the light of the sun, but due to a weakness in the eyesight of the bats. Thus those whose eyes had been opened to behold its majesty were compelled to borrow, from the lowly depths of the world of those who converse in languages, an expression that might convey, from the first principles of its realities, something exceedingly feeble. So they borrowed for it the name \"power\" (qudrah), and we, by reason of their borrowing, grew bold in speech, and said: \"To God most high there is an attribute which is power, and from it issue creation and invention.\" Then creation, in its existence, divides into kinds, and into specific attributes; and the source of the division of these kinds and their being distinguished by their specific attributes is another attribute, for which was borrowed, in like manner to the necessity that preceded, the expression \"will\" (mashī'ah). For it suggests of it something obscure to those who converse in languages—which are but letters and sounds by which people understand one another—and the inadequacy of the word \"will\" to indicate the inner reality (kunnah) of that attribute and its true nature is like the inadequacy of the word \"power.\" Then the acts issuing from power divided into what sweeps on to the terminus which is the goal of its wisdom, and into what halts short of the goal. And each of these had a relation to the attribute of will, by virtue of their reversion to the specifications (ikhtiṣāṣāt) through which the division and the differences are completed. So for the relation of that which attains its goal, the expression \"love\" (maḥabbah) was borrowed; and for the relation of that which halts short of its goal, the expression \"aversion\" (karāhah) was borrowed. And it was said that both of these are included within the description of will, but each has another particular property in its relation, which the words \"love\" and \"aversion\" suggest of them as something summarized for those who seek understanding from words and languages. Then His servants—who are also of His creation and invention—divided into: one for whom the eternal will (al-mashī'ah al-azaliyyah) had previously decreed that He would use him to halt His wisdom short of its goal, this being compulsion (qahr) in their regard, by the subjugation of motives and incitements upon them; and one for whom it had previously been decreed in eternity that He would use him to drive His wisdom to its goal in certain matters. So each of the two groups had a particular relation to the will; and for the relation of those used in completing wisdom through them, the expression \"good pleasure\" (riḍā) was borrowed; and for those by whom the causes of wisdom were halted short of its goal, the expression \"wrath\" (ghaḍab) was borrowed. Thus there appeared, upon him against whom wrath was decreed in eternity, an act by which wisdom was halted short of its goal; for this, the expression \"ingratitude\" (kufrān) was borrowed, and it was followed by the infliction of curse and blame as an increase in chastisement. And there appeared, upon him whom He approved in eternity, an act by which wisdom swept on to its goal; for this, the expression \"thanksgiving\" (shukr) was borrowed, and it was followed by the robe of praise and eulogy, as an increase in good pleasure, acceptance, and approach. So the outcome was that He most high gave beauty, then praised; and gave chastisement, then made ugly and destroyed. And His likeness is that a king should cleanse his servant of his filth, then clothe him in the finest of his garments; and when he has perfected his adornment, he says: \"O beautiful one, how beautiful you are, and how beautiful your garments, and how clean your face!\"—so that in reality, he is the one who beautifies, and he is the one who praises beauty; thus he is the praised one in every case. And it is as though, in meaning, he praised none but himself; the servant is merely the target of praise in respect of the outward appearance and form. Even so were the matters in eternity, and even so do causes and effects string themselves in sequence, by the decreeing of the Lord of lords and the Causer of causes. And that was not by chance or deliberation, but by will, and wisdom, and a true judgment, and a decisive command, for which the word \"decree\" (qaḍā') was borrowed. And it was said: it is like the glance of the eye, or nearer still. Then the seas of measured destinies (maqādīr) overflowed by the ruling of that decisive decree, with what predetermination (taqdīr) had previously passed. So for the ordering of the individual determined things (al-maqdūrāt), one upon another, the word \"measure\" (qadar) was borrowed. Thus the word \"decree\" (qaḍā') stood in correspondence to the one, unitary command, and the word \"measure\" (qadar) stood in correspondence to the detailed differentiation extending without end. And it was said that nothing of all this lies outside decree and measure. Then it occurred to some servants to ask: \"Why did the division necessitate this differentiation, and how did justice cohere with this disparity and preference?\" And some of them, due to their deficiency, could not bear to observe the inner reality of this matter or to encompass its totality; so they were bridled, from what they could not bear to plunge into its deep, with the bridle of prevention, and it was said to them: \"Be still—for this you were not created. He is not questioned about what He does, but they shall be questioned\" {«لا يسئل عما يفعل وهم يسئلون»}. And the niche (mishkāh) of others was filled with light borrowed from the light of God most high in the heavens and the earth; and their oil was at first pure, well-nigh luminous, even had no fire touched it; then fire touched it, and it blazed, light upon light, and the regions of the dominion (malakūt) shone before them with the light of their Lord. So they perceived all things as they truly are, and it was said to them: \"Be disciplined with the disciplines of God most high, and be still.\" And when...", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 988", "section_title_en": "The Borrowed Language of Divine Attributes: On the Inadequacy of Human Terms for the Realities of Qaḍā', Qadar, and the Eternal Decree", "section_index": 988, "anchors": "- Root: قدر (q-d-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes measuring, determining, and ordaining a thing's extent and limit; al-Farahidi records its sense of precise appraisal and decreeing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"القدرة\" when ascribed to God negates incapacity ('ajz) absolutely; it is not a modality of acquisition as in creatures, but the sheer source whence creation issues—here Ghazali uses it as a borrowed term (isti'ārah) for an ineffable divine reality.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah) sense is \"ذو قدرة\" (possessor of power); metaphorical (majāz) extension appears in \"فرس بعيد القدر\" (a horse of far-reaching stride)—so too here, \"القدر\" is extended to the serial ordering of ordained particulars, not a spatial or physical measure.\n- Root: قسم (q-s-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Division and apportioning of shares; al-Farahidi ties it to partitioning lots.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"القسم: إفراز النصيب\" — the separation of portions; Ghazali exploits this to show how creation's division into kinds and specific attributes demands a further attribute (the will) that specifies each portion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: dividing wealth among parties; metaphorical: \"قلبه متقسم\" (his heart is divided by cares)—likewise, the division of acts under the will is a conceptual, not physical, partition.\n- Root: لفظ (l-f-ẓ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Utterance as a casting out from the mouth; al-Farahidi notes its source-verb sense of ejection.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اللفظ بالكلام مستعار من: لفظ الشيء من الفم\" — speech-utterance is borrowed from physical ejection; Ghazali applies this to show how terms like \"power,\" \"will,\" \"love,\" and \"wrath\" are merely cast-out approximations, not containers of divine essence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Not separately cited; the root's rhetorical force lies in its inadequacy—words are sounds and letters, unable to convey the \"kenne\" (inner reality) of the divine.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Ghazali's repeated \"إنما\" constructions (e.g., \"وإنما العبد هدف الثناء\") restrict the predicate to a single focused truth, isolating the apparent agent from the real one, per Sibawayh's rule that \"إنما\" confines judgment to what follows it, negating all else.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم يستغفر للعالم ما في السموات والأرض (3) وأي منصب يزيد على منصب من تشتغل ملائكة السموات والأرض بالاستغفار له وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم إن الحكمة تزيد الشريف شرفا وترفع المملوك حتى يدرك مدارك الملوك (4) وقد نبه بهذا على ثمراته في الدنيا ومعلوم أن الآخرة خير وأبقى وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم خصلتان لا يكونان في منافق حسن سمت وفقه في الدين (5) ولا تشكن في الحديث لنفاق بعض فقهاء الزمان فإنه ما أراد به الفقه الذي ظننته وسيأتي معنى الفقه وأدنى درجات الفقيه أن يعلم أن الآخرة خير من الدنيا", "english": "How He, Glorified and Exalted, began with Himself, then seconded with the angels, and thirded with the people of knowledge — and sufficient for you is this as honor, excellence, splendor, and nobility. And God, Exalted, said: {«يرفع الله الذين آمنوا منكم والذين أوتوا العلم درجات»} — \"God will raise those who have believed among you and those who have been given knowledge, in degrees.\" Ibn ʿAbbās, may God be pleased with them both, said: For the scholars there are degrees above the believers, seven hundred degrees, and between each two degrees is a distance of five hundred years' journey.", "book_title": "Contemplation", "section_title_ar": "Section 4", "section_title_en": "The Book of Knowledge — Section 4: The Excellence of Knowledge and the Scholars", "section_index": 4, "anchors": "- Root: علم (ʿ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of knowing, perceiving a thing in its true reality; the act of apprehension and judgment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality, divided into apprehension of the essence and judgment of existence or non-existence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage denotes genuine knowing; metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to signs, landmarks, and traces that guide to knowledge.\n- Root: درج (d-r-j)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Degrees, steps, ascending stations; the root denotes gradation and progressive elevation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الدرجة نحو المنزلة، لكن يقال للمنزلة: درجة إذا اعتبرت بالصعود\" — a degree is a station considered by ascent, not horizontal extension.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical steps of ascent. Metaphorical: \"لفلان درجة رفيعة\" — lofty rank; \"امش في مدارج الحق\" — walk in the paths of truth.\n- Root: يوم (y-w-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A day; a period of time from sunrise to sunset, or any duration whatsoever.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اليوم يعبر به عن وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها. وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — the day denotes the time of the sun's rising to its setting, and may denote any period of time whatsoever.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the solar day. Metaphorical: \"ذكر في أيام العرب كذا\" — historical battles and events; \"وذكرهم بأيام الله\" — His mighty deeds against the disbelievers.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction and Focused Predication) — The particle \"innamā\" restricts and confines the predicate to the subject alone, as in {«إنما يخشى الله من عباده العلماء»}, where fear of God is restricted exclusively to the scholars.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Contemplation'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "171": [{"arabic": "السلام فيأتون عيسى فيقولون يا عيسى أنت رسول الله وكلمته ألقاها إلى مريم وروح منه وكلمت الناس في المهد اشفع لنا إلى ربك ألا ترى ما نحن فيه فيقول عيسى عليه السلام إن ربي غضب اليوم غضبا لم يغضب قبله مثله ولن يغضب بعده مثله ولم يذكر ذنبا نفسي نفسي اذهبوا إلى غيري اذهبوا إلى محمد صلى الله عليه وسلم فيأتون فيقولون يا محمد أنت رسول الله وخاتم النبيين وغفر الله لك ما تقدم من ذنبك وما تأخر اشفع لنا إلى ربك ألا ترى ما نحن فيه فأنطلق فآتي تحت العرش فأقع ساجدا لربي ثم يفتح الله لي من محامده وحسن الثناء عليه شيئا لم يفتحه على أحد قبلي ثم", "english": "The evidences for intercession in the Qurʾān and the reports (akhbār) are numerous. God—exalted be He—said: {«وَلَسَوْفَ يُعْطِيكَ رَبُّكَ فَتَرْضَىٰ»} [And verily thy Lord shall give thee, and thou shalt be well-pleased] (93:5). ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ narrated that the Messenger of God—peace and blessings be upon him—recited the saying of Abraham—peace be upon him—{«رَبِّ إِنَّهُنَّ أَضْلَلْنَ كَثِيرًا مِّنَ النَّاسِ ۖ فَمَن تَبِعَنِي فَإِنَّهُ مِنِّي ۖ وَمَنْ عَصَانِي فَإِنَّكَ غَفُورٌ رَّحِيمٌ»} [My Lord, verily they have led astray many of mankind; so whoso follows me, verily he is of me; and whoso disobeys me, verily Thou art Forgiving, Merciful] (14:36), and the saying of Jesus—peace be upon him—{«إِن تُعَذِّبْهُمْ فَإِنَّهُمْ عِبَادُكَ»} [If Thou chastise them, verily they are Thy servants] (5:118). Then he raised his hands and said: \"My community, my community!\" Then he wept. So God—mighty and majestic—said: \"O Gabriel, go to Muhammad and ask him what makes him weep.\" Gabriel came to him and asked him, and he informed him—and God knows best concerning him. Then He said: \"O Gabriel, go to Muhammad and say to him: 'Verily We shall please thee concerning thy community, and shall not cause thee grief.'\"", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1346", "section_title_en": "On the Reality of Intercession (Shafāʿah) and the Means of Attaining It", "section_index": 1346, "anchors": "- Root: شفع (Sh-F-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes pairing/joining (al-ḍamm), whence shafāʿah = the intercessor joining his own plea to the petitioner's, making it two (shafʿ) rather than one (watr).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance — intercession is a divinely granted honor (jāh) bestowed upon prophets and saints, not an independent power; its efficacy is wholly contingent upon divine will and pleasure.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) — \"شفعت الشيء\" (I made it even/pair). Metaphorical (majāz) — the intercessor \"pairs\" his rank with the sinner's need, elevating the latter through the former's proximity to God.\n- Root: خبأ (Kh-B-ʾ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To conceal, hide, store away secretly (khabaʾa). Implies deliberate concealment of something precious or dangerous within a locus.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance — God's \"hiding\" (takhbiʾah) of His friendship (wilāyah), wrath (ghaḍab), and pleasure (riḍā) within creatures and acts respectively teaches epistemic humility: no outward judgment can access the hidden decree.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — physical concealment. Metaphorical — the inscrutability of divine judgment (ḥukm) regarding ultimate states (ʿawāqib), rendering contempt for any person or act a potential act of defiance against God's hidden will.\n- Root: عله (not applicable here; avoided per instruction)\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — Governing the periodic suspension of meaning across lengthy narrative sequences (e.g., the extended ḥadīth of intercession), where the syntactic resolution (jawāb) is deferred to heighten rhetorical impact, yet remains grammatically anchored.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "الله عليه وسلم من جاءني زائرا لا يهمه إلا زيارتي كان حقا على الله سبحانه أن أكون له شفيعا (4) فمن قصد زيارة المدينة فليصل على رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم في طريقه كثيرا فإذا وقع بصره على حيطان المدينة وأشجاره قال اللهم هذا حرم رسولك فاجعله لي وقاية من النار وأمانا من العذاب وسوء الحساب وليغتسل قبل الدخول من بئر الحرة وليتطيب وليلبس أنظف ثيابه فإذا دخلها فليدخلها متواضعا معظما وليقل بسم الله وعلى ملة رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم رب أدخلني مدخل صدق وأخرجني مخرج صدق واجعل لي من لدنك سلطانا نصيرا ثم يقصد المسجد وي", "english": "I say: The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Whoever visits me after my death, it is as though he visited me during my life.\" {من زارني بعد وفاتي فكأنما زارني في حياتي}", "book_title": "Mysteries of Hajj", "section_title_ar": "Section 210", "section_title_en": "Section 210 — The Etiquette of Visiting the Prophet's Mosque and Grave in Medina", "section_index": 210, "anchors": "- Root: زور (z-w-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of inclining toward, visiting, or intending a place or person; the visitor (zā'ir) is one who turns away from his own affairs toward another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological context, ziyārah (visitation) of the Prophet ﷺ after death is an act of spiritual devotion (taqarrub), not a mere physical journey; its reward is tied to the sincerity of intention (niyyah).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah) usage denotes physical approach; metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to seeking intercession (shafā'ah) and spiritual connection with the visited.\n- Root: سلم (s-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Root denotes safety, peace, and deliverance from harm; the greeting \"al-salāmu ʿalaykum\" is a prayer for the recipient's immunity from all deficiency.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The greeting upon the Prophet ﷺ is a form of supplication (duʿā') and acknowledgment of his rank; it is also a renewal of the covenant (mīthāq) of obedience.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage is the exchange of safety; metaphorical usage in the visitation context signifies the visitor's submission and the Prophet's reciprocal blessing.\n- Root: شفع (sh-f-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Root denotes pairing or joining; shafā'ah (intercession) is the act of the intercessor joining his own plea to that of the petitioner.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In Kalam, shafā'ah is a divinely granted honor (karāmah) to the Prophet ﷺ, conditional upon divine will; it is not an independent power but a mediated mercy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: making a number even by adding one; metaphorical: the intercessor's elevation of the petitioner's rank before the king.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Prepositional Interposition (al-faṣl bayna al-jār wa al-majrūr) — The text employs extended vocative and prepositional structures (e.g., \"al-salāmu ʿalayka yā Rasūl Allāh\") where the syntactic pause after each invocation is permissible and rhetorically deliberate, mirroring the classical allowance for separation between the governing particle and its object for emphasis.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Mysteries of Hajj'", "badge": "Afterlife"}, {"arabic": "معناه أن لا يكونا له بل أن يكونا له ولا يشغلانه وهو إشارة إلى قول أبي سليمان الداراني ما شغلك عن الله من أهل ومال وولد فهو عليك مشئوم وبالجملة لم ينقل عن أحد الترغيب عن النكاح مطلقا إلا مقرونا بشرط وأما الترغيب في النكاح فقد ورد مطلقا ومقرونا بشرط فلنكشف الغطاء عنه بحصر آفات النكاح وفوائده آفات النكاح وفوائده وفيه فوائد خمسة الولد وكسر الشهوة وتدبير المنزل وكثرة العشيرة ومجاهدة النفس بالقيام بهن الفائدة الأولى الولد وهو الأصل وله وضع النكاح والمقصود إبقاء النسل وأن لا يخلو العالم عن", "english": "وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم: \"لا يلقى الله أحد بذنب أعظم من جهالة أهله.\"", "book_title": "Etiquette of Marriage", "section_title_ar": "Section 296", "section_title_en": "Section 296", "section_index": 296, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Etiquette of Marriage'", "badge": "Insight"}], "172": [{"arabic": "ربكم الأعلى إذ استنكف أن يكون عبدا لله ولذلك قال تعالى إن الذين يستكبرون عن عبادتي سيدخلون جهنم داخرين وقال تعالى لن يستنكف المسيح أن يكون عبدا لله ولا الملائكة المقربون الآية وقال تعالى وإذا قيل لهم اسجدوا للرحمن قالوا وما الرحمن أنسجد لما تأمرنا وزادهم نفورا القسم الثاني التكبر على الرسل من حيث تعزز النفس وترفعها على الانقياد لبشر مثل سائر الناس وذلك تارة يصرف عن الفكر والاستبصار فيبقى في ظلمة الجهل بكبره فيمتنع عن الانقياد وهو ظان أنه محق فيه وتارة يمتنع مع المعرفة ولكن لا تطاوع", "english": "Know that the one against whom pride is directed is either God—mighty and majestic—or His messengers, or the rest of His creation. Man has been created unjust and ignorant {«وخلق الإنسان ظلوما جهولا»}, so at times he grows proud against the creation, and at times he grows proud against the Creator. Thus, pride, considered with respect to the one against whom it is directed, falls into three divisions.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Pride and Conceit", "section_title_ar": "Section 855", "section_title_en": "Exposition of the One Against Whom Pride Is Directed, Its Degrees, Its Divisions, and the Fruits of Pride Within It", "section_index": 855, "anchors": "- Root: كبر (k-b-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes magnitude, whether physical size, age, or rank; \"الكبرة\" specifically marks the state of advanced age or an ingrained, chronic condition.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الكبير والصغير من الأسماء المتضايفة\" — bigness and smallness are relative terms. In the theological context, pride (الكبر) is the soul's self-magnification against another, a relational vice that distorts the creature's standing before the Creator.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage concerns physical or quantitative magnitude. Metaphorical (Majaz) usage transfers this to moral and spiritual stature, as in \"كبر علي ذلك\" (it weighed heavily upon him) — here, the soul's swelling against divine command is a metaphorical arrogation of greatness.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Prepositional Interposition (باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — Governing the syntactic tension between the agent of pride (المتكبر) and its object (المتكبر عليه), where the preposition \"على\" carries the full weight of opposition and elevation, structuring the periodic sentences that follow.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Pride and Conceit'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "عن عبادتي سيدخلون جهنم داخرين وقال تعالى لن يستنكف المسيح أن يكون عبدا لله ولا الملائكة المقربون الآية وقال تعالى وإذا قيل لهم اسجدوا للرحمن قالوا وما الرحمن أنسجد لما تأمرنا وزادهم نفورا القسم الثاني التكبر على الرسل من حيث تعزز النفس وترفعها على الانقياد لبشر مثل سائر الناس وذلك تارة يصرف عن الفكر والاستبصار فيبقى في ظلمة الجهل بكبره فيمتنع عن الانقياد وهو ظان أنه محق فيه وتارة يمتنع مع المعرفة ولكن لا تطاوعه نفسه للإنقياد للحق والتواضع للرسل كما حكى الله قولهم أنؤمن لبشرين مثلنا {وقولهم} إن أنتم إلا بشر مثلنا ولئن أطعتم بشرا مثلكم إنكم إذا لخاس", "english": "Know that the one against whom pride is directed is either God—mighty and majestic—or His messengers, or the rest of His creation. Man has been created unjust and ignorant {«وخلق الإنسان ظلوما جهولا»}, so at times he grows proud against the creation, and at times he grows proud against the Creator. Thus, pride, considered with respect to the one against whom it is directed, falls into three divisions.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Pride and Conceit", "section_title_ar": "Section 855", "section_title_en": "Exposition of the One Against Whom Pride Is Directed, Its Degrees, Its Divisions, and the Fruits of Pride Within It", "section_index": 855, "anchors": "- Root: كبر (k-b-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes magnitude, whether physical size, age, or rank; \"الكبرة\" specifically marks the state of advanced age or an ingrained, chronic condition.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الكبير والصغير من الأسماء المتضايفة\" — bigness and smallness are relative terms. In the theological context, pride (الكبر) is the soul's self-magnification against another, a relational vice that distorts the creature's standing before the Creator.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage concerns physical or quantitative magnitude. Metaphorical (Majaz) usage transfers this to moral and spiritual stature, as in \"كبر علي ذلك\" (it weighed heavily upon him) — here, the soul's swelling against divine command is a metaphorical arrogation of greatness.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Prepositional Interposition (باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — Governing the syntactic tension between the agent of pride (المتكبر) and its object (المتكبر عليه), where the preposition \"على\" carries the full weight of opposition and elevation, structuring the periodic sentences that follow.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Pride and Conceit'", "badge": "Insight"}]}, "wordTiming": {"abdulbasit": {"1": [[1, 400, 3035], [2, 3035, 4697], [3, 4697, 633